1 DOCUMENT
2 TITL Barton-Speechley Very Large Pedigree
2 DESCRIPTION I began compiling this in June 2009, and continue to improve it almost
3 CONC  daily. It is in large part constructed from the painstaking genealogi
3 CONC cal research of the following individuals:
3 CONT
3 CONT * My aunt June Rowe, who has explored my mother's side of the family t
3 CONC ree in great detail (the information, in a paf file, was passed along
3 CONC to me by my mother);
3 CONT
3 CONT * (Alfred) James (Robert) Sneyd in New Zealand, webmaster of http://ww
3 CONC w.burningviolin.org/family/ (and also see http://www.math.auckland.ac.
3 CONC nz/Directory/profile.php?upi=jsne010): another Boileau descendant who
3 CONC has himself (and don't we all, really) compiled information from other
3 CONC  sources, but has clearly put a ridiculously large amount of work into
3 CONC  it. Scholarly work too, with clear references that others can check f
3 CONC or themselves. It is from his work that I learned of the pleasing conn
3 CONC ection to William the Conqueror.
3 CONT
3 CONT (In my own defence, I put a lot of work into rearranging these vast ta
3 CONC ngled pedigrees into some meaningful shape!)
3 CONT
3 CONT I've also pulled in research from:
3 CONT
3 CONT * my dad Nicholas James Barton (whose voluminous research I have only
3 CONC begun to incorporate in digital form), including a huge chunk about th
3 CONC e Elliott family from:
3 CONT
3 CONT ** The Elliots: The Story of a Border Clan, a genealogical history. By
3 CONC  The Dowager Lady Eliott of Stobs and Sir Arthur Eliott, 11th Baronet
3 CONC of Stobs. Published 1974. ISBN 0 85422 081 X.
3 CONT
3 CONT * Distant cousin Malcolm Barton, who sent me a copy of:
3 CONT
3 CONT ** Chris Durrant's draft electronic edition "The Hack Family Documents
3 CONC "
3 CONT
3 CONT * Helen's mum Gill Speechley
3 CONT
3 CONT ...as well as conducting my own interviews with:
3 CONT
3 CONT * Helen's grandparents Frank and Kay Speechley,
3 CONT
3 CONT * and also, because she was there, her uncle Alan Speechley's mother-i
3 CONC n-law Margaret Jean Vear, whose memory of her family tree bordered on
3 CONC the uncanny.
3 CONT
3 CONT
3 CONT It is, obviously enough, a massive work in progress, and always will b
3 CONC e. But I'm starting to really enjoy it.
3 CONT I would ask that nobody else distribute this file but me. This is for
3 CONC three main reasons:
3 CONT 1) Privacy. There are living individuals on the tree who may not want
3 CONC their family details spreading in an uncontrolled fashion.
3 CONT 2) Respect for the source researchers, who one suspects might not want
3 CONC  all their hard work spreading in an uncontrolled fashion.
3 CONT 3) So that I can ensure that people receive the latest and most up-to-
3 CONC date version of the file.
3 CONT So if you'd like to distribute this file to someone else, please get i
3 CONC n touch with me at dbhbarton@googlemail.com.
3 CONT If, on the other hand, you've received this file from somebody other t
3 CONC han me, I'd appreciate it if you would contact me at the same email ad
3 CONC dress.
3 CONT
3 CONT Dr. David Bernard Holman Barton, August 2009
