1 NOTE In http://www.chichesterquakers.org/John%20Barton.htm:
2 CONT
2 CONT A family letter states that 'Fanny Rickman is likely to add to John's
2 CONC happiness by making her the mistress of Stoughton. Her relatives are a
2 CONC ll of the Society. She is considered a serious young woman, very unaff
2 CONC ected in her manners. His mother thinks the connexion will not throw J
2 CONC ohn any more from Friends.’  Fanny and John were engaged on 18th May 1
2 CONC 828 and married on 23rd July at Smeeth, from Smeeth Hill House, near A
2 CONC shford. On 29th July they left Dover for a honeymoon on the Continent.
2 CONC  Fanny's guardian, James Rickman, was concemed as to whether John coul
2 CONC d provide Fanny with 'those comforts the wife of a person of such prop
2 CONC erty should have', as her own income would only amount to about £110 p
2 CONC er annum and Barton apparently had a reputation for living very simply
2 CONC ; although in 1827 he estimated his income and outgoings to be £770 an
2 CONC d £600 respectively. Fanny was some 18 years younger than her husband
2 CONC and she produced nine children between 1829 and 1842.
2 CONT
2 CONT Name Frances given in NJB Papers ('Barton Family Tree')
2 CONT
2 CONT Also in "The Hack Family Documents by Chris Durrant", Figure O.9
2 CONT
2 CONT In Rev. John Barton's Family Tree book, p10, gives date of death. Also
2 CONC  lists father (incorrectly?) as "John Rickman of Stoughton & East Leig
2 CONC h"
2 CONT
