2 CONT
2 CONT Bernard Barton (January 31, 1784 – February 19, 1849) was known as the
2 CONC  Quaker poet.
2 CONT Born of Quaker parentage in London, educated at a Quaker school in Ips
2 CONC wich, passed nearly all his life at Woodbridge, for the most part as a
2 CONC  clerk in a bank. His wife died at the end of their first year of marr
2 CONC iage.
2 CONT He became the friend of Southey, Lamb, and other men of letters. His c
2 CONC hief works are The Convict's Appeal (1818), a protest against the seve
2 CONC rity of the criminal code of the time, and Household Verses (1845), wh
2 CONC ich came under the notice of Sir R. Peel, through whom he obtained a p
2 CONC ension of £100.
2 CONT With the exception of some hymns, his works are now nearly forgotten,
2 CONC but he was a most amiable and estimable man—simple and sympathetic. Hi
2 CONC s best known hymns are Lamp of our feet, whereby we trace, Walk in the
2 CONC  light, so shalt thou know, Fear not, Zion's sons and daughters, Hath
2 CONC the invitation ended?, See we not beyond the portal?, Those who live i
2 CONC n love shall know.
2 CONT His daughter Lucy, who married Edward Fitzgerald, the translator of Om
2 CONC ar Khayyám, published a selection of his poems and letters, to which h
2 CONC er husband prefixed a biographical introduction.
2 CONT
2 CONT ___
2 CONT
2 CONT from http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GSln=barton&GSfn
2 CONC =bernard&GSbyrel=all&GSdyrel=all&GSob=n&GRid=6826133&:
2 CONT
2 CONT Poet. Like John Greenleaf Whittier in America, he is known as "The Qua
2 CONC ker Poet". He was born in Carlisle, but became apprenticed to a shopke
2 CONC eper in Suffolk. In 1806, he moved to Woodbridge in the same county, w
2 CONC here, with his brother, he founded a coal and corn merchants. At about
2 CONC  this time, he married ; unfortunately, within the year, his wife died
2 CONC  in childbirth. Barton then re-located to Liverpool, but soon returned
2 CONC  to Woodbridge to take up a post as clerk in Alexander's Bank. In his
2 CONC free time, he wrote ten books of poetry, many of his works having beco
2 CONC me hymns. Among his great friends was his fellow poet, Edward FitzGera
2 CONC ld (q.v.). In 1856, seven years after Barton's death, FitzGerald marri
2 CONC ed Barton's daughter, Lucy ; unfortunately, the marriage was not a suc
2 CONC cess, and the pair soon separated.
2 CONT
2 CONT ___
2 CONT from NJB:
2 CONT
2 CONT Half brother of John Barton
2 CONT
