2 DATE 14 NOV 1905
2 PLAC Mangere, Auckland
3 _XREF @place00819@
2 BAPTISM
3 DATE 1905
3 PLAC St James, Mangere
4 _XREF @place00820@
1 DEAT Y
2 DATE 22 JUL 2006
2 PLAC Ascot House, Devonport, Auckland
3 _XREF @place00821@
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE Called Pop.
2 CONT
2 CONT He began as a naval engineer. I have his Certificate of Competency as
2 CONC a First Class Engineer in the Merchant Service, dated 1 August, 1932,
2 CONC number 2864. From 1928 to 1939 he served on the Kaikorai, Tofua, Makur
2 CONC a, Monowai (four times), Marama, Poolta, Kaimiro, Maunganui, and Niaga
2 CONC ra. I have a copy of his service record for the Union Steam Ship Compa
2 CONC ny of New Zealand, Limited. It ends “We have pleasure in stating that
2 CONC during the time Mr. Sneyd was in the employ of our Company he proved h
2 CONC imself a capable and reliable Engineer, carrying out his duties at all
2 CONC  times to our entire satisfaction. Mr. Sneyd was on the Monowai during
2 CONC  the period this vessel was under Naval control and did not return to
2 CONC our service.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Served on the Monowai during the war. (According to his service record
2 CONC , he served from 23 Oct. 1939 to 14 June 1943. He was discharged on 16
2 CONC  Dec, 1943.). Chief engineer and Lieutenant-Commander. Signed on for h
2 CONC ostilities only. Didn’t stay with her as a merchant ship. After the wa
2 CONC r worked as a refrigeration engineer (chief engineer) at Turners and G
2 CONC rowers. Built cool stores in Dunedin, Whangarei, Christchurch, Mangere
2 CONC , other places.
2 CONT Met Granny through a friend who knew Nellie (lived at 146? 148? Victor
2 CONC ia Avenue). Helped with planting trees and building a glasshouse. Pop
2 CONC lived at Mangere then. Lived in Macintyre Road, went to Mangere Bridge
2 CONC  School. Halfback for the Mangere Senior League team. High school was
2 CONC Auckland Grammar.  Long walk to school. Marine engineering at Auckland
2 CONC  Board of Trade?
2 CONT Helped to convert the Monowai to a warship at beginning of war. Just m
2 CONC issed getting sunk in the Niagra just by the Hen and Chickens. Served
2 CONC first on the Niagra during training, etc, before obtaining his chief e
2 CONC ngineer certificate (Razmak was original name of Monowhai).
2 CONT Lived at Victoria Ave after getting married, but moved to Takapuna (a
2 CONC seaside holiday cottage then) during the war, when Dad was 7. Built th
2 CONC e second story and deck to the house. Keen tennis player, with Granny,
2 CONC  at the North Shore tennis club.
2 CONT
2 CONT He’s still going strong, still living in Takapuna, and we go to visit
2 CONC him often. He loves the company of the kids, particularly the babies.
2 CONC He’s the sweetest old man, never complaining about anything, always wi
2 CONC th a good word for everybody, and with the biggest smile. His short-te
2 CONC rm memory is completely shot so one tends to have the same conversatio
2 CONC n many, many times (within the space of 10 minutes). I’m told he was v
2 CONC ery interested in math in his youth, which I suppose explains why so m
2 CONC any of his children and grandchildren are mathematically inclined.
2 CONT
2 CONT As you can tell, I’m writing this over a period of a few years, but th
2 CONC is will be the last entry probably. Pop died on Saturday, 22 July, 200
2 CONC 6, at Ascot House, a rest home in Devonport, Auckland. He was  buried
2 CONC the following Thursday at the Schnapper Rock Road cemetary on the Nort
2 CONC h Shore.
2 CONT
2 CONT The last year or so he had been visibly failing, and Mum and Dad final
2 CONC ly decided he had to go into a rest home, at the end of May, 2006. He
2 CONC didn’t want to leave 35 Ewen St., where he had lived for so many years
2 CONC , but there really wasn’t any choice. He’d had a good innings there. H
2 CONC e was still living there when he turned 100; we had a big party for hi
2 CONC m. Letters came from the Queen, the Prime Minister, and a host of othe
2 CONC r self-important dignitaries. So nobody can really complain. Still, it
2 CONC  was a sad, sad thing to put him into a rest home. We suspected he wou
2 CONC ldn’t last long after that, and we were right, he didn’t. Just a few d
2 CONC ays after, he fell and cracked his head. A week or so in hospital then
2 CONC  back to the rest home, but he just wasn’t the same. He declined rapid
2 CONC ly, caught pneumonia, and died.
2 CONT
2 CONT The day he died Monique and I and the kids all went to visit him. He c
2 CONC ouldn’t talk all that well because of the pneumonia, but he was quite
2 CONC alert and interested in things. We told him how the kids had won their
2 CONC  soccer games, how we were getting new doors put into our house, and h
2 CONC ow the weather was lovely outside. As usual, he was full of smiles. Af
2 CONC ter a while he said that he was tired and wanted to sleep, so did we m
2 CONC ind going? Sleep well, I said, and left. He took me literally, and die
2 CONC d just a few minutes after.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was the passing of an era. He was the centre of Dad’s extended fami
2 CONC ly, even when he couldn’t remember all our names. We met at Ewen St.,
2 CONC had lunch on the balcony, and looked out over the sea. He did love to
2 CONC see us gathered. He’d ask about the conversation when he couldn’t hear
2 CONC . And now we won’t be able to tell him anything ever again.
2 CONT
2 CONT At his funeral I learned a lot about him. Alfred told us all how Pop h
2 CONC ad invented the world’s first cow-drawn skateboard. It was hilarious.
2 CONC It’s probably improper to laugh at funerals, but we did anyway. Lizzie
2 CONC  seemed to know the hymns so I just followed her. The coffin was surpr
2 CONC isingly heavy. Don’t fall into the grave I said. Don’t joke about it s
2 CONC aid the usher person, very seriously. Ooops, I thought. OK, no joking.
2 CONC  But Pop would have been the first to laugh if one of us fell in, I kn
2 CONC ow he would. It was a happy funeral, as these things go. Plenty of tea
2 CONC rs too, I suppose, but the old fellow had done us all proud.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cousin Rose wrote, and read, a poem:
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------
2 CONT Pop
2 CONT
2 CONT My one hundred-year old Pop
2 CONT has apparently, finally, died:
2 CONT I had never imagined he would
2 CONT ever cease his relentless living.
2 CONT At least not in my lifetime. Pop
2 CONT always was, and always would be.
2 CONT
2 CONT We all thought he would go on
2 CONT going on, perhaps even beyond
2 CONT the day of the apocalypse itself.
2 CONT Or at least, if he would succumb,
2 CONT his vanquisher would have been
2 CONT something mightier than pneumonia.
2 CONT
2 CONT My Pop, Our Pop, had defeated
2 CONT countless and dreadful antagonists:
2 CONT a ladder on a perilous lean, bombs
2 CONT on ships, sharks I would picture
2 CONT lurking in the waters around Rangitoto,
2 CONT the Japanese, colonial New Zealand.
2 CONT
2 CONT He probably thought it a great joke
2 CONT slipping away as he did, unexpectedly.
2 CONT Unaware were we who thought
2 CONT the dreaded lurgy a pitiable foe, for
2 CONT this was the man who had received
2 CONT a letter from the Queen of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT And he was an inimitable patriarch.
2 CONT He adored seeing us gathered home:
2 CONT on the terrace, or around the majestic
2 CONT mahogany dining table. He happily
2 CONT acknowledged the blame for the ensuing
2 CONT cacophany - there are a lot of us, Sneyds.
2 CONT
2 CONT And that was the way he liked it:
2 CONT a glass of gin and a twinkling in his blue,
2 CONT seafaring eyes, he would survey his
2 CONT brilliant and burgeoning, clamorous clan -
2 CONT I suppose, remembering Granny, with
2 CONT whom he traversed a remarkable century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rose Sneyd. July, 2006.
2 CONT --------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT After Pop’s death, I got his Masonic certificate ... things. I hadn’t
2 CONC know he was a Mason. Anyway, the certificates are priceless. You just
2 CONC couldn’t make this stuff up. Even the Society for Creative Anachronism
2 CONC  would have a hard time thinking up this sort of self-important pompou
2 CONC s crap. I kid you not, here’s an extract:
2 CONT
2 CONT “ O all Illustrious Grand Inspectors General; Most Valiant and Sublime
2 CONC  Princes of the Royal Secret; Grand Inquisitors Commander; Grand Elect
2 CONC ed Knights; Excellent and Perfect Princes Rose Croix; Grand, Ineffable
2 CONC , Sublime Free and Accepted Masons of every Degree of Masonry througho
2 CONC ut the Universe [not just the world, oh no. The UNIVERSE!!]... “
2 CONT And on it goes. Honestly, people who take this seriously really need t
2 CONC o grow up. I mean, I play computer games. I kill baddies with my blast
2 CONC y fire spells and magic swords. I watch Star Trek movies. OK, all very
2 CONC  juvenile. But do I take these things seriously? I don’t think so. Uh
2 CONC Uh. I guess that’s why I’m not a Mason.
2 CONT
2 CONT Oh, and if you’re a Mason reading this, and you’re offended, do please
2 CONC  let me know. I like to think I’m properly appreciated.
