1 NOTE From [BT]: Born at Beaminster, Dorsetshire, 1858. Buried at Waikumete
2 CONC cemetery, 1922, main cemetery on Great North Road in Auckland. Ellen O
2 CONC ctavia buried there also.
2 CONT Left school at the age of 12. HIs first job was as a delivery boy for
2 CONC his grandmother who was a corsetmaker. His first delivery was a pair o
2 CONC f “wedding” corsets to a neighbouring village. He hitched a ride on a
2 CONC farm cart but fell off in the mud with the corsets. He delivered them,
2 CONC  but they were returned to the maker and a new pair had to be made in
2 CONC a great hurry for the wedding.
2 CONT After this inauspicious start JSB entered the printing trade - at the
2 CONC age of 13 being employed in a printing office. After amassing a capita
2 CONC l of five pounds beside his fare, he came to New Zealand, arriving in
2 CONC 1878.
2 CONT After arriving in New Zealand he initially worked for the Rangitikei A
2 CONC dvocate, then in 1880 settled in Cambridge taking charge of the printi
2 CONC ng department of the Waikato Mail. The paper ceased to exist a year la
2 CONC ter and he opened a general printing, book, and stationery business. H
2 CONC e started the Waikato Advocate in 1895 , which was merged into the Wai
2 CONC kato Times when he purchased the latter paper.
2 CONT James was living in Cambridge at the time of the Tarawera eruption. Wi
2 CONC th the local people being alaremd at the noise, James rode his horse t
2 CONC o Hamilton to discover the cause of the alarming noise.
2 CONT Mayor of Cambridge, 1892-1895 (elections were then held annually). Lat
2 CONC er the family continued to live in Cambridge while he went to work in
2 CONC Hamilton by train. One story (unconfirmed) is that on his return home
2 CONC one day he found his house had been accidentally burned down. Move to
2 CONC Hamilton to live in 1898.
2 CONT Mayor of Hamilton 1905-1909, at which time he retired. During this tim
2 CONC e major issues included the purchase by council of the local gasworks,
2 CONC  inauguration of a drainage scheme, building a new traffic bridge, and
2 CONC  opening of the library.
2 CONT Chairman of the Board of Governors of Hamilton High School when it was
2 CONC  formed. Instrumental in starting the Hamilton theatres. Bond St. in H
2 CONC amilton named after him. He was instrumental in starting the Waikato W
2 CONC inter Show. In the first few years he used to bring the takings home e
2 CONC ach night, sleeping with the money under the mattress. As the takings
2 CONC increased, his wife Ellen objected and the bank was persuaded to stay
2 CONC open to take the money. During the first years everyone made a great e
2 CONC ffort to make a success of the venture, exhibiting in as many classes
2 CONC as possible with needlework and produce sections.
2 CONT After Sarah died, James met his second wife when she was travelling ar
2 CONC ound the world. She returned to England and the following year (1905)
2 CONC returned to New Zealand. They were married at All Saints Church, Ponso
2 CONC nby, Auckland, returning to Hamilton where they lived in Selkirk Stree
2 CONC t “Aroha House”.
2 CONT After retirement he moved to Auckland, living at 84 Victoria Ave (late
2 CONC r renumbered 148? 206?). The section had two acres of land and the hou
2 CONC se had previously been a farmhouse, but extensively renovated before p
2 CONC urchase. Continued to be actively involved in community affairs, and w
2 CONC as an active member of the Auckland Harbour Board, Education Board, Gr
2 CONC ammar School Board, and the Seddon Memorial Technical College (now ATI
2 CONC ). Had a lifelong interest in education, perhaps because he left schoo
2 CONC l himself at the age of 12.
2 CONT Four sons served overseas during the war, three being wounded. The fou
2 CONC rth, Gussie, was listed as missing in 1915. James never gave up hope t
2 CONC hat somewhere, somehow, Gussie would turn up. It was in 1922 when he w
2 CONC as just starting for a Harbour Board meeting that he received a letter
2 CONC  from the War Office saying that Gussie’s remains had been identified
2 CONC and buried in the War Cemetery. It was such a shock that James had a s
2 CONC troke, which some weeks later resulted in his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------
2 CONT From the biographical database of the NZ Dictionary of National Biogra
2 CONC phy. Emailed to me on 8 October, 2008:
2 CONT  
2 CONT Sources [R1] Obit, Waikato Times? 27/11/22, [R2] Waikato Times index c
2 CONC ards, [R3] Obit of Ellen Octavia Bond, Waikato Times? 19/12/55, [R4] C
2 CONC emetery transcript, Hamilton West Cemetery, [R5] "Astride the River" G
2 CONC ibbons 1977, [R6] Waikato Times 5/1/60 "Origin of Waikato Winter Show"
2 CONT  
2 CONT Country of Birth ENGLAND
2 CONT Region / Place of Birth Dorsetshire [R1]
2 CONT Occupations PRINTER, GENERAL-PRINTING-BUSINESS, BOOK-AND-STATIONARY-BU
2 CONC SINESS, NEWSPAPER-OWNER, OPERATED-PUBLIC-TRUST-OFFICE, [R1]
2 CONT Date of Death 26 Nov 1922 
2 CONT Country of Death NEW ZEALAND
2 CONT Region / Place of Death Auckland
2 CONT Career / Personality Summary Achievements: Mayor of both Cambridge and
2 CONC  Hamilton. Involved in public life of both communities. Considered a m
2 CONC ore forward thinking man than previous Hamilton mayors - encouraged th
2 CONC e establishment of Waikato Winter Show. He considered the decision of
2 CONC Frankton to remain outside Hamilton borough a backward step. Not all h
2 CONC is decisions were popular such as the siting of sale yards and Carnegi
2 CONC e Library. He worked as a printer for the Rangitikei Advocate, and in
2 CONC 1880 worked for Waikato Mail. Started the Waikato Advocate in 1895, an
2 CONC d then purchased the Waikato Times. His general printing business expa
2 CONC nded into a book and stationary business. Offices Held: 1886-1895 Memb
2 CONC er, Cambridge Borough Council (Mayor 3 years), 1901-09 Hamilton Boroug
2 CONC h Council (Mayor 1905-09), Member, Waikato Agricultural and Pastoral A
2 CONC ssn, 1890 Chairman, Cambridge Licensing Committee, President (1 term)
2 CONC Waikato Winter Show Association, 1911- Board of Governors, Hamilton Hi
2 CONC gh School, Member, Auckland Harbour Board, Member, Auckland Board of E
2 CONC ducation, Chairman of directors, Theatre Royal Co Ltd, [R1]
2 CONT  
