1 DEAT Y
2 DATE 1871
2 PLAC Madras
3 _XREF @place00829@
2 SOURCE @S21@
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE [BBB]: Indian Army/RE.  Col.
2 CONT Cadet at Addiscombe Military Seminary, 1837-38, passing out first of h
2 CONC is term, and was presented by the Court of Directors of the EIC with a
2 CONC  telescope as a mark of appreciation of his conduct while at the Semin
2 CONC ary.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Lt, Madras Engrs 1838;  Lt 1845;  Capt 1853;  Bvt Major 1854;  Subs
2 CONC Major, Bvt Lt Col, 1858;  Subs Lt Col, Col, 1863.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arrived in India 1840, and was posted to C Coy Madras S & M, at Belgau
2 CONC m,   S India, and moved with it to Sind.    He went on up to Quetta wi
2 CONC th a party of 46 men, the smallest body of troops that had ever passed
2 CONC  through the Bolan Pass, 60 miles long, by deliberate marches;  but he
2 CONC  lost the whole of his baggage in the pass owing to floods.    The com
2 CONC pany was next employed on the Quetta-Kelat road, 100 miles long and at
2 CONC  a height of 6000-12000 feet.   The Sind war then broke out, and the c
2 CONC ompany received special commendations for its services in the march to
2 CONC  Imamgurh, and in the battles of Meanee and Hyderabad.
2 CONT
2 CONT Archibald then went on two years' sick leave to Europe at the beginnin
2 CONC g of 1844.   While in Dublin, he married George Wilson i's dau, Georgi
2 CONC na Elizabeth, but she died after giving birth to a girl, who also died
2 CONC  a few months later.   He then went back to Madras, and spent the next
2 CONC  ten years in the PWD in that Presidency, with its HQ in Cuddalore.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1849 he married again, his second wife being Lucy, dau of Edward Sm
2 CONC alley, Madras CS, and they had six children:   Edward Huds, Thomas Sma
2 CONC lley, Adeline Lucy, Catherine Harriet, Maria de Chal and Alice Mary.
2 CONC  At one time Edward Smalley was Collector at Nellore, and was followed
2 CONC  100 years later in that office by Cecil Brackenbury, ICS, husband of
2 CONC his grand-daughter Ethel Boileau, occupying the same bungalow.
2 CONT
2 CONT In March 1857, a small war having blown up with Persia, Archibald was
2 CONC sent with B Coy, Madras S&M, to Bushire on the Persian Gulf.   The uni
2 CONC t reached Bombay on its return, and learning of the outbreak of the In
2 CONC dian Mutiny, at once volunteered for service against the rebels.    Th
2 CONC e part played by B Coy in the Central India campaign is described in t
2 CONC he relevant section of the Chronicles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Archibald, now a Superintending Engineer, was in the following years o
2 CONC ne of those concerned with road development in S India, amd there are
2 CONC some grounds for believing that it was better done than in the north.
2 CONC    From 1861 to 1863 he was Deputy CE, Central Circle, Madras Presiden
2 CONC cy, in charge of one-third of the Presidency's engineering works.   Pr
2 CONC omoted subs Col in 1863, he would then normally have retired in 1871,
2 CONC but the Govt, having regard to his professional merits, proposed to wa
2 CONC ive the rule for two years.   Being now an officer of the Royal (Madra
2 CONC s) Engineers, he went on to an RE staff appt in Dublin for that year.
2 CONC  During this period he renewed his acquaintance with John Peter iv, of
2 CONC  which more is recorded in the Chronicles.
2 CONT
2 CONT On his return to India he was appointed Superintending Engineer, PWD,
2 CONC 4 Div, at Madras.   His health being unsatisfactory he spent six month
2 CONC s in Australia in 1867, and the next year had to take two years furlou
2 CONC gh to Europe.  He then became SE 5 Div, with HQ at Cuddalore , a port
2 CONC south of Madras.   He died there very suddenly in 1871, while only 51
2 CONC years old.
