1 NOTE [Lart, II, 13]. Some descendants given in Lart. So far I’ve only put i
2 CONC n the one, Simeon.
2 CONT [BBB]: “ In 1781 he married Jane Wilson, daughter of one George Wilson
2 CONC  and his Huguenot wife, he being the head of an old-established wholes
2 CONC ale druggist's business in Bride Street, Dublin.   A few years later h
2 CONC e became a partner in the firm, the name of which then changed to 'Wil
2 CONC son and Boileau', and on Mr Wilson's death to 'John Theophilus Boileau
2 CONC , Druggist'.   In 1799, John's three sons, Simeon iii, George Wilson i
2 CONC  and John Theophilus ii, were made partners, while still very young, a
2 CONC nd in due course they took over the business as 'Boileau Brothers'.
2 CONC  As time went on various other partners came and went, with consequent
2 CONC  changes in the name of the firm.
2 CONT
2 CONT In or before 1811, John suffered a severe paralytic stroke, supposed t
2 CONC o have been caused by extreme anxiety over the destruction of his prop
2 CONC erty by fire.   This appears to have been his residence in Dublin, sin
2 CONC ce in 1858 John Peter iv records the circumstances of the destruction
2 CONC of most of the family papers many years before in a fire at his grandf
2 CONC ather's home (ie, Simeon i, but the house may by then have passed on t
2 CONC o John Theophilus) which had deprived them of many interesting relics
2 CONC and pictures of the family's earlier days.   In spite of his illness a
2 CONC nd loss, however, John's worldly prosperity was great, according to hi
2 CONC s brother John Peter i.”
2 CONT
2 CONT On Dec 8th, 1845, page 7, the Times of London has a couple of lines sa
2 CONC ying how Messrs. Boileau, the “eminent druggists of Bride-street”. hav
2 CONC e gone bust, paying 10s in the pound.
