0 @ind04408@ INDI
1 NAME John Peter (i) /Boileau/
2 DISPLAY John Peter (i) Boileau
2 GIVN John Peter (i)
2 SURN Boileau
1 SOURCES @S7@, @S6@, @S21@
1 POSITION 6,187
2 GENOMAP John Peter (i) Boileau
2 BOUNDARYRECT -31,220,43,126
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 30 NOV 1747
1 DEAT Y
2 DATE MAY 1837
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE Of Madras C.S. and of Castelnau House. His son J.P. Boileau, created a
2 CONC  baronet in 1869. Further details in [Lart, II, 15].
2 CONT [BBB] calls his issue the second English branch and gives lots of deta
2 CONC ils. This John Peter is called John Peter i by [BBB].   John Peter was
2 CONC  the first to go out to India (in 1764) in the East India Company, thu
2 CONC s beginning a long connection between the Boileau family and India.
2 CONT
2 CONT [BBB]: “The year 1764 is a momentous one in the family's history, for
2 CONC in it began the association with India which was to continue for nearl
2 CONC y two centuries, and to play a great part in the lives of many of the
2 CONC Boileaus and their kin.   The event which occurred in that year was th
2 CONC e arrival in the country of John Peter i, at the age of 16, as a Write
2 CONC r in the Honourable East India Company's Service.   At that time the C
2 CONC ompany was still primarily a commercial concern and its administrative
2 CONC  and military activities were secondary and incidental.   John Peter w
2 CONC ent out under the patronage of Brigadier General Caillaud, a kinsman,
2 CONC Commander of the Army of the Madras Presidency.   He was employed in v
2 CONC arious offices in the Presidency, and accompanied the army in administ
2 CONC rative capacities in some of the local wars, which were not very serio
2 CONC us.
2 CONT
2 CONT At last, after nine years of service, he obtained the appointment he h
2 CONC ad long wanted, as member of the Company's council at Masulipatam, in
2 CONC the northern part of the Presidency.   This proved, as he had hoped, v
2 CONC ery profitable.    The Company's employees were able to engage in priv
2 CONC ate trading ventures, shipping goods home at favourable rates to a rea
2 CONC dy market.   There was also a great deal of graft, in the manner of th
2 CONC e time.   And so John Peter prospered, and was able to give a helping
2 CONC hand to his family, by bringing out the men to seek their fortunes, an
2 CONC d the girls to look for wealthy husbands.   In all he assisted in this
2 CONC  way two of his brothers, three sisters, half a dozen nieces, besides
2 CONC helping in obtaining appointments for various other connections.   Mos
2 CONC t of them did well, the young women making good marriages so that the
2 CONC Boileau colony and its offshoots, mostly in the Bengal Presidency, wax
2 CONC ed large and flourished.”
2 CONT
2 CONT [BBB]: John Peter i also acquired the estate of Tacolnestone Hall, abo
2 CONC ut 10 miles from Norwich, and thus initiated the connection of the fam
2 CONC ily with the county of Norfolk, which was to last until 1949.   The im
2 CONC portance of the property was enhanced when John Peter iv, in 1836, bou
2 CONC ght Ketteringham Hall adjacent to Tacolnestone.   This was an imposing
2 CONC  Tudor mansion, standing in a wooded park of 500 acres, with a small l
2 CONC ake.  Altogether, John Peter iv owned about 9000 acres.
2 CONT
2 CONT Again from BBB:
2 CONT b in Dublin 30 Nov 1747.   In Dec 1763 went to London to his uncle Cha
2 CONC rles Daniel, and under the auspices and patronage of of Brig-Gen John
2 CONC Caillaud, with whom there was a family connection and who was appt C-i
2 CONC n-C Forces on the Coromandel Coast of India, sailed for Madras in Marc
2 CONC h 1764.   On arrival there, he was nominated a writer to the EIC Civil
2 CONC  Service.   He lived with his patron, Gen Caillaud, acting as his secr
2 CONC etary, until the latter's departure for Europe in 1767.  He then took
2 CONC his appointed course in the service, employed in various offices in th
2 CONC e Madras Presidency.  In 1768 he was Secretary to the Field Deputy wit
2 CONC h the army under comd of Gen Joseph Smith, and in 1770 was appt as ass
2 CONC t at Masulipatam, the then seat of Govt.   Visited by his brother Phil
2 CONC ip in 1771 on his way to take up a cadetship in the Madras Presidency.
2 CONC   Appt in 1775 a member of Council to Ganjam in Orissa, 350 miles N of
2 CONC  Masulipatam, and later Paymr to the Army there and in the field   Ret
2 CONC urning from there he obtained a post he had long desired, that of a me
2 CONC mber of council at Masulipatam.
2 CONT
2 CONT In May 1780, John Peter went to Madras to receive his sisters, Bonne a
2 CONC nd Harriot (sic) and his brother Thomas, whom he had invited to India
2 CONC at his own expense.  Bonne immediately m Lestock Wilson, Ch Offr of th
2 CONC e ship she had come out in, at John Peter's house;  Thomas went on to
2 CONC his destination, in Calcutta;  and Harriot went back to Masulipatam wi
2 CONC th her brother until July 1781 when he took her to Bengal to leave her
2 CONC  with their cousins the Droz's.  On his return he applied himself to h
2 CONC is affairs to such good effect that after eight years on the Council h
2 CONC e was able to retire, having received a gentlemanly competence to Engl
2 CONC and, and left in 1786 after an absence of more than 22 years.   He the
2 CONC n travelled through Ireland, Scotland, France and Switzerland     At V
2 CONC ersailles in 1786 he attended the last Drawing Room held by Louis XVI
2 CONC and Antoinette; and in Paris met his cousin Henri- Camille Boileau, la
2 CONC ter visiting Italy and Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT 25 Nov 1790 he m Henrietta, dau and co-heiress of Rev George Pollen, a
2 CONC t St George's Hanover Sq.  They had issue: Henrietta Maria, John Peter
2 CONC  iii, John Peter iv, Georgiana Augusta, George Pollen and Charles Lest
2 CONC ock i.  By unknown mothers in India he had two natural daus, Frances M
2 CONC aria and Harriet.    They lived in London from 1791 to 1797, when they
2 CONC  moved to Alcester, Warwickshire for the sake of John Peter iv's healt
2 CONC h, and in 1804 after a year at Ealing he bought from a Mr Fitzgerald a
2 CONC  house and property at Mortlake, Surrey, which he renamed Castelnau Pa
2 CONC lce, and resided there a numeber of years.  He later bought Tacolnesto
2 CONC ne Hall, near Norwich, but d at Castelnau Place, 10th March, 1837.   H
2 CONC is wife had received a large accession of property when her father d,
2 CONC amd their s George Pollen succeeded his grandfather as Rector and Lord
2 CONC  of the Manor of Little Bookham, Surrey.
2 CONT
2 CONT John had not only helped Bonne, Harriott and Thomas out to India, but
2 CONC after his return he also sent out his sister Margaret and two of his s
2 CONC ister Charlotte's daus, besides six other nieces, who all m well.  He
2 CONC also was able to obtain appts for, and otherwise assist, several nephe
2 CONC ws, other nieces and cousins, to the EIC's svc, and many of these high
2 CONC ly prospered  He was a Director of the French Hospital, and a trustee
2 CONC of the French Church in London.
