1 NOTE [RBB] spells her name Jessop. However, the correct spelling appears to
2 CONC  be Jessup, and this branch of Jessups is well known, so it seems, in
2 CONC the history of the eastern US. Her father, Ebenezer also appears in th
2 CONC e Canadian Dictionary of National Biography, as his brother was also w
2 CONC ell known. This is really the only point where my ancestry diverges to
2 CONC  become vulgarly American. Whew. Just imagine how awful it must be to
2 CONC be related to Americans more closely.
2 CONT
2 CONT I quote from [JB]:
2 CONT --------
2 CONT Leah Jessup was born 7 Sept, 1767, in America, and probably in Albany
2 CONC Co., in the Colony of New York. She went with her parents to England,
2 CONC and either while there or subsequently in India married Thomas Boileau
2 CONC , and resided in Calcutta. He was an Attorney and Solicitor and practi
2 CONC sed in the Supreme Court of Judicature at Calcutta in Bengal, where he
2 CONC  died 11 June, 1806, aged 51 years, 6 1/2 months. After her husband’s
2 CONC death, Mrs. Boileau, with her two sons, John Peter and John Theophilus
2 CONC , returned to England on board the ship “Hugh Inglis”, Captain Fairfax
2 CONC , which sailed from Calcutta in February, 1807. Her sister Mrs. Debora
2 CONC h Smith, who was in failing health, returned to England at the same ti
2 CONC me. In 1813 she was living in easy circumstances at Bury St. Edmunds,
2 CONC Suffolk. Her sister writes of her the following year that she is “well
2 CONC  situated and much respected for her good care and management of her f
2 CONC amily; for having had a good education she teaches them a great deal a
2 CONC t home”. After the battle of Waterloo in 1816 she went over to France
2 CONC with a portion of her own family, some of the children of Mrs. Maddy a
2 CONC nd of Mrs. Wright accompanying her. Mrs. Boileau died in London, 22 Ju
2 CONC ne 1845, aged 78 years.
2 CONT --------
2 CONT
2 CONT [JB] quotes entire letters written by Leah Jessup to her cousin Sarah
2 CONC Dibble (who married James Waring). One of these letters is written fro
2 CONC m Quebec, on 1 Nov., 1786, saying how they will soon be moving to Lond
2 CONC on. Then she writes again on Aug. 30, 1789, from Woolwich, Kent. She w
2 CONC as sorry her grandmother had broken her arm. They have been in England
2 CONC  two years, living in London for the first year, and since then living
2 CONC  within nine miles “of that great city”. The letter informs us that th
2 CONC e Thames is the biggest river in England (she has a view), St. Pauls c
2 CONC hurch is the largest in England also (she has a view of this, too), bu
2 CONC t that everything is so expensive because of the taxes. As [JB] commen
2 CONC ts, it was most likely financial pressure that encouraged the family t
2 CONC o move to India later.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1814 Leah was living back in England, in Bury St. Edmunds, as is ap
2 CONC parent from a letter given in [JB].
