1 NOTE [RBB]: m. Anne Hanson
2 CONT
2 CONT [BBB]: Generally known as Theo.  b in Calcutta 26 May 1805. Educ at Bu
2 CONC ry St Edmunds Grammar Sch.  Cadet Addiscombe Military Seminary, 1819-2
2 CONC 0, passing with distinction into the Engrs.  Ensign, Bengal Engrs, 19
2 CONC Dec 1820.  After a year's trg at the Engr Depot Chatham, went to India
2 CONC  in 1822. There he was employed principally under the PWD, which would
2 CONC  not release him when required for active service, and he consequently
2 CONC  saw none in the whole of his military career.  In 1826 he became Garr
2 CONC ison Engr at Agra where he built the cantonment church, the gaol, the
2 CONC college and the European Barracks;  repaired the Taj Mahal and other h
2 CONC istoric buildings in Agra and district.   1834-37, he was engaged in c
2 CONC onstructing an iron bridge over the East Kala Nuddee River at Khodagun
2 CONC j, nr Fategarh, and on completion of this work he went on furlough wit
2 CONC h his family to England
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1839 the Court of Directors established observatories at Simla, Mad
2 CONC ras and Singapore, Theo being put in charge of that at Simla.  After s
2 CONC ome instruction in the use of the instruments to be provided, he was c
2 CONC harged with the preparation of all the books and forms reqd to record
2 CONC the observations.  On 10 Jan 1840 he was elected a Fellow of the Royal
2 CONC  Astronomical Society, and on 5 Mar a Fellow of the Royal Society.  In
2 CONC  1847 he was appointed Superintending Engr PWD NW Prov, with HQ at Amb
2 CONC ala for next 7 years.  Promoted Lt Col in 1846, and appt Ch Engr NW Pr
2 CONC ov in 1854 with HQ at Agra.  In 1855 succeeded to comd of the Corps of
2 CONC  Bengal Engrs, as Col.  Retd in Feb 1857 as Hon Maj-Gen.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was m at Agra in the church he had built, 23 Apr 1829, Ann dau of C
2 CONC apt William Hanson;  they had issue : 12 children : Anne Leah, a dau,
2 CONC Susan Charlotte, Thomas Boldero, William Simeon, Finetta Maddy, Sarah
2 CONC Jessup, Catherine Charlotte, Elizabeth Susanna, Jane iv, John Theophil
2 CONC us vii and Elizabeth Magdalen Thorp.
2 CONT
2 CONT Returning to England before the outbreak of the Indian Mutiny he did n
2 CONC ot relapse into idleness, but did much public and philanthropic work.
2 CONC    He settled in Notting Hill, London, and in 1867 was elected to the
2 CONC Kensington Vestry (the then equivalent of the Borough Council), and be
2 CONC came Chmn of the Special Purposes Cttee.   In 1858 he was selected to
2 CONC the Cttee of the Soldiers' Daus Home at Hampstead becoming Chmn of it
2 CONC in 1863, an appt  which he held for 23 years, taking the greatest poss
2 CONC ible interest in the children.   In 1872 he joined the cttee of the Ro
2 CONC yal Sch for Offrs Daus, Bath, becoming Chmn in 1880 and continuing the
2 CONC re until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1860 he enrolled in the 1st Middlesex Rifle Volunteers and served t
2 CONC here for five years, attending every possible parade.   He was Chmn of
2 CONC  the Building Cttee responsible for Kensington Town Hall, completed in
2 CONC  1880, and the Vestry had a marble bust of him placed in the Council C
2 CONC hamber as a mark of honour for his labours in that respect.   He also
2 CONC served on the Council of the Royal Society, and audited its accounts,
2 CONC and the same Society nominated him as a Member of the governing body o
2 CONC f Christ's Hospital.
2 CONT
2 CONT He continued all these active occupations until the year of his death,
2 CONC  being accustomed to work from 5 or 6 am until 11 pm, but ill-health o
2 CONC bliged him to give them up.   He died at Notting Hill on 7 Nov 1886.
2 CONC  The respect in which he was held was shown at his funeral, which was
2 CONC attended not only by some 20 Generals, besides other officers, and mem
2 CONC bers of the Kensington Vestry, but also by many of humble rank, shop a
2 CONC sistants, road sweepers, and the Staff and older pupils of the Soldier
2 CONC s' Daughters Home.
2 CONT
2 CONT His versatility may well be judged from the above brief account of his
2 CONC  various activities, and in addition he invented a rifle, a pattern of
2 CONC  which was in the museum of the Royal United Services Institition;  he
2 CONC  also wrote a number of books, all of a technical nature, including as
2 CONC tronomy, land area computation, rents and wages, logarithmics and a co
2 CONC de of regulations for the PWD.
