1 NOTE Jacques Boileau, 10th Baron and 5th Lord of Castelnau etc, was born in
2 CONC  1626, and succeeded his father, his elder brother Francis having died
2 CONC . In 1652 the inhabitants of Nismes elected him Councillor. In 1660, h
2 CONC e married Françoise (daughter of Noble Jacques de Vignolles) who broug
2 CONC ht him a fortune of £20,000 Sterling. The Edict of Nantes having been
2 CONC revoked by Louis XIV in 1685, Françoise took refuge in the Convent of
2 CONC the Pugin, and in 1688 removed to the Ursuline Convent at Nismes whenc
2 CONC e she fled to Lyons, and ultimately to Geneva in 1690. Her family havi
2 CONC ng escaped to Brandenbourg, she joined them in that place in 1692, and
2 CONC  having there buried 5 of her children, returned to Geneva a short tim
2 CONC e before her death (which took place in January 1700) having survived
2 CONC her husband and eleven children. The Baron Jacques, with many others o
2 CONC f the reformed religion at Nismes, was sent to Lyons and there impriso
2 CONC ned in the Chateau de Pierre Guise, in which place he had a paralytic
2 CONC stroke, and was in consequence permitted to visit the Baths of Balarne
2 CONC , but deriving no benefit from them, he was again imprisoned at St. Je
2 CONC an de Vendas, one league from Montpellier, where he died July 1694 aft
2 CONC er a captivity of 10.5 years, aged 71.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacques had 16 children. His 5th but eldest surviving son Charles emig
2 CONC rating to England (as will be more fully detailed below) he was succee
2 CONC ded by his next surviving son Maurice as 11th Baron and 6th Lord of Ca
2 CONC stelnau &c. He was born in 1678, had twelve children, and dying in 174
2 CONC 1 was succeeded by his son Charles as 12th Baron and 7th Lord of Caste
2 CONC lnau etc. He was born in 1715 and entered the Army in 1734, but quitti
2 CONC ng? it soon after his father's death in 1742 he settled at Nismes. The
2 CONC re he married  Catherine Vergez D'Aubesargues, by whom he had five chi
2 CONC ldren. In 1754 he visited Spain and Portugal, and perished in the grea
2 CONC t Earthquake at Lisbon  on 1st November 1755. He was succeeded by his
2 CONC son Charles as 13th Baron and 8th Lord of Castelnau. He was born in 17
2 CONC 45, married in 1765, had nine children, died in 1785, and was succeede
2 CONC d by his eldest son Simeon Charles Barnabe as 14th Baron and 9th Lord
2 CONC of Castelnau. He was born in 1767. In 1790 he visited his relatives in
2 CONC  Ireland, and returning to France, resided at Castelnau Castle till th
2 CONC e Peace of 1814, when he accompanied Louis XVIII to Paris. He was Mayo
2 CONC r of Nismes in 1811. His descendants and the present Baron still resid
2 CONC e in the ancient Chateau de Castelnau, 6 miles from Nismes, which has
2 CONC been for upwards of 3.5 centuries in the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lart: Jacques served in the company of gentleman cadets, and became he
2 CONC ad of the fmily at the death of his brother, Francois. Elected a Counc
2 CONC illor of Nismes in 1652. Maintained his nobility at the Visitation of
2 CONC 1668. He was imprisoned in 1685 and remained in the prison of St. Pier
2 CONC re Ancise at Lyons for eleven and a half years, where he died, 17 Jan.
2 CONC  1697, in the 71st year of his ate. His portrait was painted by Jeanne
2 CONC  Garneir Charpy, of Lyons, dated 15 April, 1694.
2 CONT
2 CONT [C-D, III, 386] : “Jacques Boileau, born on 15 January, 1626, died on
2 CONC 17 July, 1697, served in the Cadets-Gentilshommes, and became head of
2 CONC the family at the death of his brother François. In 1652, Louis XIV ma
2 CONC de a declaration which give to the inhabitants of Nimes, of the Reform
2 CONC ed Religion, the right to elect the most qualified of their number [to
2 CONC  the council], to balance, in the Conseils Politiques of that town, th
2 CONC e voice of the Bishop. Jacques Boileau was chosen to hold this place.
2 CONC In 1668 he was examined by the Royal Commissioners, who verified his l
2 CONC etters of nobility, and confirmed him and his descendants in the nobil
2 CONC ity.”
2 CONT
2 CONT [BBB]: “Served in the Cadets Gentilhommes.  Studied the law at Orange
2 CONC and took Doctor's degrees, 28 Oct 1642;  admitted Advocate at  Nîmes 3
2 CONC 1 Oct 1642.   Succeeded his father as Seigneur de Castelnau et de Ste
2 CONC Croix, 15 Jan 1657, his elder brother François having d in his father'
2 CONC s lifetime.
2 CONT
2 CONT He m 14 Nov 1660 Françoise, dau of Noble Jacques de Vignolles, Seigneu
2 CONC r de Prades, Major of Cavalry, by his wife Louise de Bachy d'Aubais.
2 CONC  She had a portion of 20,000 livres.   They had in all 22 children, am
2 CONC ong whom were :  Anne ii, Francois ii, Henri iii, Francoise iii, Jean-
2 CONC Louis i, Louis ii, Charlotte i, Marguerite ii, Charles ii, Madelaine i
2 CONC ii, Alphonse i, Maurice i, Francois iii, Louise iii, Henri iv and Loui
2 CONC se iv.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family no doubt suffered from the increasing restrictions imposed
2 CONC on the Huguenots, which culminated in the Revocation of the Edict of N
2 CONC antes on 22 Oct 1685.    Jacques was unable to escape from the persecu
2 CONC tions which followed, and was arrested in Nîmes with several other Hug
2 CONC uenot gentlemen, and was imprisoned in the castle of Pierre-Encise, ne
2 CONC ar Lyons on 12 Jan 1686.   His captivity lasted for 10 years;  he was
2 CONC stricken with paralysis in 1696 and was permitted to go to the baths a
2 CONC t Balaruc for a cure, but derived no benefit, and d at St Jean de Veda
2 CONC , near Montpellier, on 17 July 1697.   he had made his will the year b
2 CONC efore at the prison of the Archbishopric in Lyons.
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife, Francoise, had been sent to the convent of Payen in 1686, fr
2 CONC om where she was transferred to that of the Ursulines at Nîmes in 1688
2 CONC , and she escaped two years later from there and out of France to Gene
2 CONC va, and d there in 1700.   They were survived by four of their ss;  tw
2 CONC o were later killed in action while serving the Elector of Hanover, wh
2 CONC ile Charles ii went on to found the not inconsiderable British branch
2 CONC of the family, and Maurice remained at Castelnau to keep the French br
2 CONC anch alive, but there it withered on the vine.   For much more detail
2 CONC on Jacques and his immediate family see also Sections 'Jacques ii' et
2 CONC seq of the Chronicles.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Again from BBB:
2 CONT
2 CONT Imprisonment of Jacques ii
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 	In January 1686, Jacques himself was arrested.  A friend of his, d'Es
2 CONC perandieu, had a plan for getting out of France, but had been prevente
2 CONC d by illness from putting it into effect.   In that month the latter h
2 CONC ad gone to a meeting of the 'newly converted' Nobles, meaning to join
2 CONC someone who had decided on flight.   Apparently Jacques also was prese
2 CONC nt, and fell in with his friend's plan.   But it became known to the a
2 CONC uthorities, and these two and another friend called Riffard were appre
2 CONC hended, their property was confiscated and they were sent without tria
2 CONC l to confinement in the castle of Pierre-Encise, at Lyon, the bastille
2 CONC  of the Archbishop.   There Jacques was to remain for more than ten ye
2 CONC ars.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	We know something of what this imprisonment meant, for there is a let
2 CONC ter written to the King in 1694 written by Jacques and some of his fel
2 CONC low-sufferers.   They had previously written to ask for release and th
2 CONC at they should not have to go on paying the cost of their food and the
2 CONC ir maintenance, which crushed their families, deprived as they were of
2 CONC  the property of the prisoners, and wearied their friends.   Now they
2 CONC appealed to the King in the following terms :-
2 CONT
2 CONT 	'Sire : Esperandieu, Castelnau (sic), Riffard, the brothers Baudan, o
2 CONC f Nîmes and Uzès in Languedoc, detained by your orders in your castle
2 CONC of Pierre-Encise at Lyon, since 19th January, 1687, appeal to your cle
2 CONC mency, and pray you very humbly to have the charity to order that the
2 CONC subsistence which it has pleased your Majesty to accord since the mont
2 CONC h of April 1693, may be regularly paid to them.   They have until now,
2 CONC  Sire, lived by the help of their friends, who being no longer willing
2 CONC  to continue this for fear of losing what they have lent, they thus se
2 CONC e themselves reduced to the state of wanting for bread, if your Majest
2 CONC y does not take pity on them and has not the charity to provide it.
2 CONC   It is, Sire, that they have reason to expect justice and kindness fr
2 CONC om your Majesty, who has always taken care to give needed relief to th
2 CONC e miserable;  the suppliants who find themselves of this number will h
2 CONC ave the happiness of obtaining the favours that they ask with all poss
2 CONC ible respect in this submission, and by the vows and prayers which the
2 CONC y address unceasingly for the continued good health of your sacred per
2 CONC son, for that of all the Royal Family, and for the prosperity of your
2 CONC State and of your Arms'.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	But the King was deaf to these cries of distress, and the unhappy pri
2 CONC soners died in the end with no relief.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	An idea of the effect that imprisonment had on Jacques is to be found
2 CONC  in the following inscription on the portrait he had painted during it
2 CONC .  The Latin in which it is written is abstruse, but this is the sense
2 CONC  of it :-
2 CONT
2 CONT 	In lasting memory of his hard imprisonment at Pierre-Encise the most
2 CONC Noble Jacques Boileau, Seigneur de Castelnau et de Sainte-Croix, &c ha
2 CONC d painted by the hand of the worthy woman, Joanne Garnier Charpy, of L
2 CONC yon, in the year of salvation 1694, the 15th day of April;  of his age
2 CONC , 68 years and 3 months;  of his captivity, 8 years and 3 months and 3
2 CONC  days;   he died 17th July, 1697, in the 71st year of his life, the 6t
2 CONC h month and the 2nd day, and of his captivity, 11 years, 6 months and
2 CONC 5 days;  this portrait, showing the sadness of his heart, by the dirt
2 CONC and leanness, the ugly wrinkles on his furrowed brow, the bristly bear
2 CONC d, his face watered by copious tears drawn forth by the bitter fate of
2 CONC  his exiled and best beloved wife and unhappy family scattered through
2 CONC  various parts of Europe.  O Lord, him who thou hast kept from the wom
2 CONC b, forsake him not in his old age'.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	There is a discrepancy between the dates given for Jacques's committa
2 CONC l to prison, the inscription making it January 1686 and '1754' making
2 CONC it January 1687.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	It is not known when the inscription was put on the portrait, but obv
2 CONC iously not until after his death in 1697.  It is quite possible that t
2 CONC here were some confusions over the old and the new styles in the calen
2 CONC dar, the old carrying the year over to the end of March, and the new e
2 CONC nding the year at the end of December.  Jacques's name also appears at
2 CONC  the head of a list of a convoy of prisoners arriving at Lyon from Nîm
2 CONC es in January 1687.   (He may have been kept in confinement initially
2 CONC at Nîmes).
2 CONT
2 CONT 	There appear to be two versions of the portrait, if not two different
2 CONC  portraits.   One is at Rampisham, which does not bear out the woeful
2 CONC picture presented by the inscription above, as Jacques is shown with b
2 CONC rown hair and beard, red cheeks and a bright complexion.   The other n
2 CONC ow exists, as far as is known, only in photographs, and depicts grey h
2 CONC air, and a haggard sad face, much more likely to to be true to life fo
2 CONC r a man of 68 with the weight of many sorrows on him.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Jacques became paralysed in August 1696, and in May 1697 was allowed
2 CONC to go to the baths at Balaruc on the south coast for treatment, but wi
2 CONC thout avail, and he died at St. Jean de Vedas, near Montpellier, presu
2 CONC mably on his way back to Lyon, on 17th July 1697, having been in capti
2 CONC vity 10 years, 6 months and 5 days.    He never saw his family or esta
2 CONC tes again.
