1 NOTE [BBB]: Françoise and Jacques had twenty-two children, among whom were
2 CONC :  Anne ii, Francois ii, Henri iii, Francoise iii, Jean-Louis i, Louis
2 CONC  ii, Charlotte i, Marguerite ii, Charles ii, Madelaine iii, Alphonse i
2 CONC , Maurice i, Francois iii, Louise iii, Henri iv and Louise iv.
2 CONT [C-D, III, 386]  gives the marriage as November 1660, and her death in
2 CONC  1701.
2 CONT
2 CONT [BBB]: “Jacques's wife, Françoise, was among the first to suffer under
2 CONC  the new measures.  The Protestant women were even more obstinate in t
2 CONC heir adherence to their faith than the men, and this spirit was shown
2 CONC by Françoise and her daughters and other Boileau women, as will be see
2 CONC n.
2 CONT
2 CONT She must have refused to abjure, as she was confined in a convent in 1
2 CONC 686, and two years later was transferred to another at Nîmes.  Having
2 CONC somehow escaped, she fled to Lyon, and then in February 1690 got out o
2 CONC f France into Geneva, where she stayed until 1692.   Two at least of h
2 CONC er daughters then living were sent to convents:  Françoise iii, aged 1
2 CONC 9, was taken in flight and kept in a convent for some years until she
2 CONC too escaped from France;   her sister Marguerite iii died in a convent
2 CONC  when she was 17.    Anne ii and Charlotte i also left the country, wh
2 CONC ile Madeleine iii  followed her mother to Switzerland, dying there in
2 CONC February 1691, aged 16.
2 CONT
2 CONT We have no details of their escapes, but they must have been such as m
2 CONC any other well-born girls endured, who disguised themselves as peasant
2 CONC s, lackeys, and footmen, and had to walk hundreds of miles through woo
2 CONC ds and across mountain ranges.    All the ways out of France were patr
2 CONC olled and guarded by troops and gendarmes;   but the emigration was co
2 CONC nducted on a regular system;   itineraries were secretly distributed,
2 CONC in which the safest routes and hiding places were described.    By an
2 CONC Edict of May 1686, any captured fugitives and their guides were to be
2 CONC condemned to death, but still the flow of refugees went on.”
