1 NOTE Nicolas Boileau, 9th Baron and 4th Lord of Castelnau &c was born in 15
2 CONC 78. He studied in Geneva, and took his degree as Avocat at Valence in
2 CONC 1598. In his profession he rose to great eminence and having amassed t
2 CONC he sum of £1400 Sterling, he resolved to visit foreign countries. In 1
2 CONC 617 he travelled through England, Ireland, Holland and Flanders; and t
2 CONC he following year through Germany, Italy and Spain. In 1619 he married
2 CONC  Anne de Calviere, by whom he had eleven children and died in 1657. Th
2 CONC e record in the College of Arms, London, commences with him.
2 CONT
2 CONT [C-D, III, 386] “ Nicolas Boileau, born on the 21st of December, 1578,
2 CONC  travelled in Italy, Germany, England, Flanders and Holland. He became
2 CONC  a lawyer, in which field he gained a great reputation, with his decis
2 CONC ions being followed in all the Courts of Justice, and, being asked to
2 CONC answer the call of Arrière-ban, he was excused on the basis of his pro
2 CONC fession.”
2 CONT
2 CONT [BBB]: “ The eldest son, Nicolas ii, succeeded his father as Seigneur
2 CONC de Castelnau et de Sainte-Croix.  He was a Doctor of Law, having taken
2 CONC  his degree at Geneva, and was received as an advocate at Valence in 1
2 CONC 598.  He was Advocate to the Seneschal at Nîmes, and otherwise worked
2 CONC only in chambers.   He was regarded as one of the cleverest of his tim
2 CONC e in his profession, his advice and writings having been followed eage
2 CONC rly in all the courts.  He settled innumerable cases of litigation, no
2 CONC t allowing his clients to go to court, if at all possible.  His compet
2 CONC ence, ability and integrity were so well known, and his reputation was
2 CONC  so well established, that he was still often asked for as Advocate as
2 CONC  long as ten years after his death.   Although he took no money from p
2 CONC oor litigants or from Nobles, he earned nevertheless more than 100,000
2 CONC  francs.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was probably Nicolas who is mentioned as 'de Boileau', as one of th
2 CONC e five gentlemen who, in 1619, at a time when civil war was threatenin
2 CONC g, were given the task of going to assure Louis XIII of the fidelity o
2 CONC f his subjects in Languedoc.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was also active in the affairs of Nîmes, particularly in the troubl
2 CONC ed conditions of the time.   So, in 1620, he represented the Consuls a
2 CONC t a meeting of the leaders of the community and of the Reformed Religi
2 CONC on, called to deal with the town affairs at a time of civil disturbanc
2 CONC e.  In 1621, he was deputed to represent Nîmes in the matter of a levy
2 CONC  for supplies to the Huguenot forces.   Later that year, he represente
2 CONC d the Town Council in discussion with the magistrates when the demolit
2 CONC ion of the Catholic Cathedral by the Protestant faction was liable to
2 CONC cause disorder. In 1625 he was present at an assembly of leading citiz
2 CONC ens called by the Duc de Rohan, a Huguenot leader, to establish a coun
2 CONC cil of direction for the conduct of the affairs of the Town.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two years later, the Duc de Rohan, then general of the Languedoc Prote
2 CONC stant forces, set up in Nîmes a council of sixteen with powers to take
2 CONC  such action as they thought fit for the security and welfare of the t
2 CONC own.
2 CONT Its members were to be changed periodically.   Nicolas was one of the
2 CONC first to be nominated.   Before his marriage in 1619, he travelled wid
2 CONC ely in western Europe, including a visit to England.    He married his
2 CONC  cousin, Anne de Calvière-Boucoiran, and they had eleven children, (qv
2 CONC );   the  eldest, François i, a professional soldier, was drowned on a
2 CONC  voyage to Naples in his father's lifetime, and so Nicolas, who died i
2 CONC n 1657, was succeeded by his second son, Jacques ii, for whom see belo
2 CONC w. “
