1 NOTE Jean Boileau,  8th Baron, and 3rd Lord de Castelnau &c, born in 1545,
2 CONC was 1st Consul of Nismes in 1605, and Syndic of the diocese. In 1571 h
2 CONC e married Honora de Blanche, who died leaving one daughter. In 1576 he
2 CONC  married, secondly, Rose de Calvière, and by her had twelve children.
2 CONC In 1600 the inhabitants of Nismes deputed him to Montpellier where an
2 CONC assembly was to be held relative to the putting into execution the Edi
2 CONC ct of Nantes. He died in 1618, and in his widow attained a great age,
2 CONC dying in 1644.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was summoned by the Constable de Montmorency to join him with his f
2 CONC ollowing in the expedition in Haut-Languedoc, and answered the call of
2 CONC  Ban and arrière-ban.
2 CONT
2 CONT [C-D, III, 385] In 1596 he was ordered by the Constable de Montmorency
2 CONC  to join him, with his following, in the trip to Haut-Languedoc. He pr
2 CONC esented himself, in 1594, to the call l’arrière-ban.
2 CONT
2 CONT [BBB]: “ Jean v succeeded his father as Seigneur de Castelnau, but not
2 CONC  yet as Treasurer, perhaps because he was no more than 17 years old in
2 CONC  1562.   By that time the Protestants were becoming strong in Nîmes, a
2 CONC nd there were disturbances between them and the Catholics.   In 1567,
2 CONC at least one hundred Catholics were massacred in Nîmes by the Huguenot
2 CONC s on the Feast of St Michael and All Angels, from which the occurrence
2 CONC  was known as the Michelade.   Among those who were adjudged to be imp
2 CONC licated and who were deprived of their goods were Robert le Blanc, fat
2 CONC her of Jean i's first wife:  Nicolas de Calvière, father of his second
2 CONC  wife, and Jacques Lageret, husband of Claudine i, Jean's sister.   Je
2 CONC an does not appear to have been involved, although there is little dou
2 CONC bt that he was a Protestant at the time:  he certainly was to become o
2 CONC ne not much later.
2 CONT
2 CONT In spite of the Michelade, it is remarkable that shortly after the eve
2 CONC nt of this massacre of Saint Bartholemew reached Nîmes, the townspeopl
2 CONC e of both parties met in council to consider measures to avert disorde
2 CONC r, and the orders from Paris for the Catholics to retaliate and kill t
2 CONC he Protestants were not carried out.   Peace was maintained by mutual
2 CONC accord, and a guard of both religions was placed on the gate.
2 CONT
2 CONT A joint delegation was sent to the Seneschal to ask for his support.
2 CONC  Jean is on record as having been among those present and consenting.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later he appears as Syndic of the Diocese of Nîmes, no doubt in its se
2 CONC cular administrative character:  ie, he was its representative in its
2 CONC affairs.  In 1586 he was summoned as a Noble to join the Constable Duc
2 CONC  de Montmorency who was the  Governor of Languedoc, in his journey thr
2 CONC ough the upper part of the Province;   and in 1594 he presented himsel
2 CONC f for service on the call-up of the arrière-ban, or feudal levy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1600, the people of the city deputed him to represent them at an as
2 CONC sembly at Montpellier for putting into effect the provisions of the Ed
2 CONC ict of Nantes, given by King Henry IV, which granted complete tolerati
2 CONC on to the Protestants and placed them in entire equality with Catholic
2 CONC  subjects.   In the same year he was a deacon in the Consistory, or Pr
2 CONC esbytery of the Reformed Church at Nîmes, the duties of the office bei
2 CONC ng to collect and distribute the alms for the benefit of the poor, the
2 CONC  prisoners and the sick, to visit them and to care for them.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1605 he was 1st Consul of Nîmes.   The consuls were the municipal m
2 CONC agistrates of the towns in the south of France, forming the executive
2 CONC council.  They were elected annually.  In Nîmes. there were four. of w
2 CONC ho the first was a Noble or advocate, alternately;    the second, a bo
2 CONC urgeois or merchant;  the third, a notary or artisan, and the fourth,
2 CONC a labourer.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married - first, 1571, Honorade, daughter of Robert le Blanc, Juge-
2 CONC royal ordinaire of Nîmes;  and second, 1576, Rose, daughter of Noble N
2 CONC icolas de Calvière de St. Cosme, a distinguished Huguenot, and had thi
2 CONC rteen children.   Of these Nicolas ii was to succeed his father as Sei
2 CONC gneur;  Jacques i founded the branch of Boileau d'Uzès, for which see
2 CONC below;  while three other sons took arms in the religious and civil wa
2 CONC rs of the period.”
