1 NOTE Jean Boileau, 7th Baron and 2nd Baron de Castelnau &c &c was born in 1
2 CONC 500 and succeeded his father as Treasurer in 1534 (being the 5th in su
2 CONC ccession in that office). He was the first of the family who embraced
2 CONC Protestant opinions, for which he suffered imprisonment, was tortured
2 CONC and beheaded by the Duc de Nemours in 1560 [This appears to be incorre
2 CONC ct. See below]. In 1538 he married Anne de Montcalm, leaving one son a
2 CONC nd six daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT [C-D, III, 385]
2 CONT
2 CONT [BBB]: “The son Jean succeeded both as Treasurer and as Seigneur de Ca
2 CONC stelnau et de Sainte-Croix.   He had been acting in the office for som
2 CONC e years before his father's death, and he did homage for it in June 15
2 CONC 35.
2 CONT
2 CONT It has been stated that he was the first of the family to embrace the
2 CONC Reformed Faith, but there is in fact no positive proof of this.  It ma
2 CONC y, however, be inferred from the circumstances that the family of his
2 CONC wife, Anne de Montcalm, was favourable to the Reform, and that the Nîm
2 CONC es branch of it went over to Protestantism in a body in 1551.   Also,
2 CONC at least three of their children married Protestants.
2 CONT
2 CONT It has been further said of Jean that, as a Protestant, he was impriso
2 CONC ned, tortured and beheaded in 1560.   This is quite definitely incorre
2 CONC ct.  Here it need only be stated that he has been confused with Jacque
2 CONC s de Castelnau-Chalosse, who suffered that fate, being executed on 30t
2 CONC h March, 1560, while Jean was living on 31st May that year, when he ob
2 CONC tained confirmation of the office of Treasurer from the King.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1552 he represented the inhabitants of Nîmes before the newly estab
2 CONC lished Presidial court there.    This was a Court of First Instance, a
2 CONC nd composed of the Juge-mage, three other judges and twelve Councillor
2 CONC - magistrates.   It was thus evidently a legal preserve, and this may
2 CONC have been the reason for the rejection of Jean's claim to be a member,
2 CONC  as the right and honour of his office as Treasurer demanded.   He had
2 CONC  appealed to the King for support for his claim, but the support was n
2 CONC egative, his rejection being perhaps due to the fact that he was not,
2 CONC as far as is known, a lawyer.”
