1 NOTE Antoine Boileau,  6th Baron, became treasurer, the reversion of the of
2 CONC fice being executed to him by Charles VIII in 1494. He was confirmed i
2 CONC n the office by Louis XII in 1498, at Senlis, and again in 1513 at Val
2 CONC ence, also by Francis I at Paris in 1515. Antoine purchased the estate
2 CONC  of Castelnau-de-la-Garde, and of St. Croix de Boirac, from the Comte
2 CONC Secoudin de St. Felix, for £400 and in 1500 took possession of the pro
2 CONC perty, receiving the oath of fidelity from his vassals. In 1497 he mar
2 CONC ried Françoise, daughter and heiress of Dr. Jean Troussellier (council
2 CONC lor and principal physician to Charles VIII) and by her had four child
2 CONC ren. Pope Leo X, in an indulgence dated August 1516 recognized Antoine
2 CONC  and his descendants as Noble,  and this document remained among the a
2 CONC rchives of the family till the year 1755. Nicolas, his youngest of thr
2 CONC ee brothers, is believed to have been the ancestor of the Poet Boileau
2 CONC  Despreaux, born 1635.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoine bought the seigneuries of Castelnau la Garde and Ste. Croix de
2 CONC  Boirac, 5 Feb 1500, in the dioc. of Uzès. First sr. de Castelnau.
2 CONT
2 CONT [C-D, III, 385] : “On the 5 Feb, 1500, Antoine Boileau bought the land
2 CONC s of Castelnau de la Garde, and de Ste. Croix de Boirac, in the dioces
2 CONC e of Uzès. He joined his name to that of Castelnau which his descendan
2 CONC ts have carried ever since, and died at Nimes in 1530.”
2 CONT
2 CONT According to [BBB], it was Antoine II that purchased the lands and sei
2 CONC gneurie of Castelnau. [BBB] points out that the lands of Castelnau did
2 CONC  NOT constitute a Barony, as has been supposed, and has a lot more det
2 CONC ails about the lands and castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT [BBB]: “Antoine succeeded his father as Treasurer by virtue of the let
2 CONC ters of reversion mentioned above, but he obtained confirmation of the
2 CONC  appointment soon after his father's death.   He appears in July 1496
2 CONC as Treasurer in an order to pay certain fees, and is described as a 'l
2 CONC icencie-en-lois', a grade of lawyer between 'bachelier' and 'docteur'.
2 CONC    In 1498 he was again confirmed in office on the accession of Louis
2 CONC XII.   For some reason unknown, however, he was suspended from office
2 CONC from August 1511 to April 1512;  this may account for his obtaining ye
2 CONC t another confirmation of office in 1513, while yet another was necess
2 CONC ary on the accession of François 1.
2 CONT
2 CONT The suspension evidently did not affect his reputation, for in 1516, o
2 CONC n the occasion of the Duchesse d'Alençon's visiting Nîmes, he was take
2 CONC n into her service, she being Marguerite de Valois, the King's sister;
2 CONC  while his wife Françoise was appointed her Dame d'honneur, and their
2 CONC son Jean, described here as Seigneur de Sainte-Croix, an officer of th
2 CONC e Household.    These honours, it is reasonable to believe, indicate t
2 CONC he high esteem in which the family was held.   Marguerite was always v
2 CONC ery favourable to the Reform, and this may ultimately have influenced
2 CONC the family into becoming Protestants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoine and his wife, however, were devout Catholics.  In 1516 Pope Le
2 CONC o X granted indulgences for them and their family.   These documents h
2 CONC ave the additional interest in that Antoine's Noble status was recogni
2 CONC sed in them.    Also, their portrait painted in 1519 had originally in
2 CONC  the background a representation of the Madonna and Child;   but this
2 CONC evidently caused one of their Huguenot descendants to fear that this m
2 CONC ight be a temptation to worship the Virgin, and he therefore erased he
2 CONC r figure from the picture, leaving the Child and her hands only.   The
2 CONC  painting displays the earliest known emblazonment of the family coat-
2 CONC of-arms, as 'Azure, a castle triple turreted argent, in base a crescen
2 CONC t or' which arms are also shown dimidiated with those of Françoise, an
2 CONC  unusual method.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1525, one Noble Marcelin Doumergue appears as Treasurer so commissi
2 CONC oned by the King.    The circumstances of this appointment are not kno
2 CONC wn, but it may have been under a new system of financial administratio
2 CONC n introduced by the King in 1523.   It did not last very long, for Ant
2 CONC oine reappears in June 1526 as Treasurer and Receiver-Ordinary, and ag
2 CONC ain in 1531 and 1533.   Letters of survival were issued on 21st August
2 CONC  1534, to Antoine and his son Jean iv, continuing the office to the la
2 CONC tter on his father's death, which took place at some date before Jean'
2 CONC s marriage in 1538.   Antoine and his wife had three daughters, as wel
2 CONC l as Jean:   they were Catherine, Magdelaine and Etiennette (qv).”
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Arms according to Rietstap: D’azur a une tour carrée commée de trois t
2 CONC ourelles, acc. en p. d’un croiss, le tout d’or. Crest: un pélican dans
2 CONC  son aire, la poitrine ch. d’un flanchis de gu. Motto: De tout mon coe
2 CONC ur.
2 CONT Arms according to Lart: D’azur, au château d’argent, maçonné de sable,
2 CONC  au croissant de même en pointe.
2 CONT Arms according to C-D:  D’azur, au château d’argent, maçonné de sable,
2 CONC  au croissant de même en point. Le caique: ouvert d’argent. Cimier: un
2 CONC  Pélican d’or, donnant son sang à ses petits. Devise: de tout mon coeu
2 CONC r.
2 CONT
2 CONT The shield is clear from this description, as well as from the picture
2 CONC  I have of Jean Boileau (see the family tree) and the illustration in
2 CONC Holland’s supplement to Rietstap. The castle and star are silver accor
2 CONC ding to Lart, gold, according to Rietstap. Lart is almost certainly fo
2 CONC llowing C-D (some of the pedigrees are word-for-word identical, and La
2 CONC rt was later than C-D) but I shall follow the silver anyway.
