1 NOTE Giullaume Boileau, 5th Baron, succeeded his father as Treasurer. In 14
2 CONC 70 he married the Hon. Etiennette (daughter of Jean Bourdin, Receiver
2 CONC General of Poitou) with a portion of 400 gold crowns. He died in 1494,
2 CONC  having had a family of eleven children. His tomb is still shown at Ni
2 CONC smes, bearing the following inscription (in Latin, here translated): T
2 CONC o the blessed memory of Sieur Giullaume Boileau, treasurer of our most
2 CONC  Serene King. His son, also Treasurer, caused this monument to be cons
2 CONC tructed, with a chapel for himself and his family, in the year of our
2 CONC Lord 1494 September. Pray to the dead, that they may rest in peace.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Lart, Guillaume was the Treasurer of the Royal demesnes o
2 CONC f Nismes and Beaucaire.
2 CONT [C-D, III, 384] : Guillaume moved to Nimes and sold his lands in Monte
2 CONC reaux.
2 CONT
2 CONT [BBB]: “ There is only the statement in '1754' to show that Guillaume
2 CONC i was the son of Antoine i;  but the fact that Guillaume's son was als
2 CONC o an Antoine may be taken as a corroboration.   Guillaume himself is t
2 CONC he first ancestor of the family from whom its descent is definitely su
2 CONC bstantiated.   Said to have been born in 1420, we know nothing of him
2 CONC until,  on 20th October 1469 he was commissioned by the then Treasurer
2 CONC , Mathieu Picot, to receive the payments on his behalf;   and he conti
2 CONC nued in this employment for 15 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1470 he married at Bourges Noble Etiennette Bourdin, and in the mar
2 CONC riage contract he is described as keeper of the salt barns at Montpell
2 CONC ier, an appointment connected with the gabelle.   On 24th June 1484, h
2 CONC e took over the appointment of Treasurer for himself from the then hol
2 CONC der, Jean Berri.   As part payment he was obliged, by Michel Gaillard,
2 CONC  Chancellor-General of France and the husband of Marguerite Bourdin (p
2 CONC erhaps a relation of Guillaume's wife) to make over to Picot's childre
2 CONC n his inheritance of Argenteuil, near Paris.   This consisted of a str
2 CONC ong house, with battlements, lands, vineyards, fields and gardens, and
2 CONC  120 to 140 livres of income.  As far as it is known, this marks the s
2 CONC everance of the last tie of the family with the north of France:   hen
2 CONC ceforth they belong in the south.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1487 he appears in a curious little incident involving his wife's b
2 CONC rother Antoine, who was Receiver of the poll-tax in the Diocese of Nîm
2 CONC es and who had arrested two men for debt on a market-day, contrary to
2 CONC the privileges of the town.   The Consuls made representations to the
2 CONC Seneschal, whose Lieutenant sat to adjudge the dispute, with the Judge
2 CONC  of the Criminal Court, the King's Advocate, the public prosecutor and
2 CONC  Guillaume Boyleaue, the Treasurer of the Seneschalship.   The Lieuten
2 CONC ant took the advice of these officers and, on their unanimous opinion,
2 CONC  he upheld the petition.   Guillaume is recorded as having agreed with
2 CONC  the decision, but also as having spoken on his brother-in-law's behal
2 CONC f.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following year, the Commissioners of Account in Paris addressed an
2 CONC  instruction to him as the Commissioner for the investigation of franc
2 CONC s-fiefs, or noble heritages.   The original of an order directed to hi
2 CONC m as Treasurer in 1493 is among the Rampisham papers.    In the previo
2 CONC us year, he had obtained from the King letters of reversion of the Tre
2 CONC asurership to himself and to his son Antoine.   He died on 6th Septemb
2 CONC er 1494 and his tomb, in the family house at Nîmes, was to be seen as
2 CONC late as 1754, with the following inscription, translated from the Lati
2 CONC n original :-
2 CONT
2 CONT IHS   To the blessed memory of Lord Guillaume Boileau
2 CONT Treasurer of our most serene King, Lord Antoine Boileau,
2 CONT his son and Treasurer, caused (this tomb) to be erected
2 CONT with  a chapel for himself and his (family)
2 CONT in the year of our Lord 1499, in September.
2 CONT
2 CONT 'Pray for the dead, that they may rest in peace'.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had 11 children. ”
