1 NOTE Antoine Boileau, 4th Baron, succeeded his father (at his death in 1400
2 CONC ) in the office of Treasurer, in virtue of which office he had precede
2 CONC nce of all others in the seneschalship, except the Chief Justice.
2 CONT
2 CONT Chesnaye-Desbois skips Antoine for some reason.
2 CONT
2 CONT [BBB]: “It is not known what was the object of the petitions mentioned
2 CONC  above;  it seems possible that it was to ask for dispositions for the
2 CONC  young men, or to obtain relief for them from any vows they had taken.
2 CONC    At any rate, this notice of Antoine's substantiates his existence,
2 CONC and bears out in part the statement in '1754' that he was the son of R
2 CONC egnaud and father of Guillaume.   At that date, this was all that was
2 CONC known of him, there being no deeds or papers to indicate what was his
2 CONC occupation, or the date of his death;  but, adds the account, it appea
2 CONC rs that it was he, who, after the death of his father, went to live at
2 CONC  Montpellier.   It is quite positive that he was not Treasurer, as som
2 CONC e accounts state.”
