1 NOTE Regnaud Boileau, 3rd Baron, was made by Charles VI in 1390 Treasurer o
2 CONC f the Seneschalship of Beaucaire and Nismes, and was the first in the
2 CONC family who resided in Languedoc. In 1391 he was commissioned by Charle
2 CONC s VI to build the Castle of Nismes, the great towers of which still re
2 CONC main. He was permitted to bear the Crescent in his Coat of Arms in mem
2 CONC ory of his father's gallantry at the battle of Nicopolis. His arms (a
2 CONC castle, etc) are still seen over the door of a home in which he had fo
2 CONC rmerly resided, in Montereau, in the Province of the Isle of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT [C-D, III, 384]  “Regnaud Boileau, who died in 1400, was in 1391 commi
2 CONC ssioned by King Charles VI to build the Castle at Nimes, of which two
2 CONC towers still remain near the gate of Carmes (porte des Carmes). He dro
2 CONC pped the arms of Etienne Boileau, to carry instead D’azur, au chateau
2 CONC d’argent, which his descendants retained, and which can still be seen
2 CONC on the door of the house in Montereau-Faut-Yonne, where the family liv
2 CONC ed before its time in Nimes [Not sure of the translation here]. We don
2 CONC ’t know the reason why Regnaud dropped the arms of Etienne.”
2 CONT
2 CONT [BBB] has a lot more information. Says that the connection of Regnaud
2 CONC to the earlier Boileau is entirely hypothetical. Even the armorial bea
2 CONC rings changed.  Personally, I think it most likely that Regnaud purcha
2 CONC sed his title (or his son did) and the family later made up spurious c
2 CONC onnections to other well-known Boileaus.
2 CONT [BBB}: “However, with Regnaud we can leave the region of tradition and
2 CONC  surmise for that of historical fact.   His name first appears in the
2 CONC records in 1390,as Treasurer of the Domain of the King in the Senescha
2 CONC lship of Beaucaire and Nîmes, in the province of Languedoc.   He was t
2 CONC he first of the family to go and live in that province, having (as sta
2 CONC ted above) previously lived at the family residence, about 40 miles so
2 CONC uthwest of Paris, in the Ile-de-France.   There he had a house, which
2 CONC he sold for 4,500 livres, and his coat-of-arms was above the door.”
