1 NOTE Ennobled by Charles I in 1341 and became 1st Baron Boileau. A certific
2 CONC ate of the Patent of nobility granted to him and his posterity was giv
2 CONC en by the Bishop of Paris in 1667 and registered in the Chamber of Acc
2 CONC ounts there.
2 CONT
2 CONT [BBB] gives the following translation of this Patent:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Extracted from the Register entitled 'The Register of Documents of th
2 CONC e Chamber of Accounts of the Lord the King', begun at All Saints, 1362
2 CONC , and finished at 1st January, 1388.
2 CONT
2 CONT Master Jean Boileau, Notary to the King, by the favour done to him by
2 CONC his (ie, the King's) letters, given in the month of September AD 1371,
2 CONC  concerning his (ie, Jean's) nobility and that of Master Jean, his son
2 CONC , together with their posterity.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issued and delivered on payment of the sum of 119 livres, 4 sous, 1 de
2 CONC nier, which must be recovered by the Treasury, according to the schedu
2 CONC le of the said Treasury given the 27th of October 1372, and returned t
2 CONC o the said Master Jean.
2 CONT
2 CONT Below is written:  Compared with the original, signed, made an extract
2 CONC ed as above by me, Conseiller, Secretary to the King, House and Crown
2 CONC of France, and his finances and Writer in the Chambre des Comptes, sig
2 CONC ned, Richer'.    This fact is pronounced by the Notaries of the King a
2 CONC t the Chatelet of Paris.  Undersigned the 12th April 1667:   Le Bouche
2 CONC r, Levesque, signed with their official signatures."
2 CONT
2 CONT [BBB] says that this Jean II could be the same as Jean IIa, and hence
2 CONC the nomenclature.
