1 NOTE Etienne Boileau is the first of family whose name is on records in 125
2 CONC 3. He was appointed by Louis IX Grand Provost of Paris, then the most
2 CONC eminent Officer of (the?) 31 years before the French Parliament existe
2 CONC d.
2 CONT Lart is scornful of these first few generations, with no proofs of des
2 CONC cent or marriages given.
2 CONT
2 CONT [C-D, III, 384] “Etienne Boileau, Grand-Provost of Paris in 1250, a ve
2 CONC ry important position before the Parliament existed. Louis IX, called
2 CONC Saint Louis, before his voyage to the Holy Land, chose him to reestabl
2 CONC ish justice. This magistrate, worthy of the highest praise, called the
2 CONC  late President Hénault (?), travelled through the different regions o
2 CONC f Paris, its communes and confréries, making many rules and regulation
2 CONC s, which have since  been copied and imitated, for the discipline of t
2 CONC hese communities, which had been formed a long time ago (?). Saint Lou
2 CONC is seated(?) him by his side, when he was at the Castle, to encourage
2 CONC him and to show that he was strongly in support of his Justice reforms
2 CONC .”
2 CONT
2 CONT It seems most likely that the Boileau bought their way into the nobili
2 CONC ty (and were originally drapers, according to Peter de Loriol).  Etien
2 CONC ne certainly existed (he is mentioned by Joinville) but his connection
2 CONC  to the later Boileau line is unproven and probably fabricated.
1 HYPERLINK http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89tienne_Boileau
