1 NOTE J.P., F.S.A. of Lincoln’s Inn. Admitted an attorney of King’s Bench Ju
2 CONC ne 28th, 1773, and a Fellow of the Society of Antiquities, 29th April,
2 CONC  1808. He succeeded to the family estate at Edmond Castle, but only su
2 CONC rvived his father a few years. He died June 23rd, at Clapham Commons,
2 CONC and was buried in the vault belonging to his wife’s family at Acton, M
2 CONC iddlesex.
2 CONT
2 CONT Became a partner in James Coulthard’s law firm.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note that his children had extensive connections with Clapham Commons
2 CONC and Eastbourne. Clearly this is where the family was based, not at Edm
2 CONC ond Castle. He was clearly the heir of Edmond Castle, but for one reas
2 CONC on or another chose to live elsewhere, possibly to further his law pra
2 CONC ctice.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Lambeth Archives Department:
2 CONT “The Graham family of Edmond Castle, Cumberland, became linked with Cl
2 CONC apham through the marriage, in 1791, of Thomas Graham with Elizabeth S
2 CONC usannah Davenport. Her father, John, a wealthy merchant (he was Master
2 CONC  of the Merchant Taylors' Company in 1775) and woollen draper to the K
2 CONC ing had bought a large estate on Clapham Common Northside, near the pr
2 CONC esent Sugden Road. Thomas and Elizabeth had five children, Thomas Henr
2 CONC y, John, Elizabeth Maria, (known as Maria), Emily and Ann Margaret. Af
2 CONC ter Thomas' death in 1813 his eldest son inherited Edmond Castle but t
2 CONC he children continued to live with their widowed mother at The Hall fo
2 CONC r some years. John moved to Rose Lodge (later Northfield) Clapham in 1
2 CONC 833, and returned to The Hall on his mother's death in 1844. He left i
2 CONC n 1853, and in 1886 the estate was sold for development Of his three s
2 CONC isters, Maria remained unmarried, and was instrumental in the building
2 CONC  of St George's Church, Battersea with its associated school and vicar
2 CONC age. Emily married the Rev Thomas Collins, and Anne married Edward Pol
2 CONC hill. The Polhill family had a large house on the site of Crescent Gro
2 CONC ve.”
