1 NOTE Of Clapham Common, Surrey.
2 CONT A church plaque in Hayton reads:
2 CONT “Elizabeth Susanna, relict [sic. Relict!? What the hell is that?] of T
2 CONC homas Graham Esq died at her house at Clapham Common, Surrey, Aug 10 1
2 CONC 844 in her 82nd year, and was buried in the vault of the Davenport fam
2 CONC ily at Acton.....This tablet was erected to the memory of his revered
2 CONC and beloved mother, as a tribute of his warm and tender affection, by
2 CONC her son Thomas Henry Graham.”
2 CONT
2 CONT “ESD was the author of 1) "Eighteen Maxims on Neatness and Good Order"
2 CONC , Hatchard under the pseudonym Theresa Tidy.  2) Writing as Lemuel Gul
2 CONC liver Jr. "Voyage to Locuta" Hatchard 1818. 3) The Snow-Drop. 1830. I
2 CONC [PB] have a manuscript diary of hers completed between 1780-83
2 CONT She lived at The Hall on Clapham Common, Northside until her death.”
2 CONT
2 CONT A copy of these books was available in 2006 through abebooks.com, for
2 CONC the measly sum of only $778 USD. The description read
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------
2 CONT Eighteen maxims [with:] Voyage to Locuta
2 CONT Graham, Elizabeth Susanna
2 CONT
2 CONT Book Description: London 1818., 1818. Eighteen maxims of neatness and
2 CONC order. To which is prefixed an introduction. By Theresa Tidy. London:
2 CONC printed for J. Hatchard, 1818. 39(1) pp. [Bound with:] Voyage to Locut
2 CONC a; a fragment: with etchings, and notes of illustrations. Dedicated to
2 CONC  Theresa Tidy, . . . by Lemuel Gulliver, Jun. London: printed for J. H
2 CONC atchard, 1818. 47(1) pp. + an etched frontispiece and five other plate
2 CONC s, and 4 pp. of publisher's advertisements at the end. Two vols. in on
2 CONC e, 12mo, original drab boards (slight wear). Sixth edition of the firs
2 CONC t title. An exceedingly popular guide to tidiness for children, first
2 CONC published in 1817; a 19th edition of 1828 was followed by American edi
2 CONC tions in 1829 (Boston) and 1833 (New Haven). The introductions contain
2 CONC s references to Mrs. Teachum, from Sarah Fielding's Governess, and the
2 CONC  writings of Mrs. Barbauld. All printings are very uncommon; there is
2 CONC no copy of this one in OCLC or COPAC. Osborne, p. 710 (16th edition).
2 CONC First edition of the second title. A pastiche of Gulliver's Travels, i
2 CONC ntended to teach grammar to young children. With six etched plates. In
2 CONC  fine condition. Block, p. 89; Teerink 1264; Osborne Collection, pp. 7
2 CONC 10-11. Bookseller Inventory # B5462
2 CONT ------------
2 CONT
2 CONT This coat-of-arms on the bookplate is very interesting indeed, showing
2 CONC  as it does the Graham escallops on the diagonal. This is just like th
2 CONC e coat-of-arms of Thomas Graham’s son  (Thomas Graham), as carved on a
2 CONC n outside wall of Edmond Castle, and joined with the Carnegie coat of
2 CONC arms. Maybe the Grahams of Edmond Castle, contrary to everything else
2 CONC I’ve been told, or seen, did actually use the escallops in bend, not i
2 CONC n chief. Strange, indeed.
