1 NOTE J.P. and D.L., High Sheriff 1824, M.A. Trinity Coll. Camb., F.S.A. Die
2 CONC d d.s.p. (without children) and was succeeded by his nephew, the eldes
2 CONC t son of his brother, John. He had no children with Mary.
2 CONT A plaque in Hayton church is so sickly sweet that I think he must have
2 CONC  given them a lot of money. Devoted to the service of God, self-denyin
2 CONC g and unwearying efforts for the spiritual welfare of the young, etc e
2 CONC tc.
2 CONT
2 CONT On a different plaque in Hayton church it says how he built and endowe
2 CONC d Talkin Church and Hayton schools and built the chancel of the church
2 CONC . No wonder they gave him a lovely plaque. It also mentions how his wi
2 CONC fe was the aunt of the 9th Earl of Southesk. Oh dear, as if the fact t
2 CONC hat she was aunt to some Earl was the most important thing about her,
2 CONC while her husband gets lauded to the skies. Bloody hell.
2 CONT
2 CONT On an external wall of Edmond Castle (which I photographed in 2005) th
2 CONC ere is a coat-of-arms below the inscription TH 1829 MG(C?). I reproduc
2 CONC e the photograph in my article on the Grahams of Edmond Castle and dis
2 CONC cuss it more there. It presumably commemorates the marriage of Thomas
2 CONC and Mary (one side of the coat-of-arms is the Carnegie eagle), but is
2 CONC a strange version of the Graham coat. I don’t understand why. On the p
2 CONC laque in MMC appears just the Graham three scallops, but with the towe
2 CONC r as crest and the motto N’Oublie. Yet another version of his arms. I
2 CONC don’t get it.
