1 NOTE Most likely the painter of the watercolours of Edmond Castle that were
2 CONC  auctioned by Lunds in the 1990s or early 2000s.
2 CONT
2 CONT A plaque in Hayton church reads:
2 CONT
2 CONT “In memory of ROBERT GRAHAM POLHILL, Lieutenant in her Majesty’s 95th
2 CONC regiment, who fell in front of his regiment defending the colours at t
2 CONC he battle of Alma. September 20th 1854, aged 26. In this memorable act
2 CONC ion the 95th was most conspicuous, and its loss enormous. Lieutenant P
2 CONC olhill was the second son of Edward Polhill, esqre, of Brighton, and o
2 CONC f Anne Margaret, the youngest daughter of the late Thomas Graham, Esqr
2 CONC e, of Edmond Castle, Cumberland, and great grandson of Nathaniel Polhi
2 CONC ll, of Howbury Esqre, M.P.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Their first child was Emily, born 1824
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Lambeth Archives:
2 CONT “The collection consists of verses, letters and a year's edition of th
2 CONC e Family Chronicle, a news paper produced by the children detailing th
2 CONC e week's happenings in an imitation of grand society reporting. Many o
2 CONC f the other verses may have been intended for this Chronicle (also kno
2 CONC wn as the Strawseat Chronicle), which appears to have continued for lo
2 CONC nger than the year's editions preserved here. The verses by Mrs Graham
2 CONC  are particularly interesting as she had published, under the pseudony
2 CONC m Theresa Tidy, a popular work called Eighteen Maxims on Neatness and
2 CONC Order.
2 CONT
2 CONT The letters Reveal a great deal about the family, about individuals an
2 CONC d incidentally about the social life of the time. Many concern Anne, w
2 CONC ho after her marriage in 1823 became somewhat weighed down with domest
2 CONC ic cares. For some years the Polhills lived in France, where they coul
2 CONC d manage on less money, but nothing is known of the latter part of Ann
2 CONC e's life. She died in Brighton in 1882.”
