1 NOTE (From THB Graham, TCWAAS, VII, 13) His first wife was Elizabeth Garnet
2 CONC t and they were married in 1746 by Chancellor Burn at Orton in Westmor
2 CONC land. The father’s marriage coincided with the date of the first Scott
2 CONC ish rebellion, and the son’s with that of the second. It was on the oc
2 CONC casion of his first marriage that Thomas Graham the younger planted th
2 CONC e large Scotch fir tree which is still (1907) standing in front of the
2 CONC  house at Edmond Castle, and it has always been regarded as a memorial
2 CONC  of the invasion of the county by the young Pretender. Elizabeth died
2 CONC 16 months later, as did  also her child.
2 CONT
2 CONT A family prayer-book contains  the following entry:- “ My dear wife di
2 CONC ed the 15th day of December, in the year of Our Lord, one thousand sev
2 CONC en hundred and forty-seven, being 24 years of age. Her funeral sermon
2 CONC was preached by the Reverend George Gilbanks, minister at Wetheral.” O
2 CONC n the fly-leaf is scrawled in a childish hand:-
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Garnett’s book
2 CONT God gave her grace on it to look
2 CONT And when the bells.............
2 CONT
2 CONT a popular rhyme which concludes:-
2 CONT
2 CONT ..........begin to toll
2 CONT The Lord have mercy on her soul
2 CONT
2 CONT This Thomas Graham, his three sons (Thomas, James and William) and his
2 CONC  son-in-law, Richard Graham of Stonehouse, were all Justices of the Pe
2 CONC ace for the county, and their names appear in the commission of 1802.
2 CONT Died in 1807 and was buried in the family vault at Hayton.
2 CONT
2 CONT That large Scotch fir was (I’m pretty certain it’s the same one) still
2 CONC  in front of Edmond Castle in 2005. I have a photograph of it, and the
2 CONC re’s more detail in my little piece of Edmond Castle, accessible from
2 CONC my main web page.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their second son James (the Baronet) had a huge memorial plaque carved
2 CONC  and put on the wall of Hayton church. Sort of like an early genealogy
2 CONC  web page, but carved in stone instead. Plus ca change, huh? Just for
2 CONC completeness, here it is:
2 CONT
2 CONT “Sacred to the memory of
2 CONT THOMAS GRAHAM OF EDMOND CASTLE
2 CONT who died October 20, 1807, aged 89, and
2 CONT MARGARET his wife, daughter of Thomas Coulthard,
2 CONT of Scotby, who died July 25, 1816, aged 91.
2 CONT They were a happy couple,
2 CONT religious, benevolent, with great simplicity of manners,
2 CONT and beloved by all their neighbours.
2 CONT They had four sons and three daughters:-
2 CONT THOMAS, born 1751, married 1791, Elizabeth Susanna,
2 CONT daughter of John Davenport, Esq, of Clapham Common.
2 CONT He died June 23, 1813, and was buried at Acton, Middlesex.
2 CONT They had six children:-
2 CONT Thomas Henry, born 1793, High Sherriff of Cumberland, 1824,
2 CONT John, born 1791, married 1821, Caroline Eleanor, daughter
2 CONT of E.E. [sic] Curteis Esq. M.P. for Sussex, and have one child
2 CONT Reginald John, born 1822.
2 CONT Eliz. Maria, born 1795
2 CONT Emily, born 1797
2 CONT Harriet; born 1798, died 1806, buried at Acton,
2 CONT Anne Margaret, born 1801, married 1825, Edward Polhill, Esq, and
2 CONT have one child, Emily, born 1824.
2 CONT JAMES, born November 18, 1753,
2 CONT whom his late Majesty George III, on the 24th Sept. 1808,
2 CONT was graciously pleased to create a Baronet;
2 CONT he was elected M.P. for the Borough of Cockermouth
2 CONT in 1802, 1806 and 1807,
2 CONT and for the city of Carlisle in 1812, 1818 and 1820.
2 CONT He married 1781, Anne, daughter of the Rev. John Moore,
2 CONT Kirkstall, Co. York; She died August 28, 1821, and
2 CONT was buried at Hendon, Middlesex, with three of her children.
2 CONT They had three sons and two daughters:-
2 CONT Anne, born 1782, married 1812, Adolphus John Dalrymple, Lieut. Col.
2 CONT in the Army, M.P. for the borough of Appleby, the
2 CONT eldest and now only surviving son of General Sir Hew
2 CONT Whiteford Dalrymple, Baronet.
2 CONT James, died 1785, buried at Hendon.
2 CONT Thomas, died 1807, buried at Hendon.
2 CONT Sandford, born 1789, M.P. for the borough of Ludgarshall, married 1819
2 CONC ,
2 CONT Caroline, daughter of John Langston, Esq. of Sarsden House,
2 CONT County of Oxford. They have four children:-
2 CONT Sandford, born Feb. 1821,
2 CONT James, born Feb. 1822
2 CONT Caroline, born March 1823
2 CONT William Henry, born Dec. 1824,
2 CONT Margaret, born 1791, died 1799, and buried at Hendon.
2 CONT MARY, born 1756.
2 CONT She married in 1791, Richard Graham of Stonehouse,
2 CONT who died 1807, aged 61, buried at Hayton.
2 CONT They had one child, Elizabeth Margaret, born 1792, who
2 CONT married 1816, Lieut. Col. Sir Hew Dalrymple Ross, K.C.B.
2 CONT They have had five children:-
2 CONT Hew Graham, born 1817,
2 CONT Mary Jane, born 1819, died 1823, buried at Hayton,
2 CONT Elizabeth Ann, born 1820,
2 CONT George, born 1822,
2 CONT Mary, born 1824.
2 CONT ELIZABETH, born 1758, died 1762, buried at Hayton,
2 CONT JOHN, born 1761, died 1791, buried under the chapel, Lincoln’s Inn,
2 CONT WILLIAM, born 1763, died 1811, buried at Hendon,
2 CONT MARGARET, born 1770, died 1794, buried at Littleham, Devonshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT This memorial was erected by
2 CONT JAMES, second son of THOMAS and MARGARET GRAHAM
2 CONT in grateful remembrance of his
2 CONT honoured parents and beloved brothers and sisters
2 CONT MDCCCXXV.”
