1 NOTE Sir James Graham of Kirkstall, co. York, created a Baronet 3 October 1
2 CONC 808, Recorder of Appleby, M.P. for Cockermouth 1802-5, for Wigtown Bur
2 CONC ghs 1805-6, again for Cockermouth 1807-12, and for Carlisle 1812-25. B
2 CONC loody hell. No wonder they made the poor bugger a baronet. Married Ann
2 CONC e Moore of Kirkstall, Yorks, and had issue. His son was sir Sandford G
2 CONC raham, 2nd Bt. etc etc. More issue. This baronetcy lasted only to the
2 CONC fifth generation, dying out in 1895. The Grahams of Kirkstall had the
2 CONC same coat-of-arms as the Grahams of Edmond Castle, but with the motto
2 CONC “Fideliter et diligenter”. It last appears in the 1895 edition of Burk
2 CONC e’s Peerage and Baronetage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Became a partner in James Couthard’s law firm.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Graham planted the old cherry tree growing near the house when h
2 CONC e first went as a boy to London, in November, 1769.
2 CONT
2 CONT THB Graham  reproduces a story about James Graham (and his future brot
2 CONC her-in-law Richard Graham) about how their workers found a keg of bran
2 CONC dy while pulling down some old houses near Edmond Castle, most likely
2 CONC the result of smuggling.
