1 NOTE Apparently descended from a branch of the Grahams of Esk (according to
2 CONC  Burke’s, but then Burke’s makes so many mistakes one can’t really be
2 CONC sure). I’ve made a separate page for the Graham ancestry bit. Burke’s
2 CONC Landed Gentry (1937 edition) stops here, but the Graham pedigree is co
2 CONC ntinued and expanded in THB Graham’s article. Trans. CWAAS N.S. VII, p
2 CONC .13.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was Thomas Graham of Edmond Castle. Arms: Per pale indented erminoi
2 CONC s and sable, and a chief per pale of the last and or, three escallops
2 CONC counterchanged. Crest: Two armed arms ppr., garnished or, embowed issu
2 CONC ing out of the battlements of a tower, also ppr., holding an escallop
2 CONC gold. Motto: N’Oublie. Seat: Edmond Castle, Cumberland.  This must hav
2 CONC e been an earlier building than the one that stands there now, which w
2 CONC as apparently built in the early 1800s.
2 CONT
2 CONT They married the same year that the Old Pretender was proclaimed King
2 CONC of England at Brampton (close by). They resided for many years at Warw
2 CONC ick Bridge but eventually returned to live with their father and mothe
2 CONC r at Edmond Castle.
