1 NOTE Magdalene de Vergèze d'Aubussargues, the wife of Théophilus Desbrisay,
2 CONC  came from a Huguenot family that has a rather uncertain history, diff
2 CONC icult to piece together from the available sources. Probably the most
2 CONC reliable account is that given in the 1911 volume of the Bulletin de l
2 CONC a Société de l'Histoire du Protestantisme Français, and this is what I
2 CONC  follow here.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first of this family who became a Protestant was Antoine de Vergèz
2 CONC e. His son Claude, married in 1570 to Domergue de Joanis, had a son Ni
2 CONC cholas, who, in 1621, was in charge of the fort of Sainte-Anastasie,
2 CONC overlooking the Gardon river (and thus, interestingly, close to Castel
2 CONC nau. Aubussargues is almost exactly halfway between Castelnau and Uzès
2 CONC ).
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean d'Aubussargues, the son of Nicholas, married Bonne de Barjac in 1
2 CONC 638, and had a number of children, two of whom, Jacques, the elder, an
2 CONC d Jean, are known to have fled France at the time of the Revocation of
2 CONC  the Edict of Nantes.  It's difficult to know what they did abroad, as
2 CONC  they were both usually just called Daubussargues, or Daubessargue, an
2 CONC d only sometimes do we also find any other identifying name.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon leaving France, Jacques succeeded the Vicomte de Saint-Bonnet as
2 CONC the commander of the Grand Musketeers, in Brandenburg, where he also c
2 CONC ommanded the Horse Grenadiers, an elite corps formed by the Grand Elec
2 CONC tor at the same time as the Grand Musketeers, that admitted only those
2 CONC  of honourable birth or proven valour. However, by 1698 Jacques was in
2 CONC  Ireland, a Colonel in Galway's regiment. He died in Dublin in 1720.
2 CONT
2 CONT When he left France, Jacques left behind his wife, Madeleine Gasc (the
2 CONC  daughter of the co-seigneur de Sanilhac and the consul of Uzès in 163
2 CONC 6), and his ten children, to protect their inheritance. She raised the
2 CONC ir children, who inherited the family wealth, her husband having fled,
2 CONC  and finally rejoined her husband in Dublin, in about 1702, where she
2 CONC died sometime after 1714. Apparently, she called herself Madon de Gas.
2 CONC  One of the ten children she raised by herself in France was another J
2 CONC acques, while two of the girls were called Madeleine and Jeanne. I kno
2 CONC w nothing about any others. It's likely that this younger Jacques was
2 CONC a Captain in Galway's regiment in 1698, the same regiment where his fa
2 CONC ther was a Colonel, although his name isn't given.
2 CONT
2 CONT Finally, it is this younger Jacques who was, according to family tradi
2 CONC tion, the father of Magdalene, who married Théophilus Desbrisay. This
2 CONC is, let me emphasise, only tradition, not genealogy. Historically, the
2 CONC re is essentially no doubt of the connection between our Madeleine and
2 CONC  the other de Vergèze d'Aubussargues; genealogically, there is no spec
2 CONC ific proof, and is thus technically unacceptable.
2 CONT
2 CONT Just to add to the confusion, there were at least three Madeleine de V
2 CONC ergèze d'Aubussargues in Dublin at various times. One was the wife of
2 CONC Colonel Jacques, who signed herself Madon de Gas. Another was the Made
2 CONC leine who married Théophilus Desbrisay, while the third was the wife o
2 CONC f Jean de Vergèze d'Aubussargues, the brother of Colonel Jacques. Jean
2 CONC  and his wife lived in Dublin for a time, before moving to Switzerland
2 CONC , where they remained for the rest of their lives.
2 CONT
2 CONT The d'Aubussargues coat-of-arms was De sinople à un lévrier courant à
2 CONC fasce d'argent accolé de gueules bordé d'or accompagné de 4 roses d'ar
2 CONC gent boutonnées. That is, a dark green background, with a silver runni
2 CONC ng greyhound, collared in red and bordered in gold, surrounded by four
2 CONC  silver roses. Very stylish.
