2 COLORS
3 GENDER
4 FILL #00FF00
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1671
1 DEAT Y
2 DATE 1761
2 PLAC Dublin, Ireland
3 _XREF @place00853@
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE [Lart, II, 15].
2 CONT I found a lot of Desbrisay information at http://www.islandregister.co
2 CONC m/desbrisay1.html, at
2 CONT http://jmw.dynip.com/Genealogy/ and at http://www.desbrisay.ca/. I hav
2 CONC e used this to sort out the Desbrisay line as far as I can. I quote so
2 CONC me of it below. Thanks to Don Lowe and Jean-Marie White for their help
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT However, there still seems to be enormous confusion over this Theophil
2 CONC us and his son. They are possibly the same person, it seems to me.
2 CONT
2 CONT The de Brisay are given in [C-D, IV, 206]. According to what follows,
2 CONC it is possible that Theophile was the grandson, or other close descend
2 CONC ant, of Jacques de Brisay [C-D, IV, 206]. This connection is roundly c
2 CONC hastised by BBB, who calls it certainly incorrect. According to [BBB]:
2 CONC  “The story which first appears in the large genealogical chart, givin
2 CONC g her [a granddaughter, Magdalena Elizabeth] name alternatively as 'de
2 CONC  Brize', and so connecting her with a very distinguished French family
2 CONC , is incorrect;   there is no connection whatever between the two.” I
2 CONC have to admit that I trust BBB.
2 CONT
2 CONT If you assume the link to be true, this extends the Desbrisay line far
2 CONC  back into the mists, through the Valois kings of France, to Charlemag
2 CONC ne. Nevertheless, unless the connection can be substantiated the ances
2 CONC try remains speculative and so I don’t include it here.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------
2 CONT The family of "de Brisay" or Desbrisay, as it was at one time called,
2 CONC now existing in England, is without doubt of Huguenot descent, their c
2 CONC ommon ancestor having been a Théophile de la Cour de Brisay, an office
2 CONC r in the Regiment of Frédéric Guillaume Comté de Marton afterward, Ear
2 CONC l of Lifford, 4th Son of the Comté de Roye, which Regiment was origina
2 CONC lly raised by Cambon &called after him.
2 CONT
2 CONT We may presume that this "Capitaine Théophile desbrisay ", whose name
2 CONC we find in a "List of the Staff and Standing Officers of the Earl of L
2 CONC ifford's Regiment of Foot" in the year 1698, & who crossed over from H
2 CONC olland with the French Huguenot Regiments was a descendant of Pierre d
2 CONC e Brisay III Chevalier and Seigneur de Denonville and Jacqueline D'Orl
2 CONC eans Longueville his wife (see part lst p.61.).
2 CONT
2 CONT This Pierre de Brisay was one of the first, (though an Abbé of the Rom
2 CONC ish Church) to embrace the Reformed Faith.
2 CONT
2 CONT His Son Jacques de Brisay , Chevalier and Seigneur de Denonville who w
2 CONC as born at Denonville on 4 January, 1579 fled to Holland and died at H
2 CONC eusden in 1625 leaving, by his wife Judith D’Argenson, a Son, Pierre d
2 CONC e Brisay IV , and it is more than probable that other children were al
2 CONC so born to Jacques de Brisay during his exile in Holland.  Pierre de B
2 CONC risay abjured the Reformed Faith and on his return to France, succeede
2 CONC d to his Father's Estates, he alone is mentioned by the French Genealo
2 CONC gists. Descendants given in [C-D, IV, 206].
2 CONT
2 CONT This Pierre de Brisay, the welcome apostate, was created a Vicomte de
2 CONC Montbazillac, and Marquis d'Avesnes & ect; he enjoyed many favors and
2 CONC dignities at the Court of France.  Of his fourteen Children by his wif
2 CONC e Louise d'Alès, nine of them subsequently held high positions and pre
2 CONC ferments at the Same Court; we may therefore conclude, that they adher
2 CONC ed to the original faith of their forefathers and were all Roman Catho
2 CONC lics.
2 CONT
2 CONT Presuming therefore, that there were other children born to Jacques de
2 CONC  Brisay who settled and died in Holland cir. 1625 and that they, with
2 CONC their Father continued Huguenots & exiles from France, the descendant
2 CONC from one of the Sons, was most probably the Father of the Théophile de
2 CONC  la Cour de Brisay of the Earl of Lifford's, French Huguenot Regiment,
2 CONC  whose commission, we find, is dated in 1689, four years after the Rev
2 CONC ocation of the Edict of Nantes and 64 years after the death at Heusden
2 CONC  in Holland of the Refugee Jacques de Brisay; who may have been his Gr
2 CONC andfather (see part 1 St page 35)
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of the Family since its flight from France, has been variousl
2 CONC y spelt, as De Brisay, Du Brisay, Des Brisay and Desbrisay etc.  This
2 CONC may be accounted for, by the Dutch people, instead of pronouncing the
2 CONC "e" in "de" as "deux" in French, gave to the "e" the sound of "a", as
2 CONC in "day", the "s" being subsequently added and the name written as one
2 CONC  word "Desbrisay", such as we find it in the Muster Rolls of the Frenc
2 CONC h Regiments, who crossed to England with William of Orange in 1689.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arms: fascé d'argent et de gueules de huit pieces
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: This is a transcription of a small part of the 100 page handwrit
2 CONC ten manuscript by Rev.  DesBrisay, a photocopy of which is in the poss
2 CONC ession of Richard B. DesBrisay (Moncton, NB).  Names which are uncerta
2 CONC in in transcription from the original handwritten record are underline
2 CONC d.  The original punctuation has been retained.
2 CONT Douglas L. Smith         26 Feb 1990
2 CONT Webster, NY
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT  
1 _UID BA4011B7D6074EC08A323B11713C090C77B5
1 CHAN
2 DATE 11 OCT 2008
1 FAMS @fam03039@
0 @ind04510@ INDI
1 NAME Madelaine Boisrond /de St. Leger/
2 DISPLAY Madelaine Boisrond de St. Leger
2 GIVN Madelaine Boisrond
2 SURN de St. Leger
1 SOURCES @S6@
1 POSITION -5090,-2310
2 BOUNDARYRECT -5127,-2284,-5053,-2371
1 DISPLAY
2 COLORS
3 GENDER
4 FILL #00FF00
1 SEX F
1 DEAT Y
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE [Lart, II, 15].
2 CONT
2 CONT Although it's technically correct that I know nothing at all about Mad
2 CONC elaine Boisrond de St. Leger (the wife of Captain Th\'eophile de la Co
2 CONC eur Desbrisay), I believe I do know a little. It is known that Réné de
2 CONC  St. Leger, Seigneur de Boisrond et de Orignac was the Protestant comm
2 CONC ander of a French regiment at the time of the Revocation of the Edict
2 CONC of Nantes. Although he converted to Catholicism, his wife and children
2 CONC  did not, deciding to flee the country. One of his sons, Samuel de St.
2 CONC  Leger de Boisrond, entered Brandenburg service before joining Cambon'
2 CONC s Huguenot regiment in England. Another of his sons, Henri, also serve
2 CONC d in Ireland in one of the Huguenot regiments. It is implausible that
2 CONC Madelaine Boisrond de St. Leger was anything other than Madelaine de S
2 CONC t. Leger de Boisrond, and closely related to Samuel and Henri, either
2 CONC their sister or the daughter of one of them. Note that Samuel was in t
2 CONC he exact same regiment as Théophile; indeed, he was a lieutenant-colon
2 CONC el (commissioned in September, 1690), while Théophile was a mere capta
2 CONC in. They appear together in the list in Agnew's book.\footnote{{\it Pr
2 CONC otestant Exiles from France in the Reign of Louis XIV: The Huguenot Re
2 CONC fugees and their Descendants in Great Britain and Ireland}, Rev. David
2 CONC  C. A. Agnew, Reeves & Turner, London, 1874}. So it's most likely that
2 CONC  Madelaine was the sister or daughter of Samuel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yet another of Réné's sons was Henri; in the act of Parliament that na
2 CONC turalised him it states that he was the son of Réné Boisrond de St. Le
2 CONC ger, by Benine, his wife, born in the province of Saintonge in France\
2 CONC footnote{{\it The Huguenot Settlements in Ireland}, by Grace Lawless L
2 CONC ee, Longmans, 1936.}. One of Henri's sons was called Théophile, who wa
2 CONC s presumably named after the good Captain, either his aunt's husband o
2 CONC r his cousin's husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnew claims that the wife and daughter of Réné were refugees in Engla
2 CONC nd (which confirms that Réné had a daughter), and says that the daught
2 CONC er was imprisoned in France, being conveyed from one convent to anothe
2 CONC r from 1685 to 1688, but, upon proving `obstinate', was banished. We d
2 CONC on't know this daughter was Madelaine, but it might have been. It's ce
2 CONC rtainly consistent with a marriage date of 1692.
2 CONT
2 CONT All in all, there can be little doubt that we've now found Madelaine's
2 CONC  family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pursuing the St. Leger family a little further, a 1698 list of the nob
2 CONC ility of the region around La Rochelle lists ``de SAINT-LEGER, seigneu
2 CONC rs de Boisrond, d'Orignac, etc., ... De gueules, à la croix écartelée
2 CONC d'argent et d'azur, cantonnée de 4 fleurs de lys d'or." So a red backg
2 CONC round, a silver and blue cross with golden fleurs de lys. Sounds quite
2 CONC  nice, doesn't it? It'd make a nice cushion. More history of the St. L
2 CONC eger family appears in Archives Historiques de la Saintonge et de L'Au
2 CONC nis, published by the Société des Archives Historiques de la Saintonge
2 CONC  et de L'Aunis. The first Réné de Saint-Légier, Seigneur de Boisrond e
2 CONC t de la Montagne, married Péronne de Pradel in 1560, and was still liv
2 CONC ing in 1582. His son, another Réné, became the Seignuer d'Orignac by v
2 CONC irtue of his 1578 marriage to an eleven-year-old heiress, Marie Le For
2 CONC estier, dame de Lussac et d'Orignac. This second Réné was killed at th
2 CONC e siege of Brouage in 1585, when Marie would have been only 18, but th
2 CONC ey still managed to reproduce. One can only feel sorry for Marie.
2 CONT
2 CONT The final mention of this family in the archives of Saintonge is in 16
2 CONC 86 (or 1687), when François-Louis de Bourbon wrote a letter to Monsieu
2 CONC r de Boisrond, in Pons. The recipient was  ``Réné de Saint Légier de B
2 CONC oisrond, IV du nom, dit `Le Forestier' et surnommé `Fine Plume' "; thi
2 CONC s  Réné was also the author of some memoires, but I have not been able
2 CONC  to find out much about them. The only extract from them I've found\fo
2 CONC otnote{In {\it Société Archéologique et Historique de la Charente -- B
2 CONC ulletins et mémoires}, Série 6 Tome I, 1890--1891} is a couple of page
2 CONC s that don't mention his family, and a short introduction that says ``
2 CONC Boisrond is a Saintongeais Gentleman; a converted protestant by necess
2 CONC ity, and combining in his person the grace, spirit and legendary insou
2 CONC ciance of the French Nobility. His wife and children were refugees in
2 CONC Holland; his person, his servants and his goods were in peril; but he
2 CONC did not cease, for a single day, to continue his gay parties in Paris,
2 CONC  in Saintes, and in the chateaux of his friends. The style of his Memo
2 CONC ires is sharp and clever, with a Gallic twist; there is nothing more a
2 CONC miable." Right. So his wife went off to danger and privation, while he
2 CONC  stayed behind with the money and partied on. Quite the insouciant Fre
2 CONC nch noble, no doubt. Vomit making. I'm quite sure I don't like Réné de
2 CONC  Saint Légier de Boisrond, IV du nom, dit `Le Forestier' et surnommé `
2 CONC Fine Plume'.
