1 NOTE Louise is an excellent example of how annoying Chesnaye-Desbois can be
2 CONC . He gives her as marrying Jacques de Vignolles (in the Vignolles pedi
2 CONC gree [C-D, XIX, 746]), and gives the pedigree of her father in detail
2 CONC (in the Baschi pedigree [C-D, II, 443]) but doesn’t actually mention t
2 CONC hat Louise was the daughter of Louis Baschi d’Aubais and Anne de Roche
2 CONC more. After all, she was only a girl. Silly bugger. So if you only had
2 CONC  C-D to rely on you couldn’t trace her ancestry. Fortunately, Lart giv
2 CONC es enough details to identify her definitively (assuming one can belie
2 CONC ve Lart).
2 CONT
2 CONT http://homepages.rootsweb.com/~addams/personal/baschi.html  (construct
2 CONC ed by William Addams Reitwiesner [RW])  has a detailed, fully referenc
2 CONC ed, pedigree of Louise, constructed by Reitwiesner from Chesnaye-Desbo
2 CONC is and Europäische Stammtafeln , Neue Folge, herausgegeben von Detlev
2 CONC Schwennicke [Marburg: Stargardt].
2 CONT
2 CONT From [RW]:  Louise de Baschi and her husband Jacques des Vignolles, Se
2 CONC igneur de Prades ,  were seventeenth-century Protestants living in Sou
2 CONC th-Eastern France.  After King Louis XIV revoked (22 October 1685) the
2 CONC  Edict of Nantes, which had allowed  freedom of worship to French Prot
2 CONC estants, many of Louise and Jacques' children and grandchildren left F
2 CONC rance for other places, such as Prussia, Switzerland, and Britain, whe
2 CONC re they could continue to practice their faith.  Most of their grandch
2 CONC ildren ended up in Dublin ,  from where, a few generations later, they
2 CONC  spread to much of the rest of the English-speaking world, such as Bri
2 CONC tish India,  British North America (including the Caribbean), and  Aus
2 CONC tralia.
