1 NOTE When young he was called Bert, and later on was always called Mac. Nev
2 CONC er called Robert. At Marlborough College (where he taught metalwork an
2 CONC d Mum went to school) he was old Mac, and Mum was young Mac.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gummy. I don’t remember him all that well, except that he used to come
2 CONC  down to visit us in Cliffs Rd with a budgie. I have his copy of Helmh
2 CONC oltz’s book The Sensations of Tone etc. He was a keen musician and a s
2 CONC trong supporter of my mother’s education. Mum will write a lot more ab
2 CONC out him, or else. Actually, my most vivid memory of him is his death,
2 CONC when Mum cried. I was young enough to wonder why, and old enough to re
2 CONC member it vividly.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a pattern maker at the railway yards on Hillside Road, and a me
2 CONC talwork teacher at Marlborough College (starting in 1928, the same yea
2 CONC r as Freda Neal. This is where they met.). He also worked at Cousins a
2 CONC nd Black in Dunedin before he became a teacher. After leaving Dunedin
2 CONC in the depression when jobs were scarce, he went to Dannevirk, and lat
2 CONC er down to Marlborough. He took violin lesson while in Dannevirk, but
2 CONC had to travel quite a distance on his motorbike (maybe to Hastings) to
2 CONC  get them. He strung his violin around the wrong way, and learned to p
2 CONC lay back to front, as he’d lost the fingers on his fingering hand. Par
2 CONC t of three fingers of his left hand were gone. He lost his fingers in
2 CONC his father’s workshop, in a circular saw, when he was 13. When he got
2 CONC back to school he got the strap, and the teacher burst all the stitche
2 CONC s.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was very good with his hands, and made hair brushes out of wood and
2 CONC  lots and lots of pieces of split whalebone (for the brush hairs). I h
2 CONC ad one for years before it finally fell apart. He started a company in
2 CONC  Blenheim, called H. Charleston & Co, which was certainly in existence
2 CONC  in 1947 (as I have a letter, signed by Robert, on his company letterh
2 CONC ead). Harry Charleston did the donkey work, in the factory all day, wh
2 CONC ile Bert did the organising and thinking. Plastics were short during t
2 CONC he war, and hence the need for the company. Started off with wooden co
2 CONC mbs, but then they went on to splitting the whalebone; Bert made the m
2 CONC achines to do this. The whale bone was obtained from Gilly Perano (a w
2 CONC ell-known whaling family, down by Tory Channel, or somewhere that Mum
2 CONC can’t exactly remember). The tradename of the brushes was Macarl (a co
2 CONC mbination of Mac and Charleston.) Then they also went on to import pop
2 CONC corn machines from the US; they bagged up the popcorn and sold it. Mum
2 CONC  thinks it was called Macarl popcorn.
2 CONT
2 CONT They made a reasonable amount of money, which paid for their trip to t
2 CONC he US, when Mum was about 16 or so. They were away for six months, so
2 CONC they must have done pretty well.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Mum, Harry Charleston’s wife was a bit of a liability. Ha
2 CONC rry wasn’t all that reliable, and he’d have to dash home every so ofte
2 CONC n. Mum says this entirely fair, and I shouldn’t write it down.
2 CONT
2 CONT The 3/4 size violin that Mum learned on, that I learned on, that my ch
2 CONC ildren learned on, and that my siblings all learned on, was his origin
2 CONC ally, I believe. I’m told (by Mum) it was originally used by Queenie w
2 CONC ho refused to practise, whereupon she and the violin got thrown down a
2 CONC  bank. I believe it.
2 CONT
2 CONT He didn’t trust banks, and kept a lot of money in a biscuit tin which
2 CONC he buried in the garden. Mum hopes he remembered to dig it up.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was considered, by his in-laws, as quite hopeless. He was only a ci
2 CONC ty boy. So he took great delight in growing enormous cabbages, and tak
2 CONC ing them to Kate and John Frederick Neal.
