1 NOTE My great grandmother. I remember her well, living in that old flat alo
2 CONC ng Forbury Road, and walking over to Cliffs Rd regularly. Cold baths e
2 CONC very day took her to a great old age. She’d always bring along a tin o
2 CONC f something to eat, and she’d rest at the bus-stops on the way home. G
2 CONC reat old lady, I remember her with great fondness. Never a cross word
2 CONC from her. She never believed that men ever reached the Moon. “The pape
2 CONC r won’t refuse the ink”, she said. More than once. Dad used to tease h
2 CONC er gently about Billy Graham. Not sure why.
2 CONT She died when I was 12 or so, when we were in Wales. I remember Mum cr
2 CONC ying when she heard the news.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mum wrote down a lot of Kate’s recollections in about 1973 or so, not
2 CONC long before she died. I have no independent confirmation of most of th
2 CONC is information, but I see no reason to doubt it. None of the informati
2 CONC on is implausible, and Kate is unlikely to have made up wild stories.
2 CONC Here they are in full:
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------
2 CONT This is a Background History of Kate Neal (2nd )Wife of Jack Neal and
2 CONC Mother of Freda McPherson (Dunedin), Algy Neal (Christchurch) and Rang
2 CONC ie Neal (Blenheim).
2 CONT
2 CONT Kate Neal related this information to her Grand-daughter Dr. Rosalie S
2 CONC neyd of Dunedin before she died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Paternal Grandmother, Sara King-Hall whose father was an Overseer on t
2 CONC he London Docks and brother was an admiral. Then followed 2 generation
2 CONC s of admirals. Stephen King-Hall wrote an autobiography.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sara King-Hall married William Norgrove, an orphan. Met him while tyin
2 CONC g up a pig – said she got him through his stomach! William Norgrove’s
2 CONC father bit by a rabid dog and died. William Norgrove broke his leg whi
2 CONC le saving a child from a runaway horse and carriage. Taken to an inn a
2 CONC nd whilst recovering, talk came around to ghosts. He was visited by a
2 CONC “ghost” that night, and nearly killed him with his walking stick! Came
2 CONC  to NZ on “Gertrude” with 2 sons. Settled in Pi(g?)itea St, Wellington
2 CONC . Oscar, the third son and Kate’s father, was born in Wellington. The
2 CONC big Wellington earthquake broke all the china. Had to travel up the hi
2 CONC ll for water. Then Grandfather made a pump.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Norgrove cut the Monument to massacred men at Tua Marina. Also
2 CONC  cut the rooster on the Town Hall clock. Went to Nelson after Wellingt
2 CONC on and thence to Blenheim, presumably by boat because they got stuck o
2 CONC n the bar for 3 weeks. First house in Bradford Quay. Then they built a
2 CONC  house in Dillon St. where Aunt Emma lived. Aunt Emma a great gardener
2 CONC . Owned from corner of Dillon St. up to corner going up to College. Un
2 CONC cle Walter built houses on this property but lost it in the slump. Osc
2 CONC ar went to the Gold Rush and made 300 in 3 weeks at Wakamarina. Horace
2 CONC  married Carrie Whiting and lived in Picton. Issue, 2 boys and 4 girls
2 CONC . Boys, Horace and Harold. Horace died. Harold a market gardener in Au
2 CONC ckland. Annie married…….. Walter married Esther Gain and lived in Well
2 CONC ington. Frank, Leslie, May (died 9 years – Congenital heart). Stanley
2 CONC and Elsie.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gertrude and Joe Dempsey – Wellington – 9 children – 7 boys – 2 girls.
2 CONC  Visited Kate and Charlie and her children with Valerie and Kate and h
2 CONC ad the more-ish coconut ice. Eldest, John, Manager of Wisemans Saddle
2 CONC Shop in Auckland. One boy died about 9.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ovid a carpenter. Charlie in Wellington 0.0. Alfred – Gertrude her dau
2 CONC ghter wrote “The Little World of Stewart Island” etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bill Norgrove was also responsible for the rape of several nuns at the
2 CONC  convent in Christchurch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Doris, Arthur, Charlie, Alice, Kate and Sydney. (Sydney, Headmaster in
2 CONC  Wellington in Lambton Quay. Killed in World War I). Kate never marrie
2 CONC d. Lived with Aunt Emma and was very fat. Built a shop in their front
2 CONC garden. Groceries and Sweets. Sydney Nellie Wills (niece of M.P.) Ovid
2 CONC , Nellie and Mary. Lived in Scott St. Blenheim. Nellie married Arthur
2 CONC Small – 5 children. Mary looked after Aunt Emma, who was blind.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maternal Grandparents
2 CONT
2 CONT Sara Brown – kept school for young ladies when widowed. Married Richar
2 CONC d Brook. Both from London. Owned candle factories and ruined during th
2 CONC e Crimean War. Kate’s mother remembered being held up to the window to
2 CONC  watch soldiers marching to Crimea. Crummy, the maid, was crying becau
2 CONC se her boyfriend was going.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah Brown – Richard Brook – children: Edmund and Mortimer (Twins). E
2 CONC dmund a Sergeant, killed by being thrown from horse. Largest funeral i
2 CONC n Aldershot. Mortimer sent to the Klondyke gold rush and never heard o
2 CONC f again. Arthur, and Alfred who was a photographer in Bournemouth. Als
2 CONC o had 6 daughters; Agnes, single. Was engaged to a Catholic doctor and
2 CONC  father stopped it. Francis, Helen, Isabella (died aged 15 years), Edi
2 CONC th (Kate’s mother) had rheumatic fever and came out to NZ for her heal
2 CONC th with Nellie Follett. Bertha (single). Edith landed at Auckland Poin
2 CONC t, Nelson. Married at 29 to Oscar Alfred Norgrove.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ted married Mary Ann Annan who had come from Scotland with her family.
2 CONC  Tots married Horace Melville – 2 sons, Eric (minister) married Helen
2 CONC and Allan (builder). Arthur went to sea in “Temple Knight” and the fir
2 CONC st boat to pick up signals from the Titanic. Killed soon after returni
2 CONC ng. Intended settling down. Went to Auckland to look for a job. Had a
2 CONC temporary job on a coal hulk. Helped friend to close hatch and fell do
2 CONC wn 45 ft and broke his neck. Oscar fell out of trap and ruptured liver
2 CONC . Lived 10 days. 65th year. Horse frightened by cans a boy was carryin
2 CONC g and shied. Builder and contractor. Edith Brook died 2 weeks before 9
2 CONC 0.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gold diggings – Havelock – Park Terrace (where Kate was born), Wainuio
2 CONC mata – 5 months. Oscar and Walter bought a sawmill. Kate not quite 2 w
2 CONC hen they (left?) on Xmas Day. House just off Main S. – South St. (live
2 CONC d for 7 years) Bradford ??.
2 CONT Kate was looking after a friends little girl for ?? years at Spring Cr
2 CONC eek and met Grandfather. Both taught Sunday School. Left school when n
2 CONC early 12. Helping at home. Learning dressmaking. Mrs Sturtevant paid h
2 CONC er passage to Dunedin when she was 16 to help with children. Mrs Sturt
2 CONC evant was pregnant. Stayed for 7 weeks. Came to Dunedin again when she
2 CONC  was ??, again to help Mrs Sturtevant because her brother was coming o
2 CONC ut from England. Stayed for 4 months. Lived up Esther Crescent then at
2 CONC  St. Clair down on the flat somewhere.
2 CONT
2 CONT Neal:
2 CONT
2 CONT Grannie Neal – 12 children. No doctor to any of them. Home-made butter
2 CONC , bread, cheese. Crayfish from Robin Hood Bay. Lived at Marshlands on
2 CONC Whites Bay Rd.
2 CONT Selena Busch born in Nelson and lived up a gorge. No schooling but wou
2 CONC ldn’t beat her for 3d. Neither read or write. Her parents came out fro
2 CONC m Mechlinburg. Farmers.
2 CONT Grannie Neal’s children: Charlie, John Frederick, Frank, Amelia (Milli
2 CONC e), Helen, Martha, Mary, Tom, Albert, Ted, Laura, Montague. Great-gran
2 CONC dfather Neal was one of 7 boys and 1 girl (Charlotte). Grandfather Nea
2 CONC l born in NZ. Kate never knew grandfather’s parents. Cousin Ginny was
2 CONC the daughter of one of the 7 boys. Grandfather’s parents were farmers.
2 CONT Grannie Neal: - Selene, Sophie – Mrs Kinsett, Joe and Fred.
2 CONT
2 CONT Earliest Recollections: (Kate)
2 CONT
2 CONT Xmas Day 1883 when 1 year 10 months, going to get tickets to go to Wel
2 CONC lington on Boxing Day. Father and uncle Walter bought a flaxmill. Boat
2 CONC  went up the river and over the bar. Kate dressed coat and bonnet made
2 CONC  out of father’s old overcoat turned. Given a 6d wooden doll. Remember
2 CONC  standing on boat and seeing cook’s dirty galley. Cook coming out with
2 CONC  the pannikins of tea. Mother 6 (5?) months pregnant with Arthur when
2 CONC they went to Wellington. 2 weeks before Arthur born Kate dressed with
2 CONC shoes with brass toecaps and put out to play and saw a hen lay an egg.
2 CONC  Heard Uncle Walter and Father quarrel and Uncle Walter punched him on
2 CONC  the nose which bled. Aunt Esther didn’t pull her weight feeding the m
2 CONC en at the mill. 3 roomed cottage – sheets of iron. Mrs Purser the nurs
2 CONC e when Arthur was born. Mrs Purser had to sleep with mother. Went into
2 CONC  Wellington to register the baby. Came back and said “Arther Oscar” is
2 CONC  his name and Kate could remember the children all repeating it. Retur
2 CONC ned to Blenheim on the river boat “Waihi”. Remembers her mother lying
2 CONC on a rug on deck with a paint pot to be sick into. Kate put to be and
2 CONC didn’t remember anymore till reached the river. Stayed in Main St. the
2 CONC n father bought a section and built house in Hawkshaw (later Muller Rd
2 CONC .) – I think. Six rooms with French doors to outside from 2 front room
2 CONC s. When father’s sick mortgagee claimed house (when Kate 5 _) they wen
2 CONC t to a little house off Main St. They rented a house from Mr. Ratchett
2 CONC  in South St. Flood came and father ran to river to fix his boat. Came
2 CONC  back and said that Dodson’s bank had broken. Some minutes later water
2 CONC  2 ft deep inside. Children and Mother on bed, and Tots caught a chamb
2 CONC erpot as it floated by. Ted and Father on kitchen table. There all day
2 CONC  (Sunday). Only crusts to eat and tea without milk. Went for a row nex
2 CONC t day (Monday) and found a little patch of dry ground at corner of Sco
2 CONC tt and Stevensons Sts. Visited Aunt Kate who made Potato cakes. Stayed
2 CONC  there until flood receded. Mother’s bedroom table was a packing case
2 CONC draped with white muslin. White crotcheted quilt with water lilies on
2 CONC it, and handsome bed the main furniture. Went to live in Bradford Quay
2 CONC  – had 3 floods through one January. Father went down with gout. A two
2 CONC -storeyed house. Sent Ted down to the river to see how it was going an
2 CONC d piece of the bank fell in. Ted just managed to jump off. There 3 yea
2 CONC rs, then moved to Main St. When 15 – Wellington for 3 months. Holiday
2 CONC with Aunt Esther and Gertrude. Left school in Nov. before 12 years. He
2 CONC lped with the house and minded the baby. 5/- p week. Then learnt dress
2 CONC making when 16 from Mrs Bunnicliff. Started school when nearly 9. Over
2 CONC  a mile to school and ran home to dinner. One day late back after lunc
2 CONC h after dragging Arthur home to lunch. Roamed round Redwood St. and me
2 CONC t friends of Mothers. Each had a message for Mother “Tell Mother I’m c
2 CONC oming round etc…” Life revolved around boats. Followed Father down to
2 CONC the Bar when 11. Rowed all the way in a flat-bottomed punt. Went with
2 CONC Nellie Ball who got a sick headache. Sent her 2 younger brothers home
2 CONC and squabbled over who would row. Shifted to Eckfords and left boys to
2 CONC  row. Sigden(?) Eckford and Nellie smooged!
2 CONT
2 CONT Billie Carr:
2 CONT
2 CONT Kate’s mother knew Mrs. Carr. Mrs Sewman(?) went to Carr’s grocers sho
2 CONC p under spreading willow tree which dripped on her. Mrs S. grizzled to
2 CONC  Billie while he was washing at the pump. He threatened to throw a buc
2 CONC ket of wather over her if she didn’t shut up. She didn’t, and he did!
2 CONC Taken to court and fined 5 pounds. Said as he left the dock – “I’d wil
2 CONC lingly pay another 5 pounds to throw another bucket of water over her
2 CONC too”! A lady from Picton in a hurry to catch a train was buying a cham
2 CONC ber pot and Billie slow getting the paper to wrap it. Lady said “Hurry
2 CONC  up, haven’t you got a bit of paper?”
2 CONT Billie: “Why, you don’t want to use it now, do you?”
2 CONT Took carpentry jobs and got Ted to do them. He didn’t like the Wesleya
2 CONC ns – claimed(?) he didn’t have something when they came in. His old Mo
2 CONC ther serving in the Crockery Dept. said “Yes, William, there’s one up
2 CONC there” – whereupon Billie said “Can’t you keep your tater trap shut?”
2 CONC He married late and had no children. No will found when he died – Angl
2 CONC ican.
2 CONT
2 CONT Electricity came in when Kate was a married woman with big children. F
2 CONC irst car in Spring Creek when Algy was 14 months. Only 2 others. Dove
2 CONC grey. Much brass -–Cricketers dropped bats and ran to see car go past
2 CONC Redwood St. ground. Curtains pulled aside down Grove Rd. Aberhart the
2 CONC greengrocers had a black Ford. Dr. Redmond had a little two-seather. G
2 CONC randma’s car was the 3rd in the district. A 4-seater. Traded it in for
2 CONC  a Willies Knight. Grandfather married 4 years when lost his 1st wife.
2 CONC  Pneumonia. Grannie Watson took the baby (Marian) 3 weeks old. 7 years
2 CONC  later married Kate. Both teaching Sunday School at Rapaura. Grandfath
2 CONC er in his 30s and Kate was 24. Went to Sydney, Melbourne, and Adelaide
2 CONC  and Melbourne again. Had first motor ride there at Duncan and Frasers
2 CONC . Paid sovereign to salesman for taking them up Mt. Lofty. Leaving Wel
2 CONC lington going round Cape Palliser, boat stood on its end twice. Went o
2 CONC n the Westralia (?). came down river Yarra. Called at Hobart and Mt. W
2 CONC ellington. Returned to Bluff(?) and came up overland, staying at Temuk
2 CONC a with Adam Gibson so Grandfather could buy a sprayer for his potatoes
2 CONC . Joined boat at Lyttelton and home by Picton Ferry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Spring Creek threatened by floods and wanted a bigger place. “Lucknow”
2 CONC  advertised on Wednesday, and they bought it on Saturday. Bought it fr
2 CONC om Mr Bliej(?), who had called it “Lucknow”, because he was lucky to g
2 CONC et it!
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------
