4 _XREF @place00863@
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE Engineer. Came to NZ with his parents in 1861 on the “Robert Henderson
2 CONC ” from Clyde. The presence of Benjamin on that ship is not certain, be
2 CONC cause the children were described as 5 girls. Most likely this was jus
2 CONC t a mistake.
2 CONT
2 CONT At first they lived in Manuka Creek, up near Lawrence.  Later, Benjami
2 CONC n and Christina lived at 3 Howard St, behind the Railway Station, but
2 CONC Mum thinks that was probably after Christina died, and the family was
2 CONC much poorer. At some stage they lived at Riverton. And close to the Ov
2 CONC al in Dunedin, opposite the pub on the south side (Robert, as a child,
2 CONC  would take a sandwich and sit under the trees in the Oval). When Chri
2 CONC stina died, or before, the family lost all their money. Benjamin specu
2 CONC lated, Mum thinks, and was most likely an alcoholic. Robert Adair said
2 CONC  that his father spent his last 60 pounds burying Christina. Christina
2 CONC ’s unmarried sister, Aunty Kate, came and looked after the children. A
2 CONC unty Kate subsequently married and lived in Auckland. She came to Mary
2 CONC -Jane’s christening (Mary Jane, my elder sister) and Mum has a photogr
2 CONC aph of her. She gave Mum that big heavy crystal jug that she keeps in
2 CONC the buffet cabinet; it had a star pattern on the bottom and we always
2 CONC served fruit juice in it. She died not long after.
2 CONT
2 CONT Buried in the Southern Cemetery, Lot 3, Block 103A (same place as Chri
2 CONC stina). The cemetery record says that he died 20 Nov, 1929, aged 79. E
2 CONC ngineer. Resident of 7 Forbury Crescent. Born Scotland. Lived 68 years
2 CONC  in NZ.
2 CONT
2 CONT I have a copy of his death certificate, which says he died on November
2 CONC  20th, 1929, at “11 St. David St From 1 Forbury Cresc., St. Kilda”. (I
2 CONC ’m not sure what exactly this means.) His profession was listed as eng
2 CONC ineer, and his father’s profession as Miner. Cause of death was senile
2 CONC  mycarditis. Five living children at the time of his death (4 males, 3
2 CONC 9, 36, 33 and 23, and 1 female, 30).
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage certificate of his son, Robert Adair, lists Benjamin’s pr
2 CONC ofession as Marine Engineer, which is the only place I’ve seen the mar
2 CONC ine bit. Could be true.
