1 NOTE M.P., of Windmill Hill Place, and Herstmonceaux Castle, Sussex. Also o
2 CONC f Knell, Sussex. M.P. for Sussex from 1820 to 1830.
2 CONT (From the East Sussex County archivist. 2003) “On the death of Edward
2 CONC Jeremiah in 1835 the Windmill Hill Place estate was inherited by his s
2 CONC on Herbert Barrett Curteis (1793-1847) and then in turn to his son Her
2 CONC bert Mascall Curteis (1824-1895) and to his son Herbert Curteis (1849-
2 CONC 1919). On his death in 1919 the estate was sold by his son Herbert Cha
2 CONC rles Curteis. Robert Mascall Curteis (1851-1927), the second son of He
2 CONC rbert Mascall Curteis, married Florence Henrietta the daughter of the
2 CONC Rev Robert Shuttleworth Sutton.”
2 CONT
2 CONT The Curteis family of Windmill Hill is continued in Burke’s Landed Gen
2 CONC try, 1952 edition, of which entry I have a copy.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------
2 CONT A letter copied from the East Sussex Archive Office.
2 CONT
2 CONT Postmarked 1827. I think it is addressed to Miss Inglis and Mrs Powis,
2 CONC  Shane Street, Kensington. From E.J. Curteis [it is a letter from Edwa
2 CONC rd Jeremiah Curteis to his granddaughter, Mary, daughter of Mary Curte
2 CONC is who married Steuart Boone Inglis. Mary Curteis was long dead by thi
2 CONC s date].
2 CONT
2 CONT Windmill Hill Battel
2 CONT Sept. 23 1827
2 CONT
2 CONT My Dear Mary
2 CONT I am desired to forward to you the inclosed letter from your Father, b
2 CONC y your Grandmamma, & I take the opportunity of saying to you that all
2 CONC of this family are well - we are glad to find that you like your new s
2 CONC ituation & we hope that you will make the most & best of it for Improv
2 CONC ement [sounds like a Grandfather, indeed]. We expect your Uncle and Au
2 CONC nt Darby [that would be Laura Curteis, daughter of E.J., and her hubby
2 CONC ] to come over in the [?] for some months - the Graham families [that
2 CONC would be Caroline and John et al] are all of them in France - the Edwa
2 CONC rds we have lately heard of - the Coll[?] has the Govt of the Province
2 CONC  of Cutch[?] & the Command of the Army there. Mrs. Mascall & Anne [not
2 CONC  sure exactly whom he's talking about here. Anne is most likely Anne K
2 CONC atherine, but Mrs. Mascall could be one of a number] are with us, they
2 CONC  have been on a very pleasant tour in the West. Mr. Frankland Lewis Mr
2 CONC s Hare's son, is appointed as Secretary to the Treasury - the Wagner f
2 CONC amilies are all assembled at Hurstmonceux. Past Bowne[.....?. I really
2 CONC  can't read it here]. Mr. [?] is at Rose Cottage but the lodge is unle
2 CONC t. We have had a good deal of papering & painting done at the Hill thi
2 CONC s Summer - which now seems to be over, for of late we have had a great
2 CONC  deal of rain & wind.
2 CONT Your uncle Herbert [Herbert Barrett] is coming back from Scotland. Reg
2 CONC inald is still here & Edward is at B[?] and has been on a visit from t
2 CONC here to Mrs Collet [E.J.'s sister married a Collet] at the Jungle. Mr.
2 CONC  Luxford has purchased a house at Robert's Bridge, called Highhome - a
2 CONC  very pretty place - Miss Emily Graham [sister of John Graham, who mar
2 CONC ried Caroline Curteis] I am glad to say is quite restored to health in
2 CONC  all [?]. Mr. Young our Curate has just been presented by his Lady wit
2 CONC h a little Boy - the Greenalls are living together at the Living in Su
2 CONC ffolk near Cambridge - which is a very good one - Your Grandmama & I h
2 CONC ave both of [us] been better in health than usual this Summer - & your
2 CONC  Aunt Eliz [Elizabeth Julia, one presumes] has been on the whole very
2 CONC well. We have a great many tame Pheasants running about on the Lawn -
2 CONC & there is an abundance of game this year. We all unite in kind love t
2 CONC o you and in every fond wish. I am My Dear GrandDaughter, Always
2 CONT Most Affectionately Yours
2 CONT E.J. Curteis
2 CONT --------------------------------
