1 NOTE Of Tenterden and Rye.
2 CONT (From the Sussex Record Office, the Senior Archivist. 2003)
2 CONT “The Curteis family were originally from Tenterden in Kent. Jeremiah C
2 CONC urteis (1735-1806) established himself as an attorney in Rye, where he
2 CONC  was Town Clerk from 1756 to 1800; he also served as steward of the Fr
2 CONC ewen's estate at Brickwall in Northiam. Both he and his son, Edward Je
2 CONC remiah Curteis (1762-1835), purchased extensive estates in the Rye are
2 CONC a and also at Tenterden, Goudurst, Hawkhurst and in Romney Marsh.
2 CONT From 1807 their principal residence was Windmill Hill Place in Wartlin
2 CONC g. The marriage of Jeremiah's daughter Martha to Robert Mascall in 179
2 CONC 0 and the marriage of Caroline Sarah Mascall to Herbert Barratt Curtei
2 CONC s in 1828 brought them an interest in the Peasmarsh Place estate. “
2 CONT This family appears last in Burke’s Landed Gentry, 1952 edition.
2 CONT Coat-of-arms: Ar. a chev. sa. betw. three bulls’ heads, cabossed, gu.
2 CONC Crest:- a unicord pass. or, betw. four trees ppr. This was the same as
2 CONC  the arms of the Curteis family of Appledore, Kent, and is seen in the
2 CONC  roof of the cloisters of Christ Church, Canterbury.
