1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE Most of their descendants stayed in France.  The current lords of Cast
2 CONC elnau are descended from this branch. [C-D, III, 387]  gives only one
2 CONC more generation in detail.
2 CONT
2 CONT I’m following the dates given by Lart which differ only slightly from
2 CONC those in C-D.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT From BBB:
2 CONT
2 CONT “Maurice, seven years old at the time of the Revocation of the Edict o
2 CONC f Nantes, and probably taken from his parents under the decree of Dece
2 CONC mber 1685, by which every Protestant child from five to sixteen years
2 CONC old was to be taken from its parents and brought up as a Catholic.   T
2 CONC his certainly happened in Maurice's case, and it seems probable that h
2 CONC is sister, the youngest child, Louise iv, only two at the time of the
2 CONC Revocation, was sooner or later also taken, as she remained in France,
2 CONC  and eventually married a 'new convert'.”
2 CONT
2 CONT “Maurice i, Charles's younger brother, continued the family in France.
2 CONC    Taken from his parents to be brought up as a Catholic, it has been
2 CONC said that he was left to the care of servants and ran wild, and that w
2 CONC hen he grew up he dissipated much of the property, but there is no rea
2 CONC l authority for this.   In 1714 the Intendant de Basville commented on
2 CONC  him that he was rich, and without contradiction the worst 'New Cathol
2 CONC ic' in Languedoc;  he was in consequence not allowed to sell a house t
2 CONC o pay the debts of his de Vignolles grandfather.”
2 CONT
2 CONT “As he was aged only 7 at the time of the Revocation of the Edict of N
2 CONC antes, he would presumably have been removed from the care of his pare
2 CONC nts and brought up as a Catholic.   It is said that he was neglected a
2 CONC nd ran wild, dissipating the property.   He concluded certain financia
2 CONC l arrangements with his brother Charles in 1717, by which the latter r
2 CONC enounced in Maurice's favour all claims on the property in France, and
2 CONC  who thus became Seigneur de Castelnau.   He m 11 Dec 1708, Eve de Gui
2 CONC ran, dau of the President of the Parliament of Orange, an historic tow
2 CONC n some fifty miles to the north of the Seigneury.   They had issue : 1
2 CONC 2 children, of whom the following are known:- Henri vi, Jean-Louis ii,
2 CONC  Anne iii, Marguerite v, Henri-Camille i, Marguerite-Francoise and Lou
2 CONC is iv.  Maurice d 1741.”
2 CONT ------------------
