1 NOTE An extract from [TN]: “The Curteis family came from Appledore in 16th
2 CONC century, William Curteis who died in 1582 was twice married, first to
2 CONC Joane Bunting, and secondly to Joane Pattenden.  His second son Thomas
2 CONC  was Bailiff in 1598,  Mayor in 1606,  he married a Short.  The eldest
2 CONC  son William, like his father, was twice married, some of the descenda
2 CONC nts of the first wife left  Tenterden, among them have been  several R
2 CONC ectors of Sevenoaks,  the Misses Curteis, who were nieces to Jeremiah
2 CONC Cliffe,  and lived next the  Town Hall 100 years ago,  belonged to thi
2 CONC s branch.  This Wm. Corteis was Mayor in 1603, his son Robert (by his
2 CONC second wife) in 1632, and grandson in 1658.  George Curteis Mayor in 1
2 CONC 699, was the youngest son of George, Mayor 1658, and from him are desc
2 CONC ended the late Canon Curteis, the existing Tenterden family of Curteis
2 CONC ,  the Beales and the youngest generation of Maces.
2 CONT Going back to 1621, Stephen Curteis, who was Mayor that year was son t
2 CONC o the second wife of the Wiliam who died 1582;  he married Elizabeth S
2 CONC horte, his grandson was Mayor in 1663 and 1668, and great  grandson in
2 CONC  1696.  Representatives of this branch are to be found in the Curteis
2 CONC family of Rye, the Peels and the Pomfrets.  Edward Curteis, M.D., whos
2 CONC e "Charity" is a well known institution in Tenterden, was grandson to
2 CONC Jeremiah, Mayor in 1696, and first cousin to Jeremiah of Rye,  and to
2 CONC his brother Richard
2 CONT who bought Morghew from the Colepeppers in 1781, and later took over t
2 CONC he Heronden estate.  These two brothers married two Misses Giles,  mem
2 CONC bers of a Biddenden Family.
2 CONT Richard Curteis was the largest ratepayer in the Borough 100 years ago
2 CONC , and did not hold the Corporation of Tenterden in great respect, for
2 CONC he said he did not care who was Mayor, he would be King.  One of his d
2 CONC aughters married Mr. Wm. Croughton, the other Mr. J. B. Pomfret, also
2 CONC connected with Biddenden, where the uncommon name Virgil borne by his
2 CONC son, who died in 1875, may be traced in parochial references to the fa
2 CONC mily many years before.”
