1 NOTE of Appledore, Kent.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burke claims that his wife was Joan Twaights, daughter of Edward Twaig
2 CONC hts, Lord Warden of the Cinque Ports. This Edward Twaights doesn’t app
2 CONC ear in any list of Lords Warden I’ve ever seen. I don’t believe in him
2 CONC .  I suspect the Twaight connection was a pure fabrication, designed t
2 CONC o try and give the Curteis family some illustrious forbears.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, it is supported by a document in the National Archives (that
2 CONC I have not yet been able to see):
2 CONT 'This [recte The] Desent of the Antient Family of the Curteises in the
2 CONC  County of Kent, Gentlemen, faithfully collected out of the Office of
2 CONC Armes the publicke Records of the Kingdom Private Evidences of this [r
2 CONC ecte the] Family and other venerable Monuments of Antiquity'.
2 CONT
2 CONT This document, written on four membranes of parchment, is a copy of a
2 CONC pedigree exemplified by William Segar, Garter King of Arms, from the o
2 CONC riginal in the hands of Sir George Curteis of Otterden in Kent in 1619
2 CONC ; the bottom membrane is turned up and the remains of a seal-tag survi
2 CONC ve
2 CONT From the reference to armorial glass at Romney church in 1612, it is l
2 CONC ikely that the original pedigree was compiled in that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT The pedigree closes with a large achievement of the arms of Norton Cur
2 CONC teis, eldest son of George Curteis of Maidstone, with Curteis (argent,
2 CONC  a chevron sable, three bulls' heads caboshed gules) quartering Twaigh
2 CONC ts and Segrave. Those arms derive from the marriage of Thomas Curteis
2 CONC of Appledore, who died in 1527, to Joan, daughter and co-heir of Edwar
2 CONC d Twaights, Lord Warden of the Five Ports.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents:
2 CONT Pedigree of the Curteis family of Tenterden and elsewhere in Kent, dra
2 CONC wing descent from Stephen Curteis of Appledore, c1380, to 1612
2 CONT Information quoted from escheator's inquisition, wills at Canterbury,
2 CONC armorial glass in Romney church in 1612, an armorial roof-boss in the
2 CONC cloisters of Canterbury Cathedral, a pedigree of Cobham by Robert Glov
2 CONC er, Somerset Herald (d1588) and armorial glass in a chapel in Chilham
2 CONC church
2 CONT Subscribed: 'This Coate of Armes doune to Norton Curteis Eldest Son of
2 CONC  George Curteis of Maidstone, is a true Copie, taken out of the Origin
2 CONC all now in the hands of Sir George Curteis of Otterdein in Kent which
2 CONC was Exemplified by the Authority of the office of Armes testified by t
2 CONC he Scale and confirmed under the hand of William Segar Garter Principa
2 CONC ll Kinge of Armes Anno Domini 1619'.
2 CONT
2 CONT So this all sounds pretty official. But then who the hell was this Edw
2 CONC ard Twaights? He wasn’t a Lord Warden of the Cinque Ports.
