1 NOTE Receveur Général des Finances of Poitou. C-D comments that this is all
2 CONC  we know about the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arms from C-D:  D’azur, à une colombe d’argent, perchée sur un écot d’
2 CONC or, & tenant en son bec un rameau d’olivier de même. C-D then goes on
2 CONC to comment: “Others are given: de gueules, à trois têtes de daims d’ar
2 CONC gent, 2 & 1. If these last arms are correct, it appears that they woul
2 CONC d be a branch of the preceding [?]”
2 CONT
2 CONT Now, this very strange. The first set of arms must surely be those sho
2 CONC wn for Françoise Trousellier, wife of Antoine Boileau, who was the gra
2 CONC ndson of Jean Bourdin (see the picture for her). In fact, C-D gives th
2 CONC ese very same arms for Trousellier (XIX, 37) also, as well as Bourdin.
2 CONC   I think we can be reasonably sure that these arms belonged to Franço
2 CONC ise Trousellier, because, after all, we have a contemporary picture. W
2 CONC ell, I think it’s a copy of a contemporary picture, but I don’t know f
2 CONC or sure. And anyway, it looks like C-D wasn’t too sure himself. It’s p
2 CONC ossible his sources got confused with the arms of the wives of the two
2 CONC  generations of Boileau (Guillaume and Antoine).
2 CONT
2 CONT Rietstap gives nothing on Trousellier, and I have not yet consulted hi
2 CONC m on Bourdin.
