1 NOTE The second son of William and Sarah was Oscar, but he was the oldest t
2 CONC o survive into adulthood, the eldest son, Ovid, dying when he was only
2 CONC  18. Oscar was born in 1842, a year or so after his parents arrived in
2 CONC  Wellington, and his brother Horace was born four years later. Oscar f
2 CONC ormed various business partnerships with Horace, and later with anothe
2 CONC r brother Walter, although I have to admit that it's not always clear
2 CONC which brothers are meant when the Norgrove Bros. are mentioned. Possib
2 CONC ly all three of them.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the earliest jobs (at least that I know of) that the Norgrove B
2 CONC rothers did was in 1868, when they were contracted to do the painting
2 CONC and paperhanging at Mr. Ewart's new Hotel. It sounds like they initial
2 CONC ly followed in their father's footsteps; I imagine Dad helped out. How
2 CONC ever, even at this early stage they didn't restrict themselves to a na
2 CONC rrow and blinkered approach, as shown by the fact that they also made
2 CONC fireworks for a Blenheim celebration. (They organised a big fireworks
2 CONC display in May 1868, and made the fireworks themselves, or had them im
2 CONC ported.) It seems like they did everything they could get their hands
2 CONC on. However, despite their well-rounded efforts they were as unsuccess
2 CONC ful as their father tended to be. On the 21st of November Oscar was ch
2 CONC arged and fined for obstructing a bailiff. Clearly he hadn't been payi
2 CONC ng his debts, somebody sent the bailiffs around to cart away his posse
2 CONC ssions, and Oscar had a go at him. Dear oh dear.
2 CONT
2 CONT On March 20th of 1868 they also advertised in the Marlborough Express
2 CONC to sell eight sections on Maxwell Road, Blenheim. “The Land is securel
2 CONC y Fenced with a live quick hedge, and planted with a choice selection
2 CONC of Fruit and Forest Trees, together with Dwelling House and Vinery, st
2 CONC ocked with choice Grapes. A well of excellent water and a stream of wa
2 CONC ter runs through Paddock and Garden."  It is hard to know whether or n
2 CONC ot this sale was related to the huge Blenheim flood of February, 1868,
2 CONC  but it might have been. At any rate, the Norgrove Brothers were seen
2 CONC to play a hero's role in that flood, as they rowed around saving a num
2 CONC ber of people. Their career almost came to an untimely end if the acco
2 CONC unt in the Marlborough Express is to be believed; apparently the Norgr
2 CONC ove Brothers were rescuing a boatload of people but got washed away do
2 CONC wnstream, past the two-storey Marlborough Express office and the Liter
2 CONC ary Institute. They managed to get out of the currrent when they reach
2 CONC ed some shoals formed by drays, and thus survived the day, to receive
2 CONC at least two sets of grateful thanks in the following week's newspaper
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1870s the Norgrove Brothers were heavily involved in the coasta
2 CONC l shipping trade. In the late 1860s they built (and Horace sailed) a k
2 CONC etch called the “Amateur”, but by 1873 he was sailing the “Unity”, a v
2 CONC essel that the Norgrove Bros. had built in Picton, the first to have b
2 CONC een built there. According to the Marlborough Express:
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT On the morning of Saturday last, one of those pleasing events which ma
2 CONC rk the progressive prosperity of the Province came off in Picton, name
2 CONC ly the successful launch of the first vessel built in Picton proper, a
2 CONC nd but the second within the precincts of Queen Charlotte Sound;
2 CONT
2 CONT ... The skill and enterprise of Messrs. Norgrove Bros. have thus far b
2 CONC een crowned with a well-deserved success and we most heartily wish the
2 CONC  gallant little bark a long and prosperous career.
2 CONT
2 CONT ... the general plan of her construction has been similar to that adop
2 CONC ted by Messrs. Norgrove in the first schooner they built, the Amateur,
2 CONC  with such further improvements as experience and careful forethought
2 CONC could suggest.
2 CONT
2 CONT ... Having thus far described her, we have now to state that punctuall
2 CONC y at the hour appointed the shore dogs were knocked away, and in a few
2 CONC  brief moments the labour of seven months was entrusted to the care of
2 CONC  Old Ocean, amidst the cheers of the assembled crowd, and took her sea
2 CONC t upon the water like a bird. Just as she glided away Miss Norgrove br
2 CONC oke the accustomed bottle and named her the  Unity. She is intended fo
2 CONC r the general coasting trade, and we hope her spirited builders and ow
2 CONC ners may long have the pleasure of seeing her ``walking the waters lik
2 CONC e a thing of life".
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT
2 CONT As captain, Horace was a busy lad, sailing in and out of Wellington, B
2 CONC lenheim, Havelock, Patea, the East Coast, Wanganui, and probably many
2 CONC other places as well. Oscar, with family in tow, must have done an awf
2 CONC ul lot of sailing also. Oscar's daughter Kate (my G-grandmother) remem
2 CONC bered how her early life revolved around boats; up to Wellington, back
2 CONC  to Blenheim, up to Wainuiomata, back to Blenheim again. It is easy to
2 CONC  underestimate just how fluid life could be at that time. I have often
2 CONC  fallen into the trap of thinking that, because they didn't have cars
2 CONC and planes and nice paved roads, they must have stayed put most of the
2 CONC  time, moving only in desperation, and that not often. Because of this
2 CONC  it took me a long to time to realise just how much the Norgrove broth
2 CONC ers moved around. For example, for many years I was confused because t
2 CONC he Norgroves appeared in the Wainuiomata records while I thought they
2 CONC lived in Blenheim. What I didn't realise that, to them, it was all clo
2 CONC sely connected, and they were perfectly happy to zip from one place to
2 CONC  the other on a regular basis.
2 CONT
2 CONT In typical fashion, the Norgrove brothers' fertile minds were not rest
2 CONC ricted to traditional ship-building channels. On the 20th of May, 1892
2 CONC , a most interesting letter appeared in the Marlborough Express:
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT Dear Sir:-- In your Tuesday's issue you published an article referring
2 CONC  to the new patent for three-keeled ships, and under it a letter from
2 CONC Mr. F.M. Levin, dated May 6th, in which he claims Mr. O. A. Norgrove a
2 CONC s the original inventor.\footnote{Apparently, so says the letter from
2 CONC Mr. Levin, the idea originally came to Oscar Norgrove after he observe
2 CONC d the flight of the albatross. I have to admit the connection between
2 CONC an albatross and a three-keeled ship is not immediately apparent to me
2 CONC .} I must ask you for space to state the facts. Twelve years ago I mad
2 CONC e the first model, and at once saw all the advantages to be derived fr
2 CONC om ships and steamers being built on those lines. My brother Oscar Alf
2 CONC red Norgrove visited me when I lived in Broadway, about the time I was
2 CONC  satisfied with the success of my experiments. As may be expected, I t
2 CONC ook him into my confidence and showed him all the possibilities, and t
2 CONC his is where he first got his ideas. If my brother is asked he will no
2 CONC t deny this. I further developed the invention into a submarine steame
2 CONC r, and three years ago I took my crude drawings to the Defence Departm
2 CONC ent with a model of the same. I have now full drawings and specificati
2 CONC ons that have been laid before the English Admiralty, bearing the stam
2 CONC p of the Agent-General's office, returned to me with a letter stating
2 CONC that the plans and specifications were not accepted by the Admiralty -
2 CONC - that I must construct and demonstrate. Being a poor man those condit
2 CONC ions were an impossibility. If there is any claim for the first invent
2 CONC ion, I claim it against all comers. -- I am, \& c, Horace Norgrove, Pi
2 CONC cton, May 18th, 1892.
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Well, well, a submarine steamer. It rather boggles the mind how this w
2 CONC ould work. We see here the fertile Norgrove imagination in full flight
2 CONC , coupled with an almost paranoid determination not to be cheated by a
2 CONC nybody.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, three-keeled ships and submarine steamers notwithstanding, in
2 CONC  1874 the Norgrove Bros. went bankrupt and dissolved their partnership
2 CONC . Horace continued sailing the coastal trade, and opened a fish curing
2 CONC  works in Blenheim in partnership with the Whiting Brothers; Horace ma
2 CONC rried Caroline Eliza Whiting and had two boys and four girls. One of t
2 CONC he girls, Eliza, died in 1876, aged 5 weeks, and his eldest son, Horac
2 CONC e, may also have died young. The other son, Harold, survived to become
2 CONC  a market gardener in Auckland, or so my G-grandmother Kate said. By 1
2 CONC 886 Horace was worth £4,000, had six permanent employees and produced
2 CONC around 10,000 cases of fish anually. Horace appears many times in cour
2 CONC t records of the day as he was a remarkably litigious man, always suin
2 CONC g or being sued over money paid or not paid, conditions fulfilled or n
2 CONC ot fulfilled.
2 CONT
2 CONT Oscar seems to have been less successful. We have already seen how he
2 CONC went to the Wakamarina gold rush and earned £300 in three weeks, but I
2 CONC  doubt this money lasted long. He and his brother Walter had a busines
2 CONC s venture in Wainuiomata, where they operated a sawmill (or possibly a
2 CONC  flax mill), and appear on the 1884 list of original settlers. They al
2 CONC so had 400 sheep in 1884, so their activities were not confined to mil
2 CONC ling alone, but since they had no sheep at all in 1885 I doubt they we
2 CONC re notable sheepfarmers. Oscar also continued in his father's trade as
2 CONC  a painter until at least 1900, when he won a tender to paint some new
2 CONC  buildings in Blenheim. The mill at Wainuiomata was the cause of at le
2 CONC ast one quarrel between Oscar and Walter, in which Walter punched Osca
2 CONC r on the nose. The scoundrel! It seems that Oscar thought that Walter'
2 CONC s wife, Esther Gain, hadn't been pulling her weight, and hadn't been f
2 CONC eeding the men at the mill properly. Walter took offence. Well, who wo
2 CONC uldn't?
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 18th of June, 1878, Oscar married Edith Brook, born in London o
2 CONC n the 3rd of December, 1848, who had come to New Zealand for her healt
2 CONC h, as she had rheumatic fever. Edith Brook's father was a candlemaker
2 CONC called Richard Brook, and her mother, Sarah Brown, kept a school for y
2 CONC oung ladies after the death of Richard (which happened I don't know wh
2 CONC en). They lived at 20 Bridge St., Southwark, Surrey, at some stage. Ac
2 CONC cording to Kate Norgrove (Edith's daughter),  young Edith could rememb
2 CONC er being held up to the window to watch the soldiers marching to the C
2 CONC rimea. Crummy, the maid, was crying because her boyfriend was going. T
2 CONC he Crimean War did for more than Crummy's love life; it also ruined th
2 CONC e candle factory.
2 CONT
2 CONT Oscar and Edith had five children. The eldest, Edgar Roydon, was born
2 CONC in August, 1878, which, the observant reader will no doubt notice, is
2 CONC considerably less than nine months after June, 1878. Naughty Oscar and
2 CONC  Edith. Edgar married Mary Ann Annand, and one of their children was J
2 CONC oan Norgrove, later Joan McNaught, who has been such a help to me in f
2 CONC inding out information about the Norgrove family. The next was a daugh
2 CONC ter, Bertha Emma Mabel, born in 1880, but who committed suicide (by dr
2 CONC owning) in 1911. Bertha married a Melville, had a son, Eric Jack, who
2 CONC had a son, Ian, in turn. Ian Melville is another of the people who hav
2 CONC e sent me an enormous amount of information about the Norgroves, and a
2 CONC nother to whom I owe a considerable debt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Next to come was my G-grandmother, Kate Norgrove (who married a Neal,
2 CONC as described in Chapter \ref{chap:neals}), and after her were two more
2 CONC  sons, both of whom died young. Arthur Oscar (born in 1884) died on th
2 CONC e 19th of June, 1914; he “fell down the hold of the collier Kauri at t
2 CONC he Queen's Wharf this afternoon and was killed. The doctor of HMS Psyc
2 CONC he went to the man's assistance, but death had been instantaneous. Dec
2 CONC eased was single, about 24 years of age, and belonged to Blenheim." Ac
2 CONC cording to his sister Kate he went to sea in the “Temple Knight” and w
2 CONC as on the first boat to pick up signals from the Titanic. The youngest
2 CONC  child, Alfred Brook, born in 1886, died in 1918 during the flu epidem
2 CONC ic.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 22nd of May, 1886, something occurred that throws enormous ligh
2 CONC t on the entire history of the Norgrove family in New Zealand. The Mar
2 CONC lborough Express writes:
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------
2 CONT At the R.M. Court this morning, before Mr. Allen, R.M., Oscar Norgrove
2 CONC  was brought up as a person of unsound mind, and unfit to be at large.
2 CONC  In support of this view the evidence of Drs. Porter and Nairn was tak
2 CONC en. Norgrove protested to the Court that he was not mad, though very e
2 CONC xcited about certain inventions for electric ships and perpetual motio
2 CONC n which had occurred to his mind, and stated that, if liberated, he wo
2 CONC uld go steadily to work and calm down. He admitted, however, that the
2 CONC Court in dealing with him was acting for his good, though he protested
2 CONC  that it was mistaken kindness. He also complained that he had been ta
2 CONC ken to a cell yesterday, and had not been supplied with any tea. Mr. A
2 CONC llen said that in Norgrove's present state of dangerous excitement it
2 CONC would be necessary to send him to an asylum for a short time for medic
2 CONC al treatment. His Worship added that it was for his good, and not by w
2 CONC ay of punishment. Norgrove admitted that was so, but hoped that “Valen
2 CONC tine Vox" treatment would not be dealt out to him, and that his invent
2 CONC ions would not be burked and the public hoodwinked. The unfortunate ma
2 CONC n was then removed.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Then again on the 5th of March, 1888, again from the Marlborough Expre
2 CONC ss:
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT Mr. Oscar Norgrove was committed to the Wellington Lunatic Asylum on S
2 CONC aturday by Mr. Allen R.M., Drs. Cleghorn and Nairn having certified to
2 CONC  his being insane. The poor fellow brought to our office a few days ag
2 CONC o some models, very cleverly designed, with which he had been working
2 CONC out the theory of perpetual motion. Great sympathy is felt for his fam
2 CONC ily.
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT
2 CONT And there you have it. The explanation, I think, of why the Norgroves,
2 CONC  so energetic, so intelligent, so talented, so creative, were so unsuc
2 CONC cessful. I cannot read those newspaper entries without feeling enormou
2 CONC s sadness for Oscar Norgrove; teetering on the brink of insanity but w
2 CONC ell enough to realise that he wasn't well, terrified that his idea and
2 CONC  inventions would be stolen and unable to get them out of his mind alt
2 CONC hough he realised that this was a sign of insanity, well enough to be
2 CONC terrified of the lunatic asylum (and who wouldn't be, given the condit
2 CONC ions in which the inmates of such places lived?), and yet well enough
2 CONC to know that he had to go.
2 CONT
2 CONT Poor Oscar. And poor Edith and the children, even more so. How must it
2 CONC  have been for them, with their husband and father, their only source
2 CONC of support, becoming gradually worse over the years (as he almost cert
2 CONC ainly did), until finally they could no longer cope, the community cou
2 CONC ld no longer cope, and such drastic measures became necessary? One can
2 CONC not imagine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note the dates. Oscar must have been in and out of the Wellington Luna
2 CONC tic Asylum at least twice, a couple of years apart, and possibly more
2 CONC often. We know he was working again in 1891 and 1900, when he got more
2 CONC  painting contracts, and it may be that he recovered well enough to le
2 CONC ad the rest of his life in a normal fashion. I hope he did. But I have
2 CONC  little doubt that Oscar, and most likely his father before him, was a
2 CONC  man who, for most of his life, struggled to survive on that knife edg
2 CONC e; the genius of ideas on the one hand and crazed obsession on the oth
2 CONC er. There are quite clear signs that other members of the family also
2 CONC suffered similarly. Horace exhibits signs of paranoia in his letter ab
2 CONC out the three-keeled ship and his incessant litigation, while Bertha's
2 CONC  suicide cannot plausibly be unrelated. My heart goes out to him and E
2 CONC dith. They cannot always have been happy.
2 CONT
2 CONT I must admit that Oscar's illness raises a number of questions for me.
2 CONC  In the court records he didn't sound too deranged. Indeed, he was cle
2 CONC arly well enough to make literary references, and to understand well w
2 CONC hat was happening, and where he was going and why. So why was such dra
2 CONC stic action taken? Was his family unable to cope? Unwilling, maybe? Wa
2 CONC s he dangerous to others or to himself? How long was he in the asylum,
2 CONC  and how many times? Do medical records of his visits exist? It's unli
2 CONC kely that we'll ever be able to answer all these questions.
2 CONT
2 CONT We know for sure that, whether or not they were happy, they were certa
2 CONC inly poor. You can see the poverty clearly in a photograph of Oscar, E
2 CONC dith, and their two eldest children, Edgar and Bertha, which must have
2 CONC  been taken around 1888. Their house was unkempt and simple, the garde
2 CONC n completely untended, with wood and branches in an untidy heap. Oscar
2 CONC  is holding some unidentified thing, dressed in his working clothes, a
2 CONC nd looking as scruffy as I usually do, and his family look undeniably
2 CONC grim.  It's hard to avoid the conclusion that they were poor as a resu
2 CONC lt of Oscar's mental illness; although this must have affected Kate gr
2 CONC eatly, she never made any mention of it. Ever. Kate would tell stories
2 CONC  of being sent down to the store to get food on credit as they couldn'
2 CONC t afford to pay for it, and how she was dressed in a coat and bonnet m
2 CONC ade from her father's old overcoat turned. Her 6d wooden doll was a tr
2 CONC easure and her fancy shoes had brass toecaps; her mother's bedside tab
2 CONC le was a packing case draped with white muslin. I suppose that poverty
2 CONC  was no shame to her, but a father's illness was more difficult to cop
2 CONC e with.
2 CONT
2 CONT In August 1907 Oscar finally gave up the struggle, or had it given up
2 CONC for him I suppose, to be more accurate. His horse was frightened by so
2 CONC me cans a boy was carrying, and threw Oscar off the trap, rupturing hi
2 CONC s liver. Oscar lingered on for ten days before finally giving up the g
2 CONC host.  Oscar's obituary appeared in, of all unlikely places, the West
2 CONC Coast Times:
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT The late Mr. Oscar Alfred Norgrove who died recently at his home in Bl
2 CONC enheim, from injuries received from being thrown from a trap, was (say
2 CONC s an Exchange) born in Wellington in the year 1842, and was the eldest
2 CONC  son of the late Wm. Norgrove. In the year 1854 he took up his residen
2 CONC ce in Nelson, where he resided with the rest of his family till 1861,
2 CONC when he came to the Wairau. In 1868, in conjunction with his brothers,
2 CONC  Horace and Walter, he built the ketch Amateur, of 25 tons register, o
2 CONC n the banks of Lockup Creek, near where Clouston's stables now stand i
2 CONC n Wynen St. For some years subsequent to this he followed the sea. A s
2 CONC econd boat was built at Picton, of 60 tons, called the Unity, in 1872.
2 CONC  He sailed this boat for a time, and then he and his brothers gave up
2 CONC the sea, and deceased went to the Wakamarina goldfields, but returned
2 CONC shortly after to Blenheim to work at his trade, which has claimed his
2 CONC attention ever since.
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Edith herself lived for a good long time after Oscar's death, dying in
2 CONC  1936, two weeks before turning 90. One wonders if she found it more p
2 CONC eaceful; I know very little about what she did in the last 30 years of
2 CONC  her life, and I ought to know more. Maybe one day someone will tell m
2 CONC e.
