1 NOTE 1842 was the date of the first settlers in Nelson, so Thomas Neal must
2 CONC  have been one of the very first babies born there.  May explain the c
2 CONC hoice of name. In fact, the first settler ships arrived in Feb. 1842,
2 CONC and he was born in November. Perhaps not conceived on board, which is
2 CONC hardly surprising, as Amelia’s husband wasn’t on the Lloyds, having co
2 CONC me out earlier on the Will Watch, with the original Wakefield party. S
2 CONC oon as she got to shore, though..... As Monique says, lucky he wasn’t
2 CONC born 6 months after landing.
2 CONT
2 CONT [BC] has a lot of details about Thomas, too many to reproduce here. He
2 CONC  first lived in Waimea East which is where he married Selina (the name
2 CONC  on her wedding certificate, even though it doesn’t seem to have been
2 CONC her actual name). Charles was born there, but soon after, in 1868, the
2 CONC y went off to Marlborough in search of land and work. They first settl
2 CONC ed at Waikawa, and Thomas worked on a farm near Picton. Then, on 6 Aug
2 CONC ust, 1872, Thomas and his brother-in-law, William James Kinsett, bough
2 CONC t a property at Spring Creek. Thomas later bought out William on 20 Ju
2 CONC ly 1877, for 90 pounds. In 1898 Thomas bought 200 acres of the Marshla
2 CONC nds estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT His granddaughter [Agatha Violet, daughter of Charles William Neal] re
2 CONC members him as follows:
2 CONT “ I remember grandfather, Thomas Neal, as a very quiet loving person.
2 CONC He was very deaf. When I was 12, he and Uncle Monte (Thomas’ youngest
2 CONC son) were staying with us for a holiday at St. Omar and grandfather ga
2 CONC ve me half a crown. I can tell you I thought I was made. I had never o
2 CONC wned that much money before. I didn’t have much to do with my grandpar
2 CONC ents, but grandmother was a very strict woman and always had everyone
2 CONC working....
2 CONT I remember grandfather bought an overland car when I was 16 (1919). Un
2 CONC cle Monte used to drive it, and he took Mum and I and grandfather for
2 CONC a drive around Spring Creek, and grandfather told me that the land the
2 CONC re was selling for ten pounds an acre, which he thought was a very hig
2 CONC h price.
2 CONT They first lived in an old house at Marshlands, on their farm there. U
2 CONC ncle Monte and his wife lived in the old house afterwards which has si
2 CONC nce been burnt. But they built a nice new place with conveniences, hot
2 CONC  and cold water and a water lav. on one end of the verandah........
2 CONT Grandmother was a wonderful worker and tried to make everyone keep up
2 CONC with her. Right up till the time when grandad died whe used to milk a
2 CONC couple of cows. She would leave him to get up and light the fire in th
2 CONC e range and get the breakfast ready. the mroning he died, she went out
2 CONC  to his bedroom off the verandah to see why he wasn’t up and found he
2 CONC had died in his sleep.
2 CONT Apparently for a year Thomas farmed Burnlea (Spring Creek property) an
2 CONC d Marshlands for one harvest together. Between the two farms was the o
2 CONC ld Ferry Hotel, and he usually stopped on the way home for a drink. Be
2 CONC ing harvest time and six o’clock closing, it usualy went after hours.
2 CONC One night the police came in and were busy talking names and Grandad d
2 CONC idn’t see them and being deaf didn’t hear them, so when they took him
2 CONC by the shoulder and said ‘What’s yours?’, of course his answer was ‘an
2 CONC other long one please’.
2 CONT Thomas liked to go out and shoot pigeons, but being deaf he would take
2 CONC  one of the children to point them out to him as he couldn’t hear them
2 CONC .
2 CONT Lena was not happy if Thomas didn’t turn up after a visit to the hotel
2 CONC , and she often got into the gig and went to get him. One night as the
2 CONC y drove home from the hotel, Thomas was smoking his pipe, and a spark
2 CONC fell into Lena’s lap and set her dress alight.
2 CONT Lena was described as tough by her grandson, John McDonnell......”
2 CONT
2 CONT Additional facts:
2 CONT - a John William Neale (living in Tipahi St.) was listed in the 1870 N
2 CONC elson electoral roll. Maybe no connection. I don’t know.
2 CONT - the same John William Neale, and a John Neal, were listed in the 186
2 CONC 5/66 Nelson electoral roll. John Neal had a household, John William di
2 CONC dn’t appear to.
2 CONT - No Neals are listed in the 1859 Nelson directory. Maybe they had alr
2 CONC eady all moved to Waimea.
2 CONT - A John Neal was buried in the Fairfield cemetery in Nelson, on 19 De
2 CONC c 1865. Might be the same guy as above. There is a shit-load of Neals
2 CONC buring in Nelson cemeteries, as one might expect.
