1 NOTE The first mention of Springcreek! I remember how Kate (the wife of the
2 CONC ir son, John Frederick) would always talk about Springcreek and about
2 CONC the little girl who died there. Not sure who the girl was, but her cau
2 CONC se of death varied. If we weren’t cutting up our meat into small piece
2 CONC s, then she choked to death on a large piece of meat. If we were swimm
2 CONC ing too soon after eating, then the girl died of cramps while swimming
2 CONC , etc. Quite a useful little girl, that, if you ask me.
2 CONT
2 CONT says:”[She] was born at Waimea East in 1849 and was 8 years old when h
2 CONC er parents moved into the Aniseed Valley.
2 CONT In 1865 She married Thomas Nelson Neal, the first European male child
2 CONC to have been born in the settlement of Nelson. They remained at Waimea
2 CONC  East for a few years and then moved to Marshlands in Marlborough wher
2 CONC e for a short time her husband farmed in partnership with his wife’s b
2 CONC rother-in-law, William Kinsett. After this partnership was dissolved h
2 CONC e carried on farming on his own account and Wise’s Directory of manyye
2 CONC ars ago had the following comment about the Neal farm: ‘Mr. Neal’s far
2 CONC m consists of over 200 acres of good level land, well suited to agricu
2 CONC lture purposes, and he also carries on dairying.’
2 CONT Although she had a large family, Lena was always active in work around
2 CONC the farm and, until her children were old enough to assist, she did he
2 CONC r share in the handmilking of the dairy herd.
2 CONT In later years, when some of her large family had married, there were
2 CONC enough sons-in-law to combine with her sons to form a cricket team whi
2 CONC ch was known as ‘the Neal team’ and on many occasions played in matche
2 CONC s against Picton.” [Kate used often to talk about the Neal cricket tea
2 CONC m. I suppose her husband must have played on it. I never knew what she
2 CONC  was talking about until I read this book.]
