1 NOTE The major source for William and Amelia Neal is [BC]. He came to Nelso
2 CONC n on the Will Watch, one of the first pioneer ships, with his wife fol
2 CONC lowing later on the infamous Lloyds. He was a bricklayer, and his occu
2 CONC pation in later years was listed as sawyer, labourer, carpenter, brick
2 CONC layer. He died from carditis in Waimea South, aged 57.
2 CONT A detailed account of the early history of Nelson, including a lot mor
2 CONC e information about the Will Watch and the Lloyds is given in the book
2 CONC  by Ruth Allan “Nelson: a history of early settlement”. It’s actually
2 CONC quite a good read, with a lot of detail. The Busch family is mentioned
2 CONC  explicitly, but not the Neal family.
2 CONT http://www.geocities.com/wlorac/wllwht41.txt gives a list of the men o
2 CONC n the Whitby and Will Watch, two of the ships that came to Nelson with
2 CONC  Wakefield. William Neald (sic) appears in this list, and is almost ce
2 CONC rtainly our William.
2 CONT The WHITBY & WILL WATCH sailed from Gravesend on 2 May 1841, arrived i
2 CONC n Port Nicholson on 8 September. On 8 October they sailed for Tasman B
2 CONC ay, and finally anchored in what is now Nelson Haven on 7 November, 18
2 CONC 41. Captain Wakefield came on the Whitby.
2 CONT
2 CONT A W. Neal signed up on 1 July 1848 to give 5 shillings to a subscripti
2 CONC on to repair the public thoroughfare along Lambton Quay, Wellington. (
2 CONC NZ Spectator and Cook’s Strait Guardian, 1 July 1848, page 2). A W. No
2 CONC rgrove also signed up, for the same amount. The W. Norgrove is certain
2 CONC ly my William Norgrove, but the W. Neal almost certainly isn’t. He’s l
2 CONC isted as a juror in Port Nicholson in 1847, with the occupation of wat
2 CONC chmaker. Doesn’t sound like my W. Neal at all. In fact, in 1845 there
2 CONC were at least two different William Neals in Wellington, one a watchma
2 CONC ker, the other a sawyer.
