1 NOTE [C-D, III, 386] Jacques was a lawyer (s’attacha au Barreau), the autho
2 CONC r of the branch of the Boileau d’Uzès, whose descendants are in Dunker
2 CONC que & at Saint-Domingue.  He was Captain in the Régiment de Médoc.
2 CONT
2 CONT [BBB] claims that Jacques was the son of Honora de Blanche.
2 CONT
2 CONT [BBB]: “ Jacques i, who originated this branch, settled in Uzès throug
2 CONC h the family's connection there with Robert le Blanc, mentioned above,
2 CONC  whose wife was a member of the old Noblesse of the town.   Jacques, w
2 CONC ho was born in 1584, was a doctor of medicine (not an advocate, as sta
2 CONC ted in error in  '1754', see 'C of N'), and was a leading man of Uzès.
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT He was in fact 1st Consul in 1619 and again in 1625.    In the latter
2 CONC year he and the Royal Viguier were deputed to lay before the King a st
2 CONC atement of the grievances of the townspeople, to which the King made a
2 CONC  favourable reply.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacques having promised loyalty to the King for himself and his fellow
2 CONC -citizens  was opposed to the party of the Duc de Rohan, which favoure
2 CONC d rebellion.   However, Jacques's views seem to have prevailed eventua
2 CONC lly with Rohan, for when the latter was leaving Uzès in 1625 the peopl
2 CONC e accompanied him shouting 'Vive Dieu', 'Vive le Saint-Evangile', 'Viv
2 CONC e Rohan son defenseur'.   Jacques was trying to calm the crowd when th
2 CONC e Duc, turning towards him, said "They would do better to shout 'vive
2 CONC le Roi' for what we must do is to try to secure a lasting peace".
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1627, there was an assembly in the town, in Rohan's presence, of th
2 CONC e deputies of the Huguenot towns of Lower Languedoc and the Cevennes,
2 CONC to consider whether they should join with the Huguenot forces of La Ro
2 CONC chelle, then being besieged by the royal forces.    The Consistory of
2 CONC Uzès was represented by the Minister and three deacons of whom Jacques
2 CONC  was one.
2 CONT
2 CONT But, by 1629, Protestantism in the south of France was crushed as a po
2 CONC litical force, and the privileges granted to the Huguenots began to be
2 CONC  withdrawn.   In consequence of Rohan's military activities, Jacques w
2 CONC as saddled with greater burdens than he could withstand, and he was un
2 CONC able to enjoy the fruits of his estates, which were made the object of
2 CONC  reprisals.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married twice and had three sons:  Pierre ii, Henri viii, Jean via,
2 CONC  and a daughter Roze i.   Little is known of the first two, and Jean d
2 CONC ied in his father's lifetime, leaving by his wife Catherine de Boyer s
2 CONC ix children, Jacques iii, Antoine iii, Henri v, Louis iii, David and R
2 CONC oze ii.
2 CONT Jacques himself died in 1672.”
