1 NOTE Killed at the battle of Hochstett[C-D, III, 386]
2 CONT ------------
2 CONT From [BBB]:
2 CONT
2 CONT “The first of the Boileau family to leave France were the two eldest s
2 CONC urviving sons of Jacques ii, Henri iii and Jean-Louis i.   They were i
2 CONC n one of the 'Compagnies des Cadets' established in France for the son
2 CONC s of Nobles or of former officers whose fortune was not equal to their
2 CONC  birth, and to whom military service offered more opportunities than o
2 CONC ther vocations.   They were trained in all the knowledge and drills ne
2 CONC cessary, and then placed in the subaltern ranks of the army.    Among
2 CONC them were a large number of young Huguenots, and after the Revocation
2 CONC many of these escaped to Brandenburg.   Some of the companies, includi
2 CONC ng that at Besançon, where Henri and Jean-Louis were stationed, were n
2 CONC ear to the frontier, and so it was easy for a crowd of cadets to get o
2 CONC ver.   The two young Boileaus did so on 7th December, 1685, and went t
2 CONC o Brandenburg, where the Elector was making the Huguenots welcome, and
2 CONC  especially those who were officers and soldiers.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean-Louis was also on active service again in 1702.   He was now in t
2 CONC he Hanoverian service, the Duke of Brunswick having become the Elector
2 CONC  of Hanover.   In April 1704 Jean-Louis took part with his regiment in
2 CONC  the great march of Marlborough's army from Holland to the Danube whic
2 CONC h culminated in the battle of Donauworth, or the Schellenburg, on 2nd
2 CONC July.    This victory was followed on 13th August by the crushing defe
2 CONC at of the French and Imperial armies at Blenheim, or Hochstedt.   The
2 CONC Hanoverian brigade, including de Luc's, was at first engaged in the at
2 CONC tack on Blenheim.    It was then moved to the centre, where three of i
2 CONC ts battalions met the advance of nine French and defeated them.   Jean
2 CONC -Louis was mortally wounded by a musket ball through the chest.   He l
2 CONC ived long enough to be promoted Captain by the Prince he served.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------
