1 NOTE He left France in 1685 with his father. Daniel d’Escury was Major and
2 CONC Lt.-Col. in Lord Galway’s regiment of Horse. On Pensions List, Civil E
2 CONC stablishment in Ireland, 1702. “Served in King William’s Irish Regimen
2 CONC t in Holland, Ireland and Flanders 13 years; seven children and sister
2 CONC .” He appears on the List of Oath Roll Naturalisation, 17 Nov 1698.
2 CONT
2 CONT [SF] reproduces some extracts from his diary:
2 CONT “1686, January, at Basle in Switzerland.
2 CONT [First comes some details of the nasty Dragoons and how he and his fam
2 CONC ily were in danger. I can’t translate it properly, and SF doesn’t, so
2 CONC I shall just leave it]..... We believed that the only remedy was to fl
2 CONC y, and we prepared to depart in the night (from Chateau de la Touche)
2 CONC but we were too much watched to go far. I was arrested and made prison
2 CONC er, and all I could do was to prevent my wife from coming, and to beg
2 CONC her to use every endeavour to get away by all means. She went to the C
2 CONC oudraye House, five miles from Tours, to Madmoiselle Bouilly, who had
2 CONC a rebellious maid-servant who had caused me to be taken to Tours. I fo
2 CONC und myself in a cell, arrested, and thought there was no help for me a
2 CONC nd my family. Grief took hold of me, for I feared my wife and children
2 CONC  would be arrested and put into convents. The monks informed me that m
2 CONC y family had been captured, and that they were already placed in conve
2 CONC nts. I had no news from my wife, except a note, which I received the d
2 CONC ay after my detention, in which she told me of her intention to return
2 CONC  to La Touche. I thought all was over, and begged God to put into my h
2 CONC eart what He wanted me to do for His glory and my salvation”
2 CONT
2 CONT After five or six days, Daniel was released and he wrote:
2 CONT “I thought that my wife had returned with the children to La Touche, b
2 CONC ut was much astonished when, on arriving at La Courdraie, I found one
2 CONC of my children, called Simeon, who was the youngest, and not eighteen
2 CONC months old; they told me that they could not inform me where my wife h
2 CONC ad gone with the three others, viz, Madelon, Daniel, and Henry. Ialso
2 CONC heard, through one  of my servants I had left there and whom I met, th
2 CONC at my wife was not there. At last I learned from a letter I had the sa
2 CONC me evening from her, that  she had gone  straight to Monbelliard with
2 CONC her three children. I blessed God for her resolution, and only thought
2 CONC  of taking away my poor child and following her; that was a great diff
2 CONC iculty, as I only had one arm, but God, who managed everything for our
2 CONC  salvation, had taken her to one of her friends at Boisgensy, from whe
2 CONC nce she  sent me back the only valet who was of her religion to try to
2 CONC  hear of my whereabouts. I was delighted to hear about her, and hoped
2 CONC God would not try us any more, since He had taken her so far without b
2 CONC eing arrested. It is true she thought this would have happened at Ambo
2 CONC ise, but God saved her. I could only think of reaching her. I made the
2 CONC  servant carry the child Simeon and set off directly, and ordered the
2 CONC valet to go to Orleans, where I went also. We walked without stopping
2 CONC as far as Bar, near Monbelliard, where I did not think we were safe.
2 CONT After spending a day there we started to this place, where Divine Prov
2 CONC idence has taken us for the salvation of our souls. My three eldest ch
2 CONC ildren were on one horse - the two boys in the baskets and the girl on
2 CONC  a pack-saddle, and poor little Simeon in front of the valet’s horse;
2 CONC my wife and I each on a horse. This little story is only related to le
2 CONC t my children know, when they are old enough, what great things the Lo
2 CONC rd has done in taking them out of this wretched Sodom to keep them in
2 CONC His real Bethel, the house of the Lord.”
2 CONT
2 CONT [SF]. Regarding the escape from France, Henry Maret de la Rive writes:
2 CONC  “My great Grandfather was David [he means André] Collot D’Escury. He
2 CONC stole early out at the Commencement of the Persecution, and leaving hi
2 CONC s own Country went to Holland. His and family’s Escape was thus effect
2 CONC ed. Mr. Collot D’Escury had three Asses or Mules with Paniers on each
2 CONC side, in each of which a Child was concealed, and over them some Lemon
2 CONC s and Oranges for apparent Sale. My great Grandfather and my Grandfath
2 CONC er (who helped to lead the asses or mules) did by this Artifice impose
2 CONC  on all the Intendants and Guards of the Districts through which they
2 CONC passed; and thus arrived Safely in the Dutch Territories with their Ch
2 CONC arge.
2 CONT André Collot D’Escury, my great Grandfather, had been Governor of Quin
2 CONC tin in Brittany; he on his retreat in Holland obtained a Captain Commi
2 CONC ssion in their Service, and died soon after his Refuge. My Grandfather
2 CONC  (Captain Daniel Collot d’Escury), who went to England with William II
2 CONC I., there obtained a Company of Infantry, and being orderd with his Re
2 CONC giment to Ireland, had his Arm shot off at the Battle of the Boyne (Ju
2 CONC ne 1, 1690). He had five sons. The eldest, Henry, my Godfather, remain
2 CONC ed in Holland for a time, and coming over to England with the Princess
2 CONC  of Orange (in whose suite he was), he kept the Pharaoh Bank Table at
2 CONC her Court. Wehn returning to Holland with her Majesty, he was made one
2 CONC  of the Burgomasters. Daniel died a Captain in the Dutch Service. A th
2 CONC ird died young. A fourth (named Able), a boy, when mounting a breach a
2 CONC s a Cadet or Volunteer, was kill’d; and the fifth, Simeon, died in Eng
2 CONC land, Colonel to the 23rd Foot, in 1738. Henry, the Eldest Son of my G
2 CONC randfather, left two Sons, one a Burgomaster; the second, who came to
2 CONC Ireland on a Visit to his relations, and whose Christian name was Edmu
2 CONC nd, was some Years ago Lieutenant Colonel in the Dutch Service, who (i
2 CONC f alive) has very probably a Regiment. In a letter he wrote home, he s
2 CONC igns himself le Baron Collot d’Escury.”
2 CONT
2 CONT According to [SF], there was a family tradition that, during the escap
2 CONC e from France in the panniers of fruit, a soldier became suspicious an
2 CONC d thrust his sword into one of the panniers. The child escaped unhurt.
2 CONC  [SF] also says that she believes that Daniel lost his arm before the
2 CONC Battle of the Boyne, as some Boileau papers mention that he lost his a
2 CONC rm at the Battle of Attenheim on the Rhine, in 1675.
2 CONT
2 CONT [SF]: In 1702, at Dublin, Captain Collot D’Escury made a formal statem
2 CONC ent (in French, translated by [SF]), saying: that he entered the Engli
2 CONC sh service the 29th May 1689. Heleft France because of the persecution
2 CONC  in 1685. He had a pension as captain of cavalry in Holland, 1686, and
2 CONC  passed thence into England in 1688 with King William of glorious memo
2 CONC ry; in 1689 he was incorporated as captain into the regiment under the
2 CONC  command of Schomberg, and served in that capacity in Galway until the
2 CONC  30th April 1691, when he was made second major, and had a pension of
2 CONC five shillings a day. He states he had only one arm, adn that he had s
2 CONC erved forty years in the army.
2 CONT
2 CONT He made a similar declaration on 4 February, 1714, speaking of some of
2 CONC  the above matters, as well as of his two daughters being still on his
2 CONC  hands (Anne Collot and Marie Collot, afterwards Madame Maret de la Ri
2 CONC ve and Madame Corneille). He says he had lost two sons in the service
2 CONC of the State, one a captain, the other an ensign (Henry and Simeon wou
2 CONC ld be still living). He states he is seventy-one, and has been feeble
2 CONC for five years, and had kept his room for four months. He died the nex
2 CONC t month, in March. I have a copy of his will, which needs still to be
2 CONC transcribed, it being very difficult to read.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------
2 CONT From [BHSP]:
2 CONT “On top of all this, the cruel persecution occured, in 1685, that made
2 CONC  my father and I leave our native country, without having been able to
2 CONC  return to Monsieur de Landaurent the titles and evidence of our birth
2 CONC , or the acts and letters of the king that I had been able to save by
2 CONC a miracle. My father left France in 1686, aged 76. Monsieur le prince
2 CONC d'Orange gave him a captain's pension, which he didn't enjoy for long,
2 CONC  as he died the same year he left France, at Niemègue, where I had gon
2 CONC e with my family upon leaving France. [Now for my own story?]
2 CONT
2 CONT I was about six when my very dear mother died. After her death, my fat
2 CONC her sent me to Ladomerie, to the home of my uncle and aunt de Latuleri
2 CONC e, where I stayed for two years. After that, my father took me away fr
2 CONC om there and put me in school, where I stayed another two years. In 16
2 CONC 54, my father, who was in Paris, brought me to stay with him; I stayed
2 CONC  there about six months, after which my father sent me to the province
2 CONC s with my aunt Primaudois. Fifteen days later, my father returned to t
2 CONC he provinces and took me to Saumur, where he left me to continue with
2 CONC my studies. But I wasn't there very long as Monsieur le marquis de la
2 CONC Moussaye, the god-father of Monsieur de Turenne, who had seen me in Pa
2 CONC ris, wrote to my father to ask him to send me to him as his page, whic
2 CONC h my father did. I went to Monsieur le marquis in 1655 and stayed ther
2 CONC e until 1660; in 1661 I went to Paris, to the academy, where I stayed
2 CONC for 15 months, and then in 1662, I joined the Queens Guard, a new comp
2 CONC any composed of 100 men, all gentlemen of the reformed faith. In 1665,
2 CONC  I was made a lieutenant of that company, which was disbanded in 1668.
2 CONC  I then went to see Monsieur le comte de Quentin, who kept me with him
2 CONC  for three years. But the war having recommenced in 1672, he made me a
2 CONC  lieutenant of cavalry, and in 1674, my captain having been killed at
2 CONC the battle of Saint-Sein, he gave me command of the company.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Continuation by Henry, baron Collot d'Escury, son of Daniel.]
2 CONT
2 CONT As it find it necessary for my children to know for what reason they f
2 CONC ind themselves in this country, and what events brought us here, I sha
2 CONC ll try to say here all I know, not least so that posterity can admire
2 CONC the events through which God protected those who put their full confid
2 CONC ence in his great mercy; for if ever this has happened to anyone, it h
2 CONC appened to us. So, my three dear children, put not your trust in the t
2 CONC hings of this world, or in the friends you can make, since these can a
2 CONC ll disappear in an instant by the will of God, but make for yourself a
2 CONC  treasure in heaven from which you can never be separated.
2 CONT
2 CONT A very short time after the death of M. de Turenne, my father had the
2 CONC misfortune to have his arm broken by a gunshot; it was necessary, in o
2 CONC rder to save his life, to amputate it, two fingers below the shoulder.
2 CONC  The king compensated him with an annual pension of 200 écus, for the
2 CONC remainder of his life, as one can see from an act still in my possessi
2 CONC on, and my father enjoyed this pension for the rest of the time he rem
2 CONC ained in France.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1677, on the 19th of May, my father married my dear mother, Anne-Ca
2 CONC therine de La Vallette, daughter of M. de la Vallette, seigneur de La
2 CONC Touche, who had been the king's lieutenant at Stenay, a strong fortres
2 CONC s on the Meuse. My father had five children with her, all born in Fran
2 CONC ce, four boys and one girl; the eldest died in France, and the four ot
2 CONC hers left France with my father and my mother when, in 1685, they aban
2 CONC doned their property, goods, pension and their native country, to esca
2 CONC pe the cruelties inflicted on those who refused to change their religi
2 CONC on. As all the histories of that time are full of details of this terr
2 CONC ible persecution, I shall recount only those particularly noteworthy e
2 CONC vents.
2 CONT
2 CONT My father and my mother, as I said, in 1685 went traveling with their
2 CONC four children in order to find, in a foreign country, the freedom to w
2 CONC orship God in safety, following the teachings of our holy and divine r
2 CONC eligion. My father and mother were on horseback, and the four children
2 CONC , of whom the eldest was six, were in panniers on a horse that a valet
2 CONC  was leading by the bridle. My father, who had business in Tours, when
2 CONC  into the town, taking a different route from my mother and the family
2 CONC ; he had the misfortune to be arrested and put in prison, where he sta
2 CONC yed only four days. His fears for his wife and children, who had not b
2 CONC een arrested, were much greater than his fear of death, or the sufferi
2 CONC ngs inflicted on him in an effort to make him change his religion, whi
2 CONC ch was the only thing required of him.
2 CONT
2 CONT So he was freed; as soon as he was released, he spent all his time loo
2 CONC king for his wife, not knowing what had become of her. He learned that
2 CONC  she had continued along the road to Orléans, but she had been forced
2 CONC to leave behind one of her children, Siméon, at the home of a friend,
2 CONC as he was the youngest of the four, because he was too young to endure
2 CONC  the hardships of the journey. Fortunately, my father passed by and he
2 CONC ard news of his wife; he found my brother, whom he took with him, plea
2 CONC sed that God had returned my brother to him, rather than leave him in
2 CONC a country where he would have been raised in a religion so opposed to
2 CONC the commandments of God. My father caught up with my mother in Orléans
2 CONC , and they traveled together to Basle, in Switzerland, without any oth
2 CONC er incident to them or their four children.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first thing my father did upon arriving in Basle was to give thank
2 CONC s to God for the favour he had showed them, to save him and his whole
2 CONC family from Babylon, and asked his forgiveness [there follows a few li
2 CONC nes of religious enthusiasm which are too difficult for me to translat
2 CONC e. However, the meaning is pretty clear.]
2 CONT
2 CONT My mother, upon arriving in Basle, gave birth to a son; it should be r
2 CONC emarked that her children were rather embarrassed, she was so large [?
2 CONC ]. After the birth, my father left the whole family in Basle, and went
2 CONC  to Holland. M. le prince d'Orange  gave him a pension as a captain of
2 CONC  cavalry, after which he established his wife and children at Nimègue,
2 CONC  where they stayed until 1688, when my father went to England with the
2 CONC  prince d'Orange when he invaded that kingdom. My father was made a ca
2 CONC ptain of cavalry in the regiment of Chambéry, since called Galway's re
2 CONC giment, where he was then made a major. After the prince was recognise
2 CONC d as king of England, my father went to find my mother, and brought th
2 CONC e whole family to England; the family was larger by a daughter, as my
2 CONC mother had given birth to a daughter at Nimègue. So we were four boys
2 CONC and two girls.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1689 my father went to Ireland with the army, where he campaigned w
2 CONC ith his regiment until that country had been forced to recognise the p
2 CONC rince d'Orange as the king of England. When the king had reduced all t
2 CONC hese States to obedience, he considered restarting the war with France
2 CONC , which was very popular and which he could do in the Spanish Netherla
2 CONC nds. To this effect, he sent to Flanders all those troops he didn't ne
2 CONC ed in England. The Galway regiment was one of those sent to that count
2 CONC ry. But Lord Galway, who was a close friend of my father, advised him
2 CONC to stay in England with his family, which was already large, and getti
2 CONC ng bigger almost every year, and offered to gain the agreement of the
2 CONC king. My father took this advice for the good of his family, for if he
2 CONC  had been killed they would have been in a sad and deplorable conditio
2 CONC n; he had six children, the oldest of which was ten. This is why my fa
2 CONC ther never reached a high position, which he should have done, by virt
2 CONC ue of his seniority. But not having served in the last campaign of the
2 CONC  war, he was not promoted.
2 CONT
2 CONT He stayed in England until the disbanding of the army after the peace
2 CONC of Ryswyck. He then went to Ireland on a pension where he remained; he
2 CONC  lived with my mother until her death in Dublin in 1699, aged 46, and
2 CONC died himself in 1714, aged 71. They had eleven children all told, but
2 CONC only seven survived past childhood. A daughter, born in France in 1679
2 CONC , married a Boileau de Castelnau, from Nimes. Four [he means three] bo
2 CONC ys; Daniel, born in France in 1684, Henry, born in France in 1682, Sim
2 CONC éon, born in 1682. These four children were all born at La Touche, an
2 CONC estate we owned in Touraine but abandoned, with the rest of our proper
2 CONC ty, for the sake of our religion. The fifth child, Abel, born in Basle
2 CONC , Switzerland in 1686. In 1688 my mother gave birth of to my sister An
2 CONC ne in Nimègue; Anne married a Marret de la Rive, captain of dragoons i
2 CONC n England, and she is now living with her two children in Dublin, in I
2 CONC reland. My mother gave birth in 1699 to my sister Marie; she married a
2 CONC  Corneille, captain of infantry in England, and chief engineer of Irel
2 CONC and. They have had many children, of whom seven are still surviving; f
2 CONC our others died while young.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniel died in 1710, a captain of dragoons in the regiment of Walef. H
2 CONC enry, myself who wrote this, I'll talk more about later. Siméon was co
2 CONC lonel in an English regiment that carried his name, and married, for t
2 CONC he first time, a Lady Zellard de Leefdoel, with whom he had no childre
2 CONC n. He married secondly a Lady Baron, with whom he had three children;
2 CONC a daughter who died young, and two sons, who are captains of infantry
2 CONC in the English army. Abel was killed at the siege of Bonn, in 1703, wh
2 CONC en he was a cadet in the regiment of Disselle.”
