1 NOTE Living formerly at La Touche, near Azay-le-Rideau in Touraine.
2 CONT The above armorial bearings come from [SF] and are different than the
2 CONC ones given in Lart, which are shown in the card of her father. These,
2 CONC with the escutcheon, appear in the picture of Daniel Collot D’Escury,
2 CONC and are thus very probably correct. However, it’s not at all clear whe
2 CONC nce come the arms in the escutcheon. They aren’t anything like the Val
2 CONC ette arms given in CD, as discussed in the card for Pierre.
2 CONT The arms here are:
2 CONT Azure a fess and in chief three mullets pierced or, over all an escutc
2 CONC heon of pretence, quarterly.
2 CONT 1 and 4: Per fess or and azure, in base three fleurs-de-lys of the fir
2 CONC st
2 CONT 2 and 3: Argent a lion passant gules
2 CONT
2 CONT Rietstap gives a bunch of de la Valette arms, including: D'azur, à tro
2 CONC is fleurs-de-lis d'or, au chef d'argent, ch. d'un lion issant de gueul
2 CONC es (V. Planelli de la Valette et Rochefort de la Valette.). The resemb
2 CONC lance to the above escutcheon is striking but its exact provenance is
2 CONC still very unclear.
2 CONT
2 CONT Interestingly, the notes by Henry Maret de la Rive shown in [SF] give
2 CONC a rather different picture of Anne Catherine. He says:
2 CONT “Captain D’Escury, my grandfather, was married to a Mlle. Belney, a la
2 CONC dy of good family; one of whose Uncles lived in Holland (at the Hague)
2 CONC , a Monsieur La Villette, a man of Large fortune. Mlle. Belney had an
2 CONC Estate at Montbelliard in Switzerland, which my grandfather gave my mo
2 CONC ther at the death of his wife, to dispose of for her own use and said
2 CONC purpose. ....... My mother often told me of a large Dog, of the Wolf b
2 CONC reed, belonging to her Uncle, La Villette, at the Hague, on whose neck
2 CONC  his Master would hang a basket, in which lay a piece of silver. He wo
2 CONC uld then every morning send the Dog to his Baker for bread; which the
2 CONC dog would do. The baker, after putting the bread into the Basket, woul
2 CONC d take the money, and the Dog would carry the bread home; no one in th
2 CONC e Streets daring to touch the Bread or Basket.”
2 CONT These notes raise all sorts of interesting questions about the identit
2 CONC y of the de la Valettes, supposed to be the family of Anne Catherine.
