1 NOTE Stonehouse is in Hayton. He was a J.P. for the county. Hudleston and B
2 CONC oumphrey have an entry for the Grahams of Stonehouse. Their pedigree i
2 CONC s given in the 1918? edition of Burke’s Landed Gentry
2 CONT
2 CONT Arms: 1 & 4, Or a chief Sable thereon three escallops gold; 2 & 3, Or
2 CONC a fess chequy Argent and Azure in chief a chevron Gules; over all on t
2 CONC he fess point a bee Proper the whole within a bordure engrailed Azure.
2 CONC  Crest. A sprig of oak fructed Proper between two wings Gold. Motto. R
2 CONC eason contents me.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the wall of Mary Magdalene church in Hayton is a plaque that reads:
2 CONC  “In memory of Richard Graham of Stonehouse who died May 8th 1807 aged
2 CONC  61 and of Mary Graham his wife who died May 8th 1833(35?) aged 77. Li
2 CONC kewise of their two grandchildren Mary Jane Ross who died 21st Jan 182
2 CONC 5(23?) aged 4 years and 2 months and John Richard Ross who died 28 Mar
2 CONC ch 1826 aged 7 months.” The writing wasn’t all that clear and the 3s a
2 CONC nd 5s are difficult to distinguish.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stone House was a manor just outside of Hayton. In 2005 the big house
2 CONC had been pulled down years before (according a local I spoke to) and a
2 CONC ll that was left was the name, now given to a farm. I took a photo of
2 CONC the entrance, but it ain’t very exciting.
