1 NOTE Captain in the Regiment of Normandy.
2 CONT
2 CONT [BBB]: Lieut in the regt de Normandie 1734, but he must have been serv
2 CONC ing in the regt before then, perhaps as a cadet, for he is stated to h
2 CONC ave served in the campaigns of 1733-35, in Germany;  he also took part
2 CONC  in the campaign of 1742 in Bavaria, but was then obliged to retire, t
2 CONC o attend to his affairs at Castelnau, his father having died in 1741.
2 CONC   He reached the rank of captain.
2 CONT
2 CONT During 1733 the regt took part in the siege of Kelh;  in 1734 it was i
2 CONC n the attack on the lines of Ettlingen, and in the siege of Philipsbur
2 CONC g.   The latter fortress was strongly held, and made a stout resistanc
2 CONC e, conditions being worsened for the French by the Rhine overflowing a
2 CONC nd flooding the trenches, while both the garrison and the German force
2 CONC  outside kept up a heavy fire on the besiegers.   In 1742 the regt mar
2 CONC ched from La Rochelle to Bavaria, a distance of 650 miles, and took pa
2 CONC rt in the combat at Ebersberg, where it bore the brunt, and covered th
2 CONC e retreat.  It was later in the combat at Plan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles m in 1765 Catherine de Vergese d'Aubussargues, a seigneurie no
2 CONC t far from Castelnau, and they had nine children, of whom seven were l
2 CONC iving in 1790, viz: Simon-Charles-Barnabe, Frederic-Louis, Françoise v
2 CONC , Henri-Camille ii, Louis-Alphonse, Marguerite viii and Anne Augustine
2 CONC .    Catherine was about 17 when she married.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some confusion has arisen in consequence of mistaken assumptions by th
2 CONC e compiler of the Innes chart.   They start with the statement that Ch
2 CONC arles iii visited Spain and Portugal in 1754, and that he perished at
2 CONC Lisbon in the earthquake that took place there on 1st Nov 1755.   It w
2 CONC as in fact Charles-Daniel who resided at Lisbon, where he was in the w
2 CONC holesale wine trade, but he survived the earthquake, and Charles iii i
2 CONC s not known ever to have visited Lisbon, anyway.   He is shown as marr
2 CONC ying Catherine as above, but no date is given, and they are credited w
2 CONC ith only five children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Faced then with the marriage of a Charles in 1765, father of Simeon-Ch
2 CONC arles-Barnabe, the compiler of the chart invented a second Charles, s
2 CONC of the one supposed to have died in 1755;   to this second Charles are
2 CONC  given the dates of birth as 1745, marriage 1765 and death 1785, while
2 CONC  he is credited with nine children, including Barnabe.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the entry of the register of the marriage of Barnabe, Charles iii i
2 CONC s said to have died in 1793, and Catherine in the commune of Nîmes 'le
2 CONC  4 Floreal et 2' the revolutionary equivalent for the 25th April 1793.
2 CONT
2 CONT All four of Charles's sons duly proved their Nobility before the royal
2 CONC  genealogists, as required for admission to royal schools and to milit
2 CONC ary service as officers.   To qualify for a commission in the Maison d
2 CONC u Roi, or Household Cavalry, it was necessary to prove unbroken noble
2 CONC descent from before 1400, and this the eldest son Simeon-Charles-Barna
2 CONC be, known by his last name, must have done satisfactorily, for he was
2 CONC commissioned into the Chevaux Legers, third seniority of the five Regi
2 CONC ments forming the Maison du Roi.   The Corps is described as being ele
2 CONC gantly mounted on small horses, the officers' uniform being a coat of
2 CONC gold brocade, a silver helmet with plume, and a curled peruke to the s
2 CONC houlders.    Barnabe served in the Corps for five years, until disband
2 CONC ment in 1787.  His brother Frederic-Louis entered the Royal Corps of A
2 CONC rtillery, Regiment de Metz, in 1786, while Henri-Camille ii joined the
2 CONC  Royal Marine Corps in 1787.
