1 NOTE We know quite a lot about him from [BHSP]. In my translation, which co
2 CONC mes with no guarantees:
2 CONT “But for my father, God delivered him from such misfortune, although f
2 CONC or two years after the death of his father he was at the mercy of his
2 CONC unloving mother and stepfather. As his temperament was strong, even a
2 CONC little violent, full of honour, courage and heart, be began to resist
2 CONC the abuse of his stepfather, and would never rest until he was free of
2 CONC  it [?]. This is why he consented easily to my father's proposition, t
2 CONC o equip him and send him out to seek his fortune with Monsieur le Marq
2 CONC uis d'Antrague, whom he remembered as a very good friend of his father
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT My father left his stepfather at the age of 12, to go and find Monsieu
2 CONC r d'Antrague, who lived  in the Cévennes, two hundred leagues from Vit
2 CONC ré; he arrived there safely, and Monsieur d'Antrague received him with
2 CONC  all the affection and tenderness of a father,  bringing him up for th
2 CONC e next two years with his own children. Monsieur d'Antrague was the go
2 CONC vernor of Barnègues; he gave my father a position as ensign [?], but t
2 CONC he wars of religion arrived. Louis XIII took Barnègues and disbanded t
2 CONC he troops. My father then went to Holland where he was for two years i
2 CONC n a regiment at Utrecht. Upon his return to France, Monsieur le duc de
2 CONC  la Tremouilla raised a company of soldiers and gave the command to my
2 CONC  father, but this company did not last long, as the court forced Monsi
2 CONC eur le duc to dismiss them. Since my father found himself without a jo
2 CONC b, he went to Mademoiselle Rohan to ask of her a letter of recommendat
2 CONC ion to her brother, Henry de Rohan, who was the commander in the Valte
2 CONC line; having obtained one he went there.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was received very favourably by Monsieur de Rohan, and was made a g
2 CONC entleman of his chamber and a cornet in his guards. After the disgrace
2 CONC  of Monsieur de Rohan my father took service with the duc de Birkenfel
2 CONC d, who made him an cornet. However, the king of France having no more
2 CONC need of these troops, my father found himself again without employment
2 CONC . Monsieur de Turenne gave him a position as lieutenant in Streefs. My
2 CONC  father went on campaign and was badly wounded, with a broken arm. He
2 CONC was taken to Metz, where he thought he was going to have to have it am
2 CONC putated, but it healed. He went on a second campaign, without having h
2 CONC is own company. As he had the misfortune to be wounded in every situat
2 CONC ion in which he found himself, he received at Lamort (?) a musket ball
2 CONC  that crushed his whole nose and that he thought was going to kill him
2 CONC , but which procured him his own naval company in 1639. He commanded a
2 CONC  number of companies after that.
2 CONT
2 CONT As recompense, the King made him a gentleman in 1653, as we know from
2 CONC a patent that I have in my possession. In July, 1640, my father marrie
2 CONC d my dear mother Mademoiselle de La Primaudois, with whom he had eight
2 CONC  children, four boys and four girls. Two of the boys died when childre
2 CONC n. I was the third. All the daughters are still alive, except one who
2 CONC died at the home of my daughter in Ireland. The fourth son was killed,
2 CONC  a lieutenant in the Auvergne, at the battle of Senef; he was the seig
2 CONC neur de Landaurant.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1649 my very dear mother died, when I was about 6. At this time, my
2 CONC  father neglected to register the letters patent that confirmed his no
2 CONC bility, that he had received from the king, because he had left for th
2 CONC e army. He was thus obliged, in 1655, to have them redone, and in 1656
2 CONC , he registered them again at the chambers of the comtes de Bretagne.
2 CONC However, they could not be verified, and were not believed, which caus
2 CONC ed him great trouble and possible great harm to all of us. In 1659, th
2 CONC e king established a committee, at Rennes, to examine nobility; my fat
2 CONC her was assigned there, together with others whose letters of nobility
2 CONC  were useless, as they had not been verified; so to establish his titl
2 CONC e he had to seach in Picardy, from where our ancestors originally came
2 CONC . While my father was occupied with this research, he was condemned ju
2 CONC st because he was a Huguenot, without having been [?].  Without examin
2 CONC ing any of his documents the committee threw him out, which obliged my
2 CONC  father to present his request to the king, who, by his letters patent
2 CONC  of 1675, rescued us from the injustice of the Brittany committee.
2 CONT
2 CONT The king loved best to help those arrested [?], so it was necessary to
2 CONC  go through him, but we could still show [?] an ancient and noble line
2 CONC age, as we had found titles of more than 400 years old. My father prod
2 CONC uced these to the consul, but they were not sufficient to free him fro
2 CONC m his unjust detention, to get out of which he had to rely on the lett
2 CONC ers patent which were verified by the king in 1675. As for all the doc
2 CONC uments and evidence, they are in the possession of my cousin, the son
2 CONC of Philippe, who bore the name of Collot until 1685; but having become
2 CONC  seigneur of Landaurant,
2 CONT
2 CONT [ I don't understand this next few lines. Looks to be something about
2 CONC his cousin and the titles and evidences of nobility...]
2 CONT
2 CONT On top of all this, the cruel persecution occured, in 1685, that made
2 CONC my father and I leave our native country, without having been able to
2 CONC return to Monsieur de Landaurent the titles and evidence of our birth,
2 CONC  or the acts and letters of the king that I had been able to save by a
2 CONC  miracle. My father left France in 1686, aged 76. Monsieur le prince d
2 CONC 'Orange gave him a captain's pension, which he didn't enjoy for long,
2 CONC as he died the same year he left France, at Niem\`egue, where I had go
2 CONC ne with my family upon leaving France.”
2 CONT
2 CONT In this picture, which comes from [SF], we see the correct Collot D’Es
2 CONC cury arms, which are different from the ones given by Lart. They are:
2 CONC Azure, a fess argent, charged with a mullet, sable. They are combined,
2 CONC  it seems, with the Primaudaye arms, although the picture is difficult
2 CONC  to make out. I have not yet seen a high-quality version of the origin
2 CONC al.
2 CONT
2 CONT [SF] gives the French original of the letters patent that granted nobi
2 CONC lity to Andre. In these he was called, variously, André Collot Escuier
2 CONC  Sieur de Curie & de Laudaran, André de Collot Escuyer Sieur D’Escury
2 CONC et de L’Audaran, or André Collot Escuyer Sieur d’Escury & de Laudarran
2 CONC .
