1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE Ec. sgr. d’Escury and de Landauran near Vitré in Brittany. Married, fi
2 CONC rstly, 1n 1583, Dlle. Philippe de Villorio, who died childless in 1601
2 CONC . By this marriage did the seigneury de Landauran come into the Collot
2 CONC  family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Originally from Picardy, near Noyon.
2 CONT
2 CONT [BHSP] says:
2 CONT “Before I talk about David Collot, esquire, seigneur d'Escury, my gran
2 CONC dfather, let me say first that it is now impossible to learn more abou
2 CONC t his ancestors, because his second wife, hating the children of her f
2 CONC irst marriage, burnt the papers and the titles of the family of my gra
2 CONC ndfather, as we shall see in the following.
2 CONT
2 CONT But David Collot, esquire, seigneur d'Escury, was originally from Pica
2 CONC rdy, near Noyon, as I have read many times in his marriage contract wi
2 CONC th Françoise-Philipe de La Villorio, a family of note in Brittany, who
2 CONC m he married in 1583, and with whom he had no children [this doesn't m
2 CONC ake sense. If she didn't have children who were her heirs? I need to g
2 CONC et better at tranlating French]: she died in 1601, and although she le
2 CONC ft him everything in her will, as he was already well off, he gave to
2 CONC the heirs of his wife everything she had left to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT The said David remarried in 1606, to Marie Le Noir, whose mother was a
2 CONC  Coisnon. They had three children, to wit: Philiphe, André and Margeri
2 CONC te: he lived with her only until 1612 when he died, leaving his three
2 CONC children, of whom the oldest was only 4 and a half years old. My fathe
2 CONC r, called André, was only two years old, being born in May, 1610.
2 CONT
2 CONT They were given tutors in 1613, Messieur de Beauchans et Messieur Le N
2 CONC oir, their mother's brother. Neither these gentlemen nor the mother pr
2 CONC ofited from the children. [I don't understand the next bit. Something
2 CONC about the tutors wanting to get their hands on the money, but couldn't
2 CONC . The children were told to do everything they were told because they
2 CONC had nobody to protect their interests.].
2 CONT
2 CONT Everything that we have been able to learn about the origins of her hu
2 CONC sband, was told to my grandmother, Marie Le Noir, by her late husband
2 CONC David Collot, esquire, seigneur d'Escury. He said to her that when he
2 CONC was about 15 years old, and his brother a year younger, his mother was
2 CONC  killed in the massacre in the church at Vassy, a small town in Champa
2 CONC gne, on the Bloise, in the first religious civil war in the reign of C
2 CONC harles IX. His mother perished in the church at the hands of the army
2 CONC of the duc de Guise. While they were chanting Psalm 88, the barbarians
2 CONC  entered the church, sword in hand, and massacred everybody there, and
2 CONC  he and his brother could hear their mother, who was a widow, asking G
2 CONC od many times to save her children, for the sake of her who was dying
2 CONC for his holy name [?. Not too sure about this translation].
2 CONT
2 CONT David Collot and his brother, having been noticed by the troops to hav
2 CONC e white on their hats, as the troops had put on theirs, were thus save
2 CONC d. David went to the Bishop of Noyon, a good friend of the family, whe
2 CONC re he hid for three days, after which time the Bishop said he had to l
2 CONC eave, as if anybody discovered that he had given asylum to David, he h
2 CONC imself would be killed. So my grandfather went to Brittany, to the hou
2 CONC se of Monsieur le comte de Fretigny, who took him in very willingly an
2 CONC d looked after him until his marriage to Françoise-Philipe de La Villo
2 CONC rio.
2 CONT
2 CONT At this time, Henry IV took the throne of France. My grandfather took
2 CONC the opportunity to go to Vitré, a small town of the Messieurs de la Tr
2 CONC imouilles, in Brittany, where there was a large church maintained by t
2 CONC hose seigneurs [presumably a Protestant church]. There, finally, he wa
2 CONC s able to gain the consolation of his proper faith, in a way that had
2 CONC been very difficult to do previously.”
