2 DATE 13 JAN 1666
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE After the death of David Collot, she married Amaury de Mardeaux, who d
2 CONC ied before 1666, i.e., before she did.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to [BHSP], she hated the children of her first marriage, and
2 CONC  burnt all the documents and proof of nobility, so that they couldn’t
2 CONC inherit the property of their father. In my awful translation:
2 CONT “After the death of my grandfather, my grandmother married again to a
2 CONC Demardaux, a papist, with whom she had children that she loved much mo
2 CONC re than those from her first marriage, one of the only things it is no
2 CONC t possible to criticise[?]. She was a strong-minded woman, with a grea
2 CONC t deal of merit, but her hate for the children of her first marriage,
2 CONC as well as my father, was so great, that after having taken all their
2 CONC property and goods and given it to her husband and his children, she b
2 CONC urnt all the titles and papers of her late husband, to prevent his chi
2 CONC lden getting their hands on his property. As proof of this, it's what
2 CONC she said to me herself, having been to see her one day while passing t
2 CONC hrough Vitré, when I was around 14 or 15. ``Ah", she said to me, cares
2 CONC sing me, ``if I had known I was going to have such a pretty grandson a
2 CONC s you, I would never have done you such a great wrong as I did, and I
2 CONC would have taken greater care to preserve your titles and papers, from
2 CONC  which you could see who you are [?], until such time as you could hav
2 CONC e inherited the property of your grandfather."
2 CONT
2 CONT This Demardaux was just as zealous as their mother in persecuting the
2 CONC children of his wife's first marriage, to make them change their relig
2 CONC ion, and he succeeded in converting my uncle Philipe and my aunt Marge
2 CONC rite. But for my father, God delivered him from such misfortune, altho
2 CONC ugh for two years after the death of his father he was at the mercy of
2 CONC  his unloving mother and stepfather. As his temperament was strong, ev
2 CONC en a little violent, full of honour, courage and heart, be began to re
2 CONC sist the abuse of his stepfather, and would never rest until he was fr
2 CONC ee of it [?]. This is why he consented easily to my father's propositi
2 CONC on, to equip him and send him out to seek his fortune with Monsieur le
2 CONC  Marquis d'Antrague, whom he remembered as a very good friend of his f
2 CONC ather.”
