1 NOTE [RW], quoting [ES, III, 764]. Divorced 1165.
2 CONT
2 CONT [C-D, XIX, 45] gives a potted history of the Counts of Toulouse. The l
2 CONC ine ended in the XIII degree with Raymond VII or VIII [was C-D confuse
2 CONC d about the number?. Might this explain the confusion about Ermessinde
2 CONC  Pelet?], Count of Toulouse, Duc de Narbonne etc, who was born in 1197
2 CONC , and died in 1249. His daughter and heiress, Jeanne, Countess de Toul
2 CONC ouse, married the Count of Poitiers (one of the brothers of Roi Saint
2 CONC Louis) and had a number of trips, to Outremer, Africa, etc. After her
2 CONC death, King Philippe of France held the Countship of Toulouse, and pas
2 CONC sed it to subsequent kings of France, until King Jean, in 1351, reunit
2 CONC ed the Countship of Toulouse with the Crown, together with the Duchys
2 CONC of Burgundy and Normandy, and the Countship of Champagne.
2 CONT
2 CONT How accurate is all of this? I don’t know. I need to learn more about
2 CONC the counts of Toulouse, obviously. One possible source is LES COMTES D
2 CONC E TOULOUSE ET LEURS DESCENDANTS, LES TOULOUSE-LAUTREC,  by Jacques-Ren
2 CONC 'e Magn'e and Jean-Robert Dizel (Editions Christian, Paris, 1992).
