1 NOTE [FM,616] disagrees with [DHSP]. I follow [FM] as usual. Says she was a
2 CONC  nun at Mollégès in 1218.
2 CONT
2 CONT [DHSP, 15] says the wife of Bertran was Poncie de Mison. Her identity
2 CONC is a bit of a vexed question. She had four sons who are not specifical
2 CONC ly called the sons of Bertrand d’Agoult, and had two others, who were.
2 CONC  Raymond appears in both groups, it seems. However, D’Hozier believes
2 CONC it most logical to assume that all the sons were from Bertrand. D’Hozi
2 CONC er also believes her father was the brother of Isoard de Die, Vicomte
2 CONC de Gap.
2 CONT As D’Hozier says, it would be interesting to discover the reason why,
2 CONC beginning with this period, the name Isnard, accompanied by the surnam
2 CONC e d’Entravennes, appeared in almost every generation of this house. Th
2 CONC is name, which hadn’t been used before, was obviously that of some imp
2 CONC ortant ancestor. [FM, 625] also discusses this, concluding the name ca
2 CONC me from the Comtes de Die, and first appeared in the son of Isoarda de
2 CONC  Die.
