1 NOTE [FM, 648] differs considerably from [DHSP] (who calls this Rostaing, E
2 CONC menon, just for starters). I follow [FM].
2 CONT [DHSP, 107] gives a long discussion about whether or not this Emenon w
2 CONC as the first Sabran. Maybe one day I’ll translate it and include it he
2 CONC re. Maybe.
2 CONT [DHSP, 111]:
2 CONT “Émenon I de Sabran was present on the 18 Dec, 1029, with the Count of
2 CONC  Toulouse (Guillaume-Taillefer III) and other noble Lords, at the foun
2 CONC dation of the monastery of Saint-Pierre-de-Sauve by Garsinde, Vicomtes
2 CONC se de Béziers, widow of Bernard, Seigneur d’Anduze et de Sauve.
2 CONT Arms from [DHSP, 107]: De Gueules au Lion d’Argent. (In the picture, t
2 CONC he lion is rampant). Motto: Noli irritare leonem!
2 CONT [C-D, XVIII, 4] begins the Sabran pedigree, quoting Chazot, vol. IV, G
2 CONC énéalogies historiques, p. 517, right back in the time of the Emperor
2 CONC Honorius. Do I believe this? Hell no. Emmenon de Sabran appears on p.
2 CONC 5. He was present in 1029 with Guillaume, Count of Toulouse, at the fo
2 CONC undation of the Monastery of St.-Pierre de Sauve, made by Garsinde, wi
2 CONC dow of Bernard, Seigneur d’Anduse (Hist. de Languedoc, vol. 11, p. 160
2 CONC ).
2 CONT C-D appears rather confused about these early Sabrans. D’Hozier is muc
2 CONC h clearer, with more information.
2 CONT [ES, XIV, 179] gives a couple of earlier generations, but they are unc
2 CONC ertain.
