1 NOTE [FM,616]. He is the earliest ancestor given in [FM] and in [DHSP].
2 CONT [DHSP, 9]. Seigneur d’Apt, de la baronnie de Caseneuve, du château de
2 CONC Bonnieux &c. D’Hozier discusses the origins of the d’Agoult family in
2 CONC detail. There were legends (at which D’Hozier scoffs) about how an ear
2 CONC ly ancestor was suckled by a wolf, thus giving the wolf on the coat-of
2 CONC -arms. Sounds like nonsense to me, too. The earliest authenticated anc
2 CONC estor was this Humbert, who was an important person in the town of Apt
2 CONC  at the end of the Xth century, and thus had to be of high birth. The
2 CONC earliest document is a charter du cartulaire d’Apt in the year 993, in
2 CONC  which Varaco (or Guaracho), the brother of Rotbert, both of them powe
2 CONC rful seigneurs in Apt, that finished a succession lawsuit in which the
2 CONC y gave up certain lands of their inheritance. It’s not clear who Rotbe
2 CONC rt and Varaco were, but possibly they were the grandsons of Bertillon,
2 CONC  brother of Hugues, Comte de Vienne & de Provence, then King of Italy,
2 CONC  died in 947.
2 CONT At any rate, Humbert made two donations (in 1005 and 1008) to the chur
2 CONC ch in Apt. D’Hozier discusses them in detail, and the appearance of ot
2 CONC her names, and concludes (somewhat weakly) that Humbert belonged to th
2 CONC e same stock as the above-named great lords, of royal origin. Maybe so
2 CONC , but there aren’t details.
2 CONT
2 CONT [C-D, XVIII, 596].
2 CONT [ES, XIV, 9].
