1 NOTE [C-D, XIV, 809]. He shared with his elder brother Raymond all the good
2 CONC s of their father, by an act passed between 1060 and 1065, translated
2 CONC from Latin to French, existing in the Archives of the town of Narbonne
2 CONC , and copied word for word by Catel, in his Mémoires sur l’Hist du Lan
2 CONC guedoc, p. 582. By this division, Bernard-Bérenger ceded to his brothe
2 CONC r Raymond, and Raymond’s son, Bertrand de Pelet, the majority of the c
2 CONC ity of Narbonne, and other assorted places and castles. Some time afte
2 CONC r, for a cash settlement, Raymond returned most of what he had receive
2 CONC d from their father. He had great arguments with Guifred, Archbishop o
2 CONC f Narbonne, that was ended by arbitration on 6 Oct 1066, by Raymond, C
2 CONC ount of Saint-Gilles; Raymond, Count of Besalu; Durand-Raymond, & Bére
2 CONC nger, Bishop of Toulouse, of Elne and of Girone.
2 CONT
2 CONT Descendants given in [C-D, XIV, 809ff].
