1 NOTE [C-D, XV, 833]. The Seigneurs de Pierre, Barons de Ganges, were known
2 CONC in the XI century, most notably in the first crusade, at the siege of
2 CONC Antioch in 1098. Guillaume de Pierre, believed by Dom Vaissete, histor
2 CONC ian of Languedoc, to be the father of Pierre de Pierre, Baron de Gange
2 CONC s, played an important role in this siege. (See Dom Devic et Dom Vaiss
2 CONC ète, l’Hist. génér. du Languedoc, vol. II, p. 309.) These Seigneurs we
2 CONC re vassals of the sovereign counts of Melguel, for a part of the Baron
2 CONC y of Ganges, later a Marquisate.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the XII and XIII centuries, the Seigneurs de Pierre possessed also
2 CONC the lands of Sumène, Brissac, Popian, Montolieu, Londres, Cazillac, So
2 CONC ubeiras, the towns of Gignac & de Molières, & the Baronies of Pierrefo
2 CONC rt, Castries, Montfrin and Hierle. They were doubly linked to the Hous
2 CONC e of Toulouse, first by the marriage of Vierne d’Anduse to Raymond I d
2 CONC e Pierre, and also by the marriage, in 1253, of Alazage, daughter of R
2 CONC aymond III de Pierre, to Pierre-Bermond II d’Anduze, Seigneur de Sauve
2 CONC  & d’Alais (See l’Hist. génér. du Languedoc, vol. III, p. 477). [This
2 CONC doesn’t make sense with the numbering of Pierre-Bermond d’Anduze I hav
2 CONC e here already. I need to get the Anduze family sorted out.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Arms according to [C-D, XV, 843]: d’or, au chevron de gueules, accompa
2 CONC gnéde 3 losanges du même.
