1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE [ES, XIV, 147]. Seigneur d’Alès, de Caumont, de Rousson, de Boucoiran,
2 CONC  de Castelnau, et de Lascours.
2 CONT [C-D, XIV, 816]. Co-Seigneur d’Alais, Seigneur de Calmont d’Olt, Rouss
2 CONC on, Boucoiran &c. He was one of the principal Seigneurs in the Senesch
2 CONC alship of Beaucaire who assembled at Montpellier on 25 July 1303, to d
2 CONC iscuss the differences between Pope Boniface VIII and King Philippe le
2 CONC  Bel. In August 1310 he sold, for 4,000 livres de petits tournois [?],
2 CONC  the lands of Boucoiran, Castelnau, Lascours, Couviers, Saint-Etienne-
2 CONC de-Lolm, Lignères, Salindres, Souvignargues, Saint-Privat-des-Vieux, M
2 CONC onteros, Saint-Christol, Saint-Saturnin & Couran, to Guillaume Plaisia
2 CONC n de Vezenobres.
2 CONT
2 CONT [This Castelnau is almost certainly the same Castelnau that was later
2 CONC purchased by Antoine Boileau in 1500, in one of my main ancestral line
2 CONC s. I conclude this because of its proximity to Alais, Boucoiran and Uz
2 CONC es. Also, Boucoiran is most likely the later seat of the de Calvière f
2 CONC amily, again in my ancestral tree.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, this doesn’t quite square with what [BBB] says: “Antoine Boil
2 CONC eau bought the Seigneurie de Castelnau from Segondin de Saint Felix 15
2 CONC th February 1500.    This Seigneurie is very ancient;  it figures in (
2 CONC Gallia Christiane) for the year 1211, under the name of 'Castrum de Ca
2 CONC stro Novo'.   It belonged to the family of Caprieres de Gajan in 1270,
2 CONC  passed in 1321 to Beranger d'Uzès, and at last in 1486 Armand de Sain
2 CONC t Felix acquired it.  He sold it in 1500 to Antoine Boileau.”]
