1 NOTE [BBB]: From a New England family. A King's Loyalist soldier from New E
2 CONC ngland (who lost his property as a result). Apparently his son married
2 CONC  into the Bowes-Lyon family (whoever they are) and thus has some sort
2 CONC of link with the Queen Mother. Well, the ex Queen Mother now. I don’t
2 CONC know what this link is.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to [JB] he received an official appointment in Calcutta, Ind
2 CONC ia, and went there in 1790.
2 CONT
2 CONT From [JB] comes this statement from Leah Jessup, dated 8 July, 1838:
2 CONT -------
2 CONT In answer to your enquiries about my father, Lieut.-Colonel Ebenezer J
2 CONC essup’s property, I can state to you that it was in the Province of Ne
2 CONC w York in America, and a part of it was in and near Albany; but the wh
2 CONC ole was lost by his taking part in favor of the British Government.
2 CONT
2 CONT My father raised a regiment at his own expense, and went with it to Ca
2 CONC nada, and commanded it during the whole of the American war. He was in
2 CONC  the campaign with General Burgoyne, and was taken prisoner with hi sa
2 CONC rmy, after which he returned to Canada with his regiment, and upon the
2 CONC  Americas breaking their treaty, he served again actively during the r
2 CONC emainder of the war. On his first quitting Albany to go to Canada, my
2 CONC father buried all the deeds and papers which regarded his estates, to
2 CONC secure them against the plunder of the Americans, hoping to return in
2 CONC time to save them from being spoiled; but the war having taken an adve
2 CONC rse turn, he was so long absent that upon their being opened they were
2 CONC  illegible, and from this circumstance he wanted proof of much of his
2 CONC claims against the Government for his lost property. The American Cong
2 CONC ress were so exasperated against him for the part he took in favor of
2 CONC the British Government, that they outlawed his person and confiscated
2 CONC his property, which they valued at £150,000 when they put it up for sa
2 CONC le. My father received a very trifling compensation for all this great
2 CONC  property (I think only about £2,000), nor did the Government pay him
2 CONC for the expense of raising the regiment, which I have heard him say co
2 CONC st him about £2,000.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the conclusion of the war my father’s regiment (The King’s Loyal Am
2 CONC ericans) were put on half-pay, and as a compensation for services had
2 CONC lands allotted them in Upper Canada. My father’s portion was Lieut.-Co
2 CONC lonel Commandant was a thousand acres, and was located somewhere near
2 CONC Yorktown (now Toronto); but from my father not choosing to settle ther
2 CONC e with his family, and from neglecting to cultivate it, this property
2 CONC was not secured to him. All this I state from memory only.
2 CONT ------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ebenezer also appears in the Canadian Dictionary of National Biography
2 CONC . The relevant bits say:
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT About 1764 Edward and his brother Ebenezer moved to Albany. There they
2 CONC  formed a partnership, and over the next decade the two engaged in lan
2 CONC d speculation on a grand scale in the upper Hudson and Lake George are
2 CONC as. In their speculations they were no doubt aided by their close rela
2 CONC tionship with Sir William Johnson and John Butler. The brothers eventu
2 CONC ally established a community , with mills and a ferry, about ten miles
2 CONC  above Glen Falls on the Hudson. This settlement, which became known a
2 CONC s Jessup’s Landing, was a focus of loyalism in the years just before t
2 CONC he revolution, and when Sir Guy Garleton succeeded in driving the Amer
2 CONC ican forces out of the province of Quebec in the summer of 1776 the Je
2 CONC ssups led a party of some 80 loyalists to join him at Crown Point (NY)
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT The Jessup party was first attached to Sir John Johnson’s King’s Royal
2 CONC  Regiment of New York, but on 7 June 1777 the King’s Loyal Americans c
2 CONC orps was tentatively established with Ebenezer as lieutenant-colonel a
2 CONC nd Edward as captain. Although the corps was not fully formed, the Jes
2 CONC sup brothers took part in John Burgoyne’s campaign, with Edward as com
2 CONC mander of the bateaux service on the Hudson. Both Edward and Ebenezer
2 CONC were taken prisoner in the Saratoga campaign but were paroled and allo
2 CONC wed to make their way to Quebec.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since many members of the King’s Loyal Americans were dispersed during
2 CONC  the Burgoyne fiasco, the unit never attained its established strength
2 CONC  and remained for the next four years a semi-independent appendage of
2 CONC Johnson’s regiment, engaged mainly in building, repairing, and garriso
2 CONC ning fortifications around Montreal, Sorel and lower Lake Champlain, a
2 CONC lthough it also took part in several raids into New York. Edward went
2 CONC on such raids in October 1780 and again the following fall. It was pro
2 CONC bably these services, as well as his administrative capacities, that l
2 CONC ed Governor Haldimand to choose Edward over Ebenezer as major commanda
2 CONC nt of the new corps of Loyal Rangers, created 12 Nov. 1781, from a num
2 CONC ber of smaller military formations including the Loyal Americans. The
2 CONC new corps soon became known as Jessup’s Rangers.
2 CONT ---------
2 CONT
2 CONT From “The History of New York State”, Book IV, Chapter I, Editor, Dr.
2 CONC James Sullivan:
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT The most important historical incident of the colonial period was the
2 CONC "Tolton and Crossfield Purchase: since this covered the greater part o
2 CONC f the county. This was really the Jessup purchase, as the two whose na
2 CONC mes are connected with it, and are placed on most of the deeds since i
2 CONC ssued, were dummies for Edward and Ebenezer Jessup. Before the ending
2 CONC of the Revolution came an interest in the "unknown north." The Jessup
2 CONC brothers had great influence with Sir William Johnson, Governor Dunmor
2 CONC e and General Tryon. They wanted to buy all the land they could get ab
2 CONC ove Albany. Having already made application for 40,000 acres, it was t
2 CONC hought best to buy indirectly in the matter of purchasing some 1,115,0
2 CONC 00 more acres of the mountains section.
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 7, 1771, to their agents, Tolton and Crossfield, was sold this
2 CONC  great tract, and in the next year the Indians met in solemn conclave
2 CONC and also conveyed the land. For this the tribes received about three p
2 CONC ence an acre, or a total of £1,35. Theoretically, the land was sold, b
2 CONC ut before being sealed and the bargain concluded, some $40,000 had to
2 CONC be turned over to King George III. Most of the modern conveyances of l
2 CONC and are traced back to this original grant.
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT What a couple of bloody crooks those two brothers must have been! Ther
2 CONC e are records all over the internet of their land purchases.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is an enormous amount of information about Ebenezer in [JB]
