1 NOTE [BBB]: Educ at Christs College, Finchley and RMC Sandhurst.  The syste
2 CONC m of purchase of comissions had not been abolished when he passed out
2 CONC of the RMC, and his father could not afford to buy him one;   he there
2 CONC fore became an Asst Conservator of Forests in the Madras Woods & Fores
2 CONC ts Dept, Feb 1871, continuing there for a year.   In July the purchase
2 CONC  system was abolished and he was commissioned as Ensign, 54th Foot (af
2 CONC terwards 2 Bn Dorset Regt) on 28 Oct 1871 and joined at Jullundur, Pun
2 CONC jab, serving afterwards at Morar (Gwalior).
2 CONT
2 CONT He transferred to the Bengal Staff Corps in Nov 1876 and was posted to
2 CONC  the 44th NI, later to be 44th Gurkha Light Infantry, which was employ
2 CONC ed in Assam largely against the Naga tribes, which were in those days
2 CONC very troublesome.    Thomas was on active service every year for the n
2 CONC ext ten years, principally on escort duty with Political Officers.   I
2 CONC n Oct 1879 the Bn was on its way to take part in the Afghan War, when
2 CONC it was recalled to undertake punitive operations against a tribe which
2 CONC  had murdered a Political Agent, and was in revolt.
2 CONT
2 CONT This involved the assault and capture of a strong hill fort, Khonoma,
2 CONC near Kohima and for his part in this action Thomas, who led the assaul
2 CONC t with a Lt Ridgeway, was recommended for a Victoria Cross, as also wa
2 CONC s Ridgeway.   It was, however, awarded to Lt Ridgeway only, who was wo
2 CONC unded, on the grounds that two awards could not be made among so few o
2 CONC fficers.    Thomas was then left with the the 2ic of the Regt and 200
2 CONC men to destroy the fort;  they were practically besieged by the Nagas,
2 CONC  and were not relievd until after nearly three months of very difficul
2 CONC t conditions.   Thomas was mentioned in despatches, and received the I
2 CONC GSM, with clasp "Naga 1879-80".
2 CONT
2 CONT Returning home on leave at the end of 1884, he was recalled to India b
2 CONC ecause of one of the periodic Russian war scares.   Before sailing, he
2 CONC  m at Blackheath, 30 Mar 1885, Selina Mary, dau of James Whicher, Depu
2 CONC ty Inspector-General of Hospitals, Royal Navy;  she accompanied him to
2 CONC  India and they began their married life there at Dibrugarh in the ext
2 CONC reme north-east corner of the country.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 3rd Burma War the Regt entered Burma with the force from the no
2 CONC rth.  Thomas was engaged in the Kubo Field Force, and at Fort Kendat,
2 CONC from Nov 1886;  in an expedition against dacoits at the end of the yea
2 CONC r;  and in command of the garrison at Tammu until Aug 1887.  Clasp "Bu
2 CONC rma 1885-87", and for subsequent operations he received the clasps for
2 CONC  "Burma 1887-9" and "Burma 1889-92".   In March 1891, he was 2ic of a
2 CONC force which was sent up to Manipur, a native state between Assam and B
2 CONC urma, to effect the arrest of the ruler's brother, who had been foment
2 CONC ing rebellion.   For the subsequent events which developed around him,
2 CONC  refer to the Chronicles of that period which described how he eventua
2 CONC lly came to be removed from the service on the score of 'an error of j
2 CONC udgment in failing to carry out the withdrawal properly'.   He was in
2 CONC fact made a scapegoat, and was undoubtedly a very hardly used man.
2 CONT
2 CONT As recorded above, he and his wife started their family in Dibrugarh i
2 CONC n NE India;  they had issue : Ethel Selina Hastings, Eileen Lucy, Kath
2 CONC leen Minnie, Digby Whicher, Edward Bulmer Whicher and Thomas Whicher.
2 CONC  After his enforced retirement, with a captain's pension but without t
2 CONC he rank of major to which he was then entitled, he became an Army cram
2 CONC mer, an occupation which ceased with the outbreak of war in 1914, and
2 CONC he then acted as secretary for the Army Scripture Readers Society.   H
2 CONC e engaged in much voluntary church, public and philanthropic work;  am
2 CONC ong other things he was for a number of years a Diocesan Lay Reader in
2 CONC  the diocese of Southwark.   He d in London on 25 Dec 1933, from a chi
2 CONC ll caught while he was watching a football match played by a boys' clu
2 CONC b in which he was actively interested.
