1 NOTE [CT2]: 9. Sacred to the Memory of the Rev. Stephen BARRETT MA who died
2 CONC  26th November 1801. in the 83rd year of his age. He was born at Bent
2 CONC a very ancient Mansion of the family of Barrett in the parish of Kildw
2 CONC ick in Haven [sic; really Kildwick-in-Craven] in the County of York. H
2 CONC e was during a long and flourishing period Master of the Free Grammer
2 CONC school of Ashford and almost thirty years Rector of this parish. Likew
2 CONC ise to the memory of Mary his wife, youngest daughter of Edward JACOB
2 CONC Esquire of the city of Canterbury, who died 28th March 1786 in the 60t
2 CONC h year. This tribute was erected by their only daughter and Heiress Ma
2 CONC ry wife of Edward Jeremiah CURTEIS Esquire of Northiam in Sussex. (See
2 CONC  page 62.)
2 CONT -------------------
2 CONT From the Oxford Dictionary of National Biography:
2 CONT Barrett, Stephen (bap. 1719, d. 1801), schoolmaster and Church of Engl
2 CONC and clergyman, was born at Bent, in the parish of Kildwick in Craven,
2 CONC Yorkshire, and was baptized there on 14 May 1719, the son of Peter Bar
2 CONC rett of Sutton, Yorkshire, and his wife, Mary. He was educated at the
2 CONC grammar school in Skipton, where he excelled in poetry and classics. H
2 CONC e matriculated from University College, Oxford, on 24 March 1738 and g
2 CONC raduated BA in 1741 and MA in 1744. Having taken holy orders he became
2 CONC  rector of the parishes of Purton and Ickleford, Hertfordshire, in 174
2 CONC 4. Five years later, in 1749, he was appointed master of the free gram
2 CONC mar school at Ashford, Kent, on the nomination of Sir Wyndham Knatchbu
2 CONC ll. He raised the school's academic reputation and attracted the patro
2 CONC nage of the local gentry, who sent their sons to his school. Its succe
2 CONC ss enabled Barrett to augment the master's salary of £30 to an income
2 CONC of 120 guineas per annum, presumably by charging fees for pupils who b
2 CONC oarded. In 1751 he applied for the mastership of his old grammar schoo
2 CONC l in Skipton; it seems that both he and the rival candidate, William W
2 CONC est, offered bribes to some of the churchwardens who were electing to
2 CONC the post. Although at first Barrett disdained such means, in a letter
2 CONC dated 6 August 1751 he wrote that he would invest £100 in bribes to pl
2 CONC ease his patron, Lord Thanet. He had secured a majority of votes when
2 CONC he suddenly pulled out of the competition, fearing a scandal, should d
2 CONC etails of his bribes leak out.
2 CONT
2 CONT Barrett resigned from Ashford in 1764 but returned as headmaster two y
2 CONC ears later. By that time he had married Mary, daughter of Edward Jacob
2 CONC  of Canterbury; their only child, Mary, was baptized at Ickleford, Her
2 CONC tfordshire, on 5 August 1764. Barrett resigned the mastership a second
2 CONC  time, in 1773, when presented to the rectory of Hothfield, Kent. He w
2 CONC as a friend of Dr Johnson and Edward Cave, and a frequent contributor
2 CONC to the Gentleman's Magazine. He also published some verses, a Latin tr
2 CONC anslation of Pope's ‘Pastorals’, and Ovid's epistles translated into E
2 CONC nglish verse, with critical essays and notes; being part of a poetical
2 CONC  and oratorical lecture read to the grammar school of Ashford in the c
2 CONC ounty of Kent, and calculated to initiate youth in the first principle
2 CONC s of taste (1759). Tobias Smollett gave a withering review of Barrett'
2 CONC s works in the Critical Review: ‘though he might be an excellent schoo
2 CONC lmaster, he had, however, no pretensions to taste’ (Nichols, Lit. anec
2 CONC dotes, 3.346n).
2 CONT
2 CONT Barrett died at Church House, Northiam, Sussex, on 26 November 1801, a
2 CONC nd was buried at Hothfield on 3 December. He was survived by his daugh
2 CONC ter, who had married Edward Jeremiah Curteis, a barrister.
2 CONT -------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT In 2008 I found a history of the Ashford Grammar School at http://www.
2 CONC nks.kent.sch.uk/3-sirknatchbull.html, which gives a lot more informati
2 CONC on about Stephen Barrett. I quote the relevant bits:
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------
2 CONT 1749 saw the appointment of Stephen Barrett as Headmaster and this mar
2 CONC ked the start of a period in which Ashford Grammar School was to becom
2 CONC e highly successful academically and also very prosperous. This was re
2 CONC markable because many other English schools which had been endowed at
2 CONC about the same time as Ashford Grammar School declined badly during th
2 CONC is period. For example, Bristol Grammar School had 100 on the roll in
2 CONC 1764 but only a handful in 1800 and none at all by 1810! This was part
2 CONC ly because many of the Endowed Grammar Schools still refused to teach
2 CONC much other than the Classics whereas newly-formed schools were giving
2 CONC a broader education.
2 CONT  In Ashford there was at first little attempt to teach outside the Cla
2 CONC ssics but there was not much competition from other schools in the are
2 CONC a. The main reason why the School prospered, however, seems to have be
2 CONC en the personality of Barrett coupled with the social circles in which
2 CONC  he moved, his great reputation as a Latin scholar and, not least, his
2 CONC  business acumen. Stephen Barrett was born in Yorkshire in 1720. He wo
2 CONC n a Scholarship to Oxford where he obtained his M.A. and received Holy
2 CONC  Orders. He became a Curate in Hertfordshire but was by now well-known
2 CONC  as a Latin translator and a contributor to well-known periodicals of
2 CONC the time such as the 'Gentleman's Magazine'. Among his circle of frien
2 CONC ds was the famous Dr. Johnson and a story is told of how the Editor of
2 CONC  the 'Gentleman's Magazine' wanted a Latin poem translated. 'Give it t
2 CONC o Barrett' said Johnson. 'He will correct it for you in a minute'. Whe
2 CONC reupon Johnson and Barrett agreed to share the task which they complet
2 CONC ed in a very short time.
2 CONT ....
2 CONT In 1773 the Rev. Stephen Barrett finally left to become Rector of Hoth
2 CONC field. In 1801 he died at Church House, Northiam, the home of his son-
2 CONC in-law Edward Jeremiah Curteis. Curteis had been an Head Boy of the Sc
2 CONC hool which he attended from 1770 to 1775 and later became an M.P. for
2 CONC Sussex. Barrett was buried at Hothfield and left some £30,000—a consid
2 CONC erable fortune in those days.
2 CONT --------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT An extract from the 1886 book HISTORY OF STEETON by JOHN CLOUGH of The
2 CONC  Shroggs, Steeton. Text provided by Wendy Feather.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family of Barrett were located at the Bent for a long time, there
2 CONC was a William Barrett who lived there 1599. Peter Barrett, of the Bent
2 CONC  was buried at Kildwick 1653.
2 CONT On a stone inserted in the house wall at the Bent is : William and Ann
2 CONC  Barrett 1658 WAB :: The name of Barrett occures often from the beginn
2 CONC ing of the parish registers. The last of the Barretts of the Bent was
2 CONC the Rev. Stephen Barrett. From the 'Gentleman's Magazine' Dec 1801, is
2 CONC  extracted the following;
2 CONT " He was born at the Bent, in the parish of Kildwick, and received his
2 CONC  education at the Grammer School, Skipton, thence he went to Universit
2 CONC y College, Oxford, where he took the degree of MA and received Holy Or
2 CONC ders. In 1773 he was appointed rectory of Hothfield by the Earl of Tha
2 CONC net. He married AD 1749, Mary, the daughter of Edward Jacob Esq, of Ca
2 CONC nterbury, and had an only daughter and heiress, Mary who married April
2 CONC  14 1789, Edward Jeremiah Curteis, Esq, of Windmill Hill and the Knell
2 CONC s, Sussex, MP for the county of Sussex 1820 - 30. The grandmother of R
2 CONC ev. Stephen Barrett was the sister of Doctor Sharp, Archbishop of York
2 CONC , and the surname of his mother was Clough. . . . . . . . . .
2 CONT Bent, his family residence, and the land adjacent was the property of
2 CONC the Barretts for more than 400 years, before the general adoption of s
2 CONC urnames in England(In 1379, there were Johannes Baret, Johannes Baret,
2 CONC  junior, and Willelmus de Bent living at Sutton). . . . . . . . . . .
2 CONC .
2 CONT -------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Although the Gentleman’s Magazine obituary says that his grandmother w
2 CONC as the sister of Archbishop John Sharp, I am sceptical of this. The ar
2 CONC chbishop had a single sister (that we know of), Hannah Sharp, who marr
2 CONC ied John Richardson (according to the book “The Life and Times of John
2 CONC  Sharp, Archbishop of York”, by A. Tindal Hart). This doesn’t immediat
2 CONC ely square with the fact that his mother is claimed to be a Clough. No
2 CONC t impossible, because of possible remarriages, or additional children,
2 CONC  or maybe it wasn’t an actual sister but a cousin or something. Or may
2 CONC be this claim is just plain wrong. However, these is no easy resolutio
2 CONC n to this question.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The following book might throw some light on the Barretts of Bent:
2 CONT THE HISTORY AND ANTIQUITIES OF THE DEANERY OF CRAVEN,
2 CONT WHITAKER Rev. Thomas Dunham
