0 @I1809@ INDI
1 NAME Robert /McPherson/
2 DISPLAY Robert McPherson
2 GIVN Robert
2 SURN McPherson
1 SOURCES @S32@
1 POSITION -3850,-1580
2 GENOMAP Thomas Boileau's descendants and related
2 BOUNDARYRECT -3887,-1547,-3813,-1627
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ~1821
2 PLAC Ayrshire?
3 _XREF @place00961@
2 SOURCE @S32@
1 DEAT Y
2 DATE 1 OCT 1895
2 PLAC Manuka Creek
3 _XREF @place00962@
2 SOURCE @S32@
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE Probably born in Wallacetown, Ayrshire, a coal mining village. It’s no
2 CONC w part of the town of Ayr.
2 CONT
2 CONT Listed as a gardener, at Lawrence, on his death certificate. They had
2 CONC two sons and five daughters. I don’t know their names. He and Marion c
2 CONC ame to NZ in 1861  on the Robert Henderson from Clyde, and they lived
2 CONC at Manuka Creek, near Lawrence. He obviously headed out to Manuka Cree
2 CONC k, one of the well-known gold fields, to look for gold, but wasn’t ver
2 CONC y successful at it. Obviously not, or we’d all be rich now. Note that
2 CONC he was a miner back in Scotland, so gold mining may have seemed a natu
2 CONC ral thing for him to do.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gold was found at Gabriel’s Gully (close to Manuka Creek) in May 1861,
2 CONC  and this is usually counted the real start of the Otago gold rush. Th
2 CONC e Robert Henderson left Clyde on 11 July, 1861, and arrived in Port Ch
2 CONC almers on 5 October, 1861. So it’s not realisticallly possible that Ro
2 CONC bert McPherson came to Dunedin, and thence to Manuka Creek, specifical
2 CONC ly in response to the findings at Gabriel Gully. The timing just doesn
2 CONC ’t work out.
2 CONT
2 CONT We know he wasn’t successful, as he appears in two debtors lists, one
2 CONC in 1872, the other in 1869. In both lists he owed 67 pounds for his as
2 CONC sisted passage. Clearly he didn’t pay it off in a hurry. (Thanks to Bo
2 CONC b Matthews for showing me these lists.)
2 CONT
2 CONT The shipping list says there were 5 daughters. One of the children on
2 CONC the ship was called Marion, according to Bob Matthews.
2 CONT
2 CONT Buried in Lot 7, Block 17B in the Southern Cemetery, Dunedin. The ceme
2 CONC tery record says he died 1 Oct, 1895, aged 74, resident of Lawrence. B
2 CONC orn Scotland. Died of Pneumonia and Chronic Bronchitis. The gravestone
2 CONC , if there ever was one, has completely disappeared now.
2 CONT At time of death he had living two sons (45 and 24 years old) and 5 da
2 CONC ughters: 50, 43, 40, 29, 27 years old. Seems that the son Benjamin was
2 CONC  the 45 one. However, who was the 50-year-old daughter? This is very u
2 CONC nclear. The West Lothian census lists Benjamin as the oldest, then Mar
2 CONC garet, then Marion, then Janet. At Robert’s death they would have been
2 CONC  approximately 45, 43, 41 and 36. The first three ages match well with
2 CONC  the death certificate. But the claimed oldest daughter is a real puzz
2 CONC le. For example, if she was 50 years old in 1895, she would have been
2 CONC born in 1845, two years before Robert and Marion were married. I suspe
2 CONC ct clerical error.
2 CONT
2 CONT I also suspect that the last three children were not children of Mario
2 CONC n Waugh. If there were 29, 27 and 24 at the time of Robert’s death, th
2 CONC ey would have been born when Marion was 42, 44 and 47 respectively. I
2 CONC reckon this is way too old for a woman with her kind of life to have c
2 CONC hildren. Mind you, if the West Lothian census is correct, and Marion w
2 CONC as born in 1828, then she would have been 37, 39 and 42. Just possible
2 CONC , maybe.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Waugh (related to Marion?) came out on the Robert Henderson also
2 CONC  in 1861, as did John Waugh. Maybe a family group coming out together?
2 CONT
2 CONT There are no McPhersons on the Dunedin electoral roll of 1862. This su
2 CONC ggests they went straight to the Lawrence area, chasing gold. Two Robe
2 CONC rt McPhersons are given in the Dunedin Electoral Roll of 1865-66. One
2 CONC was at Blair Cottage and I know that isn’t our one. The Blair Cottage
2 CONC one is the subject of the book “McPherson family of Dunedin, New Zeala
2 CONC nd; 1865-1947” by Beverly Jeffery, which is available in the library o
2 CONC f the New Zealand Society of Genealogists. I’ve read this book, and it
2 CONC  deals with a different lot of McPhersons. An inferior lot I’m quite s
2 CONC ure. The other Robert McPherson could be our one, but this isn’t certa
2 CONC in.
2 CONT Possibly he had a brother, Binnie, who married Margaret in 1822 (he wa
2 CONC s 46 and she was 36), and had children Alexander Graham (2 April, 1825
2 CONC ), Robert (1827), Janet (1832).
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a detailed diary of a trip on the Robert Henderson (from Glas
2 CONC gow to Bluff) in 1862, very close to the time of our McPherson’s trip.
2 CONC  http://www.yesteryears.co.nz/shipping/diaries/thomasreid.html.
1 _UID A558002739C24E9C8CD6567B73891A7CCEB9
