1 NOTE This is the Eric who was so good to me as a boy. I visited him in Lond
2 CONC on at least twice and we exchanged some letters in French. I remember
2 CONC his club as making the most God-awful gin and tonic, with only a smell
2 CONC  of tonic, and the dinner was mediocre. However, he was as kind as cou
2 CONC ld be. He was the one who originally sent me a the copy of the Burke e
2 CONC ntry on the Grahams of Edmond Castle. I still have that copy.  He had
2 CONC a lot of family heirlooms; paintings, letters, a huge amethyst. He die
2 CONC d when we were in Ann Arbor, so around 1998 or so, leaving us, and eac
2 CONC h  of my siblings, a pile of cash.
2 CONT My sister Catherine is famous in family history because of Uncle Eric.
2 CONC  When we visited them en masse, in London when I was about 10 (1972 or
2 CONC  so), Catherine and the other two youngest children were given tea and
2 CONC  cakes at a separate table. Catherine farted loudly, all the children
2 CONC collapsed in giggles, and Mum and Dad were highly embarrassed. Farting
2 CONC  is something a young lady shouldn’t do, but I guess Cathy didn’t know
2 CONC  that. I’m quite sure she still doesn’t.
2 CONT Eric and Sylvie had no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT I found his middle name on the conveyancing deeds for the sale of Edmo
2 CONC nd Castle.
