1 NOTE Called Esq. A writer from Stirling. In this context a writer is an old
2 CONC  Scottish word for a lawyer.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 2009 I got a lot of Peddie information from a researcher called Mar
2 CONC y (I don’t know her last name), who responded to a request I made to t
2 CONC he Central Scotland Family History Society (or some such similar name)
2 CONC . If you ever read this, Mary, many thanks!
2 CONT
2 CONT All 6 children  were alive in 1841 census living  in Broad Street, Sti
2 CONC rling.  In 1851 his wife is presumably dead as are Catherine and Jane.
2 CONC  James, George and Janet were at home but by 1861 they are not listed
2 CONC so presumably they have emigrated.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1854 James remarried to a farmer’s daughter called Catherine Graham
2 CONC  who was aged 50. On the 1861 census there is just James and his secon
2 CONC d wife along with Cecilia. This remarriage may have prompted the emigr
2 CONC ation of the older three children.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Peddie lived in Broad Street in Stirling and probably carried on
2 CONC  his business from the house. In 1861 he was employing an apprentice.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the valuation roll  James Peddie is  the Trustee for property which
2 CONC  he also lived in. Interestingly the owner of this property was James
2 CONC Taylor, merchant in Crieff. Taylor was his wife’s maiden name.
2 CONT His brother William is living in property owned by James Christie, a f
2 CONC armer. Christie is the maiden name of his mother.
