1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 10 JUN 1800
2 PLAC Klein, Pravtshagen, Germany
3 _XREF @place00967@
2 SOURCE @S36@
1 DEAT Y
2 DATE 20 JAN 1873
2 PLAC Aniseed Valley, Nelson
3 _XREF @place00968@
2 SOURCE @S35@
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE There is a detailed history of Hans Busch in The Busch Line: from serf
2 CONC dom to freedom, the source I got most of my Busch information. It cont
2 CONC ains photographs of him and Dorothea, their wills, children, etc. Far
2 CONC too much information to reproduce here. He began life as a mason and b
2 CONC ricklayer, and after his marriage lived for about three years at Arpsh
2 CONC agen, a village a few kilometres east of Klutz. His first two children
2 CONC  were born here. He then moved to Reppenhagen, where he worked as a br
2 CONC icklayer and labourer. Five more children were born here, and it was h
2 CONC ere that their eldest son and second daughter died of tuberculosis, pr
2 CONC obably as a result of poor living conditions. An entry in the church b
2 CONC ooks at Damshages states: “Hans Busch never attends Sunday mass and al
2 CONC though he works very long hours seven days a week, he gets poorer all
2 CONC the time”. No wonder he emigrated to NZ. The only photos I have of Han
2 CONC s and Dorothea show him to be looking quite prosperous (obviously take
2 CONC n in Nelson in his better days), and her looking like a right old batt
2 CONC leaxe, mouth drawn down in a grim line. Ouch. I share genetic material
2 CONC  with her, a frightening thought.
2 CONT
2 CONT They took off in 1841 or so for a better life in NZ. I imagine they th
2 CONC ought it could hardly get worse, and they were probably right at that.
2 CONC  Anyway, their life in NZ is exhaustively described in .
2 CONT
2 CONT From the County of Reppenhagen; occupation: Mason. He and Dorothy and
2 CONC their five eldest children came to Nelson in the second German ship, t
2 CONC he Skiold, which arrived on 1 Sept., 1844.
2 CONT
2 CONT The book by Ruth Allan gives a detailed history of the two German immi
2 CONC grant ships to Nelson. She says that Busch was one of only two steerag
2 CONC e passengers on the Skiold to have money of their own. On page 340 we
2 CONC read: “Another Skiold establishment was that of H.H. Busch. Not being
2 CONC able to rent or buy land to his liking, he squatted on several unused
2 CONC sections close to the hills, near Ranzau [Waimea East]. He called his
2 CONC place Schönhof, ‘beautiful farm’. He arrived with £50 sterling, and by
2 CONC  1849 had farmed to such good purpose that he was worth £300. He then
2 CONC had 40 acres under cultivation, 23 cows, four sheep, 183 goats, and po
2 CONC ultry. After the 1849 reselection, he bought part of 60 Waimea East fr
2 CONC om the Kellings, and part of Rural XIII in that district from Hodgson,
2 CONC  together with two lots of land in the Aniseed Valley, 53 acres from t
2 CONC he German mission, and 24 acres from the Kellings.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans Henry Busch is listed in the Waimea electoral roll (living in Wai
2 CONC mea East) in 1870.
2 CONT
2 CONT In a book of Nelson inscriptions in the NZGS library I found the follo
2 CONC wing:
2 CONT “ Isolated tombstone inscription, Aniseed Valley, Nelson.
2 CONT Near right bank of Roding River, Waimea County, 15 miles southeast fro
2 CONC m Nelson and 3 miles east from the Nelson/West Coast Road, turning off
2 CONC  at Hope.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans Heinrich Busch born Klen, Prarshagen, Germany, died 20th Jan. 187
2 CONC 3, aged 73 years; & his wife Dorothea Busch nee Griben, born Klutz, Ge
2 CONC rmany, died 4th Feb. 1880 aged 74 years. Arrived in Nelson “Skiold” ex
2 CONC pedition 1844; settled Aniseed Valley 1857.”
