1 NOTE A great deal of my information on the Norgroves has come from Joan McN
2 CONC aught (and her daughter Helen, I believe). They have provided me with
2 CONC copies of the death notices, as well as writeups they have done on the
2 CONC  Norgrove history in Blenheim. Many thanks to them. Ian Melville, anot
2 CONC her Norgrove descendant, has also been very helpful.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT William Norgrove was born in Ardleigh, Essex, on the 22nd of September
2 CONC , 1813. His parents were called William Norgrove and Hannah Barker, an
2 CONC d that is the sum total of everything we know about the England part o
2 CONC f William Norgrove's life. Well, that's not entirely true. We know he
2 CONC was educated at the Foundation School in Colchester, where he subseque
2 CONC ntly worked as a plumber and a painter, and that he married Sarah King
2 CONC  Hall (or King-Hall) in Bow Church, Middlesex, on the 22nd of Septembe
2 CONC r, 1839; William was 26 (they married on his birthday, as you can see)
2 CONC , and Sarah was 21. They met, we are told by their granddaughter, Kate
2 CONC  Norgrove, while Sarah was tying up a pig.
2 CONT
2 CONT We know that his family was very poor. His mother, Hannah Norgrove, wa
2 CONC s on the official list of parish poor in Ardleigh, in 1825. This was w
2 CONC hen William was only 12, and it may well have been that his father had
2 CONC  died by then. Certainly, to have his mother's name appear but not his
2 CONC  father's would imply this. Funnily enough, William's parent were marr
2 CONC ied in Colchester (on the 17th of October, 1783 according to the paris
2 CONC h records) but William himself was born and baptised in Ardleigh. Poss
2 CONC ibly William and Hannah Barker were from Colchester originally and mov
2 CONC ed later to Ardleigh, where poor old Hannah ended up on the poor regis
2 CONC ter. Possible, but by no means certain. Kate Norgrove claimed that Wil
2 CONC liam Norgrove's father had died after being bitten by a rabid dog. She
2 CONC  also said that William broke his leg while saving a child from a runa
2 CONC way horse and carriage. He was taken to an inn and whilst recovering,
2 CONC talk came around to ghosts. That night `ghost' came visiting, and Will
2 CONC iam nearly killed him with his walking stick! It's not clear how old W
2 CONC illiam was when these things are said to have happened, as Kate never
2 CONC said, but I'm guessing it was before he left England. I could be wrong
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Since he was sent to the Colchester Foundation School we can infer tha
2 CONC t William was bright enough to be noticed, and thought to be worth edu
2 CONC cating. Later in life William was clearly a well educated and intellig
2 CONC ent man, so the Foundation School must have been effective.
2 CONT
2 CONT And that really is everything we know about William Norgrove in Englan
2 CONC d. Rather unimpressive, if you think about it.
2 CONT
2 CONT We are almost as ignorant about his wife, Sarah Hall. Her parents were
2 CONC  William King Hall and Anne Pickett, and she had two brothers and five
2 CONC  sisters. One of her sisters married a Richard Fowler in New Zealand,
2 CONC and so must have emigrated also. But I say “almost", for a reason, as
2 CONC we are in the fortunate position of having something that she wrote ab
2 CONC out herself. From this we learn that she was married in a silk gown no
2 CONC t a bridal dress, at 10 o'clock in the morning, and that her blind lit
2 CONC tle brother Harry was upset that she was marrying, as he wanted to kee
2 CONC p her all to herself. We learn that her Granny was a little out of it
2 CONC and didn't quite understand what was going on, and that it rained just
2 CONC  a little bit as they were leaving the church after the ceremony. We l
2 CONC earn that she cried when she was married, feeling lonely, but that she
2 CONC  hadn't really wanted a big fuss so she had gone to the church in the
2 CONC coach alone. These details are priceless. My sister Mary will no doubt
2 CONC  call them “borese", but she is wrong; they are priceless.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anyway, I shouldn't tell you what's in the poem, I should just give yo
2 CONC u the poem. So here it is.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------
2 CONT This verse written by Sarah Norgrove (King-Hall) in 1889 to her Family
2 CONC . Blenheim N.Z.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fifty years ago my children, fifty years ago
2 CONT Since your Father and I were married in that quaint old Church at Bow\
2 CONC footnote{Yes, I know the poetry is absolutely dreadful. I don't care.
2 CONC Anyway, I can hardly complain. The only `poetry' I've ever written is
2 CONC an obscene limerick.}
2 CONT It was on a Sunday morning ten o'clock the time
2 CONT September the twenty-second eighteen hundred and thirty nine.
2 CONT I had no bridal dress I wore my best silk gown.
2 CONT We wished to avoid all fuss I sent to the Church in the coach alone.
2 CONT There was no one in the Church only your Father my Brother and I
2 CONT The Clerk had gone for the Parson he was somewhere close by.
2 CONT I felt so lonely, when teh service was over I began to cry.
2 CONT As we were leaving the Church the minister kindly said goodbye.
2 CONT When we got out in the road there was a slight sprinkle of rain.
2 CONT Your Father put me into a coach I was drove to Ilford again
2 CONT It is five miles from Ilford to Bow there was only a little shower
2 CONT Your Father and Brother walked home in little more than an hour.
2 CONT My Grandmother came from Church, my Father and others to Chapel had be
2 CONC en
2 CONT I gave her a kiss and said Grandmother look at my ring.
2 CONT Granny did not know we were married she was eighty old that day
2 CONT She had walked to Church and back the Church was a mile away
2 CONT We had a nice large cake made by a cousing of mine
2 CONT She sent us some apples and grapes and a bottle of home made wine
2 CONT There was my Father Mother and Granny Cousin Robert your Father and me
2 CONT All my Sisters and Brothers how very happy were we
2 CONT My Dear little blind Brother Harry would not shin from my side
2 CONT He said Sarah, why did you marry? I want you myself and he cried
2 CONT In the evening by the bright moonlight, we left for our snug little ho
2 CONC me
2 CONT With kisses from all and good night.
2 CONT
2 CONT Our Lord has taken your Father, he has only gone before
2 CONT I often fancy I see him I hear his steps at the door
2 CONT He said he was tired and weary so feeble he wanted to go
2 CONT I remember him young and cheery fifty years ago
2 CONT When he and I were married in that quaint old Church at Bow.
2 CONT -----------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT My G-grandmother, Kate Norgrove, said that her grandmother, Sarah King
2 CONC -Hall, was part of the famous King-Hall family that contributed a line
2 CONC  of Admirals to the British Navy. The story was plausible initially, a
2 CONC s Sarah was born at the right time, in the right place, and all those
2 CONC things. However, athough I looked and I looked, I couldn't find any re
2 CONC cord that she was actually born into that family. Indeed, all the chil
2 CONC dren of that family are fairly well known, and she wasn't in it. I was
2 CONC  puzzled for a long time by this. Finally, Joan McNaught showed me tha
2 CONC t Sarah King-Hall was (fairly definitely) born into quite a different
2 CONC family. It's an interesting thing, though. Did Kate believe this story
2 CONC  about being connected to the famous King-Halls? Did she make it up he
2 CONC rself, to create some grand rellies for herself? Did her grandmother m
2 CONC ake it up and tell everybody? I wonder. But I guess I'll never know.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 19th of June, 1841, less than two years after they were married
2 CONC , William and Sarah sailed from Gravesend on the Gertrude, to arrive i
2 CONC n Port Nicholson on the 30th October, about a year after the first New
2 CONC  Zealand Company ship into Wellington. Once again we are fortunate to
2 CONC have a first-hand account of the landing, written in Sarah's distincti
2 CONC ve poetic style.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------
2 CONT This verse written by Sarah Norgrove to her family about her arrival i
2 CONC n New Zealand. Ovid the first son died at the age of 18.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the twentieth day of June, at two o'clock in the morn
2 CONT Eighteen hundred and forty our first little son was born.
2 CONT On the twentieth day of June eighteen hundred and forty one
2 CONT We were in the ship Gertrude, bound for our New Zealand Home.
2 CONT The voyage was weary and long, we were twenty weeks in the ship
2 CONT We all landed safe and strong, on the beach at Kaiwawas slip
2 CONT the second day of November eighteen forty one
2 CONT we came on shore and commenced to make our new home.
2 CONT Your Father lighted a fire and boiled fresh water for tea
2 CONT Our shipmates said what a treat, will you give some to we.
2 CONT He said you are welcome my friends, mine is a large kettle you see.
2 CONT The clearest of water close by enough for you and me.
2 CONT When your Father began to unpack saw, hammer and nails
2 CONT There were some who stood by him and said we ought to have brought the
2 CONC y ourselves.
2 CONT He said mates we must all set to work for our dear little children and
2 CONC  wives
2 CONT You know they must all have food, I feel sure you have brought knives.
2 CONT They said how funny you be, you have cheered us a bit today
2 CONT And we will let you see us try to so as you say.
2 CONT When your Father a table had made, a clean cloth on it I spread.
2 CONT We thankfully sat down to tea in that old Kiwarawara shed.
2 CONT Our darling had fresh new milk, on the voyage he had feed from my brea
2 CONC st
2 CONT Father said Ovid must learn to eat, Mother must have a rest.
2 CONT Your Father walked to the town early the very next day
2 CONT He hired an old mud wara the floor was only clay,
2 CONT Three rooms -- a toitoi thatched roof and rent sixteen shilling per we
2 CONC ek
2 CONT A shipmate shared it with us, when it rained how the roof did leak.
2 CONT No glass where the window should be some calico nailed up tight
2 CONT Through the crevices came the wind, and some of the bright sunlight.
2 CONT The Wandi was on Thorndon flat before the winter had gone
2 CONT We were living on Lambton Quay, where our dear little Oscar was born
2 CONT He was only nine days old, when a fire broke out on the beach
2 CONT Father took us onto the Terrace and placed us out of it's reach.
2 CONT The fire commenced at midnight, at Loyds the Bakers store
2 CONT All did their best to put it out, the sea was close to our door
2 CONT Many houses were burned and shops that were nearly new
2 CONT It was November the ninth, eighteen hundred and forty two.
2 CONT -----------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT So William and Sarah's first home in New Zealand was a three room mud
2 CONC wara hut, which cost them 16 shillings a week. Doesn't sound too comfo
2 CONC rtable to be honest. However, it's clear that William and Sarah were e
2 CONC nergetic and intelligent, and took a leading role among their peers. I
2 CONC ndeed, William must have had a keen interest in many things, as in 184
2 CONC 9 we find him on the board of the Wellington Mechanics' Institute. Thi
2 CONC s Institute, or its predecessor at least, was born before the first Ne
2 CONC w Zealand Company ship even sailed to Wellington, when a committee was
2 CONC  charged with the duty of making some provision for the “literary, sci
2 CONC entific and philanthropic institutions" of the settlers. The settlers
2 CONC put together a rudimentary library and some bits of scientific equipme
2 CONC nt, and the first meeting in their new home occurred on the 1st of Dec
2 CONC ember, 1840, very soon after their arrival. It occurred, appropriately
2 CONC  enough, at Barrett's Hotel, i.e., in a pub. I'm not surprised. Anyway
2 CONC , the initial Port Nicholson Mechanics' Institute Public School Librar
2 CONC y went defunct as people didn't pay their subscriptions, so another at
2 CONC tempt was made in 1848 to revive it, under the new name of the Welling
2 CONC ton Mechanics' Institute.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Norgrove was on the board of this new Institute in 1849, and a
2 CONC t a meeting on the 6th of November it was noted that “A drawing class
2 CONC has been ably and usefully conducted by Mr. Norgrove, who has cheerful
2 CONC ly devoted a large amount of time and attention to his pupils, of whom
2 CONC  there were twenty. The progress made has been very satisfactory, and
2 CONC already affords very pleasing indications of future utility. Want of s
2 CONC uitable accommodation has prevented the formation of other projected c
2 CONC lasses, but these will no doubt follow the opening of the Hall." Indee
2 CONC d, I have to admit that I was both surprised and delighted to discover
2 CONC  that William's drawing class is considered to be one of the few succe
2 CONC ssful examples of early adult education in New Zealand.  William has e
2 CONC ven been mentioned in scholarly articles (to wit, James C. Dakin (1991
2 CONC ), The prevalence of mutual improvement in adult education in New Zeal
2 CONC and 1870-1915, International Journal of Lifelong Education, 10:3, 243-
2 CONC -254, and Dakin, J.C. (1978). The origins and beginnings of continuing
2 CONC  education in Wellington, Continuing Education in New Zealand, 10, 1,
2 CONC pp. 82, 85, 91. I have to wonder what kind of drawing he taught. `Line
2 CONC ar drawing' according to the local newspapers which doesn't sound like
2 CONC  it was of the young lady variety, but was it just technical drawing?
2 CONC Certainly, his later career shows that he was a talented painter of al
2 CONC l kinds of things, from flags, to coats of arms, to signs. So, as I sa
2 CONC id, I wonder.
2 CONT
2 CONT Given his later actions in Nelson and Blenheim, it's likely that Willi
2 CONC am took an active interest in the affairs of Wellington. We know he wa
2 CONC s on the electoral roll in the 1840's, at which time they were living
2 CONC on Thorndon Quay, although that by itself tells us little. I suspect t
2 CONC hat he cannot have been very successful, as by the early 1850's Willia
2 CONC m was in the goldfields of Victoria; I know nothing about his time the
2 CONC re but it is clear that any success he had there was also marginal at
2 CONC best. I'm not surprised.  By January of 1855 he was back in Wellington
2 CONC , just in time for the famous earthquake, the largest ever recorded in
2 CONC  New Zealand. My G-grandmother Kate would tell of how the great earthq
2 CONC uake broke all the china, and the family had to travel up the hill to
2 CONC get water until her grandfather (i.e., William) made a pump.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was maybe this earthquake (not to mention the earlier ones of 1848
2 CONC which wouldn't have helped) that motivated William and Sarah to leave
2 CONC Wellington. Whatever the reason, by October 1855 the family had moved
2 CONC to Nelson, where William set up a new business in Bridge St., opposite
2 CONC  the Wakatu Hotel, as a “Plumber, Glazier, House and Sign Painter", wi
2 CONC th the added attractions of  “Baths of every description, Pumps, Beer-
2 CONC engines, Water-closets, &c, fixed and repaired. White and Sheet Lead,
2 CONC Zinc, Window Glass (all sizes), Oils and Colours of every description.
2 CONC "
2 CONT
2 CONT We can trace a lot of his life in the pages of the Nelson and Blenheim
2 CONC  newspapers, and it is clear that he was, well, an interesting man one
2 CONC  would have to say. Not a reliable one, oh no, but certainly an intere
2 CONC sting one; educated, widely read, charismatic, tremendously talented a
2 CONC nd intelligent, an excellent public speaker and organiser, a born perf
2 CONC ormer,  very good at building and making things, but, even with all th
2 CONC at, unsuccessful. One has to wonder why. By far the most likely explan
2 CONC ation is mental disease. Given that William's son Oscar was committed
2 CONC to a lunatic asylum, and that one of Oscar's children, Bertha, committ
2 CONC ed suicide, it's a good bet that William had mental troubles of his ow
2 CONC n. I have no idea what they were, exactly, although one can speculate.
2 CONC  Oscar would clearly get manic; it was during one of those bouts that
2 CONC he was committed. Bertha, on the other hand, committed suicide in a fi
2 CONC t of depression. Putting this all together, one is tempted to conclude
2 CONC  that manic-depression ran in the family, and that William also suffer
2 CONC ed from it, at least to a certain extent. I speculate, but not without
2 CONC  foundation.
2 CONT
2 CONT His time in Nelson, which lasted from 1855 to 1861, is really his life
2 CONC  in miniature, or so it seems to me. Great initial success, elected to
2 CONC  the Town Board, public performances and meetings, all followed by dec
2 CONC line, bankruptcy and flight to Blenheim. Indeed, we see the same kinds
2 CONC  of things appearing in the life of his sons as well; inventors, busin
2 CONC essmen, entrepreneurs, builders, sailors...and bankrupts.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was elected to the Board of Town Improvement, he was instrumen
2 CONC tal in organising the Fire Brigade, he served on the Board of the Nels
2 CONC on Literary and Scientific Institution, he was active in the Agricultu
2 CONC ral Society (various Miss Norgroves regularly won prizes for their flo
2 CONC wers) and he served on the Nelson Local Committee. In particular, he w
2 CONC as full of ideas for how to improve Nelson's water supply. Some of the
2 CONC se ideas were even taken up; years later, in 1868, a decade later, whe
2 CONC n the Nelson Water Works were officially opened, the official speaker
2 CONC said that it was only right that Mr. Norgrove be properly acknowledged
2 CONC  as it was all originally his idea. This was a common theme in much of
2 CONC  William's life. He was clearly interested in water, how it flows, how
2 CONC  it can be controlled and used. He was an engineer through and through
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Another of his projects, again connected with water, was the Public Ba
2 CONC ths. In 1858 William and a couple of business partners ``proposed to e
2 CONC rect a BATHING ESTABLISHMENT in Nelson, combining Hot, Cold, Vapour, a
2 CONC nd Swimming Baths, with all necessary conveniences, both for ladies an
2 CONC d gentlemen." A family ticket was going to cost $\pounds$2 per annum a
2 CONC nd a warm bath was 2s.\ 6d.\ extra. I'm guessing the project wasn't a
2 CONC great success, as no more is heard about it. It was built, I believe,
2 CONC as tickets were being sold at one stage, but it disappears without any
2 CONC  further traces.
2 CONT
2 CONT Amateur theatricals was yet another of William's interests. In 1857 we
2 CONC  find that he performed the part of Colonel Damas in the “Lady of Lyon
2 CONC s” (an 1838 romantic drama by Edward Bulwer-Lytton, one of the most po
2 CONC pular plays of its time). The theatre critic rather liked William's pe
2 CONC rformance: ``Colonel Damas was randered by Mr. Norgrove with all that
2 CONC bluff bonhomie pertaining to the old soldier, and still with the tact
2 CONC of an actor, that pleased us much." The play, apparently, was much imp
2 CONC roved from its first performance, as the actors knew their parts this
2 CONC time, and acted well. As opposed to the previous performance one imagi
2 CONC nes. A year later William was calling a public meeting to try and get
2 CONC a proper theatre built in Nelson, “expressly suitable for theatrical p
2 CONC erformances, concerts, balls, &c." I don't think that anything came of
2 CONC  this theatre proposal either.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Every so often, when searching through old newspapers, the voice of Wi
2 CONC lliam can be heard quite clearly (sometimes pleasant, sometimes not),
2 CONC and it is these extracts that tell us, most accurately, the kind of ma
2 CONC n he was. These, to me, are the real treasures. (Apart, of course, fro
2 CONC m the announcement that “Beda, the one-legged gymnast is in Blenheim a
2 CONC nd purposes giving performances at Ewart's Hall." You couldn't make th
2 CONC at stuff up.) In a meeting held in 1856 to discuss the new Education A
2 CONC ct (Nelson Examiner and New Zealand Chronicle, 21 June, 1856):
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT “Mr. Norgrove said that, like Marmaduke Magog, it was not often that h
2 CONC e spoke in public, but he must beg permission to say a few words on th
2 CONC e subject of education. He remembered attending a Chartist meeting abo
2 CONC ut 25 years ago, at which one of the great reasons urged for the passi
2 CONC ng of the people's charter was that the Government did not make proper
2 CONC  provision for the education of the people [hear, hear]. He remembered
2 CONC  that one of the speakers on that occasion had alluded to the mill gir
2 CONC ls of Manchester, who toiled from morning till night at the mills inst
2 CONC ead of going to school, and had remarked that the wonder was not that
2 CONC they were bad, but that they were so good [hear]. The same speaker wen
2 CONC t on to show that without education a people could become neither wise
2 CONC  nor good, and that it was the duty of the state to care for the educa
2 CONC tion of the people [hear, hear]. He (Mr. Norgrove) was sorry to find t
2 CONC hat the question was so mistaken here, and that people forgot that in
2 CONC paying this tax for the support of a scheme of education, they were in
2 CONC vesting for posterity [hear, hear]. He had seven children, and he shou
2 CONC ld some day be gathered to his fathers and leave a name behind him --
2 CONC it might be an indifferent one, but at all events it would be a name -
2 CONC - and it was his earnest desire to see his boys receive a better educa
2 CONC tion, and earn a better name than himself [cheers]. Should parents toi
2 CONC l on day after day and leave their children what they considered a com
2 CONC petence, without giving them some education to take care of that which
2 CONC , if they were ignorant and uneducated, some plausible scoundrel might
2 CONC  come and chouse them out of [hear, hear]? He was sorry to hear no arg
2 CONC ument on the other side; he wised to see the measure fairly tried, and
2 CONC  he had no doubt that some day or other they would all be the better f
2 CONC or it [vehement cheering].”
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT You can hear that William cared, really cared, about education. He was
2 CONC  less fond of religious bigotry, and the Catholic Church in particular
2 CONC  (Marlborough Express, 23 October, 1875):
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT Mr.\ Editor, -- A letter appeared in Thursday's {\it Times} signed ``C
2 CONC atholicus", one paragraph of which applied to myself as a private indi
2 CONC vidual. I have therefore to ask the favor of space for a few lines by
2 CONC way of reply.
2 CONT
2 CONT ``Catholicus" says my children were educated at the Catholic schools,
2 CONC and asks when did I change. He might have gone further and said I subs
2 CONC cribed to building the Church. My answer is that was my private affair
2 CONC . The funds of the Borough Schools are public property, and for the pr
2 CONC oper expenditure of which to the public alone am I responsible. As to
2 CONC why I sent my children to the Catholic schools, -- I have read that if
2 CONC  you go to supper with the Devil, take a long spoon; or, of two evils,
2 CONC  choose the least. The Catholic schools at that time were the best in
2 CONC the town; that was my reason. As to when I changed, -- I answer that I
2 CONC  have not changed; that ever since the so-called \OE cumenical Council
2 CONC , and the declaration of the Infallibility Dogma, I find that the Cath
2 CONC olic Clergy have been making all over the world strenuous exertions to
2 CONC  have a finger in the political pie, to prop a falling Church; hence t
2 CONC he troubles in Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT I, for one, wish to see all sects and creeds at liberty to follow thei
2 CONC r own particular views; -- favor to none. This it seems does not suit
2 CONC with the dogma which demands a blind unreasoning credulity. Those have
2 CONC  ever been my views; I demand the right to think freely, and speak fea
2 CONC rlessly, so I say ``Watch." -- I am, \&c.,
2 CONT
2 CONT W. Norgrove.
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT
2 CONT And he really didn't like the Chinese: (Nelson Examiner and New Zealan
2 CONC d Chronicle, 15 August, 1857)
2 CONT
2 CONT CHINESE IMMIGRATION
2 CONT
2 CONT A public meeting was held at the Courthouse, Nelson, on Thursday last,
2 CONC  for the purpose of considering ``what steps should be taken to preven
2 CONC t any Chinese immigration into the province;" W.L. Wrey, Esq., in the
2 CONC chair.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chairman briefly stated the objects of the meeting, and said that
2 CONC it was his impression, that of all the debased people on the face of t
2 CONC he earth, none equalled the Chinese in their immoral, wretched, and he
2 CONC  might say diabolical habits [hear, hear]; and he thought the people o
2 CONC f Nelson were called upon to endeavour to prevent such a race of being
2 CONC s from landing on these shores. He was glad to find by the large atten
2 CONC dance that it was a matter in which the inhabitants interested themsel
2 CONC ves, and he should like to hear the opinion of any one present upon th
2 CONC e subject.
2 CONT
2 CONT [There follows a series of statements from attendees, all vilifying th
2 CONC e Chinese in no uncertain terms, and volunteering to be the first on t
2 CONC he beaches to take arms against the foul invaders should that prove to
2 CONC  be necessary. Then our William had his say.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Norgrove, from some experience in Australia, could testify to the
2 CONC filthy and degrading habits of the Chinese in that country. The people
2 CONC  in Australia were now very anxious to get rid of these people; and, a
2 CONC s prevention was better than cure, he thought that the people of Nelso
2 CONC n would be justified in doing all in their power to prevent the contam
2 CONC ination to which an influx of the Chinese would expose them [cheers].
2 CONT
2 CONT [The meeting continued in a similar vein.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Clearly, William's rational liberality extended only to Europeans. Not
2 CONC  surprising I suppose; in that he was a man of his times. Still, unatt
2 CONC ractive.
2 CONT
2 CONT He suffered from rheumatism: (Marlborough Express, 1 July, 1876)
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------
2 CONT Sir, -- Permit me to thank you for inserting in your journal the infor
2 CONC mation that a cure for rheumatism had been discovered in the shape of
2 CONC caustic ammonia. Having suffered for a long time, and tried nearly eve
2 CONC ry remedy to little purpose, I procured from Dr. Cleghorn's shop, a sm
2 CONC all phial of the solution; two drops of which, applied with the point
2 CONC of a camel hair pencil, almost instantly removed the pain. Trusting th
2 CONC at all my fellow sufferers will find it equally efficacious. -- I am,
2 CONC &c.,
2 CONT
2 CONT William Norgrove.
2 CONT Blenheim, June 30th.
2 CONT
2 CONT [We may add to the above letter, that we had ocular demonstration of t
2 CONC he value of the remedy in Mr.\ Norgrove's case, who exhibited the wond
2 CONC erful ease with which he could swing his arm about, that a few days ag
2 CONC o was full of pain, and which he could not lift to his head without us
2 CONC ing the other for the purpose. The remedy in question is very simple,
2 CONC painless, and inexpensive, -- Ed. {\it M.E.}]
2 CONT \end{quote}
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT
2 CONT And for cricket fans, it appears that our William Norgrove was a parti
2 CONC cipant in that noble sport. In fact, he even played against the Neals
2 CONC once! And won! Hallelujah! It was on the 26th of April, 1873 that the
2 CONC Spring Creek cricket team challenged the Blenheim team to a match. Not
2 CONC  a wise move. A certain Mr. Wix on the Blenheim team was a mean bodyli
2 CONC ne bowler: “Of the bowling of Mr. Wix we can say but little; we would
2 CONC recommend that gentleman to practice straight bowling, as it was very
2 CONC evident in many cases he mistook the batsmen for the wickets." Wix too
2 CONC k 11 wickets in the match, including the scalps of T. Neal (for 3) and
2 CONC  R. Neal (for 0), our old friends Thomas Nelson, and, presumably, his
2 CONC brother Robert. Another Neal brother, Francis, was run out for 1. It c
2 CONC annot be claimed that the Neal brothers distinguished themselves in th
2 CONC is particular match. Still, the Spring Creek total was only 38, of whi
2 CONC ch 22 were byes, so the Neal brothers, with 4 runs between them, score
2 CONC d 25% of the runs that came from the bat.
2 CONT
2 CONT In return, the Blenheim team creamed the Spring Creek bowling. Nasty M
2 CONC r. Wix made 19, while the inestimable A. Budge made a phenomenal 23, f
2 CONC or a total of 103. Our William made 2, not out.
2 CONT
2 CONT I am sad to relate that Thomas Nelson Neal did even worse in the secon
2 CONC d innings, with only a single run to his credit, although brothers Rob
2 CONC ert and Francis did a little better, with 14 and 7 runs respectively.
2 CONC Curiously, a certain W. Barker was stumped and bowled by the nasty Mr.
2 CONC  Wix. Is this even possible? I wouldn't have thought so. One can't hel
2 CONC p wondering. Anyway, our William made 3 (not out) in the second inning
2 CONC s, and the Blenheim team cruised to a comfortable victory.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT But together with the fine speeches, the public meetings, and the gran
2 CONC d ideas, were debts. Lots of them, it seems. Already by 1857 William w
2 CONC as in court, being required to pay £58.6.10 to a certain Barrett. The
2 CONC judge made a telling remark, that the judgement was for the plaintiff
2 CONC  “as in former cases". Clearly this was not the first time William had
2 CONC  been in court for not paying up.  Other things went sour as well. Wil
2 CONC liam had been elected in 1858 to the Board of the Nelson Literary and
2 CONC Scientific Committee, but in the 1859 election he came in last, with o
2 CONC nly four votes. Was this an indication that people were getting sick o
2 CONC f him? Possibly. It's hard to be sure. Certainly, at the beginning of
2 CONC 1859 he gave up the struggle in Nelson, and sold up everything to pay
2 CONC his creditors, including the Baths which he had built. It's not clear
2 CONC what he did then, as it wasn't for two more years that his family left
2 CONC  Nelson. However, on the 15th of May, 1861, he put his wife, seven you
2 CONC ngest children, 26 boxes and 14 packages of effects on the schooner “C
2 CONC ity of Nelson”, headed to the Wairau, while he and his two eldest boys
2 CONC  (Horace and my GG-grandfather, Oscar) walked across the hills. Becaus
2 CONC e of the weather the “City of Nelson” couldn't cross the Wairau bar, s
2 CONC o they all had to be taken to shore in a whaling boat. That would have
2 CONC  been a nasty trip. Crossing the Wairau bar in bad weather, in an open
2 CONC  boat with seven young children. Not for the faint-hearted, that's for
2 CONC  sure.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Their first house in Blenheim was on Bradford Quay, where the fire sta
2 CONC tion is now; Blenheim was still very small, with only about 52 houses.
2 CONC  Later, in 1864, William and Sarah built a house in Dillon St. Accordi
2 CONC ng to Joan McNaught the timber for the house was white pine from the B
2 CONC ig Bush at Grovetown, and rimu from Dalton's Mill on the Picton Road.
2 CONC The roof was originally covered with shingles, but these were later re
2 CONC placed with iron. Unsurprisingly, giving William's interest in horticu
2 CONC lture, it had a beautiful garden; even before the house was built Will
2 CONC liam put up a glass house to shelter some cherished grape vines. Seeds
2 CONC  were sent from family in England and also grown in the glass house. A
2 CONC fter both William and Sarah were dead, this garden was looked after by
2 CONC  two of their daughters, Kate (not my G-grandmother, but her aunt) and
2 CONC  Emma. Aunt Emma, as my mother always called her, was blind in her lat
2 CONC er years, and would navigate around this garden by hooking her walking
2 CONC  stick over the clothes line. She and her sister Kate operated the Max
2 CONC well Road general store, which had been built by their parents around
2 CONC 1884. The store sold groceries and sweets, and its proprietors were kn
2 CONC own to all as ``the fat and the thin Misses Norgrove". I'm not sure wh
2 CONC ich was which. When her sister Kate died, Emma struggled on in the hou
2 CONC se until, after living there for over 76 years, she had to move out an
2 CONC d live with her nephew Ted Norgrove, in Redwood St. The house was pull
2 CONC ed down soon after, in 1941. Aunt Emma lived to the ripe old age of 97
2 CONC , the last 17 of which she was blind. Norgroves lived on the Dillon St
2 CONC . property for over 10 years, until the last of them, William and Sara
2 CONC h's G-grandson Alf Norgrove, left Blenheim in 1966.
2 CONT
2 CONT William's public life in Blenheim followed a similar pattern as in Nel
2 CONC son. He was elected to the local Council (in 1873) where he made many
2 CONC a speech about how best to improve Blenheim's water supply. His ideas
2 CONC were ignored for years; it wasn't until 1885 that anything was done ab
2 CONC out them, by which time William was too old and infirm to attend the m
2 CONC eetings. This was noted with regret. He served on the Blenheim Educati
2 CONC on Board, appeared in many performances of the Literary Society (givin
2 CONC g readings of various things mostly, it seems), and was active in the
2 CONC Agricultural Society, as he had been in Nelson. He even invented a nov
2 CONC el gate-fastener which was entered into the Marlborough Agricultural S
2 CONC ociety 1875 Annual Show. It wasn't generally admired.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gold was a recurrent interest, for both Willliam and son Oscar. In 187
2 CONC 8 William was able to combine his interests in water and gold when he
2 CONC went to the Wakamarina gold field to try and design ways in which the
2 CONC claims could be drained. His interest was a share of the profits; one
2 CONC would suspect there weren't any if it weren't for the story that Oscar
2 CONC 's daughter, my G-grandmother Kate, told, of how her father had made £
2 CONC 300 in three weeks at Wakamarina. Do we believe her? I'm not entirely
2 CONC sure I do. We don't even know for sure that William's visit to Wakamar
2 CONC ina had anything to do with Oscar; still, it would be very surprising
2 CONC if it didn't, so, on balance, I'm willing to allow William at least pa
2 CONC rtial credit for a possible £300 profit.
2 CONT
2 CONT As he got older, William's health declined and he stopped attending pu
2 CONC blic meetings. Eventually, on the 18th of June, 1886, he died, leaving
2 CONC  behind this obituary in the Marlborough Express:
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------
2 CONT It is with sincere regret that we record the death of Mr William Norgr
2 CONC ove, an old and respected Wairau settler, which took place at three o'
2 CONC clock on Friday afternoon, at his residence, Maxwell Rd\footnote{Yes,
2 CONC it says Maxwell Rd.\ not Dillon St. The house was on the corner of Max
2 CONC well and Dillon, with the garden originally extending south to Stephen
2 CONC son St. and west to Percy St. It was a big section.}. He was in his 73
2 CONC rd year, and the debt of nature was paid through general debility and
2 CONC decay. Mr. Norgrove was born in Ardleigh, Essex, and educated at the F
2 CONC oundation School, Colchester, in which town he afterwards served his t
2 CONC ime as plumber and painter. Having resolved to emigrate, he landed in
2 CONC Wellington, from the ship Gertrude, on the 31st October, 1841. When th
2 CONC e gold fever broke out early in the fifties he went over to Victoria,
2 CONC where he stayed two or three years. On his return to New Zealand he se
2 CONC ttled for some time at Nelson. Whilst there he was a member of the Tow
2 CONC n Board, and the first and prime mover in the establishment of water w
2 CONC orks, a fact which was prominently acknowledged at the inaugural banqu
2 CONC et some years ago. Determining to shift his fortunes to Marlborough, M
2 CONC r. Norgrove arrived in the Wairau in March, 1861, and, at the time of
2 CONC his death, had therefore been a resident amongst us for upwards of 25
2 CONC years, and a Colonist of upwards of 45 years standing. He formerly occ
2 CONC upied the position of a Borough Councillor in Blenheim. Wherever he re
2 CONC sided he worked at his trade, adding to it the practice of letter writ
2 CONC ing and cutting; the Tua Marina Memorial on Massacre Hill being an abi
2 CONC ding specimen of his work. During the last three or four years he had
2 CONC been infirm and unable to carry on his business, but it was not till a
2 CONC bout a fortnight ago that he took to his bed with the last illness fro
2 CONC m which he never rose again. His familiar figure will be greatly misse
2 CONC d from Blenheim. Mr. Norgrove was a man who took an active part in all
2 CONC  local improvements and ideas; he was full of information and genialit
2 CONC y; and possessed a fund of fertile and ingenious ideas. It will be rem
2 CONC embered to his honor that he was remarkable for the breadth, liberalit
2 CONC y, and tolerance of his opinions. He was married at Ilford Church, Ess
2 CONC ex, and his widow and seven children -- four sons and three daughters
2 CONC -- survive him.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT After having so much to say about William, it is disappointing that we
2 CONC  know so little about Sarah. However, such is the burden of the geneal
2 CONC ogist; women just don't appear. She survived William by a few years, a
2 CONC nd died on the 3rd of March, 1891, leaving an obituary in her turn (in
2 CONC  the Marlborough Express):
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT Another old colonist has passed into the silent land. We refer to Mrs.
2 CONC  Norgrove, who breathed her last at her residence, Maxwell Road, early
2 CONC  this morning. The deceased, who was the relict of the late William No
2 CONC rgrove, was married at Bow Church, Middlesex in 1839, and with her hus
2 CONC band came out to the colony by the ship Gertrude, landing in Wellingto
2 CONC n on November 1st, 1841. They removed to Nelson in 1855, and afterward
2 CONC s came to the Wairau in May, 1861. Of a kindly and benevolent disposit
2 CONC ion, though quiet and unobtrusive in her mode of life, the deceased la
2 CONC dy was highly esteemed, and perhaps by no one will she be more missed
2 CONC than by the many children of her acquaintance, of whom she was particu
2 CONC larly fond, and whom she entertained by many a simple tale. The memory
2 CONC  of her many virtues, and her cheerful demeanour amid the later years
2 CONC of an invalid life will be cherished by her numerous friends. She leav
2 CONC es a large family of grown up sons and daughters, to whom we extend ou
2 CONC r sympathy in their bereavement.
2 CONT ---------------
