1 NOTE According to Kate, Sarah came from the King-Hall family, which is now
2 CONC very well known, as her brother (and then two more generations) were a
2 CONC dmirals in the British Navy. They published extensive diaries (which a
2 CONC grees with Kate’s recollections). One difficulty: I cannot find a ment
2 CONC ion of Sarah in any genealogies of this family, or in their diaries. T
2 CONC o be honest, I don’t believe the King-Hall Admiral connection, and nei
2 CONC ther do other family researchers. In fact, it’s practically certain to
2 CONC  be a Kate fabrication. Sarah appears to have quite a different geneal
2 CONC ogy, which I’ve reproduced here.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah’s death notice says she was married at Bow Church, Middlesex. Wi
2 CONC lliam’s death notice says Ilford Church, Essex. Take your pick, I supp
2 CONC ose. But the poem Sarah wrote says the church at Bow, so that is proba
2 CONC bly correct. Mind you, it’s only five miles from Ilford to Bow, accord
2 CONC ing to Sarah. Sarah was driven in a coach. Her husband walked. So did
2 CONC her 80 year old Granny, who didn’t seem to know what was going on, acc
2 CONC ording to Sarah.
2 CONT
2 CONT Interesting question: who was her “Blind brother Harry”, who appears i
2 CONC n the poem?. Henry? Possibly. Did he remain blind the rest of his life
2 CONC ? He married, according to Joan’s information. Very puzzling.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the multimedia page you can read her death notice, and two poems sh
2 CONC e wrote. They are truly dreadful. Not just bad; absolutely and totally
2 CONC  dreadful. But very interesting nonetheless.
