1 NOTE From the Chair of the Yorkshire genealogical society:
2 CONT
2 CONT “There are many Barretts in the surviving Parish Registers of  the Lan
2 CONC g kirk of Craven, St Andrew's, Kildwick-in-Craven, which date back to
2 CONC 1575. A cursory look at our Parish Register section's published transc
2 CONC riptions of the registers reveals a Stephen Barret[t] baptised 14 May
2 CONC 1718 son of Peter and Mary of Sutton, Weaver. Sutton as its name sugge
2 CONC sts is based on a southern farmstead of the parish of Kildwick and one
2 CONC  of its listed field names is Bent. [Smith, A.H. (Editor) The Place Na
2 CONC mes of the West Riding of Yorkshire part vi].”
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT In 2008, the village of Sutton-in-Craven published on its website a co
2 CONC mplete version of the book “The History of Sutton-in-Craven”, written
2 CONC by Nellie Stell in 1927 (http://www.sutton-in-craven.org.uk/stellch01.
2 CONC asp). This book mentions the Barretts a number of times, and is a very
2 CONC  interesting read.
2 CONT
2 CONT For example, in the poll tax of 1379 there were two Barets mentioned (
2 CONC Johannes Baret & ux, and Johannes Baret junior), and one Willelmus de
2 CONC Bent & ux (and wife). The author comments:
2 CONT
2 CONT “The name William de Bent is interesting. There is still a district of
2 CONC  Sutton called 'The Bent', there is a Bent Farm and also Bent Lane. Ev
2 CONC idently William held land or lived in this area.       The surname Bar
2 CONC et occurs twice in the list. Later we know that the Baret family also
2 CONC held land near the Bent. The surname is still very common in the villa
2 CONC ge.”
2 CONT
2 CONT A later muster roll, in the reign of Henry VIII, lists a number of Bar
2 CONC ets again. “William Baret, hors & hern”, for instance.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Civil War a William Barrett of the Bent was a partisan of C
2 CONC harles I, there was a price on his head, and he had to flee to Ireland
2 CONC . Clearly he would have been a man of some importance in Sutton. Still
2 CONC  later, in 1658, there were seven Barretts on the list of ratepayers i
2 CONC n Sutton.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the parish records of Kildwick-in-Craven (of which I have a copy)
2 CONC  it is not possible to piece together a detailed genealogy. There are
2 CONC lots of Barretts all over the place, and they all seem to have the sam
2 CONC e name. Very confusing. About the best that can be said is that there
2 CONC were Barretts aplenty, and Barretts of the Bent aplenty, and had been
2 CONC for centuries.
