1 NAME Simon /Seaman/
2 DISPLAY Simon Seaman
2 GIVN Simon
2 SURN Seaman
1 SOURCES @S51@, @S50@
1 POSITION 1710,1090
2 GENOMAP Thomas Boileau's descendants and related
2 BOUNDARYRECT 1673,1123,1747,1043
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ~1568
2 SOURCE @S50@
1 DEAT Y
2 DATE 1622
2 SOURCE @S50@
2 DISPOSITION
3 DATE JUL 1622
3 PLAC Bredgar, Kent
4 _XREF @place00998@
3 SOURCE @S50@
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE [BMI] calls him minister of Bredgate, but that seems to have been an e
2 CONC rror.
2 CONT
2 CONT Simon must have been an unusually intelligent man; although his father
2 CONC  and his brothers were blacksmiths, he was lucky and clever enough to
2 CONC receive a proper education, and was raised to the dizzy heights of a v
2 CONC icarage, well above the rest of his family in income and social status
2 CONC . In 1588, when he was 20, he was awarded one of two scholarships for
2 CONC Kent, founded by the Deacon of Nottingham, and rotated around the coun
2 CONC ties in turn,  and on this scholarship went to St.\ John's, Cambridge.
2 CONC  At that time, of course, very few of the colleges had been founded; S
2 CONC t.\ John's was one of the early ones, founded in 1510.
2 CONT
2 CONT Simon got his B.A. from 1591-92 and his M.A. in 1595 (According to the
2 CONC  official list of Cambridge University Alumni, 1261--1900), and was th
2 CONC en appointed as Vicar of Bredgar, his home town, where he remained for
2 CONC  the rest of his life, from 1595--1622. His father was presumably prou
2 CONC d of him. We know almost nothing about his activities as vicar, except
2 CONC  that one thing he did (in addition to his usual duties, one assumes)
2 CONC is to transcribe into the parish ledger many of the old records before
2 CONC  he became vicar. They can still be seen (well, I've only seen photoco
2 CONC pies, to be honest, but that's still pretty cool), with a little notat
2 CONC ion “Simon Seaman, Vicar" at the bottom of each page.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1596, Simon married Susanna Wythers in London, at St. Michael Bassi
2 CONC shaw, a church that was demolished in 1899. It's a reasonable assumpti
2 CONC on that this was Susanna's home parish. It's possible that she brought
2 CONC  quite a lot of money to the marriage, as in his will Simon left his d
2 CONC aughter Mary various items, including ``my little bible ... the brasse
2 CONC  vessle my wife used to fry fritters in ... and £100 to be invested fo
2 CONC r her. The most likely place where Simon could have got this kind of w
2 CONC ealth is from his wife; it was a large sum for the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried at Bredgar 14. 6. 1622.
