3 _XREF @place00998@
2 SOURCE @S50@
1 DEAT Y
2 DATE 1605
2 PLAC Bredgar, Kent
3 _XREF @place00998@
2 SOURCE @S50@
2 DISPOSITION
3 DATE JUL 1605
3 PLAC Bredgar, Kent
4 _XREF @place00998@
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE Robert married Mar(i)e and they had eleven children: Richard, John, Wi
2 CONC lliam, Thomas, Samuel, Kathryn, Marye, Robert, Peter, Simon, Margaret.
2 CONC
2 CONT He had a smithy in The Street.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Seaman was buried at Bredgar on 5.7.1605, his wife Mar(i)e on 9
2 CONC .4.1578.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT David Kennedy, in the Department of Classics and Ancient History, Univ
2 CONC ersity of Western Australia, has transcribed Robert’s will, or as much
2 CONC  of it as he could, and commented on it. I’ll just reproduce it here,
2 CONC without change. I haven’t actually asked David if he minds, so I hope
2 CONC he doesn’t.
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of Robert Seaman
2 CONT
2 CONT In the name of God amen the 17 of June ano domini 1605 I Robert Seaman
2 CONC  dweling in the parish of Bredgar being of a sound & perfect [?] memor
2 CONC y have caused this note containing my last will and testament to be ma
2 CONC yd in manner & forme following; First I Comfitt [? Maybe commend] my s
2 CONC oul to God and my body to the earth. Item I give to Richard Seaman min
2 CONC e eldest sonne my house and Garden that belongs thereto together with
2 CONC my shopp lying in the parish of Bredgar and to his heires for ever Ite
2 CONC m I give to John Seaman my sonne Three pounds of good and lawfull mone
2 CONC y of England Item I give to my sonne William three pounds likewise I g
2 CONC ive and will to Thomas Seaman my sonne five poundes of good and lawful
2 CONC l English money and moreover I forgive all **h debts **s he oweth me.
2 CONC Item I will to Peter Seaman my sonne the summe of thirty pounds of goo
2 CONC d and lawfull money of England to be payd unto him within one whole ye
2 CONC are after the day of my decease, And if it fortune that my sayd sonne
2 CONC dye before payment bemayd according to the time limited then I will th
2 CONC at the sayd summe of xxx poundes be payd equally be even portion, to m
2 CONC y sonnes and daughters that then shall be livinge. Item I give to Symo
2 CONC n Seaman my youngest sonne x/- [= 10/-] and Calvins Institutions. I wi
2 CONC ll to Mary my daughter twenty nobles to be payd to her one whole yeare
2 CONC  after the day of my decease. Item I will to Margarett my Daughter xlt
2 CONC y [=40] shillings to be payd unto her as she shall need it presently a
2 CONC fter my decease and further I will unto her a payer of good sheets and
2 CONC  the Chest that stands [?] *** the path [?] syde of my bedd: I will be
2 CONC syds that Richard my eldest sonne his heires executors or assignes sha
2 CONC ll pay unto the sayd Margarett my daughter xs [=10\-] yearely during h
2 CONC er naturall life the which [?] xs my mynde is [?] it shall be payd att
2 CONC  two severall [?] payment that is to say five shillings halfe yearely,
2 CONC  And if the foresaid Richard his heires executors or assignes doe refu
2 CONC se [?] this to pay it Item I will that the sayd Margarett shall either
2 CONC  straine [?] any goods that shall be within the foresayd house or orch
2 CONC ard or cease upon the sayd house and orchard and keep it in her owne p
2 CONC ossession untill such time as the sayd summe of money shall be payd (a
2 CONC s aforesayd) according to the same [?] meaning of my will: I will unto
2 CONC  Richard Sellden and George Sellden sonnes of George Sellden tenn shil
2 CONC lings to be spent in clothing of them. Item I will tenn shillings to t
2 CONC enn of the poorest of this parish, All my moveable [?] goodes either w
2 CONC ithin the house or without or in the shopp that [?] I work in he payin
2 CONC g all legacyes and bequeaths I bequeathe to Richard my sonne whome I m
2 CONC ake sole and whole execuotr of this my last will and testament dated a
2 CONC nd written the day & yeare aforementioned in wittnes whereof I have se
2 CONC tte my hand and seale in the presence of Thomas Ly*ystead [?] his mark
2 CONC e, Thomas Batchelor his marke, Seaman Richard [?] Seaman his marke.
2 CONT
2 CONT Comments from Robert Kennedy:
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert made his (eldest) son, Richard, his execuotr, responsible for p
2 CONC aying all legacies and bequests.
2 CONT He bequeathed his principal property fo his son Richard: his house, ga
2 CONC rden (=orchard) and (blacksmith’s) shop together with all his moveable
2 CONC  goods in the house, outside and in the shop. The will of his son John
2 CONC  reveals that this was at Silver Street, about .5 km W of Bredgar itse
2 CONC lf.
2 CONT To his sons John and William he bequeathed £3 each.
2 CONT To his son Thomas he bequeathed £5 and cancellation of his debt to his
2 CONC  father.
2 CONT To his son Peter he bequeathed £30 to be paid within a year. If Peter
2 CONC died before payment, the money was to be divided equally between Rober
2 CONC t’s surviving children. This is several times larger than anything lef
2 CONC t to his other younger children. Peter himself, resident by then in Ch
2 CONC atham, left no property and only small sums in shillings to his relati
2 CONC ves. Perhaps his father was trying to set him up in business.
2 CONT To his son Simon he bequeathed 10/- and his copy of Calvin’s Instituti
2 CONC ons. Simon, by then vicar of Bredgar for over a decade, was far better
2 CONC  off than his father and brothers. The modest bequest reflects his rel
2 CONC igious calling, giving him a religious text and the 10\- the better-of
2 CONC f seem to have routinely paid their vicar for a full funeral service (
2 CONC Richard Seaman explicitly left 10\- for a sermon to be preached at his
2 CONC  burial).
2 CONT To his daughter Mary he left 40\-, a pair of good sheets, and the ches
2 CONC t beside his bed. She is also to receive for life 10\- annually - in t
2 CONC wo half-yearly installments of 5\- from her brother Richard or his hei
2 CONC rs etc. If he fails to pay three times she is to be permitted to take
2 CONC goods from the house or garden left to Richard or even to seize the ho
2 CONC use and orchard itself until she is paid.
2 CONT To Richard and George Sellden, sons of George Sellden, he leaves 10\-
2 CONC to purchase clothes for them.
2 CONT He leaves 10\- for the ten poorest parishioners. It is not clear if th
2 CONC is is 10\- between them or each. The former seems too small to be like
2 CONC ly.
