1 NOTE of Burwash. Get this. Their childen were called Herbert, John, Retourn
2 CONC e, Elizabeth, Goodgift, Hopestill, Fearnot, Constance, Thanckfull, and
2 CONC  Goddard. Bloody hell, hippies ain’t got nothing on the Hepdens.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hepdens are one well-known family to have used Weido Puritan Nomen
2 CONC clature; in fact they used regular names in conjunction with WPN, whic
2 CONC h was not at all unusual. Goddard Hepden, who later called himself God
2 CONC ward Hepden, appears in the book by Tyacke as an example of an upwardl
2 CONC y mobile Puritan. His father, John Hepden, made his will in 1586, at w
2 CONC hich time he called himself a yeoman, although one of his executors ca
2 CONC lled himself a gentleman. His sons, Goddard and Thomas, were also call
2 CONC ed yeoman at that time. In 1591, Goddard was reported by the vicar of
2 CONC Burwash for failing to receive communion there for over a year, but, d
2 CONC espite these differences in doctrine, both Thomas and Goddard were cal
2 CONC ling themselves gentlemen by 1610, when Goddard was a member of a gran
2 CONC d jury.
2 CONT
2 CONT Goddard built himself a house at Burwash, Homshurst, which is still st
2 CONC anding today. It is made of brick, with stone dressings, and has the i
2 CONC nitials G.H. and the date 1610 carved on the lintel. By this time he h
2 CONC ad reverted to Goddard, as opposed to Godward, although it's not clear
2 CONC  whether he was a good boy and received the communion. In the preamble
2 CONC  to his will he admits that ``the days of my pilgrimage to be both few
2 CONC  and evil" and speaks of ``the small estate and substance which God ha
2 CONC th lent me". This was clearly pretty much nothing but self-righteous p
2 CONC ious hypocrisy; he goes on to mention his freehold lands in Heathfield
2 CONC  and Mayfield, copyhold land in Brightling, and property in Burwash, w
2 CONC hich sounds like his small estate wasn't too small at all. In his well
2 CONC  he also left money bequests amounting to $\pounds$183 5s, and a furth
2 CONC er $\pounds$23 in annuities, as well as $\pounds$2 10s for the poor. S
2 CONC ince, in the 1590s, when he was called a yeoman, he had been assessed
2 CONC to be worth $\pounds$4, it's clear that he had done rather well for hi
2 CONC mself thank you very much.
