1 NOTE sir John Dawtrey of Moore-hall, co. Sussex. Quite a bit seems to be kn
2 CONC own about this family of Dawtreys, based close to Petworth in Sussex.
2 CONC Berry’s Sussex pedigrees contains his pedigree I think, but I haven’t
2 CONC seen that yet.
2 CONT
2 CONT I found this in a web page on Petworth. Believe it or not, as you wish
2 CONC :
2 CONT
2 CONT “For a concise but interesting outline of the ancestry of the Dawtrey
2 CONC family, I turn to that most charmingly written book, The Story of Fitt
2 CONC leworth, by the Hon. Lady Maxse. It has only recently been published a
2 CONC nd may well be considered a perfect example of what a village history
2 CONC should be. It combines an accuracy of research that archaeologists wel
2 CONC come with a homely, Pleaseant style. One feels that the book will be j
2 CONC ust as much a Pleaseure to the good man in the cottage who has known h
2 CONC is Fittleworth from infancy, as of solid worth to those who are learne
2 CONC d in research.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Maxse tells us that the ancestor of the Dawtreys came to England
2 CONC with the Conqueror. He was named William de Alta Ripa or de Hauterive,
2 CONC  a name which he took from Hauterive near Alencon in France, where his
2 CONC  forbears had held land. A younger son of William, named Robert, came
2 CONC to Sussex in attendance upon Queen Adeliza, the wife of Henry I. This
2 CONC Queen married secondly William de Alblni, Earl of Arundel, and in 1139
2 CONC  she and her husband granted lands in Sussex to Robert de Alta Ripa. Q
2 CONC ueen Adeliza also granted lands in the Honour of Petworth to her half
2 CONC brother, Joscelin de Louvaln, son of the Duke of Brabant. He had marri
2 CONC ed the great north country heiress, Agnes de Perci, and had taken her
2 CONC name. It was in this way that the Percy family became established at P
2 CONC etworth. It is said that Robert de Alta Ripa married the sister of Agn
2 CONC es Perci.
2 CONT
2 CONT Berry gives a pedigree of the later members of the family whose origin
2 CONC al name of foreign origin became anglicised into Dawtrey. The Chapel o
2 CONC f St. Thomas, in Petworth Church, was owned by this family of great la
2 CONC ndowners and it still contains the beautiful sixteenth-century tomb of
2 CONC  Sir John Dawtrey and his second wife, whose maiden name was Johanna S
2 CONC cardeville.”
2 CONT
2 CONT A copy of this book is in the West Sussex Archives.
