1 NOTE Extract from :
2 CONT “Although Richard is entered in the Visitation in Kent, in 1619, as Wa
2 CONC lter Colepeper's eldest son, this was not the case, as Sir John, as th
2 CONC e eldest son, inherited Hardreshull, co. Warwick, Bayhall, co. Kent, a
2 CONC nd Wigsell, co. Sussex. It appears also from the same Visitation that
2 CONC this Sir John married Agnes, daughter of John Bedgebury, but no mentio
2 CONC n whatever is there made of the undoubted fact that some time before 1
2 CONC 460 he was the husband of Agnes Gainsford, which is clearly proved by
2 CONC the Proceedings in Chancery relating to the abduction of the two Wakeh
2 CONC urst heiresses by Sir John's brothers, Richard and Nicholas,41 where i
2 CONC t is expressly stated that a sister of John and William Gainsford was
2 CONC wedded to John Culpepyr, and later on in the same suit mention is made
2 CONC  of John Culpeper and Agnes, his wife. The marriage is also alluded to
2 CONC  in De Banco Roll, Trin., 5 Edward IV., m. 118d, and it explains the m
2 CONC ention of Ottewell and George Gainsford (grandsons of the above John G
2 CONC ainsford, who married Anne Wakehurst, aunt of the co-heiresses, and so
2 CONC ns of Sir John Gainsford, by Anne, daughter of Ottewell Worsley), as c
2 CONC ousins in the will Walter Colepeper, of Calais, 1514--1516.
2 CONT The question arises, therefore, as to whether the record of Sir John's
2 CONC  marriage with Agnes Bedgebury is not due to a mistake on the part of
2 CONC the heralds. In their pedigree they certainly omit these two important
2 CONC  facts, viz., that before 1460 Sir John was the husband of Agnes Gains
2 CONC ford, and also that his father Walter's wife, of the same Christian na
2 CONC me, was the widow of John Bedgebury. It seems therefore not improbable
2 CONC  that these two marriages have been confused; such, indeed, must have
2 CONC been the case unless Sir John was twice married, and of this the Visit
2 CONC ation affords no evidence whatever. Sir John Colepeper died 22nd Decem
2 CONC ber, 1480, and was buried at Goudherst.
2 CONT His two brothers, Richard and Nicholas, under somewhat romantic circum
2 CONC stances, married Margaret and Elizabeth Wakehurst, (granddaughters and
2 CONC  co-heiresses of Richard Wakehurst, sen., of Wakehurst, in Ardingly. T
2 CONC hese two girls were confided by Elizabeth, their grandmother, Thomas E
2 CONC chyngham, Thomas Hoo, John Gaynesford and William Gaynesford, their gu
2 CONC ardians, to the care of John Colepeper and Agnes, his wife, the former
2 CONC  of whom "promysed on the faithe and trouthe of his bodye and as he wa
2 CONC s a gentylman," that they should not be wronged. In spite of this prom
2 CONC ise, however, he, with Richard and Nicholas his brethren, "with force
2 CONC and armes riotously agense the Kynges peas arayed in the manr of warre
2 CONC  at Goutherst toke and caried" them away to Bobbing, Alexander Cliffor
2 CONC d's place in Kent, and afterwards transported them to London to a plac
2 CONC e of John Gibson, "the seide Margarete and Elizabeth at the tyme of th
2 CONC eir takyng away makyng grete and pittious lamentacion and wepyng."42
2 CONT This high-handed proceeding on the part of the two fortune hunting bro
2 CONC thers was productive of much litigation, as Elizabeth Wakehurst, grand
2 CONC mother of the two heiresses, refused to resign the title deeds of thei
2 CONC r estates,43 and it was some time before a peaceable settlement was ob
2 CONC tained. Richard died without issue, but Nicholas became the ancestor o
2 CONC f the Colepepers of Wakehurst, and as the brass to him and his wife El
2 CONC izabeth in Ardingly Church shows ten sons and eight daughters, we may
2 CONC conclude that they lived long and happily together. [The famous herbal
2 CONC ist, Dr. Nicholas Culpeper, was the great-grandson of this Nicholas].
2 CONT Sir John Colepeper, of Bedgebury, by Agnes, his wife, had two sons:--
2 CONT (i.) Alexander, progenitor of the senior line of Bedgebury, in Goudhur
2 CONC st, co. Kent, whose descendants, as belonging more to Kent than to Sus
2 CONC sex, we shall here omit.
2 CONT (ii.) Walter, ancestor of the Wigsell branch of the family.”
