1 NOTE Second son. Walter’s father is given as John by the Visitation, but  a
2 CONC rgues this was incorrect. I follow  here.
2 CONT Extract from :
2 CONT “Walter Colepeper, who continued the line, married Agnes, the daughter
2 CONC  of Edmund Roper, of St. Dunstans, Canterbury, and is so described on
2 CONC her tombstone at Goudhurst. She was also the widow of John, son of Joh
2 CONC n de Bedgebury, a fact not mentioned in the pedigrees recorded in the
2 CONC Visitations, but which is amply evidenced by an undated Chancery Procc
2 CONC eding, temp. Hen. VI., where Walter Coulpepir and Agneis, his wife, la
2 CONC te the wife of John, son of John de Beggebury and Thomas Chaundeler, c
2 CONC haplain, appear as plaintiffs in a dispute relating to property in Gou
2 CONC dhurst, Cranbrook and Hawkhurst, which John, son of Roger de Beggebury
2 CONC , left to pay for two chaplains to sing masses for his soul and for th
2 CONC at of Johanna, his wife. By this marriage Walter Colepeper had, with t
2 CONC wo daughters--Margaret, married to Alexander Clifford, and Elizabeth,
2 CONC married to John Hardes, of Hardes, co. Kent--three sons, Richard, John
2 CONC  and Nicholas. Agnes, his wife, predeceased him on the 2nd December, 1
2 CONC 457, and was buried at Goudhurst, and Walter himself died on the 24th
2 CONC November, 1462, and was also buried at Goudhurst.”
2 CONT
2 CONT And further from :
2 CONT “Although the pedigree given above differs in many respects from those
2 CONC  recorded in the Visitation, it is substantiated not only by many Inqu
2 CONC isitions, Deeds and Grants,40 but also by a suit entered on membrane 4
2 CONC 84 of the De Banco Roll, Hilary, 4 Edward IV., whence the following pe
2 CONC digree is deduced:
2 CONT
2 CONT John Culpeper 
2 CONT Thomas Culpeper 
2 CONT Thomas Culpeper 
2 CONT Walter Culpeper 
2 CONT John Culpeper 
2 CONT Richard Culpeper 
2 CONT Nicholas Culpeper 
2 CONT John Culpeper 
2 CONT Nicholas Culpeper
2 CONT  
2 CONT This suit has reference to the fine levied in 1320, the John at the he
2 CONC ad of the pedigree being the son of Sir Thomas and Margery, and the pl
2 CONC aintiffs, John, Richard and Nicholas Colepeper, claiming one quarter o
2 CONC f these lands against Sir John Fogge, according to the customs of gave
2 CONC lkind, in right of their father Walter.”
2 CONT
2 CONT From :
2 CONT “At the time of their check at the hands of Edward II, the Culpepers s
2 CONC eem to have recently inaugurated their characteristic practice of land
2 CONC  acquisition by the time honored expedient of marrying heiresses. It w
2 CONC as from their first manor so acquired, that of Bayhall in the Kentish
2 CONC parish of Pembury on the southern border of the weald, that they sprea
2 CONC d, as Hasted remarks, 'over the whole face of the county' of Kent; and
2 CONC , we may add, eventually of adjacent Sussex as well.
2 CONT
2 CONT In this process, the Walter Culpeper who fought at Agincourt, being of
2 CONC  the seventh recorded generation of his family, put his roots in the g
2 CONC round a few miles southeast of Bayhall. About 1425 he married the wido
2 CONC w of the last Bedgebury of Bedgebury in Goudhurst and was buried with
2 CONC that family in Goudhurst church. His tomb described him as 'arm. filiu
2 CONC s Thorne Culpeper militis... obiit 24 November 1462' (Weever, Antient
2 CONC Funeral Monuments, 1767 ed., p. 69); which identifies him genealogical
2 CONC ly as the Walter, son of Thomas, who himself left sons, John, Richard
2 CONC and Nicholas, as rehearsed in DeBanco Roll, 4 Edw. IV, Hilary Term, me
2 CONC mbrane 484.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Fairfax Harrison, "The Proprietors of the Northern Neck"”
