1 NOTE of Bay Hall in Pepenbery, Tunbridge. Knight. Son and heir of John. The
2 CONC  original reads “Thomas Colepeper de Bay Hall in p’chia de Pepenbery p
2 CONC ’pe Tunbridge, Miles fil. et haeres Joh’is Colepeper de eadem Militis.
2 CONC ”
2 CONT
2 CONT Extract from :
2 CONT “The eldest son, Sir Thomas Culpeper, who was executed at Winchelsea i
2 CONC n 1321, seems to have married Margery, a daughter of the Bayhall famil
2 CONC y, and either by this match, or by purchase, to have acquired their es
2 CONC tates. This Thomas is called in 1306 "fil' Thom' Colepeper de Brenches
2 CONC le." The following evidence is adduced to show that Margery probably b
2 CONC elonged to the Bayhall famiIy.
2 CONT In 1299 there is the grant, mentioned before, by Benedicta daughter of
2 CONC  Thomas de Chitcroft, which family bore identically the same arms as t
2 CONC he Colepepers (and therefore Thomns de Chitcrolt may hnve been a Colep
2 CONC eper himself, or he and Thomas Colepeper may both by marriage with the
2 CONC  Bayhall heiresses have adopted their coat of arms) to Thomas, son of
2 CONC Thomas Colepeper, and Margery his wife of lands at Beghall with part o
2 CONC f a mill in Pepinbury.13
2 CONT 1308. Charter in which Cecilia, Margeria, Amicia, and Christina, daugh
2 CONC ters of John atte Bayhalle grant to Thomas the son of Thomas Colepeper
2 CONC , for 5 marcs, all their part of a mill and lands in Pepinbury which t
2 CONC hey had after the death of their grandfather William atte Bayhalle.14
2 CONT 1309. Johanna quae fuit uxor Johannis atte Beyhalle petit versus Thoma
2 CONC s Colepeper juniorem.15
2 CONT 1312. Grant from Ralph Newman and Agnes daughtor and heir of Geoffrey
2 CONC atte Beyhalle to Thomas Colepeper and Margery his wife of lands in Pep
2 CONC inbery.16
2 CONT 1312. Grant of Walter son of Jeffrey atte Beyhalle and Agnes his daugh
2 CONC ter and Ralph Neweman to Thomas Colepeper and Margery his wife for 26
2 CONC shillings "quandam granam terrae in Pepingebery."17
2 CONT 1313. Gilbert ate Beyhalle grants to Thomas Colepeper and Margery for
2 CONC 50 shillings a piece of land in Pepingbery to be held by them and thei
2 CONC r heirs for ever.18
2 CONT 1314. Charter by which Christina daughter of John atte Bayhalle for 4
2 CONC marcs grants to Thomas Colepeper lands in Pepingbery.19
2 CONT 1315. Christina de Bayhalle grants to Thomas Colepeper and Margery his
2 CONC  wife for 3s 6d the pension which William Scrivor owes yearly to her.2
2 CONC 0
2 CONT 1316. Grant from Ralph Newheman to Thomas Colepeper and Margery his wi
2 CONC fe of lands at Beahalle in fields called "Redest" and "Mesebort" in Pe
2 CONC pinbury.21
2 CONT 1316. Charter by which Christina daughter of John atte Bayhalle grants
2 CONC  to Thomas Colepeper and Margery for two shillings all that part of wo
2 CONC od "inter Rodgate et stagna de Bayhalle cum placea super quam boscus c
2 CONC rescit."22
2 CONT 1317. Christina daughter of John atte Bayhalle grants to Thomas Colepe
2 CONC per and Margery for 4 marcs a messuage and lands in Peapingbury which
2 CONC she had "post decessum Willelmi ate Beyhalle avi sui."23
2 CONT 1317. Quitclaim by Johanna widow of John atte Beyhalle to Thomas Colep
2 CONC eper and Margery his wife of a house and lands at Beyhalle for 3? marc
2 CONC s."24
2 CONT Sir Thomas Colepeper, who "pro bono servicio in partibus Scotie" recei
2 CONC ved a pardon in the 32nd year of Edward  for breaking the park of the
2 CONC Prior of Christ Church, Canterbury, at Westwell, and the park of the P
2 CONC rior of Michelham, in the 29th year of that King's reign,25 took the s
2 CONC ide of the Earl of Lancaster against Edward I, and being Governor of W
2 CONC inchelsea, was there executed in 1321.
2 CONT Previous to this, however, by a fine levied in 1320, part of his estat
2 CONC es, consisting of 2 messuages (houses), 2 mills, 405 acres of land, 20
2 CONC  acres of meadow, 60 acres of pasture, 80 acres of wood and 20 shillin
2 CONC gs annual rental in Pepyngbery, Thonebregg and Teudele, were settled o
2 CONC n himself and Margery his wife for life, with remainder to their sons
2 CONC Walter, John and Richard in succession.26
2 CONT By Inquisition taken at Tunbridge 25th February, 1 Edward II (1327), i
2 CONC t was found that Thomas Colepeper died seized of Bayhalle, with lands
2 CONC in Pepyngbery, Thonebregg and Teudele, and that Walter was his son and
2 CONC  heir, aged 22 years on the feast of the Purification of the Blessed V
2 CONC irgin Mary last past (2 Feb 1327).27 In the Inquisition de terris fori
2 CONC sfactis, 17 Edward I, taken at Lamberhurst, mention is made of Thomas
2 CONC Colepeper's estate in Pepynbery, included in the fine levied in 1320,
2 CONC besides which it is stated that he had acquired 50 acres in ffoghelesd
2 CONC enne from Thomas Colepeper, senr., in 1310, 1 messuage and 1 carucate
2 CONC of land (land sufficient to support a family farm) in Bernette and Ram
2 CONC herste from Richard Wych in 1320, 1 messuage and 60 acres of land in B
2 CONC ocstede from Ralph Marscot, 10 acres in Bayrugge from Michael de Bette
2 CONC sfield, and 40 acres in ffernth (i.e., Frant) from Roger de fferrugge.
2 CONC  All these lands had been seized by the King on November the 6th, 1321
2 CONC , on account of the felony of the said Thomas, and for no other reason
2 CONC .28
2 CONT It was not long, however, before all these estates were restored to th
2 CONC e family. By deed bearing date 1 Jul 1288 (17 Edward I), Margery, wido
2 CONC w of Thomas Colepeper, agreed to grant the Pepinbury estate to the Kin
2 CONC g for the term of her life on the payment of 12 marks per annum from t
2 CONC he Exchequer.29 But apparently she soon repented of this bargain, and
2 CONC addressed a petition to the King praying that "le manoir de la Bayehal
2 CONC le" might be restored to her, the grounds for the request being tllat
2 CONC the King's ministers had not only neglected to pay the rent, but had l
2 CONC et her houses go to ruin, "a g'nt damage de l'avantdite Marg'ie de xll
2 CONC i."30 On this the King issued a commission to Henry de Cobham and othe
2 CONC rs to investigate the matters set forth in the petition,31 and the dir
2 CONC ect result of this enquiry was an order for the immediate restoration
2 CONC of all the, property. The outlying estates were to be restored uncondi
2 CONC tionally, and if lands had been "demised at ferm" the farmers were to
2 CONC be satisfied for their expenditure on the land,32 while with regard to
2 CONC  Bayhall and the land included in the fine levied in 1320, there was t
2 CONC his saving clause, viz., that this portion of the property was to reve
2 CONC rt to the King in case all the parties mentioned in the fine died with
2 CONC out issue.33
2 CONT From this order it appears that Thomas Colepeper acquired the Buxted p
2 CONC roperty mentioned above in 7 Edward II, and in 13 Edward II he purchas
2 CONC ed from Reginald, son of Reginald Burgeys, of Boxstede, 1 messuage and
2 CONC  50 acres in Boxstede and Marsefeld. The 40 acres in Fernth (Frant), c
2 CONC o. Sussex, is supplemented in this order by 10 acres of wood bought in
2 CONC  10 Edward II from Roger, son of Richard de Ferrugge, while another 20
2 CONC  acres in the same town is stated to have been acquired from William s
2 CONC on of John de Netteworth.”
