1 NOTE Extract from :
2 CONT “Most pedigrees agree in giving John as the Recognitor's son, followed
2 CONC  by Sir Thomas as his grandson. If the pedigrees are correct then this
2 CONC  Sir Thomas, of Bayhall, must have been an old man in 4 Edward II (131
2 CONC 0). Assuming that the grandfather was fifty years of age when he serve
2 CONC d as Recognitor, then the two generations succeding him must have cove
2 CONC red a period of some eighty years. This would make Sir Thomas Colepepe
2 CONC r, in 4 Edward II, when his son Thomas and Margery his with purchased
2 CONC of him 50 acres in Foulsden,6 a fairly old man, and although, indicted
2 CONC  in 1305, with his son Thomas, for stealing the goods of the vicar of
2 CONC Ringmer, we can hardly believe that he took any active part in the mat
2 CONC ter.
2 CONT
2 CONT For this reason it was not probable that it was this Thomas who was po
2 CONC rter or janitor of Leeds Castle in 1292; it was more likely his son of
2 CONC  the same Christian name. In 1296 (25 Edward I) there is an important
2 CONC reference to Thomas Colepeper, sen.; the executors of the will of Sir
2 CONC William de Montfort brought an action against Thomas Colepeper and Joh
2 CONC n his son concerning the manor of Newenton, in Kent.7
2 CONT From this it is clear that there was, besides his sons Thomas and Walt
2 CONC er, who were executed, another son John, and there was doubtless anoth
2 CONC er son named Nicholas. All four were implicated in the Earl of Lancast
2 CONC er's rebellion, but John and Nicholas evidently in a Iesser degree tha
2 CONC n Thomas and Walter. There was an order issued in 1322 to the Sheriff
2 CONC of York to receive John Colepeper and others into custody in York Cast
2 CONC le.8 This looks as if John Colepeper took part in the Battle of Boroug
2 CONC hbridge, and Weaver, in his Ancient Funeral Monuments, p. 272, speaks
2 CONC of Sir Thomas Colepeper siding with the Earl of Lancaster and being ha
2 CONC nged, drawn and quartered at Winchelsea. The place fatal to the Earl w
2 CONC as Pontefract, so it seems certain that both Thomas and John were with
2 CONC  Lancaster's forces at Boroughbridge.
2 CONT After remaining a close prisoner during the remainder of the reign in
2 CONC the Castles of Berkhampstead and Gloucester,9 John Colepeper was relea
2 CONC sed on the accession of Edward III., and in the restoration of confisc
2 CONC ated lands which then took place those of John Colepeper, of Lynlegh,
2 CONC were included.10 He was alive eleven years later, when John Colepeper,
2 CONC  of Lynlegh, with Johanna, his wife, appear as deforciants in a fine r
2 CONC elating to 20 acres of land in Wythyhame.11
2 CONT With regard to the other two sons of Sir Thomas Colepeper, sen., Walte
2 CONC r and Nicholas, they both suffered for their refusal to admit Queen Is
2 CONC abel to Leeds Castle. Walter "sticked not to tell him" (the Queen's ma
2 CONC rshal) "that neither the Queen nor any other should be lodged there wi
2 CONC thout the commandement of his Lorde the owner." On the Queen coming to
2 CONC  the gate in person "the Captaine most malapertly repulsed her, insomu
2 CONC ch that shee complained grievously to the King," who besieged the plac
2 CONC e and eventually took it. "Then tooke he Captaine Colepeper and hoong
2 CONC him up." Captain Colepeper was doubtless Walter, as the release of Nic
2 CONC holas, his only brother yet unaccounted for, forms the subject of the
2 CONC following order issued in 1323 to Henry de Cobeham, Constable of Roche
2 CONC ster Castle: "Whereas Nicholas Colepeper and others are imprisoned in
2 CONC Rochester Castle because they adhered to certain rebels who held the K
2 CONC ing's Castle of Ledes against him. The King, compassionating their est
2 CONC ate, and being unwilling to detain them longer in prison, orders the C
2 CONC onstable to release those of them whom he shall find by Inquisition to
2 CONC  have no lands, and to cause those of them who have lands to come befo
2 CONC re the King within 15 days from Easter at their own cost and to do and
2 CONC  to receive what the King's Court shall consider in the matter."12
2 CONT
2 CONT Of the four sons of Thomas Colepeper, grandson of the Recognitor, we c
2 CONC an find no trace of John and Nicholas, while from Captain Walter Colep
2 CONC eper sprang the Colepepers of Oxen Hoath and Aylesford, with whom, as
2 CONC being more connected with Kent than Sussex, this paper has no concern.
2 CONC ”
