1 NOTE Extract from :
2 CONT “With regard to the derivation of the name Colepeper no satisfactory e
2 CONC xplanation has ever been given, but as the first of the family of whom
2 CONC  we have any mention, the "Recognitor Magnae Assisae tempore Regis Joh
2 CONC annis," is called Thomas de Colepeper, we may fairly conclude that the
2 CONC  name either bore a local signification, or it refers to the occupatio
2 CONC n, or calling in life, of those who first adopted it. If the name is a
2 CONC  local one two places have been suggested from which it may be derived
2 CONC , viz., Gollesberghe, in Sandwich, co. Kent and Goldspur, or Culspore,
2 CONC  a hundred in the Rape of Hastings. If, on the other hand, the name is
2 CONC  connected in some way with the occupation of those who first assumed
2 CONC it, then it is argued that in the same manner as Coleprophet means a f
2 CONC alse prophet, and Coletragitour a false traitor, so Colepeper may mean
2 CONC  a false pepperer, or sham grocer, i.e., one who traded outside the Fr
2 CONC aternity of Pepperers, the Guild whence sprang the Grocers' Company, w
2 CONC hich was incorporated in 1345. Another suggestion points to the possib
2 CONC ility of Colepeper meaning Blackpepper, while another hints at the lik
2 CONC elihood of there having been formerly some industry in which the culli
2 CONC ng or picking of pepper may have formed one of the chief branches.
2 CONT
2 CONT The armorial bearings of the family, Arg: a bend engrailed, gu., may p
2 CONC ossibly furnish a clue to its origin. Papworth, in his Ordinary of Bri
2 CONC tish Armorials, mentions some sixty families as bearing the bend engra
2 CONC iled, but apparently only two of them, viz., Chitcroft and Walrand, di
2 CONC splayed identically the same coat as the Colepepers. As Robert Walrand
2 CONC , in the Roll of Arms, temp. Henry III., appears as the owner of this
2 CONC coat,1 the Colepepers probably got it somehow through him, and they we
2 CONC re using it as early as 3 Edward III. (1329), when John, the son of Si
2 CONC r Thomas Colepeper, is recorded as bearing it, and his brother Richard
2 CONC  differenced it with a label of three points.2 The Chitcrofts also wer
2 CONC e probably either Colepepers or closely connected with them, as not on
2 CONC ly are their arms identical, but we find the two families associated t
2 CONC ogether at a very early period. In 1299 Benedicta, daughter of Thomas
2 CONC de Chitcroft, granted land in Beghal, with a mill in Pepinbury, to Tho
2 CONC mas, son of Thomas Colepeper, and Margery his wife,3 while in 11 Henry
2 CONC  IV (1409) the names of John Chitcroft and Thomas Colepeper chivaler,
2 CONC appear coupled as defendants in an action brouglht by John Mortymer, r
2 CONC elating to the manor of Asshen, co. Northants.4 An investigation of th
2 CONC e early Walrand and Chitcroft pedigrees would doubtless reveal some co
2 CONC nnection with Colepeper, but would probably give no clue to the origin
2 CONC  of the name, which may, therefore, be left to the choice of the reade
2 CONC r or to his further researches.”
2 CONT
2 CONT And again from :
2 CONT “Thomas de Colepeper is stated to have been a Recognitor of the Grand
2 CONC Assize, on the authority of Phillipott, in his Villare Cantianum where
2 CONC  he quotes "Bundels of incertain years in the Pipe Office," but no dir
2 CONC ect reference being given we have, unfortunately, been unable to trace
2 CONC  him. We would, however, point out that a Recognitor was not a Judge,
2 CONC as is asserted in the article referred to.
2 CONT The Grand Assize was not an assize in the sense which we now use the w
2 CONC ord, but it was a proceeding or enquiry like an assize of novel dissei
2 CONC sin, or an assize of mort d'ancestor, &c., while the Recognitors, who
2 CONC were summoned on such tribunal, were the jurors whose function it was
2 CONC to investigate all cases involving questions of right, and who, being
2 CONC probably neighbours of the disputing parties, were bound to "recognise
2 CONC " and speak the truth concerning the matter at issue.5”
