1 NOTE He published diaries which are now relatively well known, as did his d
2 CONC escendants, 3 of whom became admirals in the British Navy.  The diarie
2 CONC s are mostly excrutiatingly dull, with very little in the way of famil
2 CONC y information, just a lot of naval stuff. You know, Boy Stuff.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------
2 CONT From http://www.kinghallconnections.com/:
2 CONT
2 CONT Our story starts in about 1780 when a young man named Joseph Hall left
2 CONC  his home in the West Riding of Yorkshire and with his wife, came down
2 CONC  to London. Joseph's family were Quakers and had lived for generations
2 CONC  near Settle as weavers and clothiers. On arriving in London he set up
2 CONC  in trade and from his home in New Inn Yard, Shoreditch , ran a weavin
2 CONC g business in nearby Spittalfields.
2 CONT We know very little about Joseph except that he and his wife had a twi
2 CONC n son and daughter born in 1783 and a son that they called James who w
2 CONC as born a year later. Our story of the King-Hall family begins with th
2 CONC e second son, James Hall.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unlike the sons of most weavers James did not follow his father into t
2 CONC he same trade. Instead of being set to work as a child apprentice at a
2 CONC n early age, he received a basic education at a Quaker school. At the
2 CONC age of ten, under circumstances that are not known, he attracted the a
2 CONC ttention of a physician, called Daniel Williams, who took a liking to
2 CONC him and gave him his chance in life by starting him in the medical pro
2 CONC fession. His benefactor made himself responsible for the expenses of J
2 CONC ames's training at Saint Thomas's Hospital.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the early 1800s James made two important decisions which vitally in
2 CONC fluence our story. Having finished his training he went to sea, and at
2 CONC  the age of 21 he started to keep a diary. By going to sea and subsequ
2 CONC ently becoming a Naval Surgeon, he set a pattern for the next four gen
2 CONC erations of his family which did not finally end its connection with t
2 CONC he Royal Navy until 1995. With his diaries he set another pattern. His
2 CONC  diaries were the first of four generations of diaries and memoirs, wh
2 CONC ich between them give one a remarkable picture of a family rising, as
2 CONC a result of their success in the Royal Navy, from comparatively humble
2 CONC  origins to a distinguished position in society.
2 CONT There has also been another feature of these diaries. While the diaris
2 CONC ts, with one possible exception, apparently led respectable and conven
2 CONC tional lives, their diaries have given one leads, which with further r
2 CONC esearch, have shown that some of the diaristsâ connections in the form
2 CONC  of offspring, siblings, cousins and relations by marriage, have had r
2 CONC ather more varied careers.
2 CONT This combination of a naval setting and a set of diaries and memoirs c
2 CONC overing nearly one hundred and fifty years provides a story that has a
2 CONC  surprisingly wide historical and geographical background. Starting du
2 CONC ring the Napoleonic Wars the historical background goes on to cover th
2 CONC e rise of the British Empire to the peak of its power in the late 19th
2 CONC  Century and its subsequent decline during the 20th Century. The geogr
2 CONC aphical spread is equally wide ranging. The King-Halls and their conne
2 CONC ctions were, above all, an Empire family and their activities took pla
2 CONC ce in places as far apart as Australia, China, India, South Africa, Ca
2 CONC nada and the West Indies.
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------
