2 DISPLAY Walter Norgrove
2 GIVN Walter
2 SURN Norgrove
1 PICTURES @pic00035@, @pic00038@
2 PRIMARY @pic00035@
1 SOURCES @S61@
1 POSITION -2651,-1298
2 GENOMAP Thomas Boileau's descendants and related
2 BOUNDARYRECT -2688,-1265,-2614,-1345
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 13 OCT 1850
1 DEAT Y
2 DATE 2 JAN 1916
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE He appears in the Wainuiomata Settlers’ Roll, and Kate has some recoll
2 CONC ections of him, but I don’t know too much.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------
2 CONT Evening Post 18 January 1883
2 CONT W. BURTON vs J. DEMPSEY
2 CONT
2 CONT At the civil sittings of the Resident Magistrate's Court this
2 CONT morning, evidence was taken on both sides in an action brought
2 CONT by W. BURTON against J. DEMPSEY to recover the sum of 100
2 CONT pounds as damages for the refusal of the latter to endorse a
2 CONT promissary note of W. NORGROVE, his brother-in-law, in respect
2 CONT to the purchase of the plaintiff's farm at Wainuiomata. His
2 CONT Worship gave judgment for the plaintiff for the amount claimed,
2 CONT to be reduced to one shilling if defendant endorses the note
2 CONT within seven days. Mr OLLIVIER was for the plaintiff, and Mr
2 CONT BRANDON for the defendant. The following cases were adjourned:-
2 CONT W. C. FITZGERALD v A. D. BENNETT, 14s: and W. C. FITZGERALD v
2 CONT Wm. CORBETT, 2 pounds 15s 6d.
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT (Note that his sister Gertrude married Joe Dempsey, hence the relation
2 CONC ship.)
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1889, according to the Marlborough Express, Walter applied for a pa
2 CONC tent for scutching and cleaning flax, entitled “The Anti-friction Scut
2 CONC cher and Cleaner”.
1 _UID 35DED33F68E94932A2742A1E2A726BB006E0
1 CHAN
2 DATE 6 JAN 2009
1 FAMC @F1481@
1 FAMS @F1666@
0 @I2496@ INDI
1 NAME Horace /Norgrove/
2 DISPLAY Horace Norgrove
2 GIVN Horace
2 SURN Norgrove
1 SOURCES @S64@
1 POSITION -3116,-1414
2 GENOMAP Thomas Boileau's descendants and related
2 BOUNDARYRECT -3153,-1381,-3079,-1461
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 3 DEC 1846
2 SOURCE @S64@
1 DEAT Y
2 DATE 13 JAN 1928
2 SOURCE @S64@
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE Again, Kate just mentions the name, but I don’t know anything more abo
2 CONC ut him. They lived in Picton apparently.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Kate: “Horace married Carrie Whiting and lived in Picton. Issue,
2 CONC 2 boys and 4 girls. Boys, Horace and Harold. Horace died. Harold a mar
2 CONC ket gardener in Auckland. Annie married……..”
2 CONT
2 CONT The Wellington Evening Post, 28 June, 1872, describes in detail a cour
2 CONC t case that H. Norgrove brought against G. Thomas, for £40, for the ba
2 CONC lance of his wages, for sailing, manning, and victualling the ketch Am
2 CONC ateur, and for a month’s salary instead of notice. (Horace didn’t actu
2 CONC ally appear in court, being represented by Mr Ollivier.) Apparently, H
2 CONC orace did so until the 8th of May, but the ketch was then put in the s
2 CONC lip for repairs and Horace wanted £1 a day “as his time was worth that
2 CONC  to him in Picton.” (Clearly, Horace was living and working in Picton
2 CONC then.) Lots of details about the agreement follows, and then, in concl
2 CONC usion, the Magistrate ruled for the defendant (i.e., against Horace),
2 CONC saying the claim was excessive and that Horace was fully informed as t
2 CONC o the change in the agreement. Cost were also awarded. Poor Horace.
2 CONT
2 CONT This explains the many Port of Wellington announcements in the Evening
2 CONC  Post, which read, for example,
2 CONT “May 7 [1873] - Amateur, ketch, 25 tons, Norgrove, for Picton.”
2 CONT “January 24 [1871[ - Amateur, ketch, 25 tons, Norgrove, for Blenheim.”
2 CONT Amateur (with Norgrove), also left for Patea on June 16, 1871. Our Hor
2 CONC ace was a busy lad.
2 CONT There’s also
2 CONT “April 6 [1873] - Unity, schooner, 46 tons, Norgove, from Picton”, in
2 CONC the arrivals. This sounds like Horace going up in the word, to a bigge
2 CONC r vessel.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are many other similar entries, with trips to Havelock, Patea, t
2 CONC he East Coast, Picton and Blenheim. He must have moved around a lot. O
2 CONC ne child was born in Wanganui, in 1876, for example.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Wellington Evening Post, 31 January, 1870, has Owen (as trustee) v
2 CONC  Norgrove - Claim £50. “This was an action brought to recover the valu
2 CONC e of certain goods alleged to have been damaged while on board defenda
2 CONC nt’s vessel on their way to Terawiti. Adjourned till to-morrow.” This
2 CONC referred to Horace, as shown by the Evening Post of Saturday, February
2 CONC  12, 1870, which read:
2 CONT “The adjourned case of Owen (as trustee) v. Norgrove - a claim for dam
2 CONC age done to goods while being conveyed in defendant’s vessel, the Amat
2 CONC eur, to Terawiti - was heard, Mr. Brandon appearing for the plaintiff,
2 CONC  and Mr. Travers for the defendant. Mr. Travers called Mr. Kebbell for
2 CONC  the defence, who, on examining a sample of the damaged flour, (on whi
2 CONC ch the claim was principally based), said he did not think the damage
2 CONC could be so extensive as was alleged - 30s a ton would, he considered,
2 CONC  cover it. He did not think it possible that the damp from the green t
2 CONC imber could have penetrated to the centre of the bags; in the case of
2 CONC the Falcon, which sank alongside the wharf, witness had half a ton of
2 CONC flour on board, and, though it was actually under water, yet it was on
2 CONC ly wet a small distance round the outside of the bags; had the flour d
2 CONC amaged in the Amateur been sifted, the greater portion of it would hav
2 CONC e been fit for use.” The case was adjourned, to be later settled on Tu
2 CONC esday, 8 March, with the award of £37, 7s to the plaintiff, with costs
2 CONC  of £9, 10s. Horace didn’t have too much luck in court.
2 CONT
2 CONT Horace was clearly interested in many things, as shown by this extract
2 CONC  from the Marlborough Express of 20 May, 1892.
2 CONT “THE PATENT THREE-KEELED SHIP. - The following letter appears in the M
2 CONC arlborough Press:- Dear Sir, - In your Tuesday’s issue you published a
2 CONC n article referring to the new patent for three-keeled ships, and unde
2 CONC r it a letter from Mr. F.M. Levin, dated May 6th, in which he claims M
2 CONC r. O.A. Norgrove as the original inventor. I must ask you for space to
2 CONC  state the facts. Twelve years ago I made the first model, and at once
2 CONC  say all the advantages to be derived from ships and steamers being bu
2 CONC ilt on those lines. My brother Oscar Alfred Norgrove visited me when I
2 CONC  lived in Broadway, about the time I was satisfied with the success of
2 CONC  my experiments. As may be expected, I took him into my confidence and
2 CONC  showed him all the possibilities, and this is where he first got his
2 CONC ideas. If my brother is asked he will not deny this. I further develop
2 CONC ed the invention into a submarine steamer, and three years ago I took
2 CONC my crude drawings to the Defence Department with a model of the same.
2 CONC I have now full drawings and specifications that have been laid before
2 CONC  the English Admiralty, bearing the stamp of the Agent-General’s offic
2 CONC e, returned to me with a letter stating that the plans and specificati
2 CONC ons were not accepted by the Admiralty - that I must construct and dem
2 CONC onstrate. Being a poor man those conditions were an impossibility. If
2 CONC there is any claim for the first invention, I claim it against all com
2 CONC ers. I am, &c., HORACE NORGROVE, Picton, May 8th, 1892.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Well, well. I wonder what Oscar thought of that. Sound like he and Hor
2 CONC ace didn’t always get along.
2 CONT
2 CONT Horace  opened a fish curing works in Blenheim in partnership with the
2 CONC  Whiting Brothers, that by 1886 was worth £4,000, had six permanent em
2 CONC ployees and produced around 10,000 cases of fish anually. Horace appea
2 CONC rs many times in court records of the day as he was a remarkably litig
2 CONC ious man, always suing or being sued over money paid or not paid, cond
2 CONC itions fulfilled or not fulfilled.
