0 @I2563@ INDI
1 NAME Geoffrey /de Saye/
2 DISPLAY Geoffrey de Saye
2 GIVN Geoffrey
2 SURN de Saye
1 POSITION 2390,2740
2 GENOMAP Thomas Boileau's descendants and related
2 BOUNDARYRECT 2360,2773,2420,2679
1 SEX M
1 DEAT Y
2 DATE 1230
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE A signatory of the Magna Carta.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------
2 CONT From http://www.magnacharta.org/Barons/baron_geoffrey_de_saye.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT GEOFFREY de SAYE, the Surety, was in arms with the other Barons agains
2 CONC t the King, and consequently his extensive lands and possessions in te
2 CONC n counties were seized. These were given to Peter de Crohim. Six of th
2 CONC e counties we can name: Northampton, Cambridge, Essex, Norfolk, Suffol
2 CONC k and Lincoln, but we cannot be sure of what Castles in those areas we
2 CONC re Geoffrey's, or which other four counties he could claim.
2 CONT While William d'Albini and his companions were holding Rochester Castl
2 CONC e, they had been assured that other Baronial leaders would relieve the
2 CONC m if the Castle were to be besieged by King John. Such a rescue would
2 CONC not have been easy unless the Royal guards were lax in watching the br
2 CONC idge over the Medway. If this bridge were under guard, a march to Roch
2 CONC ester from London along the Dover Road would prove impossible, the com
2 CONC pany then being forced to detour and approach Rochester from Maidstone
2 CONC . Nevertheless, on 26 October, they moved in as far as Dover, where th
2 CONC ey soon heard that the King was on his way to meet them. They promptly
2 CONC  returned to London, leaving the Rochester garrison to do the best it
2 CONC could.
2 CONT Perhaps the march on Rochester was a sop to the Barons' consciences. H
2 CONC ad it been a serious move, it would have been an extraordinarily fooli
2 CONC sh one. The only other attempt to save Rochester was negotiatory. On 9
2 CONC  November King John issued letters of conduct for Richard de Clare, Ro
2 CONC bert FitzWalter, Geoffrey de Saye and the Mayor of London, to confer w
2 CONC ith the Royal emissaries: Peter de Roches, Hubert de Burgh and the Ear
2 CONC ls of Arundel and Warren. There is no certainty that these men ever me
2 CONC t. If indeed they did, nothing came of it. We suspect that the meeting
2 CONC  was originally planned with the hope that a proposal would be accepte
2 CONC d, and it is not unlikely that the proposal would have been a willingn
2 CONC ess to surrender Rochester Castle to the King if the garrison could go
2 CONC  free, but no such move resulted. Yet despite the futility of the meet
2 CONC ing, at least we see Geoffrey de Saye connected, if lightly, with Roch
2 CONC ester Castle. And this is the only Castle with which we are able to li
2 CONC nk his name.
2 CONT Geoffrey de Saye returned to the Royalist party when the civil war was
2 CONC  over, and sided with King Henry III, thereby regaining his lost lands
2 CONC  after the expulsion of the Dauphin. He died 24 October 1230 leaving a
2 CONC  son, William, as his heir, by Alice, daughter of William de Cheney.
2 CONT ------------------------------------
