1 NOTE From BBB:
2 CONT Henrietta ii was with her husband, Samuel Davis, District Judge at Ben
2 CONC ares when a rebellion broke out.   Its leader, Vizier Ali, who had jus
2 CONC t killed three Europeans, was heading for the Davis's house with a ban
2 CONC d of rebels and they shot down a sentry just as Samuel was returning f
2 CONC rom a morning ride on an elephant.   He saw there was no time to lose,
2 CONC  and calling to Henrietta to take the two children, aged four and one,
2 CONC  up to the flat roof of the house, he started to go into the house and
2 CONC  fetch his firearms.   But an armed rebel was already in the doorway a
2 CONC nd Samuel had to think again.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	He remembered that he had a pike upstairs, such as was used by runnin
2 CONC g footmen in India, made of iron and plated with silver to give a bett
2 CONC er grip, more than six feet long and with a big and very sharp triangu
2 CONC lar blade 20 inches in length.    He reckoned that with this he could
2 CONC defend the narrow staircase going up to the roof, ran up the stairs an
2 CONC d quickly saw that the height of the parapet around was very low and e
2 CONC xposed them all to the expected enemy fire.    He made Henrietta and t
2 CONC he ayahs and the children crouch down in the middle of the roof, and p
2 CONC osted himself with his pike by the hatchway at the top of the stairs.
2 CONC   This stairway was of considerable height, and very narrow, permittin
2 CONC g no more than one man at a time to come up, as it was spiral.    He p
2 CONC icked off two who came up, who withdrew wounded, while firing had star
2 CONC ted inside and outside the house.   However, the delay caused by Samue
2 CONC l's one-man defensive effort was vital, and cavalry arrived in the nic
2 CONC k of time from some miles away, forcing the rebels to retire, some two
2 CONC  hundred in number.    (What became of the elephant, one wonders.  We
2 CONC shall never know).
