1 NOTE Emily's memoir p81-82:
2 CONT [1876] "In October we took a furnished house in Strathmore Gardens a f
2 CONC ew doors off my dearest Mother, who had made a home there for herself
2 CONC and At.Mary and fitted it up with Tattingstone furniture.  There was a
2 CONC  good deal of vigour about the dear Mother's mind and manner, though s
2 CONC he was much crippled with rheumatisms and gout in her eyelids made her
2 CONC  almost blind.  Many of her watercolour and pencil sketches are known
2 CONC to you all but her clever hands could no longer paint or work.  Yet sh
2 CONC e never allowed herself to be dull - because she had a wonderful gift
2 CONC for throwing herself into the interests of others and of living in the
2 CONC ir lives; and of picturing their friends and surroundings which she ha
2 CONC d never seen, nor would see. This true unselfishness made her greatly
2 CONC beloved by all who knew her. Outwardly she might have been thought a w
2 CONC reck of a lovely, and in some ways a brilliant woman - she certainly w
2 CONC as this last in conversation, and in linguistic talent; and in the rap
2 CONC idity with which she read & mastered books so long as her eyesight las
2 CONC ted; as well as in her remarkable memory, for we often tried in vain t
2 CONC o puzzle her with a question in history.  But spiritually and intellec
2 CONC tually there were no signs of age about her.  She liked to keep up wit
2 CONC h all the best books of the day - and was read aloud to much by At.Mar
2 CONC y and by all of us in turn.  She greatly enjoyed music, and when the C
2 CONC anes were staying with her, and we had the help of Uncle J's sweet ten
2 CONC or voice, we managed a good deal together. Her powers of learning by h
2 CONC eart too were first rate - so that within a week of her death she and
2 CONC At.Mary used to see who could learn a chapter in the Epistles first! M
2 CONC other drove out now in a brougham most days, and I was often her compa
2 CONC nion, not being able for much walking.
2 CONT We had a bright Christmas with her, and our children delighted her wit
2 CONC h carols.  And then - a slight chill taken brought on bronchitis, and
2 CONC after only three days in bed, God took her Home, on Jany.3rd 1877.  It
2 CONC  was a crushing and sudden blow to us all; for there seemed no reason
2 CONC why she should not have enjoyed some years of love, joy and peace surr
2 CONC ounded by her children, and their children.  But it was all gain for h
2 CONC er to go hence - and she smiled when we reminded her shortly before th
2 CONC e end that her Arthur (24) had said 'I will be at the Gate to welcome
2 CONC you, Mother'. We laid her in the vault at Tattingstone, where our Fath
2 CONC er, and Grandparents, and Uncle William Elliott (14) all lie, and with
2 CONC  her 'passing' the Chapter of my early life seems to close."
2 CONT
2 CONT In Rev. John Barton's Family Tree book, p7,23- giving middle name and
2 CONC dates, including d.o.b. 28/01/1806, but there is a problem with this (
2 CONC see below).
2 CONT
2 CONT At http://genforum.genealogy.com/walkden/messages/40.html there is men
2 CONC tion of a Mary Stephen Dougan, daughter of John Dougan and Clarissa Sq
2 CONC uire. She was christened on 18 May 1806, Holy Trinity, Clapham, Surrey
2 CONC , England. Then married to Richard Walkden (his second marriage) on 17
2 CONC  July 1833 in Old Church, St Pancras. London, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Might she and Emily be Twins? Seems the only possibility, unless her 1
2 CONC 806 d.o.b. is wrong. This may be the case:
2 CONT
2 CONT At http://holmesacourt.org/d4/i0009529.htm#i9530 she is said to have b
2 CONC een born in 1811, which would settle it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Except that at http://www.familysearch.org/eng/search/IGI/individual_r
2 CONC ecord.asp?recid=500152194072&lds=1&region=2&frompage=99 her birth and
2 CONC christening are recorded as 28 Jan 1806, and 19 Mar 1806 (at Holy Trin
2 CONC ity, Clapham, Surrey).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Stephen Elliott, at
2 CONT http://www.familysearch.org/eng/search/IGI/individual_record.asp?recid
2 CONC =500152192623&lds=1&region=2&frompage=99 is born 21 Jul 1804, and chri
2 CONC stened at the same time (19 Mar 1806 at Holy Trinity, Clapham, Surrey)
2 CONC .
2 CONT
