1 NOTE Called Ama. Chadwick’s book has a whole chapter on her, her wedding to
2 CONC  William Gurney, and her relationships with her siblings and parents.
2 CONC One of her children died suddenly, very young, in January 1854.  I’m n
2 CONC ot sure which one, as Chadwick doesn’t say, but presumably her first.
2 CONC She was originally going to marry Owen Stanley, apparently, but he die
2 CONC d in Sydney, while in the Navy.
2 CONT
2 CONT From BBB:
2 CONT “The eldest child, Anna Maria, always known as Ama, was robbed of the
2 CONC happiness she might have had.   She was a plain little girl, of an aff
2 CONC ectionate, unselfish mature, and very religious.   She was close frien
2 CONC ds with the Stanley girls, daughters of the Bishop of Norwich, and fav
2 CONC ourite even with their critical family.   In their home she met and fe
2 CONC ll in love with the son, Owen Stanley, an officer in the Royal Navy, w
2 CONC ho was  to gain a measure of fame as the surveyor of the coasts of nor
2 CONC th-eastern Australia, and of New Guinea, and to give his name to the c
2 CONC entral ridge of the latter island.   After a time he learnt to love he
2 CONC r too, but he had not at that time very good prospects.  Ama, at 19 wa
2 CONC s not considered to be sufficiently experienced to know her own mind,
2 CONC and their respective mammas interfered effectually to stop the romance
2 CONC .   The lovers were very miserable and embarrassed because neither kne
2 CONC w how the other felt about it.   There was an indirect line of communi
2 CONC cation through one of his sisters.  At last he seized an opportunity t
2 CONC o declare himself, and she accepted him.   A formal engagement was not
2 CONC  however permitted.   Soon after, he was commissioned to the command o
2 CONC f HMS 'Rattlesnake', to carry out the survey which made his name.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Ama was not allowed to write to him, or to receive his letters, but t
2 CONC he obliging sister kept them in touch.   He had been away for four yea
2 CONC rs, and was about to return home, when he died in Sydney.   Two years
2 CONC later Ama married a clergyman of a Norfolk county family, who was well
2 CONC -connected and rich enough to be considered as a suitable match for he
2 CONC r, despite the fact that he was lame  and squinted.    No doubt she ma
2 CONC de him a good wife.   They had ten children.”
