1 NOTE Chadwick’s book has a great deal on Caroline. She came under the influ
2 CONC ence of the vicar who so argued with her father, and became excessivel
2 CONC y religious. I have to say, given her behavior, her obsession with dea
2 CONC th, and how she ended up living, you’d have to conclude she was a bit
2 CONC of a nutter. After her father died, she became a travelling preacher,
2 CONC zipping around the country preaching to drunks and prostitutes, holdin
2 CONC g prayer meetings in streets and halls, spending long hours in solo pr
2 CONC ayer.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died at the age of 48, in Bryanstone House in Harrowgate, after a
2 CONC long illness, and from the effects of an operation.
2 CONT
2 CONT When she was younger, her two younger sisters, Mary and Theresa, were
2 CONC also heavily influenced by the vicar, and became excessively religious
2 CONC , but they didn’t follow her so far down the path of weirdosity.
2 CONT
2 CONT As BBB puts it:
2 CONT “After their father's death in 1869 his three unmarried daughters were
2 CONC  left to their own devices.   The two younger lived quietly at Torquay
2 CONC , but Caroline, free at last, spread her wings.   At first she had the
2 CONC  idea of being a nurse, but a few years later she had become a Methodi
2 CONC st preacher, travelling round the country.”
