1 NOTE After his elder brother died, he inherited the estate. He was rather w
2 CONC ild when young, apparently, but settled down in later life.
2 CONT
2 CONT From BBB:
2 CONT “Educ at Eton, where he won the Prince Consort's prizes for French and
2 CONC  Italian, and at Christ Church, Oxford;  BA.   Barrister-at-law; admit
2 CONC ted Lincoln's Inn 1852.   Succeeded his father as 2nd Baronet 1869.  H
2 CONC e was Deputy Lieutenant and County Alderman for the county of Norfolk,
2 CONC  JP, Hon Lieut Col 3rd Norfolk Volunteer Rifles and received the Volun
2 CONC teer Officers' Decoration, awarded for twenty years service.   Knight
2 CONC of the Order of St John of Jerusalem.  Fellow of the Society of Antiqu
2 CONC aries : Fellow of the Society of Literature.
2 CONT
2 CONT He owned about 4000 acres in Norfolk, including the estates of Ketteri
2 CONC ngham, Tacolnestone and Hethel Hall. m 1869 at St George's, Hanover Sq
2 CONC uare, London, Lucy Henrietta, dau of Sir Henry Nugent, Bart., and had
2 CONC issue : Arthur John Vernon, John Francis Elliott, Maurice Colborne, Ma
2 CONC rgaret Lucy Augusta and Raymond Frederic.   He d 1900, and was succeed
2 CONC ed as 3rd Baronet by his eldest surviving , Maurice Colborne.”
2 CONT
2 CONT His children are not listed in BBC, which is too early I suppose. Funn
2 CONC ily, the date of the wedding given in BBC, although difficult to read,
2 CONC  is clearly NOT 1869, or even close. Looks more like 1830. Maybe the c
2 CONC hart author got muddled with his date of birth.
