1 NOTE The bad apple, according to Chadwick. Disappeared off to Australia at
2 CONC a relatively young age, and was not seen by the family again.
2 CONT
2 CONT BBB says:
2 CONT “b 1831, probably at Thursford Hall, Norfolk.   Joined the Royal Navy
2 CONC as a cadet in 1846, and served in the frigate 'Raleigh' in the Western
2 CONC  Approaches, and from 1846 to 1850 was on the South American station.
2 CONC   He gave his father a great deal of trouble with his wild ways, runni
2 CONC ng into debt and drinking heavily.    He was only kept on in the Navy
2 CONC through his father's influence and string-pulling, but after failing h
2 CONC is professional examinations he was finally discharged in 1854.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father tried in various ways to reform him, finally sending him to
2 CONC  Australia to sink or swim with an allowance of £5 a month as long as
2 CONC he stayed there.   He never returned home.   His occasional letters ge
2 CONC nerally indicated more trouble, but in the end he seems to have made g
2 CONC ood.     He was drowned in Corlo Bay, Victoria, in 1883.
2 CONT
2 CONT He m 1870, Bridget, dau of James Walsh, and had issue :  Francis James
2 CONC , Noel Gilbert Elliott, John Peter viii, Edmund Charles and Catherine
2 CONC Theresa.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Again from BBB:
2 CONT “Edmund joined the Royal Navy as a cadet at the age of 15, but was in
2 CONC constant trouble, ran into debt, failed to pass the required examinati
2 CONC ons and was only retained for eight years through his father using wha
2 CONC tever influence he had.    When that came to an end Edmund was sent ou
2 CONC t to Australia to make his own way on a small allowance.   He never re
2 CONC turned home.   He is heard of from time to time in his father's diary,
2 CONC  generally as in some sort of trouble not stated, for the next fourtee
2 CONC n years.   Then he married, and his descendants in the second (now Aus
2 CONC tralian) branch of the family have prospered, and the baronetcy has pa
2 CONC ssed to them.”
