1 NOTE From BBB:
2 CONT George Boileau-Pollen died in 1847 and was succeeded as Lord of the Ma
2 CONC nor, for what that was worth, but not as Rector of Little Bookham, by
2 CONC his son John Douglas.   The latter was of an unbalanced temperament an
2 CONC d had been giving trouble at Oxford.   His uncle, Sir John, was well a
2 CONC ware of this, and yet we find him recommending his nephew to get himse
2 CONC lf ordained as soon as possible, so that he would have the living as a
2 CONC  means of supporting himself, his mother and sisters.   However, nothi
2 CONC ng came of it which was, no doubt, just as well for the Parish, but th
2 CONC e fact does give another sidelight on the curious notions regarding th
2 CONC e clergy at that time.
2 CONT 	John Douglas never married, and his brother and sisters had no childr
2 CONC en.   So, when he died in 1906, the interest in the Pollen estate pass
2 CONC ed by way of Charles Lestock i _, as next younger son of John Peter i
2 CONC and Henrietta, to Henry Davis Willock (husband of Mary Elizabeth i), w
2 CONC ho then became Lord of the Manor, and took the additional name of Poll
2 CONC en.
