1 NOTE According to BBB:
2 CONT “b at Alcester, Warwicks, 1800.   Commissioned 2 Lt 95th Foot (Rifle B
2 CONC de) 1814 but is shown on returns as 'on leave at the Military College'
2 CONC  (Sandhurst) until his 16th birthday.  Lt 1821;  Capt 1824;  Major 183
2 CONC 5.   No active service.  Served in India and in 1833 was extra ADC to
2 CONC the Governor of Madras Presidency, Lt Gen Sir Frederick Adam, whose on
2 CONC ly dau Amelia he m in Madras in 1833.  She d at Bangalore the same yea
2 CONC r, aged 21.
2 CONT
2 CONT He m 2nd, in 1836 Margaret, dau of William Stirling and widow of Claud
2 CONC ius Kerr, EIC and they had a dau Mary Elizabeth iii.  In 1845 this mar
2 CONC riage was dissolved (there was a court case in 1844, in which Charles
2 CONC accused Margaret of adultery), and in 1848 he m 3rd, Maria, dau of Edw
2 CONC ard Denham.   There was no issue of this marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT He d at Castelnau, Barnes in 1889, and was buried at Little Bookham, S
2 CONC urrey.  (See also Section 'Castelnau - Barnes').”
2 CONT
2 CONT Apparently, the third wife was his daughter’s former governess, and th
2 CONC e rest of the family did NOT approve. Charles went into real estate he
2 CONC avily, and constructed a big sub-development (as it would now be calle
2 CONC d, I suppose) in the northern part of the borough of Barnes. The names
2 CONC  are typical; Castelnau Villas, Castelnau Row, Castelnau Place, and Go
2 CONC thic Cottages. Yuk. In that area there is still a Boileau Road, and ro
2 CONC ad was renamed Castelnau. More details in BBB
