1 NOTE From Wikipedia:
2 CONT Percy Ellen Algernon Frederick William Sydney Smythe, 8th Viscount Str
2 CONC angford (November 26, 1826–January 9, 1869) was a British nobleman and
2 CONC  man of letters.
2 CONT He was born in St Petersburg, Russia, the son of the 6th Viscount Stra
2 CONC ngford, the British Ambassador. During all his earlier years Percy Smy
2 CONC the was nearly blind, in consequence, it was believed, of his mother h
2 CONC aving suffered very great hardships on a journey up the Baltic Sea in
2 CONC wintry weather shortly before his birth. His education was begun at Ha
2 CONC rrow, whence he went to Merton College, Oxford. He excelled as a lingu
2 CONC ist, and was nominated by the vice-chancellor of Oxford in 1845 a stud
2 CONC ent-attache at Constantinople.
2 CONT While at Constantinople, where he served under Lord Stratford de Redcl
2 CONC iffe, Smythe gained a mastery not only of Turkish and its dialects, bu
2 CONC t of almost every form of modern Greek, from the language of the liter
2 CONC ati of Athens to the least Hellenized Romaic. He had already a large k
2 CONC nowledge both of Persian and Arabic before going east, but until his d
2 CONC uties led him to study the past, present and future of the sultan's em
2 CONC pire he had given no attention to the tongues which he well described
2 CONC as those of the international rabble in and around the Balkan peninsul
2 CONC a.
2 CONT On succeeding his brother as Viscount Strangford in 1857 he continued
2 CONC to live in Constantinople, immersed in cultural studies. At length, ho
2 CONC wever, he returned to England and wrote a good deal, sometimes in the
2 CONC Saturday Review, sometimes in the Quarterly Review, and much in the Pa
2 CONC ll Mall Gazette. A rather severe review in the first of these organs o
2 CONC f the Egyptian Sepulchres and Syrian Shrines of Emily Anne Beaufort (1
2 CONC 887) led to a result not very usual the marriage of the reviewer and o
2 CONC f the authoress. One of the most interesting papers Lord Strangford ev
2 CONC er wrote was the last chapter in his wife's book on the Eastern Shores
2 CONC  of the Adriatic. That chapter was entitled "Chaos," and was the first
2 CONC  of his writings which made him widely known amongst careful students
2 CONC of foreign politics. From that time forward everything that he wrote w
2 CONC as watched with intense interest, and even when it waa anonymous there
2 CONC  was not the slightest difficulty in recognizing his style, for it was
2 CONC  unlike any other.
2 CONT On his death in 1869 his titles became extinct. A Selection from the W
2 CONC ritings of Viscount Strangford on Political, Geographical and Social S
2 CONC ubjects was edited by his widow and published in 1869. His Original Le
2 CONC tters and Papers upon Philology and Kindred Subjects were also edited
2 CONC by Lady Strangford (1878).
