1 NAME Fanny Elizabeth /Knyvett/
2 DISPLAY Fanny Elizabeth Knyvett
2 GIVN Fanny Elizabeth
2 SURN Knyvett
1 SOURCES @S74@
1 POSITION -512,206
2 GENOMAP John Theophilus (i) Boileau's descendants
2 BOUNDARYRECT -541,232,-482,145
1 SEX F
1 DEAT Y
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE From BBB:
2 CONT
2 CONT After the defeat at Chhinhut, Sir Henry Lawrence accepted the necessit
2 CONC y of a siege, and he fortified the Residency at Lucknow and brought in
2 CONC to the defended area all the Europeans, men, women and children.  Of t
2 CONC he Boileau family these included Thomas Theo _, his wife Jessie and th
2 CONC eir three children, and Fanny, wife of George Wilson ii _, and her fou
2 CONC r children.
2 CONT
2 CONT 		On arriving at the Residency, Fanny and the rest of the party were p
2 CONC ut into a large room with a bare floor and no furniture.  She was in d
2 CONC espair until a friend, Mrs Fayrer, wife of a doctor attached to the Re
2 CONC sidency,came and invited her to their home, known as Fayrer's house, a
2 CONC  large one with a flat roof protected by sandbags all round, and defen
2 CONC ded by sepoy pensioners.   It was much exposed to enemy fire and there
2 CONC  was an underground room, called a tykhana, which served as a shelter
2 CONC for those living in the house.   However, it is interesting to observe
2 CONC  in Fanny's diary the gradual change from alarm to indifference at any
2 CONC  firing.   By the 17th July she wrote: 'Four rounds shot, the first of
2 CONC  a series, into the ladies' room next to mine', without further commen
2 CONC t.  On the 20th July there were eight rounds shot into the house.  On
2 CONC the 16th August, 'an eight-inch shell burst in the verandah of the roo
2 CONC m where Mrs Fayrer was lying ill.  I was bathing her.  The shell burst
2 CONC  within three yards of me, and had I not thrown myself flat on the bed
2 CONC  I must have been killed.'   From that date to the 26th she records th
2 CONC at no event of any importance occurred, the firing continuing as usual
2 CONC , sometimes more, sometimes less.   'Third rations today, children hal
2 CONC f.'   On 1st September her youngest child, Georgina (Ina) was sick wit
2 CONC h ulcerated sores and diarrhoea.    She lingered on wretchedly until t
2 CONC he 13th, when she died, about three months old, and was buried in the
2 CONC Residency Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT 		Fanny's other children kept well and in good spirits - indeed four-y
2 CONC ears old George was rather a mischievous nuisance.   Anna was highly p
2 CONC leased with a little writing desk her mother gave her for her seventh
2 CONC birthday present.  On the 25th September General Outram entered the Re
2 CONC sidency with a relief force. but it was not strong enough to raise the
2 CONC  siege.  The General took up quarters in Fayrer's house, and Fanny say
2 CONC s the tea-table was soon beset by officers and ourselves, asking and a
2 CONC nswering questions, one of the first being whether Queen Victoria 'was
2 CONC  still alive or not'.   Next day she says that 'in one of the sorties
2 CONC today two fowls and a pigeon were brought in as loot and hailed with g
2 CONC reat joy'.
2 CONT
2 CONT 		On October 9th 'news reached of the fall of Delhi, 6000 men making L
2 CONC ucknow-wards'.    November 12th.   The relief force, under Sir Colin C
2 CONC ampbell, had reached the neighbourhood of Lucknow.   Some sort of sign
2 CONC alling apparatus had been arranged, which Fanny called the 'electric t
2 CONC elegraph' but which seems to have been a semaphore for communication w
2 CONC ith the relief force.   In a real sporting spirit she says that the en
2 CONC emy made two splendid shots at our telegraph on the Residency, one str
2 CONC iking the tower within a yard of the post, the other bursting directly
2 CONC  over the tower.
2 CONT
2 CONT 		The second relief force came in on the 18th November and Sir Colin o
2 CONC rdered the immediate evacuation of the non-combatants, to begin next m
2 CONC orning.   But strangely there were no arrangements made for the transp
2 CONC ort of the women and children.   Fanny was anticipating an 8 mile walk
2 CONC  with her children in a burning sun but was lucky enough to meet a fri
2 CONC end of George's who found her a carriage and a horse, and she drove of
2 CONC f.   At the General's headquarters they all enjoyed their first proper
2 CONC  breakfast for months, to the children's intense delight - fresh bread
2 CONC  and butter, tea with milk and sugar, cold beef.   They went on in dho
2 CONC olies at 10 pm, reaching the Dilkhoosha at 1 am.   Here she wandered a
2 CONC bout for at least two hours hopelessly looking for accommodation, unti
2 CONC l at last an officer found her and her children a place in someone's t
2 CONC ent.
2 CONT
2 CONT 		After five days there they all left at noon and a fearful march ensu
2 CONC ed - the greatest confusion - ladies' carriages, bazaar hackberries an
2 CONC d lame camels, all in one moving mass.
2 CONT
2 CONT 		They reached Alumbagh at 7 pm and here, once again, Fanny was in luc
2 CONC k, for Captain Roberts (afterwards Field Marshal Lord Roberts of Kanda
2 CONC har) came to her rescue and put them in his own tent, himself turning
2 CONC out of it.   In the next few days they moved to Cawnpore.  The enemy w
2 CONC ere in the vicinity and the refugees were near enough to come under ar
2 CONC tillery fire.   Roberts took Fanny along with the artillery and cleare
2 CONC d the way for her, so that she accomplished the journey in half an hou
2 CONC r, while the other ladies took eight hours.
2 CONT
2 CONT 		This was the end of her trials.   The expected child, Charles iv, wa
2 CONC s born seven weeks later, but died in September 1859, before he was tw
2 CONC o years old.
