1 NOTE From BBB:
2 CONT John George i, only survivor of the eight sons of John Theophilus ii _
2 CONC , had started in it [his father’s druggist business] as a young man, b
2 CONC ut in 1850, owing to a disagreement with his uncles, he left and set u
2 CONC p for himself.   He became associated with a Mr Boyd, in a similar lin
2 CONC e of business, and when the Boileau firm came up on the market the two
2 CONC  associates bought it and continued as 'Boileau & Boyd', which name it
2 CONC  has kept ever since.  John became managing director, a position in wh
2 CONC ich he was a decided success, enjoying general esteem and goodwill.
2 CONC When he died in 1883 Mr Boyd continued as sole proprietor, although Ed
2 CONC ward John ii, John's son, was possibly employed there for a short time
2 CONC .   In any event, the latter's early death caused the end of the direc
2 CONC t connections of the family with it, after a hundred years, although t
2 CONC he Boileau part in its history is till remembered, more than just in t
2 CONC he name.   There was, however, still an indirect connection in that fo
2 CONC r many years the firm had business dealings with a chemist in Dublin,
2 CONC Charles Digges, whose wife Kathleen was Edward's daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT b In Dublin 15 Nov 1825.  Sometime head of the firm of wholesale drugg
2 CONC ists in Dublin then called 'Boileau' and later 'Boileau and Boyd'.  JP
2 CONC .  M 26 Apr 1853 at St George's Hanover Sq, Ruthana, dau of John Hunte
2 CONC r MD   They had issue : Edward John ii, Caroline Frances, Mary, Thomas
2 CONC ina, Jessy Elizabeth and John Hunter Brandram  d 1883.
