3 _XREF @place01012@
2 SOURCE @S89@
1 DEAT Y
2 DATE 1778
2 PLAC Montreal, Canada
3 _XREF @place01013@
2 SOURCE @S89@
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE To quote from :
2 CONT -----------
2 CONT Although the Jessups had lived in Connecticut for several generations,
2 CONC  Joseph Jessup moved his family to the “Upper Nine Partners Patent” in
2 CONC  Dutchess County, N.Y., in 1744.
2 CONT ------
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From [JB]:
2 CONT --------
2 CONT The career of Joseph Jessup, of Stamford, Conn., and his descendants i
2 CONC s in striking contrast with that of his brothers, Edward and Jonathan.
2 CONC  He was the first and only member of his father’s family to seek a hom
2 CONC e beyond the bounds of New England, and neither he nor his children co
2 CONC uld have anticipated the results which were to follow from this remova
2 CONC l. That both he and his sons were men of more than ordinary business a
2 CONC bility cannot be doubted. ...
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of his wife in 1743, he emigrated with his three sons,
2 CONC  Edward, Joseph Jr, and Ebenezer, to Dutchess County, in the adjoining
2 CONC  colony of New York, - a distance of less than one hundred miles, and
2 CONC yet so far that his mother in her will appears to think it possible he
2 CONC  might “not return alive”. ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Here begin those transactions in real esteate, both on their own accou
2 CONC nt and as agents for other parties, which rapidly increased in number
2 CONC and importance during the next fifteen years, and whichled them eventu
2 CONC ally to locate at Jessup’s Landing near the head-waters of the Hudson
2 CONC River.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sons, Edward and Ebenezer especially, were the friends of Sir Wm.
2 CONC Johnson, the British Superintendent of Indian Affairs in New York, and
2 CONC  one of the most remakrable and influential men in that colony. ... Th
2 CONC e Jessup brothers were also in various ways associated with many of th
2 CONC e English Colonial officials, - more especially Governor Dunmore and G
2 CONC en. Wm. Tryon, the last of the royal governors, to whom they were inde
2 CONC bted for many favors in securing for themselves and for their associat
2 CONC es grants of large tracts of land obtained either directly from the go
2 CONC vernment or indirectly by purchase from the Indians. They were shrewd
2 CONC and successful men of business, inheriting the financial talents of th
2 CONC e father, and ready to take hold of any adventurous scheme which might
2 CONC  aid them in bettering their fortunes.
2 CONT
2 CONT ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Jessup was born in Fairfield, Conn. (parish of Green’s Farms),
2 CONC in 1699 (baptized 4 July). He settled quite early in Stamford, - not l
2 CONC ater than 17 Dec., 1723, when he received from his father, then living
2 CONC  in Stamford, a deed of land of the value of £199.  ... Joseph Jessup
2 CONC went to Montreal, Canada, at the opening of the Revolution, and died t
2 CONC here in 1778, aged seventy-nine.
2 CONT --------
2 CONT
2 CONT As a side note, it seems the process for stealing Indian land was for
2 CONC “the purchaser ... to be at the expense of first vesting the Indian ri
2 CONC ght and title in the Crown before he could obtain the patent which alo
2 CONC ne could guarantee him in possession of his purchase. The fees exacted
2 CONC  were often very considerable.” In other words, the brothers had to pa
2 CONC y large bribes to officialdom before they were allowed to steal the In
2 CONC dians’ lands. What a couple of rogues. Still, perhaps they were no wor
2 CONC se than anybody else of their time.
