2 DATE 1732
2 PLAC Stamford, Connecticut
3 _XREF @place00945@
2 SOURCE @S89@
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE From [JB]:
2 CONT -------------
2 CONT Edward Jessup was born in 1663 in either Newtown or West Farms, N.Y.,
2 CONC either just before or soon after his father had crossed the East River
2 CONC  to his new plantation on the north shore of Long Island Sound. He was
2 CONC  but three years old at his father’s death, and removing thus early wi
2 CONC th his mother to Connecticut on her second marriage, he was more a cit
2 CONC izen of the latter colony than of New York. In due time he became a fr
2 CONC eeman of the town of Fairfield, acquired property, and established a h
2 CONC ome and family in the ancient parish of Green’s Farms. ...
2 CONT
2 CONT About 1720 Edward removed from Green’s Farms to Stamford, and lived th
2 CONC ere the rest of his life. ... His younger children went with him to St
2 CONC amford, six of them in fact, which accounts for their settling in that
2 CONC  part of Fairfield county, or emigrating still farther west into the c
2 CONC olony of New York. ...
2 CONT
2 CONT The Stamford Records say: “Mr Edward Jessup died Dec. 28, 1732”, and t
2 CONC he inscription on his toubstone adds “in the 70th year of his age”. Hi
2 CONC s will, as recorded in Stamford, was dated Aug., 17, 1731, provedn by
2 CONC witnesses in Norwalk, Jan 5, 1732-33, and probatd at Stamford February
2 CONC  5th of the same year. His son, Joseph Jessup of Stamford, is appointe
2 CONC d sole executor. The inventory was about £650.
2 CONT -----------
2 CONT
2 CONT [JB] gives the text of Edward’s will.
2 CONT
2 CONT [JB] also relates how Edward was involved in a curious incident in Sep
2 CONC t, 1692, when he (aged 29) gave witness against a supposed witch, sayi
2 CONC ng how food on her table had changed its appearance so unaccountably t
2 CONC hat he was afraid to eat it; that he couldn’t read from the woman’s Bi
2 CONC ble, that he couldn’t control his horse afterwards, etc. Sounds to me
2 CONC like he was just a drunken sot. Anyway, the woman was convicted but no
2 CONC t punished, apparently.
