1 NOTE No good reference for the marriage date, only a web page. Seems to be
2 CONC a good one, though.
2 CONT http://www.dibblehistory.org/history2.htm.
2 CONT
2 CONT From that same web page:
2 CONT -------
2 CONT Regardless of who his parents were, Jonathan/Jonathon was known as Lie
2 CONC utenant Dibble. He married Sarah Jessup on November 11, 1736. (Sarah's
2 CONC  parents were Edward Jessup and Elizabeth Hyde.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children were:
2 CONT Sarah Dibble, born October 11, 1737. (She later married Jacob Valentin
2 CONC e.) Deborah Dibble, born June 26, 1739, in Stamford. (She married Jame
2 CONC s Varian, and died on April 12, 1823.) George Dibble, born December 2,
2 CONC  1740, in Stamford. Abigail Dibble, born March 31, 1743, in Stamford.
2 CONC (She later married Edward Jessup.) Elizabeth Dibble, born April 25, 17
2 CONC 45 in Stamford. (She married Ebenezer Jessup.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lieutenant Dibble owned land on the west side of Chatham Square in New
2 CONC  York City and "held a lease of the premises in the Bowery, known as t
2 CONC he 'Bull's Head Tavern.'" He also owned land on the Hudson River in Wa
2 CONC llkill, near Poughkeepsie, NY. Jesup reports that, "In consequence of
2 CONC the small-pox becoming epidemic, probably about 1760," he sold his New
2 CONC  York City holdings and bought a large farm that straddled the NY-CT l
2 CONC ine, with parts in Bedford, NY and parts in Stamford, CT. His official
2 CONC  residence seems nearly always to have been in Stamford, as four of hi
2 CONC s five children are recorded as being born there, including his only s
2 CONC on George (citation: "Edward Jessup Geneology" via Bernhard). However,
2 CONC  Reverend Jesup says that George, from whom our line is descended, was
2 CONC  actually born in the Bull's Head Tavern.
2 CONT ---------
