1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE His wife was Jean Peddie, and his son-in-law was W.A.J. Dutch, for wha
2 CONC t that’s worth.
2 CONT
2 CONT At some stage he lived in MacLaggan St., Dunedin, as his sister Janet
2 CONC was married from there in 1861. This is very likely the James Peddie w
2 CONC ho appears in the Dunedin Electoral roll of 1865, and possibly the one
2 CONC  who was married in Knox Church on the 31st of December, 1866, to a Mu
2 CONC rray.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the recipient of a Crown grant for land, as on the 5th of May,
2 CONC 1864, he is listed as someone who hasn’t picked up their grant yet, an
2 CONC d please hurry up and do it.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was still living in Dunedin when his daughter, Annie, married in 19
2 CONC 01. Clearly, he later moved up to Auckland to be with her, as he died
2 CONC at his son-in-law’s house in Auckland.
