1 NOTE Of Appledore. Born around 1380 according to the Curteis pedigree writt
2 CONC en in about 1612, and now in the National Archives.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other early Curteises:
2 CONT On Aug 4th, 1361, Edward, the Black Prince, founded 2 Chantries in the
2 CONC  Under Croft of Canterbury Cathedral & named a John Curteys as one of
2 CONC the first Chantry priests. In a manor Court roll of Brookland of the y
2 CONC ear 1397 to 1401 the name of John Curthose appears.
2 CONT 	During the reign of Henry 6th (1422-1461), William Curteis was Abbot
2 CONC of Bury St. Edmonds, the campanile or bell tower of which he repaired.
2 CONC  In 1433 he entertained Henry VI, the Duke of Gloucester, and the Cour
2 CONC t, for several months at his abbey. He died in 1445 (vide Dugdale’s Mo
2 CONC nasticon). His arms appear to be the same as those borne by the Curtei
2 CONC s family to this day.
2 CONT 	A family of Curtehose or Curteys held lands in or near "The Manor", K
2 CONC ent, from early in the reign of Edward I.
2 CONT 	In the Diocesan Probate Office in Canterbury is the Will of Stephen C
2 CONC urteys of Brookland dated Feb 8th 1461, the 1st year of Edward 4th's r
2 CONC eign. In the roof of Canterbury Cathedral Cloisters are the Curteis ar
2 CONC ms several times repeated. The compartments in which they are (numbere
2 CONC d from the Martyrdom westwards) are 23, 26, 445, 446, 455, 536, 537. A
2 CONC ll the subscribers to  rebuilding  the [?] were apparently commemorate
2 CONC d by  their arms being introduced as ornaments. The Cloisters were bui
2 CONC lt between 1390 and 1411. The Curteis arms were also placed in windows
2 CONC  in the Churches of Chilham and New Nomrey [?] about  the same time, &
2 CONC  have always  been considered to  be those of Stephen Curteys of Apple
2 CONC dore & Brookhaven.
