1 NOTE Seigneur de Lançon, chamberlain of the duc de Calabre.
2 CONT
2 CONT This Jean is the first entry in [CD]. Established at Marseilles in the
2 CONC  reign of Rene d’Anjou, King of Sicily, Comte de Provence. He was Coun
2 CONC cillor and Maitre d’Hotel of this prince, then chamberlain of the duc
2 CONC de Calabre, the son of Rene d’Anjou. King Rene appointed him as Admira
2 CONC l, as seen in letters dated the 8th of January, 1453. It was in this c
2 CONC apacity that Moro, the Doge of Venice, wrote to him on the 19th of Mar
2 CONC ch, 1463, asking him to give a safe-conduct to the galleys and vessels
2 CONC  of the Republic, who needed to go to Aigues-Mortes to get (or drop of
2 CONC f) trade goods. Jean was alive when Provence became part of the French
2 CONC  crown in 1481 [no he wasn’t. I guess I’m not translating this correct
2 CONC ly], by the will of Charles du Maine, the last Comte de Provence. Loui
2 CONC s XI, king of France, became the Comte de Provence, and employed Jean
2 CONC in grand and important affairs, at which he acquitted himself with wis
2 CONC dom and great success, dying in 1477.
