2 DATE 30 DEC 1822
1 DEAT Y
2 DATE 22 JUN 1897
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE of Edmond Castle, Cumberland, and of The Elms, Eastbourne, Sussex. J.P
2 CONC . and D.L. for Sussex, and J.P. Cumberland. Lineage in Burke’s Landed
2 CONC Gentry. Educated at Harrow and Trin. Coll. Camb. M.A. Inherited the ti
2 CONC tle from his uncle, who had no issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT The monument in Hayton Church of Reginald John Graham shows a beautifu
2 CONC l rendition of a joint coat-of-arms, with the left half the Graham thr
2 CONC ee escallops in chief, and on the left the three towers and crescent o
2 CONC f the Boileau de Castelnau. I reproduce the photo in my wee piece on t
2 CONC he Grahams. But I’m puzzled by the use of the three escallops in chief
2 CONC  like this. That’s just the standard Graham arms and didn’t (I thought
2 CONC ) apply to the Grahams of Edmond Castle. Puzzling.
2 CONT
2 CONT Personally, I suspect that the “official” arms of the Grahams of Edmon
2 CONC d Castle (in Burke’s Landed Gentry) were invented; possibly copied dir
2 CONC ectly from the arms of the Baronetcy which lasted only a few generatio
2 CONC ns in a side branch before dying out (according to Burke the arms of t
2 CONC he Baronetcy were identical to those of the Grahams of Edmond Castle).
2 CONC  Thus, I suspect that the only arms ever used by the non-Baronet line
2 CONC were the usual three scallops. There is no evidence that any other arm
2 CONC s were ever used. However, the tower as a crest was used as more speci
2 CONC fic identification; I have an old silver christening mug from the Grah
2 CONC ams of Edmond Castle, and this has the tower on it. I also have an old
2 CONC  gold signet ring from them, with the tower on it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beneath the arms [MMC] reads:
2 CONT “In loving memory of Reginald John Graham of Edmond Castle, in this pa
2 CONC rish, and of The Elms, Eastbourne, Sussex, a Justice of the Peace for
2 CONC the County of Cumberland, and a Justice of the Peace and Deputy Lieute
2 CONC nant for the County of Sussex, who died June 22nd, 1897, aged 74 years
2 CONC .
2 CONT And Ellen Leah, his wife, daughter of Thomas E.J. Boileau, A Judge in
2 CONC the Honourable East India Company’s Service at Madras, who died August
2 CONC  10th 1918, aged 80 years. Both lie here.”
