2 DATE 7 MAR 1733
2 SOURCE @S6@
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE [BBC] gives his date of birth as 1673, and date of marriage as 1704. T
2 CONC hey agree on the date of death, as 1733.
2 CONT
2 CONT [RBB]: Charles Boileau, the 5th but eldest surviving son of Jacques Bo
2 CONC ileau, 10th Baron, born in 1673 was the rightful heir to the Barony of
2 CONC  Castelnau, but escaping from France when his father was imprisoned he
2 CONC  took refuge in England. Determining not to return to the land which h
2 CONC ad martyred his father, but to settle in England, and having entered t
2 CONC he British Army, firm to his Protestant faith, he formally made over t
2 CONC o his younger brother Maurice, all right and title to the family posse
2 CONC ssions in France, by a will, dated 2nd March, 1722, which was proved i
2 CONC n the Prerogative Court of Canterbury, 20 January, 1733. By Charles' r
2 CONC enumeration (repudiation?) of his country, on Maurice devolved likewis
2 CONC e the titles of the family, consequently he became the 11th Baron and
2 CONC 6th Lord of Castelnau, as stated above. In 1704 Charles visited Hollan
2 CONC d, and there married Marie Magdalen, daughter of Daniel Collot D'Escur
2 CONC y, Major in Lord Galloway's French Regiment of Cavalry in England. In
2 CONC 1711 he sold out of the army and took up his residence with his family
2 CONC  in Southampton. In 1722 he removed to Dublin and there died in 1733.
2 CONC He had 10 children of whom 4 died in infancy. Three died, leaving no f
2 CONC amily, one of whom, Charles Daniel (the eldest son born at Southampton
2 CONC  in 1711 and died in London in 1772) administered to his father's will
2 CONC  in 1733, the executors being deceased. His youngest daughter Marie, b
2 CONC orn in Dublin in 1723, maried in 1745 Henry Hardy, merchant in Cork, b
2 CONC y whom she had 10 children, but of these, only one, Henry Simeon, born
2 CONC  at Southampton in 1717 married in 1741 Magdalene Desbrisay, or De Bri
2 CONC zé - (a family distinguished in the annals of France - a daughter of M
2 CONC arechal le Duc de Brizé and niece of Cardinal Richelieu, by name Clare
2 CONC e Clemeull? de Maille, was married to the great Conde, Prince of the B
2 CONC lood Royal). [RBB got a lot of the above wrong. Granny got a bit confu
2 CONC sed, I think] For particulars of the families of both Marguerite and S
2 CONC imeon see the annexed charts [This, surely, refers to BBC, so Granny m
2 CONC ost likely saw the original, or a copy of it].
2 CONT
2 CONT Lart:  Left France with his brothers and entered the English army as e
2 CONC nsign in Farringdon’s Regiment, 1698; Lieut., 1703. Naturalised 4 May
2 CONC 1699 (“Charles Castelnau, born at Kismet (sic) in Languedoc in France,
2 CONC  son of James de Boileau by Frances his wife”). Taken prisoner by the
2 CONC French in 1704, and exchanged at Valenciennes 1 Feb 1709. Left the arm
2 CONC y in 1711 and lived at Southampton till 1722, when he went to Ireland,
2 CONC  where he became a wine merchant in Bride Street, Dublin. Died in Dubl
2 CONC in, 7 March, 1733.
2 CONT
2 CONT [C-D, III, 386]
2 CONT
2 CONT [BBB]: “Founder of the British branches of the family.   Left France i
2 CONC n 1691, and went to Brandenburg, where he joined the Musketeers.  Then
2 CONC , his uncle Charles de Vignolles having bought him an ensigncy in the
2 CONC British service, he went to England, and was commissioned on 25 May 16
2 CONC 94.  His name appears in a list soon afterwards as Cha. de Castelnau.
2 CONC   He m in Dublin, 30 Dec 1703, Marie-Madelaine, b 1679 dau of Daniel C
2 CONC ollot d'Escury, Seigneur de Landauran and Major in Lord Galway's Frenc
2 CONC h regiment of Cavalry.  They had ten or twelve children, but the names
2 CONC  of only ten are known, viz: Jacques-Daniel, Marguerite iv, Charles Da
2 CONC niel, Henry Charles, Marie-Louise, Georgette Madeleine, Simeon i, Pier
2 CONC re, Jean and Marie.    The children were all christened in the French
2 CONC form of their names, but the English form came in course of time to be
2 CONC  used for practical purposes.   All but the two eldest and the younges
2 CONC t of the children were born in Southampton, where there was a consider
2 CONC able Huguenot colony, and these were baptised in the Walloon Church th
2 CONC ere.
2 CONT Charles visited his relations in Nîmes in 1727 and 1730.  His wife d i
2 CONC n 1731, and he followed her in 1733.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Again from BBB:
2 CONT “Marguerite iv, eldest daughter of Charles and Marie-Madelaine, was th
2 CONC e first Boileau born in England, in London on 9th December 1709.  Char
2 CONC les then settled in Southampton, where there was a Huguenot colony.
2 CONC  He was a popular man and was known as 'the gallant refugee', which se
2 CONC ems to have referred to his social, rather than his military, qualitie
2 CONC s.   In 1717 he entered into an agreement with his younger brother Mau
2 CONC rice, under which, probably in consideration of some financial settlem
2 CONC ent, he renounced all right and title to to the property in France, wh
2 CONC ich would have been his inheritance had he remained there.   He did no
2 CONC t, be it noted, succeed to the title of Seigneur, for Maurice was the
2 CONC Seigneur de Castelnau, being so described on a copy of the Certificate
2 CONC  of Nobility in 1698.   Charles, however, continued to be known as Boi
2 CONC leau de Castelnau.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	He lost £ 20,000 speculating in the South Sea Bubble in 1720, and it
2 CONC was perhaps because of this that in 1722 he set up as a wholesale wine
2 CONC -merchant, the occupation of many Huguenots.   The venture was success
2 CONC ful and was carried on by his sons.   Charles died in Dublin in 1733,
2 CONC leaving four sons and two daughters living.”
