1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE [Lart, II, 13].
2 CONT My first source for Thomas and his children was [RBB] but I later foun
2 CONC d out a lot more in [BBB], which says:
2 CONT “Went to India under the auspices of his brother John Peter in 1780, b
2 CONC eing admitted as an attorney of the Supreme Court at Fort William in B
2 CONC engal, 23 Oct 1780, where he practised in that court, and also as a no
2 CONC tary public in Calcutta.   He became Under-Sheriff of Calcutta in 1791
2 CONC ;  this was a very lucrative and much sought-after appointment.    In
2 CONC 1794 he was appointed one of the four JPs, whose function it was to ad
2 CONC minister the municipal affairs of Calcutta;  and in 1800 he was appoin
2 CONC ted Police Magistrate of the City.
2 CONT
2 CONT He m 24 Mar 1796, Leah, dau of Lt Col Ebenezer Jessop, of a New Englan
2 CONC d family, who had been a loyalist officer in the American War of Indep
2 CONC endence and had lost all his property as a result;  going to India, he
2 CONC  obtained the appointment of Coroner of Calcutta.   Thomas and Leah ha
2 CONC d issue : Thomas Ebenezer John, Elizabeth Magdalene, Simeon John, Leah
2 CONC  Ann, John Peter v, John Theophilus iii and Alexander Henry Edmonstone
2 CONC .   By an unknown mother in Calcutta, in 1782, Thomas had previously h
2 CONC ad a s, Simeon Henry (qv).
2 CONT Thomas d at Calcutta on 11 Jun 1806, and his widow returned to England
2 CONC  and went to live at Bury St Edmunds with their children.”
2 CONT
2 CONT [BBB]: “Thomas i became an attorney in Dublin, and when he was 25 he w
2 CONC ent to India under his cousin John Peter's auspices, where he was admi
2 CONC tted as an attorney to the Supreme Court at Fort William in Bengal, pr
2 CONC actising there and also as a notary public in Calcutta.   It was he wh
2 CONC o introduced Lt Col Ebenezer Jessop, a King's Loyalist soldier from Ne
2 CONC w England, (who lost his property as a result), to a post in Calcutta,
2 CONC  and also married the latter's daughter Leah.   Col Jessop's son marri
2 CONC ed into the Bowes-Lyon family, thus giving Leah's descendants a distan
2 CONC t link with the Queen Mother [like we care].    The line then goes on
2 CONC with a string of regular officers in the Indian and British armies. “
