1 NOTE [ES, III, 805] gives his wife as Garsinde de Barcelona, which differs
2 CONC from the Garsinde Taillefer given by [CD], but it seems very likely it
2 CONC ’s the same Bérenger, Vicomte de Narbonne. I need to check in [ES, III
2 CONC , 341] but haven’t done so yet.
2 CONT
2 CONT [C-D, XIV, 808]. He was named, with his mother, his wife, and Raymond,
2 CONC  his son, in an act of 1032. He fought with Guifred de Cerdagne, Archb
2 CONC ishop of Narbonne, and complained to the Pope’s legate, at the Council
2 CONC  of Toulouse, in 1046. On 4 February 1066, he and his wife purchased,
2 CONC from Raymond-Bérenger, Count of Barcelona, a fief called Oser, and the
2 CONC  Castle of Solterre, for the sum of 180 gold écus of Barcelona, called
2 CONC  Manausses [?]. We have records of many of the homages he made, but th
2 CONC ey have no date. In 1049, he, his wife, and his children, gave to the
2 CONC Monastery of Saint-Michel du Mont-de-la-Cluse, in Italy, the lands of
2 CONC Montblaurez, in Ligurie, that they had obtained by an exchange between
2 CONC  Raymond, Count of Toulouse, and Matfred his grandfather. They also ga
2 CONC ve, the preceding year, to the Canons of Narbonne, all the tithes of f
2 CONC el [?] and fishing in the Vicountship of Narbonne.
