1 NOTE [ES, III, 738]. Comte d’Albon, Dauphin de Viennois
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://53.1911encyclopedia.org/D/DA/DAUPHIN.htm
2 CONT DAUPHIN (Lat. Delphinus), an ancient feudal title in France, borne onl
2 CONC y by the counts and dauphins of Vienne, the dauphins of Auvergne, and
2 CONC from 1364 by the eldest sons of the kings of France. The origin of thi
2 CONC s curious title is obscure and has been the subject of much ingenious
2 CONC controversy; but it now seems clear that it was in the first instance
2 CONC a proper name. Among the Norsemen, and in the countries colonized by t
2 CONC hem, the name Dolphin or Dolfin (doUr, a wound ) was fairly common, e.
2 CONC g. in the north of England; thus a Dolffn is mentioned among the tenan
2 CONC tsin-chief in Domesday Book, and there was a Dolphin, lord of Carlisle
2 CONC , towards, the end of the 11th century. It has thus been conjectured b
2 CONC y some that the dauphins of Vienne derived their title from Teutonic s
2 CONC ources through Germany. But in the south, too, the name not necessaril
2 CONC y derived from the same root was not unknown, though exceedingly rare,
2 CONC  and was moreover illustrated by two conspicuous figures in the Cathol
2 CONC ic martyrology: St Delphinus, bishop of Bordeaux from 380 to 404, and
2 CONC St Annemundus, surnamed Dalfinus, bishop of Lyons from C. 650 to 657.
2 CONC Whatever its origin, this name was borne by Guigo, or Guigue IV. (d. 1
2 CONC 142) ,count of Albon and Grenoble, as an additional name, during the l
2 CONC ifetime of his father, and was also adopted by his son Guigue V. Beatr
2 CONC ice, daughter and heiress of Guigue V., whose second husband was Hugh
2 CONC III., duke of Burgundy, bestowed the name on their son Andr, to recall
2 CONC  his descent from the ancient house of the counts of Albon, and in the
2 CONC  charters he is called sometimes Andreas Dalphinus, sometimes Dalphinu
2 CONC s simply, but his style is still count of Albon and Vienne. His succes
2 CONC sors Guigue VI. (d. 1270) and John I. (d. 1282) call themselves someti
2 CONC mes Delphinus, sometimes Delphini, the name being obviously treated as
2 CONC  a patronymic, and in the latter form it was borne by the sons of the
2 CONC reigning dauphin. But even under Guigue VI. foreigners had begun to co
2 CONC nfuse the name with a title of dignity, an imperial diploma of 1248 de
2 CONC scribing Guigue as Guigo Daiphinus Viennensis.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was not until the third dynasty, founded by the marriage of Anne, h
2 CONC eiress of John I., with Humbert, lord of La Tour du Pin, that dauphin
2 CONC became definitely established as a title. Humbert not only assumed the
2 CONC  name of Delphinus, but styled himself regularly Dauphin of the Vienno
2 CONC is (Dalphinus Viennensis), and in a treaty concluded in 1285 between H
2 CONC umbert and Robert, duke of Burgundy, the word delphinatus (Dauphiri) a
2 CONC ppears for the first time, as a synonym for comitatus (county). In 134
2 CONC 9 Humbert II., the last of his race, sold Dauphin to Charles of Valois
2 CONC , who, when he became king of France in 1364, transferred it to his el
2 CONC dest son. From that time the eldest sons of the kings of France were a
2 CONC lways either actual or titular dauphins of the Viennois. The canting a
2 CONC rms of a dolphin, which they quartered with the royal fleurs de lys, w
2 CONC ere originally assumed by Dauphin, count of Clermont, instead of the a
2 CONC rms of Auvergne (the earliest extant example is appended to a deed of
2 CONC 1199), and from him they were borrowed by the counts of the Viennois.
2 CONC Guigue VI. used this device on his secret seal from his accession, the
2 CONC  earliest extant example dating from 1237, but, though no specimens ha
2 CONC ve survived, M. Prudhomme thinks it probable that the dolphin. was als
2 CONC o borne by Andr Dauphin. It was also assumed by Guigue V., count of Fo
2 CONC rez (1203-I241), a descendant of Guigue Raymond of the Viennois, count
