2 DATE 9 SEP 1087
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE [ES II:81]
2 CONT
2 CONT from Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT William I (about 1027 or 1028[1] – 9 September 1087), better known as
2 CONC William the Conqueror (French: Guillaume le Conquérant), was Duke of N
2 CONC ormandy from 1035 and King of England from late 1066 to his death. Wil
2 CONC liam is sometimes also referred to as "William II" in relation to his
2 CONC position as the second Duke of Normandy of that name. In particular, b
2 CONC efore his conquest of England, he was known as "William the Bastard" (
2 CONC French: Guillaume le Bâtard) because of the illegitimacy of his birth.
2 CONT
2 CONT To press his claim to the English crown, William invaded England in 10
2 CONC 66, leading an army of Normans, Bretons, Flemings, and Frenchmen to vi
2 CONC ctory over the English forces of King Harold Godwinson (who died in th
2 CONC e conflict) at the Battle of Hastings, and suppressed subsequent Engli
2 CONC sh revolts in what has become known as the Norman Conquest.
2 CONT
2 CONT His reign, which brought Norman-French culture to England, had an impa
2 CONC ct on the subsequent course of England in the Middle Ages. The details
2 CONC  of that impact and the extent of the changes have been debated by sch
2 CONC olars for over a century. In addition to the obvious change of ruler,
2 CONC his reign also saw a programme of building and fortification, changes
2 CONC to the English language, a shift in the upper levels of society and th
2 CONC e church, and adoption of some aspects of continental church reform. M
2 CONC ore controversial are possible changes in law, royal administration, t
2 CONC rade, agriculture, the peasantry, women's roles and rights, and educat
2 CONC ion
1 HYPERLINK http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_I_of_England
