1 DEAT Y
2 DATE 2 NOV 1083
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE [ES II:5]
2 CONT
2 CONT from Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT Matilda of Flanders (c. 1031 – 2 November 1083) was Queen consort of t
2 CONC he Kingdom of England and the wife of William I the Conqueror.
2 CONT She was the daughter of Baldwin V, Count of Flanders, and Adèle (1000-
2 CONC 1078/9), daughter of Robert II of France.
2 CONT At 4'2" (127 cm) tall, Matilda was England's smallest queen, according
2 CONC  to the Guinness Book of Records. According to legend, Matilda (or "Ma
2 CONC ud") told the representative of William, Duke of Normandy (later king
2 CONC of England as William the Conqueror), who had come asking for her hand
2 CONC , that she was far too high-born (being descended from King Alfred the
2 CONC  Great of England) to consider marrying a bastard. When that was repea
2 CONC ted to him, William rode from Normandy to Bruges, found Matilda on her
2 CONC  way to church, dragged her off her horse by her long braids, threw he
2 CONC r down in the street in front of her flabbergasted attendants, and the
2 CONC n rode off. Another version of the story states that William rode to M
2 CONC atilda's father's house in Lille, threw her to the ground in her room
2 CONC (again, by the braids), and hit her (or violently shook her) before le
2 CONC aving. Naturally Baldwin took offense at this but, before they drew sw
2 CONC ords, Matilda settled the matter by deciding to marry him, and even a
2 CONC papal ban (on the grounds of consanguinity) did not dissuade her. They
2 CONC  were married in 1053.
2 CONT There were rumours that Matilda had been in love with the English amba
2 CONC ssador to Flanders, a Saxon named Brihtric, who declined her advances.
2 CONC  Whatever the truth of the matter, years later when she was acting as
2 CONC Regent for William in England, she used her authority to confiscate Br
2 CONC ihtric's lands and throw him into prison, where he died.
2 CONT When William was preparing to invade England, Matilda outfitted a ship
2 CONC , the Mora, out of her own money and gave it to him. For many years it
2 CONC  was thought that she had some involvement in the creation of the Baye
2 CONC ux Tapestry (commonly called La Tapisserie de la Reine Mathilde in Fre
2 CONC nch), but historians no longer believe that; it seems to have been com
2 CONC missioned by William's half-brother Odo, Bishop of Bayeux, and made by
2 CONC  English artists in Kent.
2 CONT Matilda bore William eleven children, and he was believed to have been
2 CONC  faithful to her, at least up until the time their son Robert rebelled
2 CONC  against his father and Matilda sided with Robert against William. Aft
2 CONC er she died, in 1083 at the age of 51, William became tyrannical, and
2 CONC people blamed it on his having lost her. Contrary to the belief that s
2 CONC he was buried at St. Stephen's, also called l'Abbaye-aux-Hommes in Cae
2 CONC n, Normandy, where William was eventually buried, she is intombed at l
2 CONC 'Abbaye aux Dames, which is the Sainte-Trinité church, also in Caen. O
2 CONC f particular interest is the 11th century slab, a sleek black stone de
2 CONC corated with her epitaph, marking her grave at the rear of the church.
2 CONC  It is of special note since the grave marker for William was replaced
2 CONC  as recently as the beginning of the 19th century. In 1961, their grav
2 CONC es were opened and their bones measured, proving their physical statur
2 CONC es.
1 HYPERLINK http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matilda_of_Flanders
