1 NOTE from Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT William I Longsword (French: Guillaume Longue-Épée, Latin: Willermus L
2 CONC onga Spata, Scandinavian: Vilhjálmr Langaspjót; 893 – 17 December 942)
2 CONC  was the second Duke of Normandy from his father's death until his own
2 CONC  assassination. The title dux (duke) was not in use at the time and ha
2 CONC s been applied to early Norman rulers retroactively; William actually
2 CONC used the title comes (count).
2 CONT Little is known about his early years. He was born in Bayeux or Rouen
2 CONC to Rollo and his wife Poppa. All that is known of Poppa is that she wa
2 CONC s a Christian, and the daughter to Berengar of Rennes, the previous lo
2 CONC rd of Brittania Nova, which eventually became western Normandy. Accord
2 CONC ing to the William's planctus, he was baptised a Christian.
2 CONT William succeeded Rollo sometime around 927. It appears that he faced
2 CONC a rebellion early in his reign, from Normans who felt he had become to
2 CONC o Gallicised. Subsequent years are obscure. In 939 William became invo
2 CONC lved in a war with Arnulf I of Flanders, which soon became intertwined
2 CONC  with the other conflicts troubling the reign of Louis IV. He was kill
2 CONC ed by followers of Arnulf while at a meeting to settle their conflict.
2 CONC  His son Richard the Fearless, child of his first wife, Sprota, succee
2 CONC ded him. William also left a widow, Liègard (Liutgard), who died in 98
2 CONC 5.
