1 NOTE Rollo was a Viking leader of contested origin. Dudo of St. Quentin, in
2 CONC  his De moribus et actis primorum Normannorum ducum (Latin), tells of
2 CONC a powerful Danish nobleman at loggerheads with the king of Denmark, wh
2 CONC o had two sons, Gurim and Rollo; upon his death, Rollo was expelled an
2 CONC d Gurim killed. William of Jumièges also mentions Rollo's prehistory i
2 CONC n his Gesta Normannorum Ducum, but states that he was from the Danish
2 CONC town of Fakse. Wace, writing some 300 years after the event in his Rom
2 CONC an de Rou, also mentions the two brothers (as Rou and Garin), as does
2 CONC the Orkneyinga Saga.
2 CONT Norwegian and Icelandic historians identified this Rollo with a son of
2 CONC  Rognvald Eysteinsson, Earl of Møre, in Western Norway, based on medie
2 CONC val Norwegian and Icelandic sagas that mention a Ganger Hrolf (Hrolf,
2 CONC the Walker). The oldest source of this version is the Latin Historia N
2 CONC orvegiae, written in Norway at the end of the 12th century. This Hrolf
2 CONC  fell foul of the Norwegian king Harald Fairhair, and became a Jarl in
2 CONC  Normandy. The nickname of that character came from being so big that
2 CONC no horse could carry him.
2 CONT The question of Rollo's Danish or Norwegian origins was a matter of he
2 CONC ated dispute between Norwegian and Danish historians of the 19th and e
2 CONC arly 20th century, particularly in the run-up to Normandy's 1000-year-
2 CONC anniversary in 1911. Today, historians still disagree on this question
2 CONC , but most would now agree that a certain conclusion can never be reac
2 CONC hed.
