1 NOTE in http://www.chichesterquakers.org/John%20Barton.htm:
2 CONT
2 CONT Barton's brother-in-law, Stephen Hack, died in February 1823 after a l
2 CONC ong ill- ness. He was buried at the Quaker ground next to the Meeting
2 CONC House. At the service that followed, William Allen, the Quaker philant
2 CONC hropist from London, gave a much admired address. In June, John Marsh
2 CONC wrote, 'Mr Bliss was married to Miss (Elizabeth) Hack, a union that ha
2 CONC d for some time been talked of from the attention he had been paying i
2 CONC n that direction since Mr Hack's death.' Rev. Bliss was not a Quaker,
2 CONC but Elizabeth Hack's brother, Thomas, had attend- ed the Grammar schoo
2 CONC l 'conducted by Mr Bliss, an Oxford man, where chiefly Latin was taugh
2 CONC t'. In 1824 Rev. Bliss was appointed curate of Funtington church and h
2 CONC is school in Chichester closed.
2 CONT
