1 NOTE Became guardian of her sister's children.
2 CONT Mentioned in http://www.chichesterquakers.org/John%20Barton.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Also in "The Hack Family Documents by Chris Durrant", Figure O.9
2 CONT
2 CONT DBHB: from the dates, she may be a child from Sarah's second marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Her death is in Emily's Memoir p100:
2 CONT "In May 1892 the first great break came to us all; and the beloved 'Au
2 CONC nt Rick', who had 'mothered' eight of the Barton family, and 'grand-mo
2 CONC thered' 44 of the rising generation received her call Home, and entere
2 CONC d into her rest, aged 84. This rushing XXth Century will see no more o
2 CONC f her calm dignified type we fear.  You always felt in a cultured pres
2 CONC ence when with her; and that she was one whose chief life was lived in
2 CONC  an atmosphere above this passing world; although she had no religious
2 CONC  phraseology.  Her sound judgment, unswayed by her heart, was such tha
2 CONC t every member of the family was guided by her more or less.  She unit
2 CONC ed a firm hand over her household with the gentlest manner, which made
2 CONC  her truly an ideal Lady 'Mother'. Her servants adored her; and it was
2 CONC  said they never left her but to marry or die.  Dear old 'Wrapson' - w
2 CONC hom she pensioned - lived 52 years in the family; and died about '98 -
2 CONC  aged I think 67.
2 CONT She loved to talk of your Grandfather Barton – her only sister's Husba
2 CONC nd - to whom she felt she owed much of her education; having lived all
2 CONC  his married life with him, and his widowed life also; and lived on af
2 CONC ter his death with his children by his request.  She travelled abroad
2 CONC with him and her sister, when she was 20, in the days of Post chaises,
2 CONC  & 'vetturinos', through France and Italy; and was his companion out r
2 CONC iding when they settled in Hants, being a good horse-woman."
