2 CONT
2 CONT "HACK, JOHN BARTON (1805-1884), farmer, merchant and accountant, was b
2 CONC orn on 2 July 1805 at Chichester, England, the eldest son of Stephen H
2 CONC ack, banker, and his wife Maria, née Barton. He was educated at Southg
2 CONC ate, Middlesex, went into the leather trade and built up a successful
2 CONC business in Sussex. On 9 July 1827 he married Bridget Watson. After an
2 CONC  illness affecting his lungs, he was advised to seek a warmer climate,
2 CONC  and with his wife, six children and his younger brother he sailed in
2 CONC the Isabella for Launceston, where he spent a month buying livestock a
2 CONC nd equipment. He arrived at Holdfast Bay in February 1837. With immens
2 CONC e energy he set out to make money in preparation for taking up farming
2 CONC  when land became available. He was one of the first to put up a 'Mann
2 CONC ing' portable cottage in Adelaide and soon made rich earnings with his
2 CONC  bullock wagon, garden and dairy; he also imported foodstuffs at good
2 CONC profit and arranged with store-keepers to dispose of his English merch
2 CONC andise. At the sale of land in Adelaide in March 1837 he bought sixty
2 CONC acre-lots (24 ha), and he served on the committee for naming the stree
2 CONC ts. For cutting the channel in the original Port Adelaide, he charged
2 CONC the government £800, double the price of his tender. His early jubilan
2 CONC t letters to England were read by the emigration agent, Henry Watson,
2 CONC in a lecture on South Australia to the Chichester Mechanics' Institute
2 CONC  in November 1837, and printed; but he had reverses. Three shipments o
2 CONC f sheep from Launceston suffered heavy casualties; a fourth, to be pai
2 CONC d for when landed, was entirely lost in the wreck of the Isabella"
2 CONT
2 CONT Birthday and much much more in "The Hack Family Documents by Chris Dur
2 CONC rant" (p6)
