1 NAME Ann / Anne Woodrouffe /Smith/
2 DISPLAY Ann / Anne Woodrouffe Smith
2 GIVN Ann / Anne
2 MIDDLE Woodrouffe
2 SURN Smith
1 POSITION -15180,-3620
2 BOUNDARYRECT -15217,-3587,-15143,-3681
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1791
1 DEAT Y
2 DATE OCT 1821
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE Mentioned in http://www.chichesterquakers.org/John%20Barton.htm:
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1811 John Barton married Anne Woodrouffe Smith, a Quaker. During th
2 CONC e marriage he received an income of £2,000 a year via his wealthy fath
2 CONC er-in-law and about 1814 he gave up his business life. In November Joh
2 CONC n Marsh wrote, 'Mr Barton, a young Quaker, who has lately married a yo
2 CONC ung, handsome and accomplished woman with an immense fortune, having a
2 CONC sked me to drink tea with them, to meet Mr Holland, I accordingly went
2 CONC  and was much pleased with Mrs Barton, who appeared to be a very sensi
2 CONC ble woman, as was her sister, Miss Smith, though not as handsome as Mr
2 CONC s Barton.' The following year Elizabeth Fry and her sister Priscilla G
2 CONC urney, 'valued friends' of the Hacks, visited Chichester and stayed wi
2 CONC th the Bartons. Fry visited again in 1816, when a family letter descri
2 CONC bes her apparently campaigning at two 'well attended public meetings'.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1818 John Barton had travelled on the Continent, returning with an
2 CONC idealised picture of Swiss rural society. On 30 Jan 1821 he visited Ri
2 CONC cardo, the economist, who read him an extra chapter that he had writte
2 CONC n for the third ( edition of his essay on Population. In this, he conc
2 CONC eded to the view expressed in Barton's first pamphlet, that the use of
2 CONC  machinery might reduce the demand for labour. It is clear from his jo
2 CONC urnal that Barton was thinking of writing a major work on the 'Science
2 CONC  of Political Economy at Large', as in February 1821 he wrote, 'This,
2 CONC however, is a distant project. Its execution must be deferred till : h
2 CONC ave had an opportunity of collecting a stock of facts.' This might hav
2 CONC e come to fruition had his not wife died the following October. Anne's
2 CONC  death also resulted in John losing more than a third of his income, a
2 CONC s he would not accept the £100,000 due to him on his wife's death. Thi
2 CONC s money was eventually claimed by his children, who shared their mater
2 CONC nal grandfather's estate
2 CONT
2 CONT Name given as Ann in 'Barton Family Tree' from Robert Barton's biograp
2 CONC hy of John Barton of Chicester. Same source gives years as 1791-1822.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Rev. John Barton's Family Tree book, p1, name spelled Anne, and yea
2 CONC r of death = 1824. On p10, year of death = 1822!
