1 NAME John /Barton/
2 DISPLAY John Barton (of Ive-Gill)
3 FORMAT Custom
2 GIVN John
2 SURN Barton
2 ALIA of Ive-Gill
1 PICTURES @pic00134@, @pic00135@, @pic00136@, @pic00137@, @pic00138@, @pic00149@
2 CONC , @pic00150@, @pic00174@, @pic00175@, @pic00176@, @pic00177@
2 PRIMARY @pic00174@
1 POSITION -15350,-3210
2 BOUNDARYRECT -15382,-3177,-15317,-3271
1 DISPLAY
2 COLORS
3 GENDER
4 FILL #00FF00
1 SEX M
1 DEAT Y
2 DATE 1747
2 DISPOSITION
3 TYPE Burial
3 DATE 20 NOV 1747
3 PLAC Dalston
4 _XREF @place01232@
2 NOTE  The Parish registers of Dalston, Cumberland. Edited with an introduct
3 CONC ion and indexes by James Wilson . Vol.2: Baptisms, 1679-1812; marriage
3 CONC s, 1679-1812; burials, 1678-1812; 1895. p255:
3 CONT "1747 / Novemb 20 John Barton of Hivegill buried"
3 CONT
3 CONT I have his will in which he leaves everything to his wife Ann.
3 CONT I also have her will, in which she splits their estate between  younge
3 CONC r son John, daughters Margaret, Jane and Mabel, and eldest son Bernard
3 CONC  who is also executor.
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE Mentioned in "Miscellanies FitzGerald including Memoirs of Bernard Bar
2 CONC ton"
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://maps.google.co.uk/maps?hl=en&q=ivegill&ie=UTF8&split=0&gl=uk&ei
2 CONC =_SdbSp6NLZjMjAeGnIgb&z=11&iwloc=A
2 CONT
2 CONT " Thy cordial approval of my brother John's
2 CONT hearty wish to bring us back to the simple
2 CONT habits of the olden time, induces me to ask
2 CONT thee if I mentioned in either of my late
2 CONT letters the curious old papers he stumbled
2 CONT on in hunting through the repositories of
2 CONT our late excellent spinster sister? I quite
2 CONT forget whether I did or not; so I will not
2 CONT at a venture repeat all the items. But he
2 CONT found an inventory of the goods and chattels
2 CONT of our great-grandfather, John Barton of
2 CONT Ive-Gill, a little hamlet about five or seven
2 CONT miles from Carlisle ; by which it seems our
2 CONT progenitor was one of those truly patriarchal
2 CONT personages, a Cumbrian statesman living
2 CONT on his own little estate, and drawing from it
2 CONT all things needful for himself and his family.
2 CONT I will be bound for it my good brother was
2 CONT more gratified at finding his earliest trace-
2 CONT able ancestor such an one than if he had
2 CONT found him in the college of heralds with
2 CONT gules purpure and argent emblazoned as his
2 CONT bearings. The total amount of his stock,
2 CONT independent of house, land, and any money
2 CONT he might have, seems by the valuation to
2 CONT have been £61 6s., and the copy of his
2 CONT admission to his little estate gives the fine as
2 CONT £5, so that I suppose its annual value was
2 CONT then estimated at £2 15s. This was
2 CONT about a century back. Yet this man was
2 CONT the chief means of building the little chapel
2 CONT in the dale, still standing. (He was a church-
2 CONT man.) I doubt not he was a fine simple-
2 CONT hearted noble-minded yeoman, in his day,
2 CONT and I am very proud of him. Why did his
2 CONT son, my grandfather, after whom I was
2 CONT named, ever leave that pleasant dale, and go
2 CONT and set up a manufactory in Carlisle; in-
2 CONT venting a piece of machinery J for which he
2 CONT had a medal from the Royal Society? so
2 CONT says Pennant. Methinks he had better
2 CONT have abode in the old grey stone, slate-
2 CONT covered homestead on the banks of that
2 CONT pretty brooklet the Ive ! But I bear his
2 CONT name, so I will not quarrel with his memory."
2 CONT
2 CONT Date of burial given in The Parish registers of Dalston, Cumberland. E
2 CONC dited with an introduction and indexes by James Wilson . Vol.2: Baptis
2 CONC ms, 1679-1812; marriages, 1679-1812; burials, 1678-1812; 1895. p255:
2 CONT "1747 / Novemb 20 John Barton of Hivegill buried"
2 CONT
2 CONT I have his will in which he leaves everything to his wife Ann. His sig
2 CONC nature on this will (the whole thing is written in matching handwritin
2 CONC g, though not too professionally) closely matches the signature of a J
2 CONC ohn Barton (alongside brother William and mother Isabell) on the 1720
2 CONC execution of the will of another John Barton.
2 CONT
2 CONT I also have his wife Ann's will, in which she splits their estate betw
2 CONC een  younger son John, daughters Margaret, Jane and Mabel, and eldest
2 CONC son Bernard who is also executor. This, we must assume, is the invento
2 CONC r Bernard.
2 CONT
2 CONT
