1 NAME Charles /Elliott/
2 DISPLAY Charles Elliott (cabinet-maker)
3 FORMAT Custom
2 GIVN Charles
2 SURN Elliott
1 POSITION -9750,-3190
2 BOUNDARYRECT -9790,-3157,-9710,-3251
1 DISPLAY
2 COLORS
3 GENDER
4 FILL #00FF00
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 CEREMONYTYPE Baptism
2 DATE 12 JUN 1752
2 BAPTISM
3 DATE 7 JUN 1751
3 PLAC Burnham, Essex
4 _XREF @place01149@
1 DEAT Y
2 DATE 15 OCT 1832
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE from
2 CONT The Elliots: The Story of a Border Clan, a genealogical history. By Th
2 CONC e Dowager Lady Eliott of Stobs and Sir Arthur Eliott, 11th Baronet of
2 CONC Stobs. Published 1974. ISBN 0 85422 081 X.
2 CONT
2 CONT p170:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Charles Elliott, the only surviving son of Charles Edward Elliot, was
2 CONC  baptised at Burnham, Essex on 7th June 1751. At the age of eighteen h
2 CONC e went to London and trained as a cabinet-maker. He quickly showed an
2 CONC aptitude for the business, in 1774 becoming a partner in the firm of D
2 CONC avis & Elliott of 97 New Bond Street (now numbered 104). A year later,
2 CONC  on 21st July 1775, he married Sarah Ann, daughter of Rev. Dr. Sherman
2 CONC  of St. James's, Piccadilly. Charles prospered in his trade, making fu
2 CONC rniture for the Royal Family and becoming one of the most noted cabine
2 CONC t-makers of the period. He eventually had two houses, Grove House in C
2 CONC lapham and Westfield Lodge in Brighton. Sarah Ann died in 1784 and Cha
2 CONC rles married 2ndly, on 2nd December 1785, Eling, daughter of Rev. Henr
2 CONC y Venn, Vicar of Huddersfield and Rector of Yelling. Charles Elliott b
2 CONC ecame the representative of the Elliotts of Larriston in 1803 followin
2 CONC g the death of Major-General William Elliot. Charles died on 15th Octo
2 CONC ber 1832, having had issue by Sarah Ann Sherman three sons and two dau
2 CONC ghters."
2 CONT
2 CONT NJB has:
2 CONT  a picture of CE
2 CONT  a photo of one of his chairs
2 CONT  copies of bills of work and images relating to work Charles Elliott d
2 CONC id for Lord Nelson's funeral.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Rev. John Barton's Family Tree book, p7: gives d.o.b. 12 June 1752
2 CONC and more details.
2 CONT
