1 NAME Charles Alfred /Elliott/
2 DISPLAY Sir Charles Alfred Elliott K.C.S.I.
3 FORMAT %T %F %M %L (%L2) %S
2 GIVN Charles
2 MIDDLE Alfred
2 SURN Elliott
2 NPFX Sir
2 NSFX K.C.S.I.
1 POSITION 4320,-610
2 GENOMAP Other Eliotts, Ellots, Elliotts
2 BOUNDARYRECT 4283,-577,4357,-671
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 8 DEC 1835
1 DEAT Y
2 DATE 1911
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE from
2 CONT The Elliots: The Story of a Border Clan, a genealogical history. By Th
2 CONC e Dowager Lady Eliott of Stobs and Sir Arthur Eliott, 11th Baronet of
2 CONC Stobs. Published 1974. ISBN 0 85422 081 X.
2 CONT p177
2 CONT
2 CONT "educated at Harrow and Trinity College, Cambridge. He entered the Ind
2 CONC ian Civil Service in 1856, became a member of the Viceroy's Executive
2 CONC Council in 1888 and was finally appointed Lieutenant Governor of Benga
2 CONC l in 1890. He served in this appointment for five years. After returni
2 CONC ng to England he acted as Financial Chairman of the London School Boar
2 CONC d and was later in 1906 co-opted as a member of the London County Coun
2 CONC cil. He died on 20th June 1911."
2 CONT
2 CONT BUT!
2 CONT
2 CONT On p178 it says:
2 CONT "Sir Charles Elliott married 2ndly 1887 Alice Louisa (d. 1930), daught
2 CONC er of Thomas Gaussen of Guernsey, and died 28 May 1911,"
2 CONT
2 CONT Which date is it? I'll report only the year of death due to this uncer
2 CONC tainty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Emily's memoir, p48:
2 CONT "I spent a quiet month at Fatehgarh, N.W. Provinces, with my cousins t
2 CONC he C.A.Elliotts (34) keeping the Baby-boy, while Father took Arthur, n
2 CONC ow nearly 3 years old, to Simla.  He wrote me that Arthur walked & rod
2 CONC e famously everywhere, proving quite a companion to him.  Our friends,
2 CONC  Dr. & Mrs.Farquhar put them up, in one of the houses occupied by Lord
2 CONC  Lawrence's suite, as Dr.P. was his private medical attendant.  Meanwh
2 CONC ile my cousin, Louise Elliott (1st wife of the present Sir Charles Ell
2 CONC iott) and I, enjoyed ourselves quietly with our little ones - their 'M
2 CONC innie' being a little older than our 'Johnnie'. Harry & Alfy, whom you
2 CONC  all know, & Frank, were not then born."
2 CONT
2 CONT 'Minnie' must be Mary (Harry=Henry, Alfy=Alfred), but this suggests th
2 CONC at the birth year given for Mary in 'The Elliots' may be in error, sin
2 CONC ce the above took place in 1867.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Rev. John Barton's Family Tree book, p19,33:
2 CONT note:
2 CONT "Knighted K.C.S.I. 1887, Lt Govr Bengal 1890-1895"
1 FAMC @fam155588963@
1 FAMS @fam155588965@
1 FAMS @fam155588966@
0 @ind1941642798@ INDI
1 NAME Julius Marshall /Elliott/
2 DISPLAY Rev. Julius Marshall Elliott
3 FORMAT Custom
2 GIVN Julius
2 MIDDLE Marshall
2 SURN Elliott
2 NPFX Rev.
1 POSITION 4530,-610
2 GENOMAP Other Eliotts, Ellots, Elliotts
2 BOUNDARYRECT 4493,-577,4567,-671
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 24 OCT 1841
1 DEAT Y
2 DATE 27 JUL 1869
1 ISDEAD Y
1 NOTE from
2 CONT The Elliots: The Story of a Border Clan, a genealogical history. By Th
2 CONC e Dowager Lady Eliott of Stobs and Sir Arthur Eliott, 11th Baronet of
2 CONC Stobs. Published 1974. ISBN 0 85422 081 X.
2 CONT p177
2 CONT
2 CONT killed in the Alps: Emily's memoir p55-56:
2 CONT "In August [1869] we heard of the sudden death of my cousin Julius Ell
2 CONC iott (27A), killed whilst ascending the Shackhorn.  It is said that he
2 CONC  fell 2000 ft.; and his spirit must have returned unto Him who gave it
2 CONC  ere he touched the deep bed of snow, in which after three days' searc
2 CONC h his body was found: no bone broken - not even a little bottle of gly
2 CONC cerine in his pocket.  He was 27, a member of the Alpine Club, and had
2 CONC  but recently taken his late Father, Mr.Henry Venn Elliott's, post at
2 CONC St.Mary's Church, Brighton.  After a parting prayer with his Scripture
2 CONC  reader before he left B. for his usual summer ramble on the Alps, Jul
2 CONC ius said to this man "There is one prayer in our Litany that I can nev
2 CONC er offer 'from sudden death Good Lord deliver us', for I always think
2 CONC how happy it would be to die thus." Strangely prophetic words.  His bo
2 CONC dy was laid by loving ones summoned from England, in Grindelwald churc
2 CONC hyard; & the grave, & adjoining marble slab with a beautiful inscripti
2 CONC on in German on the church wall, are well known to travellers.  Strang
2 CONC e that he should lie so near to his very dear College friend, & cousin
2 CONC , my brother Arthur, whom we laid to rest 7 years before at G'Steig, n
2 CONC r.Interlaken, as before mentioned."
2 CONT
2 CONT In Rev. John Barton's Family Tree book, p19, with note: "of St. Mary's
2 CONC , Brighton. Killed on Shrekhorn, buried Grindelwald"
