2 CONT
2 CONT Transactions of the Cumberland & Westmorland Antiquarian & Archaeologi
2 CONC cal Society, Volume 5, 1905 p96-97 mentions a Bernard Barton of Ivegil
2 CONC l licensed as reader and schoolmaster in 1713, and ordained deacon in
2 CONC 1715:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Of Highhead, in Dalston parish, valued in 1739 at £13, Nicolson and B
2 CONC urn say that a license was granted in 1358 to build a chapel and have
2 CONC a chaplain, but that it had never been made parochial : they christene
2 CONC d and buried at the mother church, and holy communion was administered
2 CONC  at the chapel by the vicar (of Dalston) every Maundy Thursday. In Bis
2 CONC hop Nicolson's time there was sometimes a curate at Highhead, sometime
2 CONC s a reader. On June 7, 1707, it appears by the bishop's register that
2 CONC Wm. Pool, A.M., deacon, was licensed as curate and schoolmaster, but h
2 CONC e did not remain there, for on Aug. 7, 1707, the bishop records in his
2 CONC  diary, "Mr Kanier, wth y6 new reader of High- Head, who was sent, wit
2 CONC h a Letter, to sue for Mrs Miller's concurrence" ; and on October 1, 1
2 CONC 707, Sam. Relph (literate) was licensed schoolmaster and reader. He wa
2 CONC s afterwards, in 1726, ordained deacon as curate of Allhallows. In 171
2 CONC 3, Bernard Barton was licensed as reader and schoolmaster, and in 1715
2 CONC  Bernard Barton, of Ivegill, was ordained deacon; no fresh license was
2 CONC  issued to him. In 1721 Wm. Miles was ordained deacon and licensed as
2 CONC schoolmaster and curate. In 1724 the bishop's register contains a nomi
2 CONC nation by the trustees of High-Head chapel of Isaac Wilson to be schoo
2 CONC lmaster and reader : this is followed by the bishop's certificate that
2 CONC  he had subscribed the declaration prescribed in the  Act of Uniformit
2 CONC y. On June 10, 1745, Joseph Hudson, M.A., was licensed as curate ; on
2 CONC Sept. 23, 1745, Joseph Blaine, clerk, was licensed; in 1746 Thomas Sta
2 CONC ck, literate, was ordained deacon ; and in 1749, John Scott, literate
2 CONC ; he was ordained priest in 1752. This and Mungrisdale (mentioned belo
2 CONC w) are the only cases which I have noticed at that period of such a ch
2 CONC apelry giving a title to priest's orders."
2 CONT
2 CONT This may be him.
